W1.2-0 - in progress
-------------
Changes since W1.1-3.  NOTHING IN THIS SECTION HAS BEEN RELEASED.

20 Oct 26  A SERVER ON ANOTHER SD MACHINE THAT GOES SILENT IS NOW GIVEN UP ON AT NETREPLY (AND NETWAIT FOR THE TLS HANDSHAKE), IN EVERY CASE WE KNOW OF.

             What        READ.SOCKET on a TLS socket (what SDNet uses to talk to another SD server) waited for the time it was given only while
                        nothing had arrived. After the handshake a TLS 1.3 server sends its session tickets, and a server or a link that stops in
                        the middle of a record leaves half of one; either made the socket readable, and the read behind it then waited with no limit
                        of its own. Measured on a private SD with NETREPLY=3: a server that completed the handshake and never sent its acknowledgement
                        byte was waited for 5.0 s (it was the test server's own close), one that sent half a record and then nothing 12 s. The read now
                        never blocks: it waits on the connection for the time that is LEFT, so the tickets and the half record cost what they used
                        and no more. The same server is given up on in 3.0 s now, the half records in 3.0 and 3.1 s, and a server that answers
                        1.5 s late inside a limit of 3 is still read. Second, the TLS handshake of a connection was bound by 15 seconds fixed, whatever
                        NETWAIT said (the Administrator pages said NETWAIT bounds "the connection"): a server that accepts the connection and never
                        answers the hello was waited for 15 s with NETWAIT=2, and is given up on in 2.0 s now. NETWAIT bounds the TCP connect and then
                        the handshake, each. Third, a reply that stopped in the middle of its BODY (the header read, part of the record not) was
                        reported as "a reply that cannot be used (answer -1)", message 12509, and the connection was kept although its stream was out
                        of step; it is the same lost connection as a reply that never started now: message 12502 for a read, 12514 for a change, and
                        the connection is dropped.
             Why it      A pinned server that hangs held the session that was waiting on it for as long as it stayed that way, or until its own idle
             matters    limit; the owner of that session could not stop it with NETREPLY, which is what the setting is for.
             Does not    It does not make a slow server a failed one: a server that answers inside NETREPLY is read as before. A change that got no
                        answer is still never sent again (12514). The limit is for each read, not for a whole reply: a server that trickles one byte at
                        a time and never stops is still waited for one read at a time. It does not change the API client library (libsdcli.so), whose
                        waits are its callers' own.

20 Oct 26  RECORDLOCKED() OF A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER NEEDS A WRITE DEFINITION AND THE WRITE GROUP, LIKE A LOCK (SDNET).

             What        RECORDLOCKED(f, id) of a remote file is asked of the other server only when this machine's definition of that server
                        says write AND the login there is in the other server's sdfileswrite group. A definition that says read stops the
                        program at once with message 12506 ("... is read only: recordlocked is refused. Nothing was sent"); the function has
                        no ON ERROR clause, so it always stops. On the other server, request 34 (the one RECORDLOCKED() sends) joins the
                        requests that a login in sdfiles but not in sdfileswrite may not make: that login is refused with message 12526 and
                        the audit line api refused request=34 route=sdfiles write=off user=<name>, whatever the client's definition says, and
                        the program stops with message 12505 and that text. Inside BEGIN TRANSACTION it still works for a write definition (it
                        changes nothing, so a rollback has nothing to undo). MUSTLOCK asks the same request ("do I hold this lock?"), so for a
                        login without the write group the check is refused as well: a write or delete ends with STATUS() 7000 and message
                        12505 naming request 34, where it used to end with STATUS() 3023. Nothing is written either way.
             Why it      The answer names the holder of a lock by his user number on the other server. A login that may read and nothing
             matters     else has no business learning who is editing what, and a check made only by the client's own definition can be
                        walked round by a program that speaks the API directly; the refusal on the other server cannot.
             Does not    It changes nothing about GETLOCKS() (still the local view), FILELOCK, FILEUNLOCK, the record locks, CLEAR.FILE or
                        LOCKING, and it adds no message. A login in sdfileswrite on a server whose definition here says read still cannot
                        ask RECORDLOCKED(): the definition is the administrator's choice on this machine, so change it to write to have it.

                        file lock), and STATUS() afterwards is the lock owner's user number ON THAT SERVER. It needs the same two things as
                        FILELOCK (see the entry above this one). GETLOCKS(f, user) of a remote

20 Oct 26  FILELOCK, FILEUNLOCK, RECORDLOCKED() AND GETLOCKS() NOW WORK ON A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER (SDNET).

             What        A program that opens a remote file through a VOC pointer of type N could not use these four statements: each stopped
                        with "not available" or "read only". RECORDLOCKED(f, id) now asks the other server and gives the code a local file
                        gives (-3 -2 -1 another user holds a file, update or read lock, 0 nobody, 1 2 3 this session holds a read, update or
                        file lock), and STATUS() afterwards is the lock owner's user number ON THAT SERVER. It works on a server defined
                        read as well as one defined write, and inside BEGIN TRANSACTION (it changes nothing). GETLOCKS(f, user) of a remote
                        file is the local view, as in openQM: the three counters of this machine's lock table and no lock line, because a
                        remote lock lives on the server and never appears here; it asks the server nothing. FILELOCK f and FILEUNLOCK f work
                        only for a server defined write AND a login that is in the other machine's sdfileswrite group, exactly as a remote
                        WRITE does. FILELOCK takes the LOCKED clause when the file is busy (STATUS() is the holder's user number on the
                        server), and with no LOCKED clause asks again every quarter of a second, like a record lock, so a wait never makes
                        the other server block and the break key still works. FILEUNLOCK leaves STATUS() 0 (it was yours and is free), 3011
                        (another user's, still held) or 3012 (nobody held it), the same as for a local file. A file lock belongs to the
                        connection: FILEUNLOCK, closing the file variable (or, if it just goes out of scope, the next prompt), LOGTO and the
                        end of the process all give it back, and RELEASE of the file (or a bare RELEASE) does too, as it does on the server's
                        own files. The first FILELOCK through a file variable leaves one line in the audit trail:
                        netfile filelock server=<name> file=<file>. Two things that take the file lock work on such a file because of it:
                        the verb CLEAR.FILE (it stopped with "filelock is not available for a file on server ...") and the LOCKING
                        keyword of LIST, SORT and the other query verbs.
             Why it      A program written for openQM's QMNet can lock a whole remote file to read a consistent total, or ask whether a record
             matters     is being edited, without being rewritten. A login that is not in sdfileswrite is still refused by the other
                        server, with message 12526 and an audit line there (api refused request=32 route=sdfiles write=off ...).
             Does not    It does not let a server defined read lock anything: FILELOCK and FILEUNLOCK on one still stop with "read only",
                        and nothing is sent. A change or a lock inside BEGIN TRANSACTION is still refused (message 12515, for FILELOCK and
                        FILEUNLOCK as for every other lock), because a remote change would never join a local rollback. It does not add
                        remote locks to LIST.READU or to GETLOCKS: look at the other server for those. CONFIGURE.FILE, ANALYSE.FILE, FCONTROL,
                        the index statements, OPENSEQ, creating or deleting a remote file and WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY stay refused. NOTE
                        for the administrator of the other server: a login in sdfileswrite on a write server can now hold a whole file
                        against everyone; list.readu shows the lock (type FX) and unlock file <n> filelock clears it.

19 Oct 26  A REMOTE SELECT THAT THE OTHER SERVER ANSWERS WITH AN ERROR NO LONGER STOPS THE PROGRAM.

             What        When a program makes a list of the records of a file on another SD server (SDNet) and the server answers either request of
                        the SELECT - the SELECT itself or the read of the list - with an error of the kind that takes an ON ERROR clause, the program
                        was stopped with "READLIST generated an abort event" (or "SELECT generated an abort event"), which an ON ERROR clause cannot
                        trap. It now gets the same error as every other remote file statement: message 12505 with the server's own words, or "status 0"
                        when the server sent none. The client class (the BASIC class that talks to an SD server) lets an object that has been made
                        quiet carry on after such a reply, as it already did for a READ, a WRITE and a DELETE; the remote file handler is the one
                        user that makes its object quiet.
             Why it      A server that was broken, or that was not to be trusted, could end a client's program at will, and the reason for a refusal never
             matters    reached the person.
             Does not    An object that is not quiet - the default, and every program that uses the client class directly - is stopped exactly as
                        before. It does not change which requests a server refuses or what it says. It was found by a test written for the case; no real
                        server was seen to send this reply.

19 Oct 26  A REMOTE FILE CHANGE THAT THE OTHER SERVER REFUSES NOW SHOWS WHY.

             What        When a program changes a file on another SD server (SDNet) and that server's answer to the change is an error, the error message
                        now carries the server's own words. Before, it said only "status 0", whatever the reason. The case that matters is a login on
                        the other server that has the files route but not the right to change files (sdfileswrite): the person at the client now reads
                        "This login cannot change or lock files here: ask the administrator for files-write-on (request 16)". Message 12526, that
                        sentence, was 175 characters and the client shows 100, so it is now 98. The same holds for any other error the server
                        answers with a text of that kind, such as MAXRLOCK on a lock request or a CLEARFILE of a file the server cannot write. An
                        error the server answers with no text still shows "status <n>", as before. The server also empties its error text at the start of every
                        request now: it used to send the text of its last refusal again with an unrelated error (a write with an id that is too long
                        answered "Invalid call name" after a call with no name), which would have been shown to the person.
             Why it      "Status 0" does not say that the fix is a word the administrator of the other machine has to give; the sentence does.
             matters
             Does not    It does not change which requests are refused, or who may be given the right. A program that traps the error with ON ERROR
                        still sees STATUS() 7000, as before: the text is in the message that is shown when the program does not trap it.

19 Oct 26  MODIFY.ACCOUNT FILES-OFF AND FILES-ON NOW DEAL WITH A LEFTOVER WRITE-GROUP MEMBERSHIP, ITS LINE IS IN LOWER CASE, AND A REFUSED LOGIN NAMES BOTH ROUTES.

             What        (1) Someone could be in the Windows group sdfileswrite and not in sdfiles - a removal that stopped half way, or a change made by
                        hand. That left them inert until the next files-on, which added sdfiles and made the leftover into the right to change files on
                        this server without the administrator saying so. files-off now removes the write group first and then sdfiles, reading each
                        back, and does the work for a person who is in only one of them (it used to answer "already had that access" and leave the
                        membership); if the second removal fails the person is left in sdfiles alone - read access, never write access without read -
                        and the command says the change failed. files-on removes a leftover write membership before it adds sdfiles, and stops
                        with the same failure message if it cannot. When the write group went, a line "Account <name> has files-write no" comes
                        before the usual one. (2) The usual line is now "Account <name> has files yes" (or no): it said "FILES" in capitals. (3) A login
                        that is in neither sdapi nor sdfiles is refused as before, but the audit reason now says "not a member of sdapi or sdfiles"; it
                        named only sdapi.
             Why it      A permission the administrator never gave must not come back at the next, unrelated command, and the audit line should
             matters    send the administrator to the right group.
             Does not    The wording of the other lines of modify.account is unchanged. files-write-on and files-write-off do what they did. The real
                        group changes were not run on Windows: they need an elevated SDSYS session and the groups made by the installer.
             Afterwards  If the write group went and the next step then fails (the removal from sdfiles, or the add of sdfiles after a leftover write
                        membership), the person has lost the write access, so the command says what did happen: it prints "Account <name> has
                        files-write no" and writes the audit line "modify.account files-write-off account=<name>" for the removal that worked, then
                        reports the failure.  The audit line for files-off or files-on itself is written only when the whole command worked.

19 Oct 26  THE API'S FILE LOCK AND UNLOCK (REQUESTS 32 AND 33) ACT ON THE FILE THEY ARE ASKED ABOUT.

             What        A client that locked or unlocked a whole file through the API (the wire requests filelock and fileunlock) locked or
                        unlocked whichever file the server had opened last, not the one the request named. It was found by opening three files, locking
                        the first, and asking from a second session which files were locked. They now act on the file named. The client library
                        has no function for these two requests yet, so the programs that could meet this are ones that speak the protocol directly.
             Why it      A lock on the wrong file protects nothing and blocks someone else's work.
             matters
             Does not    It does not add a client function, and it does not change record locks.

19 Oct 26  SDNET: A REPLY THAT CANNOT BE TRUE IS REFUSED AT ONCE, AND THE TWO WAITS ARE SETTINGS (NETWAIT, NETREPLY).

             What        (1) A remote file's client now checks the length a reply announces before it reads the body. A server that announces less than
                        its own ten-byte header, or more than the largest string SD holds, is treated as a lost connection immediately; before, the
                        client read for as long as the server sent, and waited 30 seconds for a body that was never coming. The ordinary client class
                        is unchanged except for lengths below ten. (2) The seconds to wait for a connection (5) and for each reply (30) were fixed in
                        the programs. They are the new sd.conf settings NETWAIT and NETREPLY, 1 to 3600 seconds, with those defaults, read by
                        set.server ... connect, set.netlogin and every remote file; CONFIG lists them. A zero is refused, so SD does not start with
                        NETWAIT=0 (openQM reads that as wait for ever). A CLEARFILE waits the longer of five minutes and NETREPLY.
             Why it      A compromised or broken server can no longer hold a client for a long time with a bad length, and an administrator who has a
             matters    slow link or a slow server can say how long is too long.
             Does not    It does not cap the size of a legitimate record, and the setting is per computer, not per server definition. NETWAIT and
                        NETREPLY have nothing to do with the QMClient programs.  The ceiling is set after the login, not during it: the login
                        exchange itself has no limit on the length a reply announces (only the test for a length under ten bytes), so a pinned
                        server that is hostile can still announce a large length in a login reply and send slowly, for NETREPLY seconds a read.
                        A server that completes the TLS handshake and then sends nothing at all, not even its acknowledgement byte, is NOT given up
                        on after NETREPLY seconds: the client waits until the server sends something or closes the connection (the session tickets
                        that TLS 1.3 sends after the handshake end the first read early, and the read that follows has no time limit).  A server that
                        sends the acknowledgement and then goes silent IS given up on after NETREPLY seconds.  Both are Linux measurements; not
                        measured on Windows.
             Fixed       A value of 65541 (65536 + 5) for NETWAIT or NETREPLY in sd.conf was read into a 16 bit field, came out as 5 and passed the
                        1 to 3600 test, so SD started with a 5 second wait instead of refusing the value; the fields are 32 bit now and SD does not
                        start.  (PWDELAY and DHCACHE are read the same way and have the same fault; they are not changed here.)

18 Oct 26  A WAY AROUND THE OS ACCESS GATES WAS CLOSED: A CONVERSION CODE (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             Why         Found by an evaluation of the OpenQM 4 manual against SD and then reproduced on a private SD with an ordinary
                        account that has SH and OS.EXECUTE off.  It needed neither a password nor an administrator.  (Linux found a
                        second, in the start of the command a print job is handed to; SD on Windows prints through Windows and does not
                        use that string, so there is nothing to close.)
             1           OCONV and ICONV with a conversion code of "U" and a name call the catalogued subroutine of that name.  The routine
                        that does it is internal, so a name beginning with a dollar sign, which only internal code may CALL, was reached
                        by an ordinary program.  Such a name is now refused as a conversion code, the same way a name that is not
                        catalogued is (status 2).  A user's own catalogued conversion subroutines work as before.
             Not done   OPEN.SOCKET still connects from any account: whether a program may open a network connection by default is a
                        decision for the owner and is recorded as a question.

18 Oct 26  SDNET, FIFTH SLICE (E): FILES ON ANOTHER SD SERVER CAN BE WRITTEN AND LOCKED, FOR A SERVER DEFINED WITH WRITE (S.65).

             What        For a server that SDSYS defined with the word write (set.server ... write), a file opened through an N pointer
                        accepts WRITE, WRITEU, MATWRITE, WRITEV, DELETE, CLEARFILE, the locking reads (READU, READL, READVU, MATREADU
                        and the rest), RECORDLOCKU and RECORDLOCKL and RELEASE.  A busy record takes the LOCKED branch, or the
                        statement tries again every quarter of a second.  The delivered mode is read, and with read every one of
                        these is refused exactly as before and nothing is sent: writing is something SDSYS turns on, per server.
             Limits      The mode is the CLIENT's setting.  The server it points at is not told, and its files-only route lets a login
                        write and delete whatever the mode says; its own voc.edit, dict.edit and bp.edit switches still apply, and the
                        client's own switches apply by the remote file's name.  Nothing here stops a modified client.
             Rules       A change or lock inside BEGIN TRANSACTION is refused (a remote change cannot be rolled back).  A change that gets
                        no answer is never sent again: its outcome is not known, and the message says so.  FILELOCK, GETLOCKS, the
                        index statements and CREATING.SEQKEY stay refused.  A write-mode file is never read from the record cache.
             Not yet     Only a test server has been used; no two machines have talked to each other.

18 Oct 26  SDNET: A SHORT RECORD CACHE FOR REMOTE FILES, AND LOGTO CLOSES THEIR CONNECTIONS (S.65).

             What        A process now keeps the last 64 records it READ from files on other SD servers for 2 seconds, and the fact that
                        a record was not there.  A second READ of the same record from the same open file in that time costs no request:
                        a SORT over a remote file made half the requests.  The entries of a file go when it is closed or SELECTed again.
             What it     A READ can show a record up to 2 seconds old.  A record over 32 KB is not kept, READV is not cached.
             costs
             LOGTO       Going into another account with LOGTO, and coming back, now closes every connection to other servers and makes
                        every remote file opened before unusable (a READ on it is an error); a new OPEN dials again.  Until now the
                        connections stayed until the process ended.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, FOURTH SLICE (D): LIST, COUNT, SORT AND SELECT WORK ON A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER (S.65).

             What        SELECT, SELECTV and SSELECT of the remote file give every record id the server holds as an ordinary select
                        list.  OPEN 'DICT' of the VOC pointer opens the file's dictionary from the same server.  With those two, the
                        query verbs LIST, COUNT, SORT, SELECT, LIST.ITEM and LIST DICT run on the VOC name, WITH clauses and BY included.
                        FILEINFO(f, FL$TYPE) of a remote file is the new FL$TYPE.NET (7), and INDICES(f) is empty.
             Cost        Each record a query needs is one request to the other server, and the whole list of ids comes in one reply.  A
                        query that reads every record of a large remote file is slow because of that; an id given on the command line
                        is read without a select.
             Not yet     Writes, locks, transactions and the server's indices are still refused; LOGTO does not yet drop the connections.
                        Only a test server has been used so far.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, THIRD SLICE (C): A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER CAN BE OPENED AND READ (S.65).

             What        A VOC entry whose first field is N, with the server's name in field 2 and the file's name on that server in
                        field 3, is a pointer to a file on another SD server defined with set.server.  OPEN by that name gives a file
                        variable that READ, MATREAD, READV, FILEINFO and CLOSE work on; the records come from the other server over the
                        TLS connection and the stored login of set.netlogin.  Nothing is sent before the NET switch, the server's
                        accounts list, the stored login and the pinned key have been checked.
             Not yet     It is read only.  WRITE, DELETE, CLEARFILE, locks, SELECT, transactions and the query verbs LIST, COUNT, SORT and
                        SELECT on such a file stop with a message, and nothing is sent.  Only a test server has been used so far; no two
                        machines have talked to each other.
             A fix       A program that kept a class object in named common (the new remote-file code does) made the process end with a
                        crash at logout, because the object's destroy code ran after the stack it needs had been freed.  The objects are
                        now released first.  No program written so far was affected.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (C): A SECOND, NARROWER API ROUTE THAT REACHES FILES AND NOTHING ELSE (S.65).

             Why         Until now a person could log in to a server's API only as a member of the Windows group sdapi, and then the
                        whole API was theirs: execute commands, call subroutines, enter other accounts, read and write sequential files,
                        and work with files.  A login that SDNet keeps for another server, if it were taken from a machine, would give
                        whoever took it all of that on the server it was made for.  There is now a second route, the Windows group
                        sdfiles.  A person who is in sdusers and in sdfiles and NOT in sdapi can log in over the API, and the session
                        is held to files.
             The rules   sdapi wins: a member of both groups has the whole API, as before.  A person in neither group is refused
                        exactly as before.  The SDSYS login is never made files-only.  If a group cannot be read the login is
                        refused rather than guessed.  The session that serves the requests works out for itself, from the two groups,
                        whether it is files-only (on Windows it is a different process from the one that checked the password).  Both groups are empty for a new account until the administrator adds the person.
             Allowed     Open and close a file; read, with or without a lock; write, write and keep the lock, delete and delete and
                        keep the lock; select, read the next id, read and save lists; lock a record or a file and ask whether a
                        record is locked; clear a file; the index requests; file information and reading one field; the mark
                        mapping; the server's revision; ask for the error text; quit.  Setting the account is allowed ONCE, which is
                        the entry to an account after login: a second one would be a logto and is refused.
             Refused     Call a subroutine, execute a command, answer its prompt or end it, the other logins, open a file on another
                        SDNet server, read a variable, system(), trans(), transactions, evaluate and the conversions, sequential
                        files, and any request this release does not know.  The decision is one small function holding an ALLOW
                        list, so a request added to the API later is refused to a files-only login until somebody decides it belongs.
                        A refused request is not carried out; the client is told "This login is for files only: request n is not
                        allowed" and the connection stays open.
             What it is  It is NOT a read-only route: a files-only login can write and delete.  Everything else about the session is
             not        as for any other API login: the account's switches, SDCLIENT in sd.conf and the API listener all still apply.
             Words       modify.account name files-on / files-off, and files-on in create.account user.  After a change SD says
                        "Account fred has FILES yes" (or no), read back from the group and not taken from the request, and a
                        change that the group does not show is reported as a failure.  The words ssh, api, both and none leave
                        files-on alone: modify.account fred none takes away ssh and the API and not the files-only login; files-off
                        takes that away.  A group or other account is refused the words.  create.account looks for the group before it
                        makes the Windows user, so on a machine without it nothing is left behind.  A change reaches the person at
                        their next login; to remove it now, files-off and end their connection.
             Audit       api login route=sdfiles user=name for a login, api refused request=n route=sdfiles user=name for a refused
                        request, and modify.account files-on account=name for the administrator's act.
             Installing  The installer makes the group sdfiles, empty, beside sdssh and sdapi, and the uninstaller removes it with
                        them.  AN EXISTING INSTALLATION DOES NOT HAVE IT UNTIL THE INSTALLER IS RUN AGAIN; until then files-on says
                        "sdfiles is not a valid Windows group" and changes nothing.  The upgrade does not put anyone in the group.
             Archives    The account archive carries route.files: 1 or 0 for a user account, and restore.account puts an account it
                        has to create back in the group.  The key is not called files because that is already the count of files in
                        the archive.  An older archive has no such line and restores the account out of the group.
             Not yet     Nothing on the client side uses the route yet: set.netlogin still makes the ordinary login exchange and no
                        SDNet client opens a file on another server, so no one has connected by this route.
             Tested      The free checks that read the programs as text pass.  NONE OF THE SD PROGRAMS FOR THE ROUTE WAS COMPILED OR RUN
                        on Windows: the login with a real user in sdfiles, the refusal of a request on a live session, files-on and
                        files-off against a real group, the installer making the group and the archive round trip have not been seen
                        to work.
             Messages    12485 (the refusal) and 12487 (the FILES state line) are new.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's Accounts and Security page has a section on the route with the full lists of what
                        is allowed and refused and the checks in the order the login makes them; its SDNet page, the Remote Access
                        page and the Getting Started account and API pages mention it; the TCL syntax card lists the new words.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (B): A PERSON'S OWN LOGIN ON A SERVER, KEPT AS DERIVED VALUES AND NEVER AS THE PASSWORD (S.65).

             Why         To use a server SDSYS has defined, a person needs a login on it.  set.netlogin server asks for the user name
                        and password the person has on that other server, makes ONE TLS connection to the server, runs the same
                        SCRAM-SHA-256 login the API uses, and keeps the values that login derives.  delete.netlogin server removes
                        them.  Both are in every account's VOC, because they are the person's own.  Nothing yet USES a stored
                        login, and no remote file can be opened.
             Not the     The password is typed hidden, worked into the derived values and cleared; it is never written, printed,
             password   logged or audited.  What is kept is the salt and iteration count the server chose and the two keys derived
                        from the password (ClientKey and ServerKey).  Be clear what that is: whoever reads the file can log in as
                        that remote user to THAT ONE server, and can try passwords against the keys offline, as fast as the iteration
                        count lets them; they cannot learn the password from the file and cannot use it on another server.  Use a
                        strong remote password that is not used anywhere else.
             Where       C:\ProgramData\SD\sd-net\<windows user>\<server>, one line, in a directory owned by the person whose access
                        list names only that person, SYSTEM and the Administrators.  It is OUTSIDE every account directory, so an
                        account program that has neither the sh nor the os.execute switch cannot read it with OPENSEQ.  AN ACCOUNT
                        THAT HAS EITHER SWITCH CAN READ IT, because it can read anything its Windows user can.  Two accounts of one
                        Windows user share the one file for a server.
             Trust       Only an internal subroutine, $netcred, touches the file.  Before each use it asks a new C test, the SDEXT
                        function SD_FILE_PRIVATE (key 111, internal programs only): a plain file and not a link, owned by the person
                        running, with an access list that allows nobody but that person, SYSTEM and the Administrators, in a
                        directory that passes the same test.  A file or directory that fails is never opened.  A login made
                        for one server key is refused once the definition holds another.
             The order   set.netlogin checks, asking for nothing: the NET switch (or the administrator), that the server is defined,
                        that the account is in its accounts list, and that the directory is ready.  Then it asks for the two
                        prompts.  Then it connects, and BEFORE IT SENDS ANYTHING compares the key the server presents with the pin
                        in the definition: a different key ends the command with nothing sent and the password unspent.  Then the
                        exchange, in which it also checks that the server's own signature is right.  Nothing else is sent: no
                        account is entered on the other server.  On any failure nothing is stored and an older login is untouched; on
                        success an older login for that server is replaced without a question.
             Directory   The person's own directory is made by set.netlogin itself, as the person, inside C:\ProgramData\SD\sd-net, which
                        the installer makes so that an SD user can add a directory to it and owns what they add (no other SD user is
                        given anything inside it).  AN EXISTING INSTALLATION DOES NOT HAVE THAT FOLDER UNTIL THE INSTALLER IS RUN
                        AGAIN; until then set.netlogin stops at the directory step and says so.
             Limits      It works in a local session of an ordinary Windows user; the administrator (sdsys) has no login of its own to
                        keep, and an API session does not pass the ownership test.  delete.server leaves people's login files, which
                        are inert while the pin differs.  The remote user is the other server's ordinary API user: the server has no
                        files-only login yet, so the stored values reach whatever that user reaches there.
             Audit       set.netlogin server=name remote=user and delete.netlogin server=name, on success only, and never a password,
                        key, salt or hash.
             Tested      The BASIC programs and the C check were NOT COMPILED OR RUN on Windows (the C had a syntax check only): the
                        exchange has not been seen to work against a live server.
             Messages    12455 to 12484 are new.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's SDNet page describes the credential model, what the file does and does not protect
                        against, both verbs and the checks in order; the TCL syntax card lists both verbs.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (A):SET.SERVER CAN CONNECT ONCE AND SHOW YOU THE SERVER'S KEY HASH (S.65).

             Why         A server definition pins the hash of the other server's public key, and until now SDSYS had to type those 64
                        digits from a message, with nothing to say they were right.  set.server now takes the word connect in place of
                        the pin: set.server name host port account connect {read | write} {accounts a,b,c} {no.query}.  Every other
                        field is checked first, and nothing is dialled for a definition that would be refused.  SD then opens a TLS
                        connection to the host and port, waiting at most 5 seconds, reads the hash of the public key the server
                        presented and closes the connection at once.  It is a handshake only: nothing is sent to the server.  It shows
                        you the hash on a line of its own, tells you to compare it with the hash the other server's administrator gives
                        you by a way that does not use this network, and asks "Trust this key (y/n)?", which is no unless you answer y.
                        On y the definition is written with that hash; on n, or when the input ends, nothing is written.  Typing the 64
                        digits works as before and connects to nothing.
             The question  It is ALWAYS asked.  no.query still skips the question about replacing a definition that exists, and
                        only that one: a script that gives connect and no.query still has to answer whether the key is the right one,
                        because that answer is the point of connecting.  A replacement with connect asks the replacement question
                        first, before any connection, so answering no to it dials nothing.
             The hash    It is the SHA-256 of the server's PUBLIC KEY, not of the certificate, so a certificate renewed over the same key
                        has the same hash and the pin stays good.  The other server's administrator can read it from that server with
                        SETTINGS.REPORT, which shows the line "public key sha256: " and the 64 digits in its [api] section beside the
                        certificate's thumbprint (it reads only the public key, and says "certificate: not yet generated" when the
                        server has taken no TLS connection yet).  The figure agrees with an independent computation (openssl) on
                        test certificates of both key types.  The thumbprint changes at a renewal; this hash does not.
             Failures    If the connection cannot be made (nothing listening, a wrong address, 5 seconds), if what answers does not
                        complete a TLS handshake, or if no hash can be read, a message of its own says which and nothing is written.
             Audit       The set.server line in the audit trail ends with pin=typed or pin=confirmed.  The hash itself is still not in it.
             SOCKET.INFO One new key for programs, SKT$INFO.TLS.SPKI (11): for a socket opened with OPEN.SOCKET and the SKT$TLS flag, the
                        same 64 lower case hex digits; a null string for any other socket.  Like the existing TLS key 8 it is SD's own.
             Messages    12417 and 12418 are reworded; 12445 to 12451 are new.
             Not there   Still no verb, statement or program connects to a server that is DEFINED; the connection above is the one
                        SDSYS makes, once, for set.server.  No user name or password for the remote account is asked for or stored,
                        there is no VOC pointer to a remote file, and the NET switch and the accounts list are still not consulted by
                        anything.  Nothing checks a pin on a later connection, because there is none.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's SDNet page describes the connect flow, its failure messages and the audit words, and
                        the Sockets page lists key 11; the TCL syntax card shows {pin | connect}.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, FIRST SLICE: THE NET SWITCH AND THE SERVER DEFINITIONS (S.65).  NOTHING CONNECTS YET.

             Why         SDNet, files on another SD server that a program opens and reads as if they were local, is being built in
                        slices.  This is the first.  It adds the permission an account will need and the list of servers SDSYS is
                        willing to have called, and nothing else: no program, verb or statement in SD opens a connection to a server,
                        and no remote file can be opened.  The switch and the definitions are in place, and can be set and listed, so
                        that the slices that follow have something to rest on.  The SDNet that was removed in September (a "server;file"
                        VOC entry, port 4245, passwords in sd.conf) is not back.
             NET switch  One more per-account switch, beside the shell, OS.EXECUTE, SD.ACCOUNTS, the three edit switches, ssh and the
                        API.  It is OFF for an account made from now on, and off for every account that already exists: nothing used
                        it, so there is no upgrade step.  SDSYS gives and takes it with modify.account name net-on and net-off, and
                        create.account user takes net-on.  After a change the command prints "Account fred has NET yes" (or no); if the
                        switch already had the value asked for it says only that the account already had that access.  Like sd-on, and
                        unlike the edit switches, it belongs to the account the person is IN: it is read at sign-on, again at logto
                        and given back when logto returns, and by the API server at every entry to an account.  It may be set on a
                        group account.  The administrator is never refused, and an SDSYS session over the local API is given it on.
                        The account archive keeps it for a user account, and restore.account gives it back only to an account it has
                        to create; an older archive restores the account with it off.  Today the switch changes nothing anyone can do.
             Servers     SDSYS defines the servers an account may one day reach; no user can.  Each definition is one line of the file
                        netservers in the SDSYS directory (mode 644): a name, a host, the PORT OF THE SERVER BEING DEFINED (chosen
                        when it is defined: 4247 for a full SD Core, 4249 for a Solo), the account to enter there, the SHA-256 hash of
                        the server's public key (the pin), a mode (read, unless write is given), the local accounts that may use it,
                        and the date.  The list of accounts is EMPTY unless SDSYS names some, so nobody may use a new definition,
                        and an account needs the NET switch as well.  The file is replaced whole, so it is never half written, at most
                        ten servers can be defined, and it holds no secret: a password or a key is never put in it.
             Verbs       set.server name host port account pin {read | write} {accounts a,b,c} {no.query} adds a definition, or
                        replaces one after a question (a replacement is the whole definition, the accounts list included); every
                        field is checked first and each wrong one has its own message, and a refusal writes nothing.  delete.server
                        name removes one.  list.servers {name} shows them, the pin cut short, or the whole pin and the date with a
                        name.  They are SDSYS's: they are in SDSYS's VOC and in no other account's, and each also refuses anybody but
                        the administrator.  Defining and deleting are written to the audit trail (set.server name=... and
                        delete.server name=..., without the pin).  Messages 12416 and 12417 to 12444 are new.
             Not there   The connection (set.server can now connect once, to show you the key hash a server presents and ask you to
                        confirm it: see the entry above); the user name and password for the remote account; the VOC pointer to a remote
                        file; and any check of the NET switch or the accounts list, because nothing yet asks them.  Writing to
                        a remote file is later again.  NETFILES in sd.conf still does nothing.
             Documents   The Administrator guide has a new page, SDNet, and the NET switch in Accounts and Security; the TCL syntax
                        card has the three verbs and net-on / net-off.

25 Oct 26  THE REFUSAL "HAS NO OS ACCESS" FOR A PRINT FILE NAMES THE ACCOUNT INSTEAD OF PRINTING "%1" (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        An account without the OS-access setting cannot make a print file outside its own directory: SETPU, SETPTR ... AS
                        PATHNAME and a "$hold" print name that climbs out with ".." are all refused. The refusal itself was right but its
                        message read "%1 has no OS access, so a file or directory outside its own account cannot be reached by pathname
                        (SDSYS can give it: modify.account %1 os-on)", with the two %1 markers left in. It now reads the same with the
                        account's name in both places, so the sentence can be followed as written.
             Why it      The second half of the message is the instruction the administrator needs, and with the marker in it the
             matters     account to give the access to had to be guessed.
             Does not    It does not change who is refused or who is allowed: only the words. The same message raised by SD BASIC
                        programs (OSCOPY, SELECT.DIR, SETPTR, the editor) already named the account.

25 Oct 26  MODIFY.ACCOUNT CARRIES OUT SEVERAL ACTIONS IN ONE COMMAND; CREATE.ACCOUNT AUDITS THE GATES IT GRANTS (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             Several     modify.account name sh-on os-on voc-on bp-on does all four.  Every word is read first, and if any is refused nothing is
             actions    changed: a word that is not an action (12795), two words that set one access to different states, such as sh-on with
                        sh-off or ssh with api (12796), or add or delete among other words, which take a user name and stand alone (12719).  The
                        words are then all carried out in a fixed order whatever order they were typed (the routes, then the switches, then
                        suspended or unsuspended), each reported and audited as before, and a command of more than one ends with a line saying
                        that every action was carried out (12798) or which were not (12799; the others are still done).  Before this, a word
                        after the first was read by nobody: modify.account fred voc-off bp-off did the first and said nothing of the second.
                        With no action at all the command now prints its usage.
             Audit      The audit line of create.account now ends with gates=, the words of the sentence that grant or withdraw an access
                        (create.account account=FRED type=user gates=ssh,api,sh-on), or gates=none.  The words up to type= are unchanged.
             Also       restore.account compares a global-catalogue program by its lower-case name, so a different program already catalogued
                        under that name is now reported (13017) instead of being overwritten.  SYSTEM(1057), a phantom's parent, tested the
                        parent's user number against the number of table cells instead of the highest user number, so a parent numbered above
                        the cell count was reported as missing.

25 Oct 26  THE SHARED ACCOUNT: FILES THAT EVERY ACCOUNT CAN OPEN BY NAME, WITHOUT LOGTO OR A POINTER (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX, S.66).

             What        The installer now makes a group account named shared (its directory is C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts\shared and
                        its Windows group is sdg_shared).  The files in it can be opened by every account by file name: OPEN "inventory" in any
                        account finds the file shared holds under that name when the account has no entry of its own for it.  The account's own
                        VOC entry always wins, so a file the account already has under that name is the one it gets.  LIST, SORT, SELECT, COUNT
                        and a BASIC program's OPEN all find it, and OPEN DICT opens its dictionary.  SDSYS puts files there by signing on to
                        shared (logto shared) and making them as in any account.
             Gates       Two new per-account switches, shown in the account's register record (fields 13 and 14).  shared.read is ON for every
                        account, new and existing, until SDSYS turns it off with modify.account name shared-read-off: it is the one switch here
                        that a blank means ON for, because the point of the account is files open to all.  shared.write is OFF until SDSYS turns
                        it on with modify.account name shared-write-on.  The words shared-read-on and shared-write-off undo them.
                        create.account user name shared-read-off shared-write-on sets them at creation; the report line is "Account fred has
                        shared.read yes, shared.write no".  A group or other account takes the words as well.
             What each   Reading a shared file needs shared.read.  A WRITE, DELETE, CLEARFILE or any verb that saves a record into a shared
             allows      file needs shared.write.  A shared dictionary needs shared.write AND dict.edit (the switch of 23 Oct).  The structure of
                        a shared file (making one by pathname, clear.file, configure.file, an index, a trigger, a resize, a rename or a delete) is
                        changed only by SDSYS signed on at a terminal: it is refused by the kernel for everyone else, a program marked internal
                        included, whatever the switches say.
             Lookup      Only a file whose entry in shared's VOC is an ordinary file pointer is found, and only when it lies inside the shared
             rules       directory.  Q pointers, N pointers, paths elsewhere, multifiles, a "name,component" request, the VOC and program files of
                        shared, and its private catalogue are never reached from another account: its verbs, sentences and programs are not
                        shared.  A name that begins with a symbol ($ & ! * _ %), and every name in the standard VOC, is never looked up, so
                        $savedlists, $hold and the like stay each account's own.  A file's records are locked exactly as in any account, so two
                        accounts see each other's locks.
             Not looked  OPENSEQ, DELETESEQ and CALL "file prog" do not follow shared; the lookup is an OPEN, and an OPEN only.  The
             up by       administrator's own OPEN does not follow it either: SDSYS reaches the files with logto shared.
             Audit       The first open of each shared name in a session writes "shared open file=name" to the audit trail; a refused name writes
                        nothing.  The administrator's words write the usual modify.account line.
             list.shared A new verb, in SDSYS's set, lists the files of shared that an account with shared.read can open by name, one to a line,
                        and a count.  With no shared account in service it says that.  It is not in the VOC of an ordinary account; if SDSYS puts
                        it there, an account with shared.read off is told so and sees nothing.
             Safer       There is no way into shared for an ordinary user except the lookup: the account's Windows group sdg_shared has no
             design      members, so logto shared and a Q pointer to it are for an elevated administrator alone.  That is what makes taking
                        shared.read away a real wall.  The directory and its files are open to the group sdusers with Modify (SYSTEM and
                        Administrators keep Full control, nothing else is granted), so the kernel can read them for everyone and, with shared.write,
                        write them.  Windows has no setgid and no file mode: the one access-control entry does what Linux's group sdusers and
                        mode 2775 do.  Someone who is to look after shared is given membership of sdg_shared with modify.account shared add name,
                        and shared-write-on on the account named shared.
             Names       The name shared is reserved.  create.account refuses it (12521) and delete.account shared is refused (12522); the
                        installer makes the account with a one-time marker that it removes, as it does for the installing user's account.  An
                        installation that already has an account named shared that is not SD's own is stopped by the installer before it changes
                        anything, with a message naming the register record to look at.
             Upgrading   An existing account has no value in the new fields, which reads as shared.read yes and shared.write no, so no account
                        loses or gains anything but the new read access; nothing is written to the existing accounts.  The installer creates shared
                        when the install is made again and keeps it when the accounts are kept.  Removing SD with the data removes the group
                        sdg_shared as well; keeping the data keeps it.
             Cautions    A file is checked for shared.read when it is opened: a file already open stays usable after SDSYS withdraws the switch,
                        until the person's next entry.  A program (a LIST, say) still writes the compiled form of a new I-type or A-type into a
                        shared dictionary by itself, as it does into any dictionary, so a reader may cause that write; an account with sh or
                        OS.EXECUTE on can reach the files at operating-system level like any other directory it can read.  Anything placed in
                        shared can be read by every account, so put nothing there that is not meant for everyone.
             Not done    The shared account is not replicated to other servers, and a catalogue of shared programs is not shared.  The API's
                        sdsys login is not carried (the API is never open to SDSYS here).  SD Core Solo has one account and does not have it.
             Tested      The decisions in the installer's two scripts were run against fixtures (the preflight, the access-control verdict entry by
                        entry, the rule that keeps the re-stamp from undoing it).  NOTHING HAS RUN ON A REAL INSTALL: the account's creation by the
                        installer, the lookup, the gates and the kernel's refusals have not been seen to work.
             Messages    12518 to 12525 are new.

23 Oct 26  THE EDIT GATES: VOC.EDIT, BP.EDIT AND DICT.EDIT (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             Gates       Three more per-account switches, beside the shell, OS.EXECUTE, SD.ACCOUNTS, ssh and the API, say whether the person who
                        signed on to an account may SAVE A CHANGE to a VOC, to a program file (source or object code) and to a dictionary. They
                        are off for an account made from now on. The installer's upgrade switches all three on, once, for every USER account
                        that exists and whose field is blank, so that no account loses what it had; group and other accounts are left off on
                        purpose. SDSYS gives and takes them with modify.account name voc-on, voc-off, bp-on, bp-off, dict-on and dict-off, and
                        create.account takes voc-on, bp-on and dict-on. The administrator is never refused. A switch takes effect when the
                        person next signs on; the command prints where all three stand.
             Which files  A file is known by the name of the file that is OPEN, not the name that was typed, so a Q-pointer to a VOC is caught:
                        a VOC is a file called voc; a dictionary is any file whose name ends in .dic (voc.dic is the dictionary of the VOC, not
                        the VOC); a program file is bp, bp.out (the object code), any name ending in .bp or .bp.out, or the private catalogue
                        directory. Capitals do not matter.
             What stops  A program's WRITE, WRITEV, DELETE and CLEARFILE of such a file: an error that says which switch is missing, or the ON
                        ERROR status. The verbs that change a file the person names ask first, before they lock or change anything, and say why:
                        ed, edit, copy and copyp into the file or moving records out of it, delete, clear.file, cname of a record, delete.file
                        of a program file or of a dictionary on its own, fixdir, format, map file, sp.view (its X key), set.trigger when it
                        installs or removes a trigger, copy.list, delete.list, get.list deleting in, save.list, sort.list, reformat, list
                        pageseq, cd data, generate, como on, the .s and .d stack commands on an existing VOC record, and create.file naming a
                        file that already exists. cname will not take a program file out of its name, or put a file under one, for a person
                        without the switch. OPENSEQ in such a place opens the file read only and cannot create one; DELETESEQ and a print file
                        made there are refused, and the CREATE.FILE statement refuses a new file inside one. For these routes by operating
                        system pathname the name of the file being made counts only if it is a directory, so a plain file called sample.bp in an
                        ordinary directory is not held back. Over the API a client's writes, deletes and file clears are held to the same rule.
                        Reading is never stopped.
             Compiling   Without bp.edit the basic, catalog, delete.catalog and decatalog commands are refused, since compiling is how source
                        becomes code that runs.
             LOGTO       The switches are those of the account the person SIGNED ON to (or, over the API, first entered); LOGTO does not change them.
             Internal programs  A program compiled in internal mode is exempt from the gates on files, which SD's own commands need. The mark is a
                        bit in the program's own object file, so it is now honoured only for an object from the global catalogue or for the
                        administrator: a person who could put an object file where SD would run it no longer runs it with every gate skipped.
             Backups      The account manifest records the three switches (edit.voc, edit.bp, edit.dict) and restore.account gives a restored
                        account what it had; a manifest from before has none, and gives none.
             After install  The installing user's own account starts with all three off like any new account; SDSYS turns on what the person needs.
                        The user table in shared memory has a new word, so SD is restarted at the end of an upgrade, as always.
             Does not    Anything done from the shell by an account that has sh-on or os-on is not stopped; nor is what SD writes as a side
                        effect of an ordinary command (the pointer CREATE.FILE makes, a saved sentence, a compiled I-type, the command stack).

23 Oct 26  A THIRD PER-ACCOUNT SWITCH, SD.ACCOUNTS, AND TWO LANGUAGE ADDITIONS, $MODE AUTOLOCK AND OPTION INHERIT.OWNERSHIP (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        modify.account name sd-on | sd-off: with sd-on, an account's programs may open, list, copy, delete and print into
                        files by path under the directory of any SD account the user could LOGTO (the register's Windows group is one the user
                        is a member of, and the account is not suspended). Reading and writing are one switch. It may be set on a group account.
                        It is off for a new account. The other account's VOC (voc, voc.dic) is never reachable this way, and a directory that is
                        not an SD account's still needs os-on. SDSYS is never refused, and os-on implies sd-on.
                        $MODE AUTOLOCK: a WRITE, MATWRITE or DELETE that does not hold a lock on the record takes an update lock, waiting for it,
                        makes the change and lets the lock go as a plain WRITE does. For programs written for environments that lock for you; not
                        recommended. WRITEV is not changed.
                        OPTION INHERIT.OWNERSHIP: CREATE.FILE gives the new file, and what is in it, the owner and group of the account directory
                        instead of the user's own, where Windows allows it; where it refuses nothing is said. Off unless asked for.
             Why it      An account that needs to read another account's data no longer needs the whole of OS.EXECUTE.
             matters
             Does not    An account archive does not carry the sd-on setting, so a restored account starts with it off.
                        INHERIT.OWNERSHIP does not change files that already exist.

23 Oct 26  A NEW ACCOUNT STARTS WITH NO SHELL, NO OS.EXECUTE, NO SSH AND NO API, AND SH AND OS.EXECUTE ARE NOW GRANTED TO THE ACCOUNT, NOT TO THE PERSON (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        SD is delivered with the tighter setting and SDSYS loosens it for the accounts that need it. Four switches, each kept
                        per account: ssh and api (the two remote doors; CREATE.ACCOUNT USER name ssh, api or both gives them, none, the default,
                        gives neither, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT changes them later); sh-on (the SH verb and "!"); os-on (OS.EXECUTE in a BASIC
                        program, the editors EDIT and MICRO, Python, and opening a file of the operating system by its path outside the
                        account's own directory). CREATE.ACCOUNT USER name sh-on os-on gives the last two at creation; MODIFY.ACCOUNT
                        name sh-on, sh-off, os-on or os-off changes either later, and they are independent, so sh-off leaves OS.EXECUTE
                        alone. After either command SD says where the account now stands ("Account name has SH yes, OS.EXECUTE no"). A change
                        takes effect the next time the account is entered, by sign-on, by the API or by LOGTO.
                        The grant used to be a record in the SD system file os.users, keyed by the Windows login name, so it followed a person
                        into every account they entered. It is now two fields of the account's own record, so it follows the account, and
                        os.users is no longer read. An account without os-on is held to its own directory (and the SDSYS entries every VOC
                        names, read only, and the directories listed in NETDIRS) for every open of a file by its path or through a VOC
                        pointer: this used to apply only to sessions that arrived over the network, and a person at the console was not held.
                        A refusal reaches the program as status 3035, so OPEN ... ELSE works as before. The same limit holds for DIR(),
                        DELETESEQ, SELECT.DIR, SETPTR ... AS PATHNAME and any print file made at a path, and a VOC pointer is held to it too:
                        an F-pointer must lead into the account (or be what the stock VOC says), and a Q-pointer is followed only into an
                        account the person could LOGTO.
             Why it      An account that was given a shell or OS.EXECUTE for one purpose no longer carries it into another person's work, and
             matters     a person at the console is held to the same rule as one on ssh.
             Upgrading   The installer copies, once, what os.users said about each USER account's Windows user into the account's two
                        fields, so nobody loses a command they were using (ssh and the API are left as they are); the administrator can then
                        turn them off one account at a time, and a later install does not turn them back on. A new installation's first
                        account starts off like any other, and SDSYS turns on what the person needs. SDSYS is never refused.
             Does not    SH-ON on a GROUP or OTHER account is refused, as before. An SH command no longer takes pipes, redirection
                        or chaining from ANY account, sh-on included (an account that was listed in os.users could use them before): the filter is the
                        same for everyone, as on Linux. The file os.users stays where it is, unread.

23 Oct 26  SDIDX -M SAYS WHY WHEN IT CANNOT MAKE THE DIRECTORY (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        Moving the indexes of a file to a directory that cannot be made (a drive that does not exist, a parent you may not write,
                        or a path that goes through a file) ended with status 1 and not one word. It now prints "Cannot create directory <the
                        path you typed>: <the reason>" (the operating system's reason, or "a file of that name exists") and still ends with
                        status 1. Nothing is moved: the indexes and the file header are where they were.
             Why it      A tool that fails without a message sends the person to look at permissions, drives and spelling with nothing to go on.
             matters     SD Core for Linux prints the same two lines, so a message means the same on both.
             Does not    It changes nothing when the directory can be made, and not the message for a target that already exists. Other
                        errors in the tools keep their own wording.

23 Oct 26  SORTING A LONG LIST OF EQUAL OR ALREADY ORDERED KEYS NO LONGER TAKES MINUTES (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        SORT, SSELECT, SELECT and LIST with BY, the SORT() functions and the index builder all use one sort. It put each key
                        into a tree one at a time, and the tree was never balanced, so a key equal to many others, or keys that arrived
                        already in order, made it walk a longer and longer chain for every new key. Measured on Linux with 100,000 records: all keys
                        equal 168 seconds, keys already in order 169 seconds, a field with five different values 141 seconds, and
                        SELECT file BY a field of five values 94 seconds; 200,000 equal keys took 329 seconds. Now the keys of a run are
                        collected and sorted once, and the same cases take a fraction of a second. SSELECT after an SSELECT, the usual way
                        to meet already ordered keys, is covered too (159 seconds for 200,000 records with a five valued second field).
             Why it      LIST or SELECT file BY a field with few different values (a status, a state, a yes or no flag) is an ordinary
             matters     query. On a file of 100,000 records it could keep a user waiting for minutes, and for no reason.
             Does not    The order is exactly the order it was: keys that compare equal still come out newest first, a sort that does not
                        fit in SORTMEM still spills to work files and merges them as before, and every other key comes out in the same
                        place. A sort with UNIQUE, a COMPOUND or STRICT mode, or a right aligned key that holds both numbers and text at
                        once (where the comparison does not give one consistent order, so only the old tree gives the old order) is
                        sorted the old way and is not faster. It does not change the hash that places records in a file, nor SORTMEM.

20 Oct 26  A CSV OR DELIMITER REPORT CAN BE WRITTEN AS A RECORD OF A DIRECTORY FILE (AS), AND A MULTIVALUED FIELD IS SPLIT OVER LINES.

             What        Two changes to the reports made by the CSV and DELIMITER keywords of LIST, SORT and the other query verbs. (1)
                        "AS file id" after CSV or DELIMITER (and their mode and delimiter) writes the report as the record "id" of the
                        directory file "file", the way TO writes an operating system file: LIST SALES BY @ID NAME QTY CSV AS EXPORTS R1001
                        NO.QUERY. A record that is there asks first (Enter is no) unless NO.QUERY is given; APPENDING adds to it. NO.QUERY
                        and APPENDING may not be used together: the command says so (message 12855) and stops before it has opened
                        anything, for TO as well. It is refused, without touching the record, for a hashed file (12856), a missing record id
                        (12857), a report that is not CSV or DELIMITER, one with LPTR, and one that also has TO. (2) A multivalued field now
                        gives one line for each value, in CSV and in DELIMITER reports alike: the first line has the record id and every
                        other column, the lines after it have only the next value of each multivalued field and blank cells for the rest.
                        A record has as many lines as its longest multivalued field has values. RPT after a field, or REPEATING, repeats
                        a single valued column on every line, as in the ordinary report. A field that is single valued in the dictionary,
                        or has SINGLE.VALUE after it, stays one cell, its value marks written as they are. Total and break lines stay one
                        line each. The same two changes apply whether the report goes to the screen, to TO or to AS.
             Why it     A multivalued field used to be written as one cell with raw value-mark characters inside it, which no spreadsheet
             matters    or import program reads as separate values; the manual of openQM shows the split form. AS lets a program or a
                        user keep a report inside SD's own file structure, with the record locked while it is written and the edit gates
                        asked, instead of needing an operating system path and the OS-access switch.
             Does not    It does not add the ENCODING clause (SD has no character sets). AS needs a directory file: a hashed file is
                        refused. A record another session holds is not waited for: the command names the holder (message 1433) and
                        writes nothing. A file that is a program file needs bp.edit on the account, as for any write; the OS-access
                        switch is not involved because no path is. An XML report with TO or AS is now refused (it used to make an empty
                        file); the delimited lines of break and total rows are not split. A report that was written before this change
                        with a multivalued field has more lines now: that is the change.

20 Oct 26  CSV IN BASIC: ANOTHER DELIMITER, QUOTE AND ESCAPE CHARACTER, A FIELD WITH A LINE BREAK, AND A WHOLE CSV TEXT IN ONE CALL.

             What        READCSV, MATREADCSV, WRITECSV and MATWRITECSV take the clauses DELIMITER d, QUOTE q and ESCAPE e (in any order,
                        after the file variable; the first character of each is used): READCSV FROM f DELIMITER ';' TO a, b, c. CSV.MODE
                        takes a third and a fourth operand, the quote and the escape character, for the statements that write
                        (WRITECSV, PRINTCSV, MATWRITECSV) and for FORMCSV(). READCSV and MATREADCSV now read a record whose quoted field
                        holds a line break as ONE row (the quote that is still open makes the reader go on to the next line); before, it
                        came back as two rows. A field that holds a line break is enclosed in quotes when written. CSVDQ() takes a quote and
                        an escape character as a third and fourth argument. Four new functions: CSVPARSE(text {, mode {, dialect}}) reads a
                        whole CSV text into a data collection (an array of rows; with CSV$HEADER the first row names the columns and each
                        row is an object; CSV$NUMBER makes a field that is a number a number; CSV$DYNAMIC gives a dynamic array instead),
                        CSVBUILD(collection {, mode {, dialect}}) writes one back (with a header row for rows that are objects, quoted as
                        CSV.MODE 1, 2 or 3 would quote, with CRLF line ends if CSV$CRLF), CSVOPEN(line {, delimiter {, quote {, escape}}}) says
                        whether a quoted field is still open at the end of a line (what READCSV uses to join lines), and "dialect" is a
                        string of up to three characters: delimiter, quote and escape character. The constants are in keys.h (CSV$HEADER ... CSV$QUOTE3). A
                        bad quote gives a null string and STATUS() 1020 with INMAT() the place. SYSTEM(1090) and SYSTEM(1091) give the
                        CSV.MODE quote and escape characters.
             Why it     A semicolon-separated file, a tab file or a file with a backslash escape could only be read by hand, and a quoted
             matters    field with a line break in it came back as two broken rows; a program that wanted a CSV file as data had to
                        write its own loop. It is the CSV part of the owner's CSV / JSON / XML list; the same rules (RFC 4180) now apply to
                        the statements, to FORMCSV() and CSVDQ() and to the CSV and DELIMITER keywords of the query verbs.
             Does not    It does not read a character set other than SD's own (no ENCODING), does not guess the delimiter, and does not
                        make READCSV take its rules from CSV.MODE (it never did; the clauses are how a reader names its dialect). FORMCSV
                        is still for programs only (no VOC verb). A program compiled before this change runs as it did: only a statement
                        that now carries a new clause needs the new compiler. Mode 1 now also encloses a field that holds a line feed or a
                        carriage return, which the old FORMCSV left bare.

20 Oct 26  JSON: INDENT FOR JBUILD(), JSON LINES, MERGE, PATCH, PATH QUERIES, SCHEMA VALIDATION, AND A JSON KEYWORD FOR QUERIES.

             What        JBUILD(c, mode, indent) with the pretty-print mode names the indent (up to 16 spaces or tabs); it was two spaces
                        only. READJSON var FROM f THEN ... ELSE ... reads the next line of a sequential file as a JSON value into var (a blank
                        line is skipped; ELSE is the end of the file or a line that is not JSON, and STATUS() says which: 3006 or 1020) and
                        WRITEJSON c TO f writes the collection as one compact line: JSON Lines through OPENSEQ. JMERGE(target, patch) is
                        RFC 7396 (null removes a name, an object merges, anything else replaces), JPATCH(c, ops) is RFC 6902 (add remove
                        replace move copy test; all or nothing: a null string, STATUS() 1023 and INMAT() the number of the operation that
                        failed), JPATH(c, query) is JSONPath (RFC 9535): names, *, .., indexes (from 0, as the RFC says; SD's own c{...}
                        paths are from 1), slices, unions and filters such as $.items[?@.price < 10 && @.tag == 'x'], with the functions
                        length(), count(), match() and search(); a malformed query gives a null string and STATUS() 1024. JVALIDATE(c,
                        schema) checks a document against a JSON Schema (draft 2020-12, the usual keywords: type, enum, const, the
                        number and string and array and object limits, properties, required, items, allOf, anyOf, oneOf, not, if / then /
                        else, $ref into the same schema) and gives an array of the faults, each with its path, the keyword and a message:
                        empty means valid. The query verbs LIST, SORT and the others take JSON (an array of objects) and JSON.LINES (one
                        object to a line) beside CSV and XML: a single valued field is a member, a multivalued field in no association an
                        array, and the multivalued fields of an association an array of row objects, as the XML keyword groups them. Like
                        XML the report goes to the screen or the printer (not TO or AS) and the XML options (ELEMENTS, WITH.DTD ...) are
                        refused with it.
             Why it     JSON was only parse and build. These are the pieces that make it a data format a program can query, patch,
                        merge and check, and the report keywords let a query go straight to a JSON consumer.
             Does not    No $ref outside the schema, no remote schema, no format-assertion keywords (format is read and ignored); a path
                        query or a patch reaches only the collection it is given, never a file, so no gate is involved. JPATH's numbering
                        (0) differs from c{path} (1) on purpose: it is the RFC's. A collection file still keeps its elements as it did.

20 Oct 26  XML IN BASIC: XPARSE(), XBUILD() AND XPATH().

             What        XPARSE(text {, mode}) reads an XML document into a data collection: an object with name (the tag, prefix and all),
                        attributes (an object; an xmlns attribute is an attribute like the rest), text (entities, character references and
                        CDATA read; line ends made line feeds) and children (an array of the same); a member that would be empty is left
                        out, white space between child elements is let go, a comment, a processing instruction, the declaration and a
                        DOCTYPE are read past. A document that is not well formed (a mismatched tag, a repeated attribute, an unclosed
                        element or section, two roots, text outside the root, an entity that is not one of the five) gives a null
                        string with STATUS() 1020 and INMAT() the place. XML$TRIM takes white space off the ends of the text. XBUILD(
                        element {, mode {, indent}}) writes the text back: the five characters XML reserves are escaped, an empty element is
                        <b/>, XML$DECL puts the declaration first, XML$PRETTY puts one element to a line with the indent (two spaces, or
                        the string given); a name that XML cannot have, a prefix no xmlns attribute declares (unless XML$NOCHECK) or a
                        value that is not an element gives a null string and STATUS() 1019. XPATH(x, query) runs XPath 1.0 over such a
                        collection: location paths with the child, descendant, parent, ancestor, sibling and attribute axes, predicates
                        (position, last(), comparisons, and / or / not), the usual string and number functions, unions and arithmetic;
                        the answer is an array of the nodes found (an element as its collection, an attribute or a text as a string) or the
                        number, string or truth value the query is; a malformed query gives a null string and STATUS() 1024.
             Why it     XML could only be written (the XML report keyword). A program can now read a document, look in it and write one
                        again without a hand-made parser, and without any XML reading the network or a file.
             Does not    No XSLT, XQuery or XML Schema validation (left out on purpose); no DTD is read, so an entity a DTD declares is an
                        error and nothing a document says can make the parser read a file or grow without bound (depth 500). Namespaces are
                        names and attributes, not resolved URIs: XPATH matches a prefix as written (local-name() ignores it).

20 Oct 26  NEW VERBS: IMPORT.CSV, IMPORT.JSON, IMPORT.XML AND CONVERT.DATA.

             What        IMPORT.CSV pathname file {HEADER} {DELIMITER x} {QUOTE x} {ESCAPE x} {ID column | GENERATE} {DICT} {OVERWRITING}
                        reads a CSV file into the records of an SD file in one line: each row is a record, the id the first column (or the
                        one ID names; GENERATE numbers 1, 2, 3 ...), the other columns the fields in order; with HEADER the first row names
                        the columns, and with DICT the names with no dictionary item get a D item for their field. IMPORT.JSON pathname
                        file reads a JSON array of objects or a JSON Lines file into a COLLECTION file, one record for each object (ID
                        name or GENERATE). IMPORT.XML pathname file reads a document in the shape the XML keyword writes (also with
                        ELEMENTS) back into the fields the dictionary of the file names, the association rows and the multivalued fields
                        included. A record that is already there is left alone and counted unless OVERWRITING. The last line says "file: n
                        record(s) written, m skipped". CONVERT.DATA from.pathname to.pathname {FROM format} {AS format} {HEADER} {NO.QUERY}
                        writes a CSV, JSON, JSON Lines or XML file as another of them; the format is what the name ends in (.csv .json
                        .jsonl .ndjson .xml) or what FROM and AS say. A table (CSV with a header, an array of objects, JSON Lines, the
                        children of an XML root; an XML document is always read as a table) goes to any of the four; a JSON object that is not
                        an array goes to JSON as it is, to XML only when it is an XML element (the form XPARSE() gives), and is refused for CSV
                        and JSON Lines.
                        A file that is there is asked about (Enter is no) unless NO.QUERY. Messages 12858 to 12867.
             Why it     Moving data between SD and a spreadsheet or a web service meant a BASIC program each time.
             Does not    The pathnames go through the OS-access gate and the file and its records through the edit gates, exactly as
                        OSREAD, OSWRITE and WRITE do, so an account without them gets the refusal it gets there and nothing is read or
                        written. No ENCODING; a CSV cell is a string (no typing on import); a value that is not text cannot become a CSV
                        cell (CONVERT.DATA says so and writes nothing); IMPORT.XML reads the report shape, not any XML document (use
                        XPARSE for that).

20 Oct 26  ENCRYPTING A FIELD: AN ORDINARY PROGRAM CAN NOW MAKE A KEY, AND A LISTING CAN SHOW THE FIELD DECRYPTED FOR WHOEVER HOLDS IT (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        Three new BASIC functions, open to every program: SDSALT() gives a fresh random salt; SDKEY(passphrase, salt) gives the
                        256-bit key for that passphrase and salt, in the form SDENCRYPT() and SDDECRYPT() accept (the key is slow to make on
                        purpose, so make it once per run, not once per field); SDRANDKEY() gives a random key.  The client library gains
                        SDChecksum, a checksum computed on the client side.  Until now SDENCRYPT() was usable only with a key that an ordinary
                        program had no way to make.
                        On top of that a dictionary item can carry the conversion XE (or XE;name for a named key).  LIST, SORT, COUNT, an I-type
                        that says OCONV(field,"XE;name") and ED then show the field decrypted for a session that holds the key and the stored
                        cipher text for a session that does not; ICONV with XE encrypts a value.  The key is never written in the dictionary.  It
                        is held in a key ring in the memory of the session's own process.  A program fills the ring with CALL !KEYSET(name, key,
                        id); !KEYCLEAR and !KEYLIST empty and list it.  A person at a terminal types a passphrase at a hidden prompt with the new
                        verb ENCRYPT.KEY SET name salt (add CONFIRM the first time); ENCRYPT.KEY CLEAR and ENCRYPT.KEY LIST empty and list the
                        ring, and LIST shows only names and key ids, never a key.  The ring is emptied by LOGTO, by sign-on and when the session
                        ends, and it is never written to disk, the audit trail or a $COMO file.  A phantom does not inherit it and has to set
                        its own key.  A second key set under a name does not replace the first: the newest encrypts and older ones still read
                        what they made.
             Why         An application can keep a salary or an identity number encrypted in the file while a person who holds the passphrase
                        still sees it in an ordinary listing.  A stored value carries a key id and a check, so SD can say "no such key", "wrong
                        key" or "damaged" (STATUS() 10208 to 10213) and never shows a wrong decryption.  Without a key, OCONV leaves the stored
                        value, so a listing shows cipher text and not a blank.  ICONV with no key returns the null string and never the plain
                        text, so a field declared encrypted cannot be filled with plain text by mistake.  SD now also wipes the buffers it copies
                        passphrases, keys and SCRAM secrets into before it frees them.
             Does not    SD does not look after the key.  There is no vault, no recovery and no administrator override: a forgotten passphrase
                        or a lost salt means the data is gone.  A SORT BY the field sorts the cipher text; WITH field = "value" finds nothing even
                        with the key, because the value is encrypted with a fresh nonce each time; TOTAL adds the cipher text, which counts as
                        zero; an index on the field holds the cipher text; COPY moves it untouched.  An administrator, or a debugger on the
                        process, can still read the key from memory.  The ENCRYPT.KEY passphrase has no strength rule, because nothing is stored
                        or compared and a long phrase beats any mixed-class rule.  This is not encryption of the files themselves: for a stolen or
                        discarded disk use BitLocker or the file system's own encryption.  It was written for SD Core for Linux and carried here;
                        it has not yet been run on Windows.

20 Oct 26  AN API CLIENT THAT HANGS UP AT THE WRONG MOMENT, OR SENDS A FALSE PACKET LENGTH, NO LONGER TIES UP THE SERVER PROCESS (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             What        The process that answers one API connection read each request as a four-byte length followed by that many bytes.
                        It believed the length, and it did not notice when the connection ended in the middle of a read. SD Core for Linux
                        found that a client that closed at the wrong moment, or sent four bytes that read as a huge length, left that
                        process using a whole processor and growing in memory until it was killed. The same code was here; what it does
                        on Windows has not been measured. Now a connection that has ended ends the session, and a packet length below
                        the 6-byte header, over 65,536 bytes before the client has logged in, or over the largest SD string (the limit
                        the client library already uses) after, ends the session at once and leaves one line in the system log.
                        CLEARINPUT no longer loops when the connection has gone.
             Why it      No login is needed to send a packet, and the process that reads the login is the LocalSystem one.
             matters
             Does not    It does not change what a logged-in client may send: a record up to the largest string still goes through.

20 Oct 26  THE FOLDER THAT HOLDS GROUP ACCOUNTS IS LOCKED LIKE THE ONE THAT HOLDS USER ACCOUNTS.

             Setup locked C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts so that other SD users cannot read or change an account's files outside SD, but
             it never did the same for C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts.  Each group account's own folder is locked when the account is
             made, so nothing was open in the normal case.  If that step failed ("Could not secure account directory ..."), the account was
             still reported as made, and its folder kept the access every SD user has to the data tree; nothing repaired it later.  Setup
             now locks group_accounts the same way and sets each group account's folder again on every install and upgrade, so a folder
             left open by a failed step is closed.  The step also skips a folder that is really a link to another place.  If you have
             made group accounts, check with: icacls C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts\<account>  (it should list only SYSTEM,
             Administrators and sdg_<account>).

19 Oct 26  SDIDX AND SDFIX, THE STAND-ALONE FILE TOOLS, NO LONGER DO SEVERAL WRONG THINGS (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

             sdidx       When it moved indexes out of a file's own directory, or deleted them, it removed the old %2, %3 ... through a name buffer
                        only 33 bytes long, so on almost any real data path the old index file stayed behind while the program reported success.
                        The leftover then stopped you moving the indexes back (error 17).  The buffer now holds any path the file header can hold.
                        If you used an older sdidx, look for a stray %2 beside %0 and %1 and delete it by hand once you have checked the index is
                        where the header says.  sdidx -d answered n at "Delete ... (Y/N)?" used to say "All indices deleted" and clear the header
                        anyway, leaving the index files on disk with nothing pointing at them; it now stops ("Indices not deleted", exit status 1),
                        and input that ends at the question counts as n.  sdidx -m and -d used to delete the whole old index directory with
                        everything in it; they now delete only the index files the header lists and remove the directory only if that leaves it
                        empty.  sdidx -p now prints what -q prints.  A relative index path given to -m or -p is made absolute from the current
                        directory before it is stored; a path argument longer than 255 characters is refused.
             sdfix       -F could not delete an index file in the file's own directory: it printed "Corrected" and removed nothing; it can now.
                        The repair for "Invalid split and/or merge loads" wrote split 50 and merge 80, the very values the check rejects; it now
                        writes the defaults SD gives a new file, split 80 and merge 50.  -Q looped for ever if its input ended while it was asking
                        "Fix?" (and at the dump-and-rebuild question and the interactive prompt): the end of input now counts as "no" and says so.
                        The interactive W command reads its address and value as the hex its help always said and writes the bytes in the order
                        typed (it read decimal and swapped bytes).  -R together with -F or -Q is refused with a message (run the fix first, then -R
                        alone).  sdfix now refuses a file that is open within a running SD, as sdidx always did.  A file whose path is 252
                        characters or more is refused: with a path that long the names of the higher index files were cut short to the overflow
                        subfile's name, and -F then deleted that subfile together with the real index.  The exit status is 1 if any named file
                        failed.
             sdconv      Named a hashed file, it used to print nothing, change nothing and exit 0, which read as a successful byte-order conversion.
                        It now says "<file> is a hashed file: sdconv converts compiled programs only, nothing was changed" and exits 1.  It
                        never did convert hashed files in this SD (the call had been lost in a compiler-warning clean-up), this SD cannot open a
                        big-endian hashed file, and both Linux and Windows are little-endian, so nothing is lost.
             Does not    Neither tool takes a backup and neither can be undone; sdidx still cannot move one index and leave the others; sdfix
                        still only reports an index tree that is damaged; and sdconv still converts compiled programs only.
             Not yet run on Windows: these were carried from Linux and compile, but have not been exercised here.

19 Oct 26  MASTER.LOGIN NOW RUNS FOR AN API SESSION TOO.

           The MASTER.LOGIN paragraph in the SDSYS VOC is run once by a session that connects through the API, when it
           first enters an account, before that account's own LOGIN paragraph - as it already was for a terminal session
           or a phantom.  There is none unless you write one.  It runs in the account just entered, under that account's
           own switches.

18 Oct 26  THE SCREEN LIBRARY AND THE SCREEN STATEMENT (FROM SD CORE FOR LINUX).

           A BASIC program can now draw a screen of its own with the SCREEN statement instead of cursor addressing:

               screen open ...            screen close            screen clear / text / box / cursor / refresh / size / key / clip
               screen msgbox / menu / menubar / pick / ask        pop-up dialogues that give the screen back as they found it
               screen form ... label, frame, entry, check, radio, list, combo, button, tabs, memo, table, tree, scroll bar, progress
               screen wait / get / set / values / focus / hide    events from a form that stays on the screen

           Everything is drawn in a named style (window, highlight, bar, hotkey, field ...) and a theme says what each looks like.
           A program that asks for nothing gets the "mono" theme, which uses only reverse video and underline; "classic" and
           "blue" use colour.  SCREENTHEME=name in sd.conf chooses the default.  The mouse (xterm SGR reports) and a resized
           terminal are handled.  A program that ends without SCREEN CLOSE has the terminal put back at the next prompt.
           Nothing is drawn in a phantom, and a colour theme is refused where the terminal cannot show colour.

           THE LIBRARY IS NOT YET TRIED ON A WINDOWS CONSOLE.  It is Linux's code, carried and compiled here; Linux tests it on a
           pseudo-terminal, which Windows does not have, so the first runs here will be by hand.  SED, CED and the IDE, which
           Linux builds on this library, are not part of this release.

           New messages 12172-12175 (Linux 11167-11170).  New kernel key K$WINSIZE (92).

18 Oct 26  THE TERMINAL'S OWN SIZE IS NOW READ FROM THE TERMINAL.

           SD used to start every session at the terminal type's 120 by 36 unless the user typed TERM w,h.  It now asks the terminal how
           big it is when the session starts and again whenever the terminal is resized, and uses the answer as the width and the
           lines per page; KERNEL(K$WINSIZE, '') returns the width, a field mark and the height, or null with no terminal (a phantom, a
           pipe, the API).  TERM w,h still sets them by hand and holds until the next resize.  New accounts no longer carry
           TERM 120,36 in their LOGIN paragraph (existing accounts keep theirs).

18 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER AND MESSAGE ... IMMEDIATE ON THE SCREEN LIBRARY; THE ACCUTERM, SDTERM AND SDCONSOLE SUPPORT IS GONE.

           The code that asked a terminal to remember the screen under a pop-up, report the mouse and rebind function keys with escape
           sequences only AccuTerm understood has been removed, with the eleven AccuTerm terminal types (vt100-at, vt220-at, vt320-at,
           vt420-at, wy50-at, wy60-at, adds4000-at, vp-at, viewpoint60-at, addsviewpoint-at, pcmon-at, and their aliases).
           SDTerm and SDConsole, Ladybridge's own Windows terminal programs, are not provided with SD and are no longer supported:
           the sdterm terminal type, its @(-29), @(-30) and @(-256) sequences and the TERM DISPLAY lines about it are gone.
           SAVE.SCREEN() and RESTORE.SCREEN still exist and do nothing a terminal could see.

             The debugger  On a terminal that reports its size and has an alternate screen (xterm and the terminals that behave like
                        it) the debugger takes the whole screen, drawn in the library's styles; F1 shows help in a box, F4 the
                        program's own screen.  A terminal without an alternate screen, which includes the "windows" terminal type
                        today, and a session with no terminal get the line-by-line debugger as before.
             MESSAGE ... IMMEDIATE  Shown in a box over the alternate screen, or as lines and "Press return to continue".
             OPTION DEBUG.REBIND.KEYS  Still accepted, no longer does anything.

           A user on AccuTerm should set the terminal type to xterm or vt100: the -at types are no longer known.

18 Oct 26  FILEINFO KEY 1021 AND THE PICK STYLE CALL "FILE NAME" (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH E).

             FL$WHO     FILEINFO(file, 1021) now answers as the openQM manual says FL$WHO does: the user numbers of
                        the other sessions that have the file open, with a field mark between them, and null when
                        there are none.  The caller is left out unless it has the file open through more than one file
                        variable.  Until now 1021 answered the Windows port's text form, "12 (don), 14 (ann)"; that is
                        at 1100 (FL$HOLDERS) and is what SD's own "cannot gain exclusive access" messages use.  A
                        program that asked FILEINFO for 1021 by number and wanted the text must ask 1100; programs
                        that use the name FL$HOLDERS or FL$WHO from keys.h are right once they are compiled again.
                        The Windows port still has the text form at 1021.
             CALL file   CALL "file name" (arguments) calls the program compiled into a file, without the catalogue:
                        the string is a file as the VOC names it, a space, and the program; the object directory is the
                        VOC entry file.out.  It can also be held in a variable (CALL @nm).  The file must be a VOC
                        name, found as OPEN finds it, so a Q-pointer follows the OS-access gate as for OPEN; an
                        operating system path, a program name beginning with $ ! * or _, a third word and an unknown
                        file are refused as an illegal call name, and a program that is not in the file as not found.
                        STATUS() is left as it was.

18 Oct 26  L-TYPE DICTIONARY LINKS IN I-TYPES, AND LINKS TO LINKS (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH D).

             Links       An L-type dictionary record names another file and how to find the record in it, and can now
                        be used inside an I-type as well as in a query: an I-type whose expression is TTL%TITLE, or
                        UPCASE(TTL%AUTHOR), or COPY : "/" : TTL%TITLE, fetches the item TITLE or AUTHOR of the file the
                        link TTL names, for the record the link's id expression gives, just as the same expression
                        written with TRANS() would.  Before, a name with a percent sign in an I-type stopped the
                        compile with an internal error message.
             Chains      A link may reach through another link: if READERS has a link OUT to BOOKS, and BOOKS has a
                        link TTL to TITLES, OUT%TTL%TITLE is the title of each book on loan, in a query or in an
                        I-type.  Up to ten links may follow one another.
             Queries     A link named in a query reached only D, I and C items one link away, and treated every
                        result as multivalued.  It now reaches A, S and E items too and follows links to links, and
                        it takes the column heading, format, conversion and association of the item it reaches.
             Field 4     An L record may have a fourth field, an expression that gives the name of the file actually
                        read, for a set of files that share one dictionary; field 3 is still the file whose dictionary
                        is searched.
             Messages    A link whose file has no dictionary, whose item is not in it, that has no id expression or
                        file name, or whose expressions have semicolons, is refused with a message naming the link or
                        the item (12406-12409 are new).  "Link item xx not found" used to name the link, not the item.
             Undefined   An I-type that named a field the dictionary does not have, other than one called F and a
                        number, aborted the compile with "Non-numeric where numeric required" in $ICOMP instead of
                        saying "Field 'xx' is not defined".  It says the second now.
             What it does not do.  A link is worked out when an I-type is compiled, so after a link is changed the
                        I-types that use it need CD (COMPILE.DICT) to follow it; a query that names the link is
                        compiled each time and does not.  The item name after the percent sign cannot contain a hyphen.
                        A record in the dictionary whose id contains the percent sign is found before any link.

18 Oct 26  RUN TIME ERRORS AS EXCEPTIONS, EXCEPTION GROUPS AND TRANSACTIONS IN TRY (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH C).

             SYS.*       Many errors that stop a program can now be caught with TRY / CATCH, under the names
                        openQM gives them: an unassigned variable is SYS.PROGRAM.DATATYPE.UNASSIGNED, divide by zero
                        SYS.PROGRAM.DIV_ZERO, a deadlock SYS.LOCKS.DEADLOCK, the record lock limit SYS.LOCKS.LIMIT,
                        a subroutine that is not catalogued SYS.PROGRAM.NOT_FOUND, a bad print unit
                        SYS.PRINTER.INVALID_UNIT, and about fifty more names for some hundred and twenty errors
                        (the list is on the program control page).
                        @EXCEPTION.DATA is the message the abort would have printed, which is also in @ABORT.MESSAGE.
                        When no handler can catch the exception - none written, or one the throw cannot reach past an
                        EXECUTE, a trigger or a dictionary expression - the error stops the program with the same
                        message as before, so a program that does not use TRY is not changed.  An error that is caught
                        prints and logs nothing.  A few errors have no name and still stop the program whatever
                        handlers there are: the failure of a transaction's commit and the refusals of the account
                        gates (OS access, SH, OS.EXECUTE).  Catching an error does not undo it: a write that failed has
                        not been made.
             Groups      An exception name is a path of components, and a CATCH takes everything below the name it
                        writes: CATCH SYS.LOCKS takes SYS.LOCKS.DEADLOCK, CATCH ACCOUNT takes ACCOUNT.INVALID.CLOSED.
                        Whole components only (CATCH ACC takes neither).  This was missing from TRY when it was added;
                        until now a CATCH took only the exact name.  Where one TRY has several clauses the first that
                        covers the name is used.
             Transactions   A transaction begun inside a TRY clause and still open when an exception leaves it is rolled
                        back, as the openQM manual says (it was not).  A transaction that was open before the TRY is
                        left open.  This applies to THROW as well as to the system exceptions.
             PDEBUG     A phantom that reaches the debugger with no PDEBUG waiting used to end.  If its program has a
                        handler for SYS.PROGRAM.NO_PDEBUG, or for a group above it, SYS$ANY or SYS$UNHANDLED, the
                        exception is thrown instead and the phantom carries on from the handler; with none it ends as
                        before.

18 Oct 26  LINE TERMINATOR MODES AND TRIGGERS ON DIRECTORY FILES (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH B).

             Line ends   A directory file, and a sequential file opened with OPENSEQ, can be put in one of four
                        line terminator modes, as openQM does it: a letter in field 6 of the F-type VOC entry chooses
                        the mode for every OPEN or OPENSEQ of the file: L (mode 0, LF only, a CR is data), C (mode 1,
                        CR LF only, so a lone CR or LF is data and WRITE and WRITESEQ put CR LF), B (mode 2, CR LF or
                        LF, a lone CR kept) and X (mode 3, CR LF or LF, every CR thrown away).  READ and WRITE of a
                        directory file and READSEQ and WRITESEQ follow the mode; a record longer than the 31,744
                        bytes SD reads at a time is converted the same however it is cut.  A file with no letter
                        behaves exactly as before (READ ends a line at CR LF or LF and keeps a lone CR, READSEQ ends
                        it at LF and drops a CR in front of it, WRITE and WRITESEQ end every line with CR LF), so
                        nothing changes for a program that does not ask.  That unset behaviour is SD Core for
                        Windows' own: SD Core for Linux reads and writes LF only until a mode is chosen.  Once a
                        letter chooses a mode, the two products convert alike.
                        What it does not do: READBLK, WRITEBLK, OSREAD and OSWRITE never convert, MARK.MAPPING OFF
                        still turns every conversion off, and openQM's FCONTROL(fvar, FC$CRLF, mode), which sets the
                        mode of one open file variable, is not here: SD's FCONTROL for programs is not there yet.
             Triggers   A directory file can have a trigger function.  SET.TRIGGER works on it, LIST.TRIGGERS lists it
                        and FILEINFO(FL$TRIGGER) and (FL$TRG.MODES) answer for it.  The trigger is kept in a small
                        text file called %header% in the directory (trigger=name, modes=n), which SELECT, COUNT and
                        ANALYSE.FILE do not count as a record, CLEAR.FILE leaves, and no record id can name.  READ, WRITE,
                        DELETE and CLEARFILE call the function for the modes that are set, with the arguments a hashed
                        file's trigger gets; it can refuse the operation or change the data.  WRITESEQ, WRITEBLK,
                        OSWRITE and OSDELETE do not call it (openQM's limit).  A change takes effect when the file is
                        next opened.  Whoever can write to the directory can change %header%, as they can add or
                        remove records, so a trigger on a directory file is only as safe as the directory.  CLEAR.FILE
                        of a directory file also stopped losing a directory handle each time it ran.

18 Oct 26  NEWVOC.MODS, PHANTOM DEBUGGER @VARIABLES, QUERY IN / NOT.IN AND USER SORT SYSTEM (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH A).

             NEWVOC.MODS  The optional @SDSYS/newvoc.mods file (already applied to new accounts
                        and by UPDATE.ACCOUNTS) is now also applied to SDSYS's own VOC when SD
                        is installed.  The installer does not ship the file; whether an
                        upgrade leaves it where it is has not been checked.
             PDEBUG     Evaluating an @variable (/@var) in the phantom debugger now returns the
                        value from the debugged phantom process rather than from the local
                        PDEBUG client session.  More @variables are known to it.
             IN, NOT.IN Query processor selection clause operators:
                          field IN {NO.CASE} listname
                          field IN {NO.CASE} "filename id"
                        and NOT.IN test whether a field value is present in a saved select list
                        in $SAVEDLISTS or in the fields of a named file record, with
                        support for NO.CASE, EVERY and WHEN clauses.  What it does not do: the
                        values in the list are compared as they are stored, with no input
                        conversion (a date field needs a list of day numbers), and an alternate
                        key index is never used.  IN and NOT.IN are now keywords.  After an
                        = sign an unquoted IN is still an ordinary value, but after OR or AND,
                        where the field and operator are repeated for you, an unquoted IN or
                        NOT.IN is the operator, so quote the value there.  A dictionary item
                        called IN wins over the keyword.  An empty list name ("") says so
                        (message 12405) instead of printing "To:".
             SORT(), SORTADD, SORTCLEAR, SORTDATA(), SORTNEXT()
                        Public BASIC sorting system supporting concurrent sort sessions via
                        sort variables (VARTYPE VT$SORT = 20; up to 255 open in one process, and
                        a sort is freed when its variable is overwritten or goes out of scope),
                        up to 32 keys with per-key SRT$ flags in syscom/keys.h (SRT$LEFT,
                        SRT$RIGHT, SRT$COMPOUND, SRT$RIGHT.FLOAT, SRT$STRICT, plus
                        SRT$DESCENDING, SRT$UNIQUE, SRT$NOCASE and SRT$DATA), and optional
                        key retrieval via SORTNEXT(sortvar, {MAT} keys) or item count limit via
                        SORTDATA(sortvar, max.items).  A BTREE statement given a sort variable
                        is refused.  A variable that is not a sort says "Not a sort variable"
                        (12403) and the 256th open sort says "Too many sorts are open at once"
                        (12404).  What it does not do: keys that compare as equal come out in
                        no promised order once a sort is large enough to spill to a work file
                        (newest first while it stays in memory), a mode above 4 is taken as 0,
                        and only the low byte of the flags is used.
             SORTING   The sort that SORT, SSELECT and the query processor use was reorganised to
                        share its code with the new one.  Its results are unchanged, with one
                        correction: a unique sort that spilled to work files now drops a
                        duplicate that lay in two different files, which the old merge kept.

18 Oct 26  CLASSES, GET, SYNC FILES, LOGTO RESET ALL, WIDE SORTS AND MORE (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH 4).

             Classes  PUBLIC / PRIVATE var = value
                        A class variable, or a matrix, may be given a starting value: a
                        number, a string, CHAR(n) or one of @FM @VM @SM @TM @IM @AM @SVM
                        @TRUE @FALSE.  It is set when the object is made, before
                        CREATE.OBJECT runs.  It is a constant, not an expression.
                       CLASS c INHERITS other(values)  and  INHERITS other AS name(values)
                        pass constants to the inherited class's CREATE.OBJECT (at most 32).
                       RUN of a class module, or of its catalogue name, makes the object,
                        runs its MAIN and lets the object go.  CALL of a class is still
                        refused.  A class with no MAIN gets the usual object message.
                       A class with 32 names or more is searched through an index built
                        the first time it is needed; a program with a class of hundreds
                        of members ran many times faster.  Behaviour is the same.
             GET var {, n} {SETTING c} FROM device {UNTIL t} {RETURNING last} {WAITING s}
                        {THEN | ELSE}
                        Reads from a FIFO, port, character device or a file opened with
                        OPENSEQ, a character at a time, with a timeout for each wait.  The
                        terminator is kept in the data.  A timeout takes the ELSE and
                        leaves STATUS() at 0.  (GET inside a class still declares a public
                        function, as before.)
             OPEN / OPENPATH ... SYNC, the S flag in field 6 of a file's VOC entry,
                        FILEINFO(f, FL$SYNC)
                        Write a file's data to disk after every write and delete, for a
                        file that must survive a power failure.  Slower, by design;
                        a file without the flag behaves as it did.  (openQM's FCONTROL
                        function can switch it on and off too; SD's FCONTROL for programs
                        is not there yet.)
             LOGTO name RESET ALL
                        From a stacked command level, casts off every level and runs the
                        new account's LOGIN at level 1.  At level 1 it is the same as
                        RESET.  Refused in an API session (message 12397).
             I-types  $MODE name{,name} ;
                        An I-type may begin with $MODE for CHANGE.NO.OVERLAP,
                        COUNT.OVERLAP, INDEX.OVERLAP, NOCASE.STRINGS, TIME.MS (and
                        COMPATIBLE.APPEND, which is accepted).  Not allowed in an I-type
                        that another one uses; any other mode is refused.
             ED        External commands: a line typed in ED that starts with an
                        external command name (EXTERNAL in $ED.OPTIONS) runs a program
                        on the record being edited.  See the ED page.
             Sorts     The disk sort handles records of any length SD can hold; the
                        lengths in the sort file are 32 bits where they were 16, so a sort
                        of long records is no longer cut off.  The count of a sort that
                        returns no data was one short when the last record ended the file.
             Hashed files  A split that finds the disk nearly full now stops before
                        the file's size is changed, with an entry in the log, instead of
                        leaving a part-split file.  It cannot undo a split that fails
                        after the new group has started to be used; that still needs a
                        file check.
             @(-6) @(-12) @(-14) @(-59)
                        Use the terminfo capabilities eblink, eprot, edim, erev and ebold
                        when the terminal's entry has them (add them in terminfo.mods),
                        and the plain attribute reset when it does not.  No entry that
                        ships with SD has them, so nothing changes until you add them.

             What these do NOT do: a class variable's value cannot be an expression; a
             timeout of GET is for each character, not for the whole read; the sync flag
             does not make a power failure safe for anything else on the machine; RESET ALL
             was not run in an API session (a test cannot host one).

18 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER, ED, LIST MERGES AND ANALYSE.FILE (QM4 FIXES TAB, NEXT BATCH).

             Debugger  A program compiled for the debugger stops before each CASE
                        condition, so stepping shows the one being tried.
             ED       In edit values mode a line number carries the positions above it:
                        2.0001 is value 1 of field 2, 2.3.0001 subvalue 1 of value 3.
                        NO.EV.POSITION in $ED.OPTIONS turns it off.
             LIST.UNION, LIST.INTER, LIST.DIFF, MERGE.LIST
                        Find a key of one list in the other without scanning it: two lists
                        of 15000 keys merge in seconds, where the time grew with the
                        product of their lengths.  The results are the same.
             ANALYSE.FILE file STATISTICS
                        Also shows a histogram of group utilisation: how many groups use up
                        to 10%, 20% ... 100% of their size, and how many are over 100%
                        (have overflow blocks).
             Correlatives  A;NOCASE;expression makes the string operations of that one
                        correlative case insensitive.

             What these do NOT do: the debugger commands /* (every variable), ^ (control
             characters as ^nnn), WINDOW n and a token as the value of SET are not here:
             they are written for the debugger on the screen library, which this
             debugger is not.  The group histogram counts a group with its
             overflow blocks together.  Not done in this sitting: IN and NOT.IN in the query
             processor (SD has neither at all, so their saved-list-file form waits for
             them), the I-type $MODE prefix, class variable initialisers, RUN of a class,
             sort items over 32 KB.

18 Oct 26  SMALL FIXES AND OPTIONS (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCHES 1 AND 2).

             NUMLOCKS      Range checked, 1 to 32767.  The lock table's cells are 16 bits,
                        so a larger value used to wrap; SD now refuses to start with it.
             PHANTOM       A phantom started while SD is suspended gives back its place in
                        the user table.  It used to stay there, looking like a process
                        that was running and could not be logged out.
             SELECT of a directory file
                        At the operating system's limit of open files the select now closes
                        the oldest cached file and tries again, where it used to answer with
                        an empty list and say nothing.
             SET.FILE      The target may be a Q-pointer, which makes a chain of pointers.
                        Not recommended, but it opens; every link is followed with the
                        account test it always had.
             CATALOG bp -name
                        A hyphen is a global catalogue prefix, like * ! _ and $.
                        DELETE.CATALOG -name removes it.
             CONFIGURE.FILE file DIRECTORY REPORTING
                        Shows how many records have been converted, every thousand and at
                        the end; IMMEDIATE shows the groups moved.  Nothing else changes.
             sdfix -Z      Clears the dead space at the end of each group (the bytes after
                        the used part of a block).  It reports how many blocks it cleared,
                        a second run clears none, and no record changes.
             dir_dict      Three items for a directory file's dictionary: DAYS (days since the
                        record was modified), DTM (date and time of the last modification)
                        and OWNER (the user name that owns it).
             OSPATH key 15  The owner's user name, for internal programs (DIR_DICT's OWNER).

             Looked at and found right already: a hangup at the break key prompt ends the
             process; the U format wraps in a report as T and L do; an inherited public
             variable is found beside a function of the same name.

             What these do NOT do: NUMLOCKS cannot be raised past 32767, so a file cannot
             hold more than that many locks at once (openQM's 65535 limit is not matched).
             sdfix -Z is for a file nobody has open.  dir_dict's items answer for a
             directory file only.  Not done in this sitting: LOGTO RESET ALL, and the five
             terminfo codes that end an attribute (eblink and the others), which need
             the extended part of a compiled terminfo entry to be read.

18 Oct 26  MODE AND RANGE IN A QUERY.

             LIST file MODE field         the value that comes most often
             LIST file RANGE field        the largest value less the smallest
                        Like MEDIAN: the figure is at the end of the report and at
                        each breakpoint, and NO.NULLS leaves the nulls out.  MODE
                        takes any values; when several come equally often it shows
                        the first.  RANGE takes numbers only.

             What it does NOT do: they are two new words, so a dictionary item
             called MODE or RANGE can no longer be named in a query
             plainly (the same is true of MEDIAN).  Accounts get the words from
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, which the installer runs.

18 Oct 26  QUERY PROCESSOR: VERTICAL REPORTS, $QUERY.DEFAULTS, WHEN, BY.EXP; $BASIC.OPTIONS SEARCH; IF IN A DICTIONARY ITEM.

             LIST file field ... VERTICALLY {FMT {"name.format"} {,"data.format"}}
                        The name column of a vertical report can be given a
                        format (FMT "25L", or FIT for a column as wide as the
                        longest heading) and the values a data format (FMT
                        ,"20L"), which every item that is not right justified in
                        its dictionary entry and has no FMT of its own takes.  A
                        record $VLIST in the dictionary or the VOC (X, then the name
                        format or FIT, then the data format) sets them for every
                        vertical report of that file; the command line wins.  A
                        second value of a multivalued field now repeats the colon,
                        and a line that wrapped has none, so the two can be told
                        apart.
             $QUERY.DEFAULTS
                        Field 3 holds default words for SELECT, SSELECT and SEARCH,
                        as field 2 does for LIST and SORT, and field 4 the format of
                        the id column the query processor makes up when the
                        dictionary has no @ID (a width and L, R, T or C).
             WHEN field
                        With no operator it keeps the values that are not null; it
                        used to keep the null ones.  WHEN field BETWEEN a b compared
                        only the first value of a multivalued field; it now compares
                        them all.  UNLIKE NO.CASE ignores the case of the pattern.
             BY.EXP f1 BY.EXP f2
                        More than one exploded sort, provided the fields are in the
                        same association; one outside it is refused.
             $BASIC.OPTIONS: SEARCH file.list
                        The files an $INCLUDE with no file name looks in, in order,
                        @ being the file the source is in.  Without it: that file,
                        then SYSCOM, as before.
             A or S dictionary item, field 8
                        May begin with an IF expression, which is taken as an
                        A-correlative without the A; in front.  It was run as a
                        conversion code and gave the field's own value.

             What these do NOT do: VERTICALLY FMT and $VLIST do not change a
             report that is not vertical.  The data format leaves a right
             justified item and an item with its own FMT alone.  The SEARCH list
             of a C-type dictionary item has no @ (it has no source file).

18 Oct 26  THE APPLICATION EVENT PROFILER.

             PROFILER ON {USER n} {event ...}
             PROFILER OFF {USER n}
             PROFILER {DISPLAY} {USER n}
                        Records what the programs of a session do and where: the
                        events CALL, OPEN, READ (READU, READV, MATREAD...), WRITE,
                        DELETE, OPENSEQ, READSEQ, WRITESEQ, EXECUTE (the first 32
                        characters of the command) and OBJECT (a call of a class
                        module's object), counted by what they were on (the file,
                        the subroutine, the command), the program and its line
                        number.  DISPLAY shows the event types with their counts;
                        type the number of a line to go down to the names, then the
                        programs, then the line numbers, B to go back and Q to
                        leave.  Only the administrator can profile another user's
                        session (USER n): a profile holds the files and commands of
                        that user.

             What it does NOT do: openQM's display uses the cursor keys, this one
             asks for a line number.  The profile lives in memory while it is on
             and is put in the $ipc file when it is turned off, not in a file in a
             temporary directory, so it is gone when SD is restarted.  It holds
             4,000 different events and says how many it could not log.  A
             user's program is shown by the pathname of its object code.  A
             session needs the new version of SD running: the installer restarts it.

18 Oct 26  OPTION STACKED.ACCOUNT.

             OPTION STACKED.ACCOUNT
                        An EXECUTE of a command that does a LOGTO now leaves
                        the process in the account it began in.  Without the
                        option it stays in the account the command went to, as
                        it always did.  The revert undoes LOGTO's change and
                        does not make a new entry: no membership test, no
                        ON.LOGTO or LOGIN paragraph, and the account's SH and
                        OS.EXECUTE switches go back to the ones the first
                        account had, so nothing the visited account granted is
                        kept.

             What it does NOT do: a LOGTO typed at the prompt is not reverted,
             and what an aborted EXECUTE leaves was not measured.  The option
             is off unless you set it.

18 Oct 26  A REPORT AS XML.

             LIST file field ... XML {ELEMENTS} {WITH.DTD | DTD.ONLY}
                                     {WITH.SCHEMA | SCHEMA.ONLY}
                        The report is an XML document: the declaration, a root
                        element ROOT, and an element for each record named for
                        the file.  A single valued field is an attribute of it,
                        a row of an association is a tag named for the
                        association, and a multivalued field in no association
                        is a tag for each value, named for the field with _MV
                        added.  ELEMENTS makes every field an element of its
                        own.  WITH.DTD puts a DOCTYPE with the DTD before the
                        data, WITH.SCHEMA an XML Schema document before it, and
                        DTD.ONLY and SCHEMA.ONLY give the DTD or the schema and
                        no data.  Any query verb that reports takes it.

             What it does NOT do: the openQM manual shows the data and not the
             DTD or the schema, so those two are SD's own.  An XML report
             cannot have breakpoints, totals or other arithmetic (it says so
             and stops); a heading, a footing, DET.SUP and PAN are ignored.
             A field with no value is left out.  A character in a field name
             that XML does not allow in a name becomes an underscore.  The six
             new words are in the VOC of an account after UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, which
             the installer runs.

18 Oct 26  STDFIL.SHARED, DEBUG.EQUATES AND COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS.

             $MODE STDFIL.SHARED
                        The default file variable, @STDFIL, as one variable
                        for every program and subroutine of a command level.
                        A subroutine you CALL uses the file the main program
                        opened, and a file it opens is the main program's
                        default afterwards.  An EXECUTE starts a command level
                        of its own with a fresh @STDFIL and gives the caller's
                        back when it returns.  $MODE STDFIL (one for each
                        program) is as it was, and the two together are STDFIL.
             $MODE DEBUG.EQUATES
                        A program compiled for the debugger with this mode keeps
                        its EQUATE and $DEFINE tokens in the object.  The
                        debugger then shows, SETs and WATCHes a token equated to
                        a variable, a matrix element or a field of a dynamic
                        array variable, and shows a token of a number, a string
                        or CHAR(n) as NAME = value.
             OPTION COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS
                        A file variable that goes (a subroutine returns) no
                        longer takes its locks with it: the file is kept open,
                        out of sight, until the program is back at the command
                        processor, or the EXECUTE that started the level ends.

             What these do NOT do: STDFIL.SHARED uses a named common block
             called $STDFIL, so do not declare one of your own.  DEBUG.EQUATES
             makes the object larger, and a token of a constant cannot be SET
             or WATCHed.  COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS does not change CLOSE, which still
             closes at once, and it holds a file open for as long as a lock on it
             is held, so a program that opens many files in a loop and drops them
             holds them all until it ends.  The option is off unless you set it.

18 Oct 26  SD -STATUS ON A SERVER THAT RECORDED NO START TIME.

             The text sd -status prints when the running SD has no start
             time ("not recorded (started by an older build)") was one byte
             longer than the buffer it was copied into.  The compiler warned
             about it on every build.  The buffer is larger now, so the
             overrun cannot happen.  Nothing else changes in the output.

18 Oct 26  $MODE SELECTV: THE SELECT LIST VARIABLE IS OPTIONAL.

             A program that begins $MODE SELECTV (SELECT then builds a select
             list variable, as SELECTV does) no longer has to name the
             variable.  SELECT f, SELECTV f and SSELECTV f with no TO put the
             list in the program's own default select list variable, and
             READNEXT with no FROM reads that variable.  A READNEXT before any
             SELECT takes its ELSE clause.  Together with $MODE PICK.SELECT,
             EXECUTE 'SELECT ...' followed by SELECT f leaves the executed
             list in the default variable.

             What it does NOT do: without the mode SELECTV and SSELECTV with
             no TO are still a compile error and READNEXT still reads list 0;
             CLEARSELECT with no list number still clears list 0 (it cannot
             name the default variable); a list made by EXECUTE is in list 0,
             which a bare READNEXT does not read in this mode (READNEXT id
             FROM 0 does).  The default variable belongs to the program: a
             LOCAL SUBROUTINE shares it, a subroutine you CALL has its own.

18 Oct 26  LISTF, LISTFL AND LISTFR: MULTIFILES AND WITH / WITHOUT.

             A multifile is now listed with a line for each subfile: the type
             column has a value for each subfile, so that each type is on the
             line of that subfile's pathname, and the first begins with Mult
             (Mult DH, then Dir, for a multifile of a hashed and a directory
             subfile).  The type column is wider (12 characters) and the
             description narrower to make room.  The three verbs also take
             WITH or WITHOUT TRIGGER, ENCRYPTION and REPLICATION, as LISTF
             WITH TRIGGER for the files that have a trigger function.

             What it does NOT do: SD has no encryption and no replication, so
             WITH ENCRYPTION and WITH REPLICATION select nothing and WITHOUT
             selects every file.  Directory files cannot have a trigger in
             SD, so none shows a T.

18 Oct 26  RESTRICTED IN A CLASS MODULE.

             RESTRICTED var {READONLY}, var ...
             RESTRICTED SUBROUTINE name{(args)} ... END
             RESTRICTED FUNCTION name{(args)} ... END
                        A name between PUBLIC and PRIVATE: it is the class's
                        own and every class that inherits it can use it
                        through the inherited object; the main program, an
                        unrelated class and a sibling that shares only the
                        parent cannot - they are told "Unrecognised
                        property/method name", as for a private name.  An
                        object that inherits the class does not pass its
                        restricted names on to the caller.  READONLY holds as
                        it does for PUBLIC.  A name is all public or all
                        restricted: a PUBLIC SUBROUTINE foo beside a
                        RESTRICTED FUNCTION foo is a compile error.

             What it does NOT do: an initial value (RESTRICTED x = 1) is not
             accepted - PUBLIC and PRIVATE do not take one either.  RESTRICTED
             is a reserved word inside a program now.  A class compiled before
             this release has no restricted names; recompiling a class that
             uses the new word is what makes one.

18 Oct 26  CLEANUP, PHANTOMS AND PHANTOM USER.

             CLEANUP=n (sd.conf)
                        Seconds between the checks that look for sessions that
                        ended without logging out (a killed terminal, a lost
                        connection) and clear them out of the user table.  30
                        to 3600; if the line is not there it is 300, the five
                        minutes it always was.  The daemon wakes every ten
                        seconds, so an interval is kept to within ten seconds.
                        CONFIG lists it, CONFIG('CLEANUP') reads it.  A very
                        short interval costs a pass over the user table that
                        often.
             PHANTOMS=u,n (sd.conf)
                        Keeps n user numbers from u for phantoms started with
                        USER.  Nobody else is given one of them: not a session,
                        not an API session, not a phantom started without USER.
                        It must lie between 1 and the highest user number and
                        must not overlap FIXUSERS or PORTMAP, or SD will not
                        start.  CONFIG lists it, CONFIG('PHANTOMS') reads it
                        ("u,n", null when none are kept).
             PHANTOM USER n command
                        The phantom is user number n, one of the numbers
                        PHANTOMS keeps.  It is refused, and no phantom is
                        made, when n is not a number SD has kept, or when a
                        phantom (or anyone) already has it.
             What changed besides: two blocks of kept user numbers side by
             side (FIXUSERS then PORTMAP, now PHANTOMS too) used to hand out
             the first number of the second block, because the number after a
             skipped block was not looked at again; both are skipped now.

             What it does NOT do: with USPLIT=1 the numbers PHANTOMS names are
             not kept (USPLIT ignores FIXUSERS, PHANTOMS, PORTMAP and USERPOOL),
             though USER n still works for a free number in them.  A change to
             either line takes an SD restart.  CLEANUP only says how often the
             check runs; what a check does is as before.

18 Oct 26  INDEX SCANS, DISABLE.INDEX, BIG LETTERS, PHANTOM GROUP AND MORE.

             BSCAN value {, ids} FROM file.var {, key} USING index
                    {RESET} {BY A | D} THEN / ELSE
                        Scans an alternate key index entry by entry, left
                        to right (BY A, the default) or right to left.  With
                        a key it goes to that entry or, if the index has none,
                        returns the nearest one in the direction of the scan
                        and takes ELSE with STATUS() 2.  RESET starts at the
                        extreme left or right.  THEN means an entry was found
                        (STATUS() 1); ELSE with STATUS() 0 is the end of the
                        index, 3 a file that is not a dynamic file, 4 an index
                        the file has not, 5 a sequence other than A or D.
             ROOT file.name, index TO scan.var THEN / ELSE
             KEY( operator, scan.var, index.key, item.id ) THEN / ELSE
                        The Pick way of the same thing.  ROOT makes the scan
                        variable (it is a string: the file name and the index
                        name; ELSE with STATUS() 3019 for an index the file
                        has not).  KEY takes C (first entry at or beyond the
                        key), L (the last entry), N (the next), P (the
                        previous), R (the first id of the key), V (is this
                        key and id in the index) and X (all the ids of the
                        key, field mark delimited), in upper or lower case.
                        Failure is ELSE with STATUS() 3030 past either end,
                        1 for no such entry, 2 for any other operator.  ROOT
                        takes the name of an index only, not an A-correlative
                        that an index was built from.  A program that has a
                        matrix called KEY still gets its matrix.  Numbered
                        list 10 is the scratch list of all of these.
             SELECTLEFTV and SELECTRIGHTV index FROM file.var
                    {SETTING key} TO list.var
                        SELECTLEFT and SELECTRIGHT into a select list
                        variable.
             DISABLE.INDEX file index...   or   DISABLE.INDEX file ALL
                        Stops an index being kept up to date and stops the
                        query processor using it, for a large load of records
                        or a batch that would change most of them.  BUILD.INDEX
                        builds it again and switches it on; until then LIST.INDEX
                        shows it as needing a build.  Like DELETE.INDEX it
                        needs the file to itself: an index switched off while
                        another session had the file open would go on being
                        used, and kept up, there.  (openQM later dropped that
                        need; SD does not.)
             BLOCK.PRINT text   and   BLOCK.TERM text
                        Print text, or show it on the terminal, in letters
                        seven rows high drawn with W.  Eight letters fit an
                        80 column line; longer text breaks at a space where it
                        can.  Letters, digits and . , ! ? - : / + = * ( ) ' have
                        shapes, small letters print as capitals, anything
                        else as a question mark.  openQM's $BIGLTRS record,
                        which changes the drawing character, is not read.
             FILE.STAT {DICT} {file.list} {LPTR} {NO.PAGE}
                        One line for each file of the account (or of the
                        select list, or of the names given): type, current
                        and minimum modulus, group size, record count, bytes
                        in the primary and overflow subfiles, and Y or N for
                        indices, trigger, statistics, case insensitive ids,
                        NO.RESIZE, encryption, replication and journalling.
                        The last three are always N here.  openQM can also
                        report on the files of other accounts; SD does not.
             SWAPMARKS(string)
                        The string as it is, which is what openQM gives on a
                        system without its extended character set.
             SYSTEM(1071)
                        After OSREAD, the STATUS array of the file that was
                        read: permissions, size, links, owner, inode, times,
                        pathname and type.  Null before any OSREAD and after
                        one that failed.
             OPTION QUERY.MERGE.PRINT
                        A query sent to printer 0 while the printer is already
                        on leaves it on at the end of the report, so that the
                        report and what the program prints round it are one
                        job.  Without it the report closes the job, as before.
             PHANTOM GROUP command
                        The phantom is logged out when the process that
                        started it ends.  SYSTEM(1073) lists the user numbers
                        of those grouped with this process (SYSTEM(1058) lists
                        all its phantoms) and LISTU shows a G after the parent
                        number of a grouped phantom.  A parent that goes before
                        the phantom has finished starting takes it with it.
             SET.QUEUE queue, width, depth, top.margin, bottom.margin,
                    mode {, options}   and   SP.ASSIGN {options} {, setptr}
                        Pick style form queues.  SET.QUEUE (0 to 999) keeps
                        what SETPTR would be given for a print unit; mode is
                        1 (printer), 3 (hold file) or 6 (both), the options
                        are SETPTR's.  SET.QUEUE queue shows one queue,
                        SET.QUEUE DISPLAY {LPTR} all of them, SET.QUEUE
                        DELETING queue removes one.  SP.ASSIGN F3 sets print
                        unit 0 from queue 3; n copies, R unit, H (hold file:
                        with S mode 3, without it mode 6), O (keep open) and
                        a SETPTR list after a comma can follow, as in
                        "SP.ASSIGN 2R4HF3,BANNER ""Sales""".  SP.ASSIGN with
                        nothing says which queue unit 0 has.  Options of the
                        queue that do not belong to the mode used (AT for a
                        hold file, AS for printing) are left out.
                        GETPU(PU$FORM.QUEUE, unit) and SYSTEM(1066) give the
                        queue last assigned to a unit and to any unit (-1 if
                        none).  THE FORMS ARE KEPT IN THE ACCOUNT, in the
                        directory file $FORMS, not in one file shared by every
                        account as openQM does: a queue names a printer and a
                        hold file, and a definition any account could change
                        would change where everybody's reports go.  An account
                        can point its own $FORMS entry at another's.
             FSTAT.USER {TOP} {USER n} {LPTR {unit}} {NO.PAGE}
                        File activity of each session: reads, writes and
                        deletes since it started (TOP puts the busiest
                        first); USER n adds opens, clears, selects, index
                        reads, writes and deletes, splits and merges.  An
                        administrator sees every session, anyone else the
                        sessions of their own login name.  It shows the
                        figures once; openQM's repeats every two seconds.
             @PSTAT     Text a program can leave in @PSTAT for PSTAT to show
                        under the command and the call stack of that process
                        ("PSTAT USER n").  Marks in it print as spaces; at
                        most 4000 characters.

18 Oct 26  SELECT.SOCKET() AND SELECTINDEXV.

             SELECT.SOCKET(matrix, milliseconds)
                        Waits on the sockets held in the elements of a
                        dimensioned matrix.  What to watch is set with
                        SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.EVENTS, events), the
                        events being SKT$READ.EVENT 1 (data, a connection
                        waiting on a server socket, or the peer closing),
                        SKT$WRITE.EVENT 2 and SKT$EXCEPTION.EVENT 4.  The
                        result has a field for each element: the letters R, W
                        and E found there.  A time-out is a null string with
                        STATUS() 1011.  SKT$INFO.EVENTS is 9: openQM's manual
                        gives no number, so it is SD's own.  A TLS socket is
                        watched at the TCP connection.
             SELECTINDEXV index {, value} FROM file.var TO list.var
                        SELECTINDEX into a select list variable.  Numbered
                        list 10 is its scratch list and is empty afterwards.

18 Oct 26  INHIBIT.LOGIN, AND A KILLED BACKUP NO LONGER LOCKS EVERYBODY OUT.

             INHIBIT.LOGIN ON / OFF     (administrator only)
                        Stops new sessions - the command prompt and the API
                        alike - while maintenance is done: they are told who
                        has logins inhibited and to try again later.  Sessions
                        already running are not touched, and nor are the
                        phantoms the administrator starts.  With neither word
                        it shows the state.  @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE is the user
                        number of the session that set it, or zero.
                        Not as openQM has it: the administrator can always
                        sign in, from a new session, and any administrator
                        session can switch it off - openQM keeps out
                        everybody, the administrator's own second session
                        included, and a lost terminal would mean restarting
                        SD.  If the session that set it is killed the
                        inhibit lapses by itself.
             A killed BACKUP.ACCOUNT or RESTORE.ACCOUNT: the hold that keeps
                        new sessions out while one runs was kept for as long
                        as the killed session's row stayed in the user table,
                        which is until "sd -cleanup".  It now asks whether
                        the process is still there.

18 Oct 26  DIGEST(), SET.TIMEZONE, KEEP.SELECT, MATWRITECSV, ANALYSE.INDEX,
           FIND.FILE, FIND.PROGRAM, COMMAND.LOGGING, USPLIT AND SYSTEM(20).

             MATWRITECSV matrix TO file.var THEN / ELSE
                        One CSV line of every element of the matrix, each
                        quoted as WRITECSV quotes, joined with the CSV.MODE
                        separator, written to a file opened with OPENSEQ.  A
                        failed write takes ELSE.  openQM's ENCODING clause
                        and ON ERROR are not supported.

             DIGEST(string, is.file, mode)
                        The digest as lower case hexadecimal, for the modes
                        MD5, SHA1, SHA256, SHA384 and SHA512.  With is.file
                        true the string is the pathname of a file, read in
                        blocks; a network session is held to its containment
                        as it is for OPENSEQ, a local one by Windows.  MD4 is
                        not there: it is broken, and OpenSSL no longer ships
                        it by default.  A null string comes back with STATUS()
                        saying why (1 the mode, 3003 no such file, 3035 not
                        permitted, 1008 not a file).
             SET.TIMEZONE zone      and      CONFIG TIMEZONE zone
                        The zone MVDATE(), MVTIME(), MVDATE.TIME() and
                        MVEPOCH() convert in, for this session only; CONFIG()
                        gives it back.  DATE(), TIME() and the clock are not
                        moved.  The zone has to be a name this machine has
                        (a file under /usr/share/zoneinfo of SD's runtime,
                        GMT or UTC): openQM takes any name and gives UTC for
                        a mistyped one, so a refused name is STATUS() 2 here.
                        The installer does not put a zoneinfo directory on the
                        machine, so GMT and UTC are the names that work until
                        one is provided.  A null string goes back to the
                        process's own TZ.
             KEEP.SELECT            With OPTION CLEAR.SELECT the command
                        processor clears list 0 when a command that found
                        it active ends; a program that means to leave what
                        is left of it says KEEP.SELECT and it stays.
             ANALYSE.INDEX file {index {STATISTICS} {DETAIL}} {LPTR {n}}
                        With the file name alone, every index with its
                        number of indexed values and the most records any
                        one value has.  With an index name, the totals and
                        how the values spread over the number of records
                        they point at; DETAIL lists every value.  The
                        index's node sizes, which openQM shows with
                        STATISTICS, are not read: LIST.INDEX shows the same
                        figures with each index's definition.
             FIND.FILE {DICT} file  What the file is, its pathname, and
                        whether its record ids ignore case, it has indices,
                        triggers, is read only.
             FIND.PROGRAM name {DETAIL}
                        The local, private and global catalogue entries of a
                        program, in the order they are loaded, with when each
                        was compiled and what it is.  DETAIL adds the object
                        size and flags; SD's objects carry no compiler
                        version or source name, so those are not shown.
             COMMAND.LOGGING ON / OFF
                        A log of every command this session runs, with where
                        it came from (User, Cmd, PA, Exec): a tab separated
                        file clog.<user number>.log in the account directory,
                        readable by its owner only, added to and never
                        emptied.  openQM puts it in TEMPDIR; SD does not, so
                        that another user cannot make the file first.  There
                        is no global CMDLOG parameter and no !CLOG hook.
             USPLIT=1 (sd.conf)     Interactive users take the lowest free
                        user number and phantoms and API sessions the
                        highest, so that the two never meet.  FIXUSERS,
                        PORTMAP and USERPOOL are ignored while it is on.
             SYSTEM(20)             How many print jobs have been passed to
                        the operating system's spooler since SD started, all
                        sessions together.  (openQM gives a job reference;
                        the spooler's own job number is not read back.)

18 Oct 26  SD BASIC FUNCTIONS AND STATEMENTS THAT OPENQM 4 HAS.

             Names and results follow the openQM 4 manual.  Only what SD does
             differently, or does not do, is written here.
             CEIL(v)  FLOOR(v)  ROUNDUP(v, step)  ROUNDDOWN(v, step)
                        Whole numbers above and below; the last two round
                        away from zero and towards it in steps of step.  A
                        dynamic array gives a dynamic array.
             IS.ALNUM  IS.ALPHA  IS.DIGIT  IS.GRAPH  IS.MARK  IS.SPACE
                        True if the first character of the argument is of
                        that kind; a null string is false.
             LEFT(s, n)  RIGHT(s, n)  Same as s[1,n] and s[n]; a count under
                        1 is taken as 1 for LEFT and 0 for RIGHT.
             LAST(s, d)  LASTS(array, d)  The final element of a delimited
                        string.
             NV(s)  NS(s)  The value or subvalue position numbers in place
                        of the values or subvalues.
             TRIMWS  MATCHESS  MATCHFIELDS  TRIMW, MATCHES and MATCHFIELD
                        over a dynamic array.
             BOOL(v)    A Boolean: false for a null string and for any
                        number that is zero (0, 0.0, 00), true for the rest,
                        the text false included.
             IS.NULL(v) True for the SQL null (JNULL()), not for a null
                        string.
             NUMERIC(v) The value as a number, as v + 0.
             ARG.PRESENT(name)  Whether the caller passed that argument.
             REUSING(name)      Whether that argument was passed with
                        REUSE().
             SORT.COMPARE(a, b, mode {, no.case})  -1, 0 or 1 by the rule in
                        mode: 0 SRT$LEFT, 1 SRT$RIGHT, 2 SRT$COMPOUND, 3
                        SRT$RIGHT.FLOAT, 4 SRT$STRICT, to which 16
                        (SRT$DESCENDING) and 64 (SRT$NOCASE) may be added.
             COMPARES(a, b {, justification})  COMPARE() over two dynamic
                        arrays, with an answer of the same shape.  The SRT$
                        and ML$ names are in SYSCOM KEYS.H.
             MERGELIST(list1, list2, modes)  The manual does not give the
                        values of ML$INTER, ML$UNION, ML$DIFF and ML$UNSORTED,
                        so SD has chosen 8, 16, 24 and 32.  The older
                        CALL !MERGELIST subroutine, with its own words for
                        the modes, is unchanged.
             CHILD(userno)  True if that user number is a phantom this
                        process started and which is still running.
             SIGNAL(userno)  Raises the signal in another process; the
                        other process reads and clears it with SYSTEM(1062).
                        It is true if that process was signalled.  SD signals
                        only a process of the same login user, or any
                        process when the caller is the administrator; the
                        manual has no such rule.
             PARSE(s, pattern, delimiter)  Matches as MATCHES does and
                        returns the string with the delimiter put between the
                        parts that matched each element of the pattern.  The
                        three dots are the same as 0X.  INMAT() is not set by
                        it.
             RMVD(array, code)  REMOVE() that stops only at field, value and
                        subvalue marks: a text mark or an item mark is part
                        of the data.
             TERMINFO.EX(name)  What TERMINFO(name) gives.  openQM lets a
                        capability have several strings; SD's terminal
                        database has one, so there is one value.
             TTYGET()  TTYSET settings  The terminal modes that PTERM sets,
                        as an array of eleven fields that TTYSET puts back.
                        Fields 1 to 5 are the manual's; 6 to 11 (mark input,
                        erase key, control character filter, binary in and
                        out, Telnet) are SD's, so that saving and restoring
                        leaves nothing changed.
             REVREMOVE item FROM array SETTING code  REMOVE going backwards.
                        SETREM n ON array puts the pointer at the nth
                        character first.  The code is the mark that comes
                        before the item, and 0 for the first.
             GROUPSTORE new IN s USING i, n {, delimiter}  The statement for
                        s = FIELDSTORE(s, delimiter, i, n, new).
             SEND s {:} TO file THEN / ELSE  WRITESEQ, or with the colon
                        WRITEBLK, to a file opened with OPENSEQ.
             ACCOUNT.PATH(account)  VOCPATH(file {, dict.flag})
                        The pathname of an account, and of a file named in
                        the VOC (the dictionary part with DICT in front or a
                        true second argument).  A path is told only where
                        the account could open it: its own directory and the
                        files in it always, anything else only to an account
                        that has the OS access gate; otherwise a null string,
                        which for ACCOUNT.PATH is also the answer for an
                        account that does not exist.
             OSREAD var FROM path THEN / ELSE  OSWRITE data TO path
             OSDELETE path
                        A whole operating system file read, written and
                        deleted by name, byte for byte.  They are made of
                        OPENSEQ and DELETESEQ, so an account that has no
                        OS access reaches only its own directory by them,
                        and one that has reaches what the user can.  OSWRITE
                        replaces the file and cuts it to the length of the
                        data; OSDELETE removes a file and not, as openQM's
                        statement does, a directory.  The ON ERROR clause is
                        not supported: STATUS() says what happened.
             CASING ON / OFF  Case sensitivity of string comparisons for the
                        rest of the program, as $NOCASE.STRINGS does for all
                        of it; SYSTEM(1086) tells which.

18 Oct 26  SELECT LIST VARIABLES AND NUMBERED LISTS: FIVE STATEMENTS AND A VERB.

             FORMLISTV array TO var       Makes a select list variable.
             GETLISTV name TO var {SETTING count} THEN / ELSE
                        A saved list into a select list variable.
             WRITELIST array TO name      A saved list written from an array
                        (ON works as well as TO).
             MOVELIST n TO m {KEEP}  MOVELISTV n TO var {KEEP}
                        Move a numbered list, or one in a variable, to
                        another; the ids already read are not moved.
             MOVE.LIST n TO m {KEEP}      The same as a command.
             EXECUTE.LIST command {; command ...}   Runs the commands for
                        each id of list 0 in turn, a pair of empty round
                        brackets standing for the id.  All the ids are taken
                        out of the list first.
             LIST.PHANTOMS {USER name}    The phantoms and no one else.  The
                        pool word of openQM is refused: SD has no pools of
                        phantoms.

18 Oct 26  NINE NEW $MODE WORDS AND DEFAULT VALUES FOR ARGUMENTS.

             $MODE  CHANGE.NO.OVERLAP  COUNT.OVERLAP  INDEX.OVERLAP
                        Whether CHANGE, EREPLACE, SWAP, COUNT, COUNTS, INDEX
                        and INDEXS see occurrences that overlap.
             PICK.CONVERT       CONVERT(string, from, to).
             PICK.SELECT        SELECTV moves a default list to its target.
             TRAP.UNUSED.MAIN   TRAP.UNUSED for the program's own variables.
             EXPLICIT           A variable has to be named in a DIM statement
                        before it is used.
             CONDITIONAL.STATEMENTS  Accepted; it changes nothing, since SD
                        always allows READ, WRITESEQ, LOCATE, SEEK and the
                        rest of the openQM list as the condition of WHILE
                        and UNTIL.
             DEFAULT.UNASS.ARGS  An argument passed unassigned takes its
                        default.
             SUBROUTINE name(a, b = 7)  A default value (a number, a string,
                        @TRUE or @FALSE) for an argument that the caller
                        leaves out; the program needs VAR.ARGS, or the mode
                        above.

18 Oct 26  SYSTEM() KEYS 14, 19, 21, 45, 1048, 1049, 1062, 1067, 1086, 1088.

             14 characters waiting at the terminal (a snapshot for piped
             input), 19 and 21 unique values from the date and time, 45 the
             name of the running program, 1048 a sequence number unique across
             all sessions until SD restarts, 1049 whether HUSH is on, 1062 the
             SIGNAL flag (reading it clears it), 1067 the default group size,
             1086 whether string comparisons are case sensitive, 1088 where
             MAXIMUM() or MINIMUM() found its element.  The names are
             SYS$... in SYSCOM KEYS.H.
             MAXIMUM() and MINIMUM() give the same answers as before.

18 Oct 26  QUERY PROCESSOR: MEDIAN AND RPT.  OPTIONS CORRELATIVE.NOCASE AND
           CORRELATIVE.REUSE.

             MEDIAN     Beside TOTAL and AVERAGE: the middle value (the mean
                        of the two in the middle when there is an even
                        number).
             RPT        Repeats a single valued field on each line of a
                        report on a multivalued one.
             OPTION CORRELATIVE.NOCASE  String operations in an I-type or
                        correlative ignore case.
             OPTION CORRELATIVE.REUSE   Its array operators act as if REUSE()
                        had been applied to each operand.  Both are read when
                        the correlative runs, not when it was compiled.

18 Oct 26  SIX NEW OPTIONS: GIT, DIR.DTM, KEEP.OLD.OBJECT, NO.PHANTOM.NOTIFY,
           SPOOL.COMMAND AND ASSOC.ALL.

             Each is off until OPTION name ON, and is the setting of one
             thing; none changes a lot at once.
             GIT          A directory file keeps .gitignore and .gitattributes
                          under their own names on the disk.  Without it every
                          name that begins with a dot has the dot replaced by
                          %D, which git does not read.  Only those two names;
                          any other name with a leading dot is mapped as ever.
             DIR.DTM      Writing a record to a directory file moves the
                          directory's date and time modified.  The system
                          moves it only when a name is added or removed, so
                          a program or a backup that looks for a changed
                          directory missed a record that was replaced.
                          Closing a written sequential file does not do it.
             KEEP.OLD.OBJECT  A compilation that fails leaves the previous
                          object code where it was, instead of a program
                          that no longer runs.  The listing of the failed
                          compile is not put back.
             NO.PHANTOM.NOTIFY  Set in a phantom, it stops the message that
                          the phantom sends its parent when it ends.
             SPOOL.COMMAND  Shows the operating system command that prints a
                          job, just before it runs.  A diagnostic aid.
             ASSOC.ALL    The query processor ignores the associations in the
                          dictionary and treats every field as one
                          association: with BY.EXP on one multivalued field,
                          each line shows the matching value of the others,
                          not all of them.

18 Oct 26  A K ENTRY OVER A LOCALLY CATALOGUED PROGRAM.

             A VOC entry of type K whose fields 3 onwards hold another entry
             hands the command over to that entry.  When that entry was the V
             CS entry of a locally catalogued subroutine, the command stopped
             with "Unable to load" and the K entry's own name, because the
             object is looked up by the name of its own VOC entry, not the
             name typed.  It now runs.  The name used is the last part of the
             object's pathname, which is the name it was catalogued under;
             a program catalogued under a call name different from its program
             name is not covered.

14 Oct 26  !SORT CAN SORT A SELECT LIST VARIABLE.

             CALL !SORT(list, result, mode)  When list is a select list
                        variable (from SELECTV, FORMLISTV and the like) its
                        ids are read out and sorted, instead of the call
                        stopping with "Data cannot be converted to a string".
                        The result is the sorted ids as a field mark
                        delimited list; the modes are as before.  The ids are
                        used up in the variable, as with any READNEXT.  The
                        call also no longer moves the REMOVE pointer of an
                        ordinary list passed to it.

18 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER'S WATCH TAKES A CONDITION.

             WATCH variable operator value
                        The program stops only when the value just assigned
                        meets the condition; otherwise it carries on without
                        showing anything.  The operators are =, #, <, >, <=,
                        >=, their word forms EQ, NE, LT, GT, LE, GE, and
                        MATCHES.  A value in quotes is that text; a number
                        compares as a number and anything else as text.
                        A WATCH with no condition stops at every assignment as
                        before, a new WATCH replaces the old one with its
                        condition, and UNWATCH clears both.  LIKE and UNLIKE
                        are not supported.  A condition on a local variable
                        works while the program that set it is the one running.

18 Oct 26  STATUS() AFTER END TRANSACTION.

             A program can now test STATUS() straight after END TRANSACTION:
             it is 0 when the transaction committed and 1021 (ER_ROLLBACK)
             when it was rolled back, whether by ROLLBACK or by running into
             END TRANSACTION without a COMMIT.  A commit that fails does not
             come back as an error code (ER_COMMIT_FAIL, 1022, is defined for
             it): a failed commit still stops the program with its message, as
             before.

18 Oct 26  EPOCH, MVDATE, MVTIME, MVDATE.TIME AND MVEPOCH.

             EPOCH()        The time now as a time zone independent number of
                            seconds since 1 January 1970 UTC (the same as
                            SYSTEM(1035)).
             MVDATE(e)      The multivalue date (days since 31 December 1967)
                            of epoch value e, MVTIME(e) the time of day in
                            seconds since midnight, MVDATE.TIME(e) both,
                            joined by an underscore, as 15020_84690.
             MVEPOCH(s)     The epoch value of a date and time written as one
                            string "date_time", and MVEPOCH(date, time) the
                            same with the two apart.
                        The zone is the one the session runs in (the
                        machine's time zone), as DATE() and TIME() use, and
                        summer time is allowed for.  Epoch values are 32 bit
                        (13 December 1901 to 19 January 2038): a value outside
                        that, a time of a day or more, or something that is not
                        a number gives the null string.  A fraction of a second
                        is cut off.  There is no SET.TIMEZONE yet, so a program
                        cannot choose a zone of its own.

18 Oct 26  SSELECTV.

             SSELECTV file TO listvar {RIGHT.ALIGNED} {DESCENDING}
                        Selects the record ids of a file (or the items of a
                        dynamic array) in sorted order into a select list
                        variable, as SSELECT does into a numbered list, and as
                        SELECTV does without the sort.  The sort options are
                        those of SSELECT and may come before or after TO.
                        TO is required.

18 Oct 26  INPUTNULL AND INPUTTRAP.

             INPUTNULL expr  A single character, typed alone as the answer to
                        INPUT @ or INPUTFIELD, makes the variable a null string
                        (what was echoed stays on the screen).  A null
                        expression cancels it, and so does returning to the
                        command prompt.
             INPUTTRAP expr GOTO label{, label...}  and  GOSUB
                        Characters (up to 16) that, typed alone as the answer
                        to INPUT @ or INPUTFIELD, send the program to a label:
                        the first character to the first label, and so on, and
                        a character past the end of the labels to the last
                        one.  The variable is set to null.  With GOSUB the
                        RETURN comes back to the statement after the INPUTTRAP,
                        not to the one after the INPUT.  A null expression
                        cancels the trap.  The trap belongs to the program that
                        set it: a subroutine it calls is not trapped, and
                        neither is a plain INPUT.  When a character is named by
                        both statements, INPUTNULL wins.  If the INPUT @ has a
                        THEN or ELSE clause, neither runs when the trap is
                        taken.

18 Oct 26  A FILL EXPRESSION FOR INPUT.

             INPUT var, length, fill  and  INPUT @(x, y) : var, length, fill
                        The first character of the fill string is shown in
                        every cell of the field that has no character in it, so
                        the width of the field is clear while you type; the
                        erase key puts it back.  When the entry is finished
                        the cells left over take the second character (a blank
                        if the fill has only one).  A third character leaves
                        the cursor at the end of the field instead of after
                        the last character typed.  The fill needs a length,
                        and cannot be used with HIDDEN (refused when the
                        program is compiled).  Without a fill nothing changes.

18 Oct 26  INPUT ... RAW.

             INPUT var, length RAW  Reads exactly length characters and puts
                        them in the variable just as they arrive: a newline
                        or an erase key is data, not an instruction, and
                        nothing is echoed.  The length is required (a program
                        without one is refused when it is compiled).  Items in
                        the DATA queue are taken whole, as for any INPUT.
                        UPCASE and TIMEOUT still work with it.  INPUT @ does
                        not take RAW.

18 Oct 26  INPUTIF.

             INPUTIF var {, length} THEN / ELSE  and  INPUTIF @(x, y) : var, length
                        Reads a line only if one is already there, from the
                        DATA queue or typed ahead on the keyboard; otherwise
                        it takes the ELSE clause at once, writes no prompt and
                        leaves the variable as it was.  The options of INPUT
                        and INPUT @ (HIDDEN, NO.ECHO, UPCASE and so on) work
                        with it.  When SD's input is a pipe or a file rather
                        than a terminal there is always something to read (the
                        end of the file counts), so INPUTIF behaves as INPUT
                        there.

18 Oct 26  EXECUTE ... STACKLIST.

             EXECUTE command STACKLIST  Keeps the default select list as it
                        was, the place READNEXT had reached included, and puts
                        it back when the command returns.  The command is
                        given the list as usual and may read it, change it or
                        replace it; the program has its own again afterwards.
                        Without STACKLIST whatever the command leaves in the
                        default list is what the program finds, as before.
                        It cannot be combined with PASSLIST or
                        RTNLIST (those give or take a list), which is refused
                        when the program is compiled.  With the
                        EXECUTE.CLEARLIST mode the command starts with the
                        list empty and the program still gets its own back.

18 Oct 26  FLUSH.DH.CACHE.

             FLUSH.DH.CACHE {LOCAL | NO.WAIT}  Makes every process close the
                        dynamic files it keeps in its cache of recently closed
                        files, for a program that is about to do something
                        (outside SD) that fails while a file is still open
                        somewhere.  The statement waits for the other
                        processes to say they have done it and gives up after
                        four seconds; a process that is waiting at an INPUT
                        does not answer until a key arrives, so it costs the
                        four seconds.  NO.WAIT asks and carries on; LOCAL
                        flushes only the process it is in.

18 Oct 26  INPUT NO.ECHO.

             INPUT, INPUT @ and INPUTFIELD take NO.ECHO  What the user types is
                        not shown: INPUT answer NO.ECHO.  The new line that
                        ends the entry still appears.  INPUT HIDDEN shows
                        asterisks; NO.ECHO shows nothing (with INPUT @, the
                        field stays blank), and the two together show nothing.
                        UPCASE, TIMEOUT and the other options work with it.

18 Oct 26  LOCATE CAN FORCE CASE.

             The BY string of LOCATE takes C and N  C forces a case sensitive
                        comparison and N a case insensitive one, whatever the
                        program was compiled to do: LOCATE 'banana' IN list<0>
                        BY 'ALN' SETTING p finds 'Banana' in a list kept in
                        alphabetical order without regard to case.  The
                        letters may come in any order and in either case
                        (NAL, aln); N alone does an unsorted search ignoring
                        case.  A BY string without C or N behaves as before.
                        One change in passing: a string whose first letter is
                        L or R (LAN) used to be read as "no sort", and is now
                        ascending, as the letter A in it says.

18 Oct 26  CSV.MODE.

             CSV.MODE mode {, delim}  Sets how PRINTCSV and WRITECSV build a
                        line of comma separated values.  Mode 1 (the default)
                        encloses an item in double quotes only if it holds a
                        double quote or the delimiter; mode 2 encloses every
                        item that is not empty or a number; mode 3 encloses
                        every item, an empty one too.  A double quote inside an
                        enclosed item is written twice in every mode.  The
                        optional delimiter replaces the comma (a null string
                        is a comma, and only the first character of a longer
                        string is used).  A mode other than 1, 2 or 3 is
                        ignored.
             SYSTEM(1043) and SYSTEM(1046)  Give the current mode and delimiter.
             The setting goes with the program: a subroutine you call starts
             with yours, a change it makes is undone when it returns, and an
             EXECUTEd command starts at the defaults and gives yours back
             afterwards.  READCSV, INPUTCSV and DPARSE.CSV are not affected;
             they take their delimiter as before.
             FORMCSV() is still not a function an ordinary program can call;
             PRINTCSV and WRITECSV use it.

18 Oct 26  CASE INSENSITIVE INDICES, AND TRANS() ON A FILE THAT WILL NOT OPEN.

             CREATE.INDEX and MAKE.INDEX take NO.CASE  The index treats keys
                        that differ only in case as one key, so a lookup of
                        'APPLE' finds the records holding 'Apple' and 'apple'
                        too.  Without NO.CASE an index is as it was.  Building
                        such an index (BUILD.INDEX, or MAKE.INDEX, which
                        builds) now keeps every record: the kernel already
                        compared these keys without case, but the build kept
                        only the last spelling's records, which no one could
                        meet before because there was no way to ask for it.
             TRANS(), RTRANS() and XLATE() on a file that cannot be opened
                        Follow the action code, as they do for a missing
                        record: X (the default) gives the null string and says
                        nothing, V gives null and prints "Cannot open", C gives
                        the record id.  They used to print the message every
                        time whatever the code was.  A program that relied on
                        the message with no code now sees nothing; add V.

18 Oct 26  FOUR SMALL ADDITIONS TO SD BASIC.

             ~= operator  Compares two values and ignores case: 'ABC' ~= 'abc'
                        is true.  The plain = is as it was.  Two values that are
                        both numbers are compared as numbers, as with =.
             COMPARE( a, b, justification )  The justification may now have an I
                        after its L or R (as in 'LI') to compare ignoring case.
                        Without the I, nothing changes.
             PRECISION INHERIT  The program takes the precision of the program
                        that called it.  A number after PRECISION works as before.
             INMAT() after MATBUILD and MATWRITE  Returns the index of the last
                        element that went into the string or record, or 0 when
                        the zero element of the matrix was added at the end.

             The kernel's list of operations gained one entry for ~= (the
             extended opcode CFE5), so programs compiled before this build and
             after it are not interchangeable if they use it; none did.

18 Oct 26  THE USER TABLE, THE HOT SPOT MONITOR AND PSTAT.

             LISTU and !LISTU show the account  Each user's line now ends with
                        the account the user is in (": jack, sales"), and the
                        ninth value of !LISTU is the account.  A user moves
                        into another account with LOGTO and the table follows;
                        a phantom starts in its parent's account.  The account
                        is shown to the administrator and, for anybody else,
                        only for the sessions of the login user who is asking;
                        another user's account is left blank.  (LISTU itself
                        is still the administrator's verb: an ordinary account
                        has !LISTU, from a program.)
             LISTU DETAIL  Adds a line above the report: the interactive users,
                        the API sessions, the phantoms that count as licensed,
                        the other phantoms, and the most users there have been
                        at once since SD started.  SYSTEM(1056) gives the same
                        five numbers.  SD has no licence count, so the third
                        number is whatever the system marks as chargeable.
             HSM  The Hot Spot Monitor counts each program's opens, reads,
                        writes and deletes as well as its calls and processor
                        time, and the report has a column for each.  What a
                        program asks for through the OPEN of a name (its VOC
                        lookup) or through READV and WRITEV (which read and
                        write the record themselves) is not counted against it.
             PSTAT  For a process that is waiting for a lock it also shows
                        when the wait began, as LIST.READU DETAIL already did.

             None of this changes what a user can do.  The shared memory
             layout changed, so SD must be restarted by the installer, as for
             any new build.

18 Oct 26  SHOW.LIST, AND SMALLER CHANGES TO THE COMMAND LINE, CATALOG AND ED.

             SHOW.LIST {n}  A new verb: it lists the items of an active select
                        list a page at a time, each with an asterisk, and lets
                        you clear and set the asterisks (C and S with numbers,
                        ranges, VISIBLE or ALL, or just the number); T N P move
                        about, Q keeps what is starred, QC keeps nothing, ? is
                        help.  The list is made again from what is starred.
             Catalogue names  A program run by typing its catalogue name,
                        not the name of a VOC verb, now takes LPTR and NO.PAGE
                        as RUN does.  The words stay in the sentence for the
                        program.  A word in quotation marks is not a keyword.
             .C-  In the command stack editor, .C- undoes the .C just before it.
                        A .L or .? may come between; anything else ends the
                        chance.
             CATALOG ... LOCAL  When the VOC already holds a local catalogue
                        pointer under that name and it points at a different
                        object file, you are asked before it is overwritten
                        (Return is no); NO.QUERY goes ahead as before, and the
                        same file again does not ask.
             ED  EV on an I-type that holds quoted text, brackets and
                        semicolons no longer corrupts the expression (a quoted
                        string was cut from the wrong place).  Text, subvalue
                        and value marks can be typed into ED without NPRINT;
                        the field and item marks still need it, because one in
                        a line would split the line unseen.

18 Oct 26  A BATCH OF SMALLER ADDITIONS (SD BASIC, THE QUERY PROCESSOR AND
           OPTION), EACH OFF UNLESS ASKED FOR.

             $MODE STDFIL  The default file variable: with it, OPEN, READ,
                        MATREAD, WRITE, MATWRITE, DELETE, CLOSE, CLEARFILE,
                        FILELOCK, FILEUNLOCK, RELEASE and SELECT may be written
                        without a file variable and use one the program does
                        not name, as in some other multivalue systems.
             $MODE WRITE.DELETE.THEN.ELSE  WRITE, WRITEU, MATWRITE, MATWRITEU,
                        DELETE and DELETEU take THEN and ELSE clauses; a trigger
                        that refuses the write or delete takes ELSE, and the
                        trigger is told it is being called with an error trap
                        set.  A statement with neither clause is as it was.
             CLASS ... INHERITS name AS variable  The object variable of an
                        inherited class can be given a name of its own.
             Method references  An empty pair of brackets is accepted after a
                        method name in a statement (obj->method()), as it
                        already was in an expression.
             Query X breakpoint control  The X option on a break field makes
                        the previous value of the field available in @BPV for
                        the I-types of that line.
             OPTION PAGINATE.ON.HEADING  With RUN ... NO.PAGE, a heading or a
                        footing still turns pagination on.
             OPTION QUERY.FOOTER.PAUSE  A report with a page footer waits for
                        RETURN after its last footer.
             OPTION FORCE.RELOAD  An unload event (compiling or cataloguing a
                        program) also refreshes the subroutine links that a
                        running program holds, so the next CALL loads the
                        current object code.
             OPTION NUMERIC.EXPONENT  Numbers in exponent form, such as
                        123.45E-2, are numeric.
             OPTION INHERIT.MFILE.PATH  CREATE.FILE puts a new subfile of a
                        multifile in the directory of the default subfile.

           None of them changes anything for a program or an account that
           does not ask for it.

18 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: PARTIAL.EXPRESSIONS.

           A program compiled with $MODE PARTIAL.EXPRESSIONS stops
           evaluating a logical expression as soon as its value is known:
           in A AND B the B is not evaluated when A is false, and in A OR B
           it is not evaluated when A is true.  That makes tests such as
           IF IDX MATCHES '1-2N' AND S<IDX> # 0 safe, and it means that a
           function with a side effect on the right hand side runs only
           when it is needed.  The value of every expression is the same as
           without the setting; only what is evaluated changes.  Without
           the setting both sides are always evaluated, as they were.

18 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: EXECUTE.CLEARLIST.

           A program compiled with $MODE EXECUTE.CLEARLIST clears the
           default select list (list 0) before the command of each EXECUTE
           statement runs, so the command does not pick up a list the
           program happened to have active.  An EXECUTE with a PASSLIST
           clause, with or without a variable, still passes the list in.
           Without the setting the list is passed into the command as it
           always was.

18 Oct 26  NEW OPTION: CHAINED.SELECT.

           With OPTION CHAINED.SELECT in effect, a GET.LIST, QSELECT,
           SEARCH, SELECT, SSELECT or SELECTINDEX that leaves an active
           select list takes the first line of the data queue and runs it
           as the next command, the way a CHAIN would: DATA 'LIST STOCK'
           followed by EXECUTE 'SELECT SALES' lists the selected records.
           When the select finds nothing the queue is cleared, so the
           commands that were waiting for a list do not run on nothing.
           Without the option the data queue is left alone, as it was.

18 Oct 26  NEW OPTION: PICK.ML.CONV.MASK.

           With OPTION PICK.ML.CONV.MASK in effect, a left bracket in an ML
           or MR conversion code no longer asks for negative values to be
           shown in round brackets: it starts a format mask that is
           enclosed in round brackets, as in MR2(#10) or ML2,(%8), which is
           how some other multivalue systems write it.  The mask is read as
           if the brackets were not there, in output and in input
           conversion.  The other negative value styles (+ - < C D) and
           the P, Z and T options are as they were, and a program that
           does not set the option sees no change.

18 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: PICK.READNEXT.

           A program compiled with $MODE PICK.READNEXT keeps the value of
           the id variable when a READNEXT takes its ELSE clause (the list
           is exhausted, or no list is active).  Without the setting the
           variable is set to the null string, as it always was.  Programs
           written for other multivalue systems often read the last id
           after the loop that read the list.  It applies to the plain
           forms of READNEXT; the forms that return value and subvalue
           positions are unchanged.

18 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: OPEN.FAIL.ZERO.FVAR.

           A program compiled with $MODE OPEN.FAIL.ZERO.FVAR gets the
           integer 0 in the file variable of an OPEN or OPENPATH that
           fails, where it is otherwise left unassigned.  Some programs
           written for other multivalue systems rely on that, and an
           unassigned variable stops them with a run-time error the moment
           they test it.  The ELSE clause is taken as always and an OPEN
           that works is not touched.  Nothing changes for a program that
           does not ask for it.

18 Oct 26  FOUR SMALL FIXES.

           CLEAR.FILE on a file that has NO.RESIZE set (CONFIGURE.FILE ...
           NO.RESIZE) empties it but keeps the size it had; it used to send the
           file back to its minimum size, as if the flag were not there.

           LIST.ITEM and SORT.ITEM number the lines of an item with more than
           999 fields with as many digits as the last number needs (0001 to
           1200, say); they used to cut the number to three digits.  An item of
           999 fields or fewer is numbered as before.

           The debugger tells you when it cannot find the source of the
           program it was asked to debug, and then lets the program run on.
           A record named $DEBUG.OPTIONS in your VOC containing the line
           NO.SOURCE.WARNING (one keyword to a line) leaves that message out.
           No other keyword is looked for.

           The terminfo entries for the ADDS 4000 terminal, adds4000 and
           adds4000-at, now say that the terminal leaves a character cell for
           a highlight marker (xmc#1), as the other terminals of its kind do.

           Two things in the same group of openQM changes were found to be
           already so and are now tested: CT opens a file whose name is in
           lower case, and SOCKET.INFO(var, SKT$INFO.OPEN) answers 0 for a
           variable that is not a socket, whether it holds a number, a string
           or nothing, instead of stopping the program.

18 Oct 26  SIX SMALL BASIC ADDITIONS.

           OPEN and OPENPATH take NON.TRANSACTIONAL, alone or with READONLY and
           in either order: OPEN "ORDERS" NON.TRANSACTIONAL TO F ...  A write
           through that file variable is made at once and is not undone when a
           transaction it happened in is rolled back.  It is for the one open
           only; the same file opened without it behaves as before.

           A trailing substring can be assigned to: S[3] = "XY" overwrites the
           last three characters of S.  A value that is too long loses its
           tail and one that is too short is padded with spaces; a count above
           the length of S is the length, and below 1 is 1.  It works on matrix
           elements too, and S[3, 2] = ... is as it was.

           REDIMENSION (short form REDIM) changes the size of a matrix that
           was passed into a subroutine as an argument, which DIM has never
           done: the caller's matrix is the one resized, and keeps its values
           as DIM keeps them.  Elsewhere REDIM is DIM.

           A matrix passed as an argument, MAT A, no longer needs a DIM
           statement in the subroutine or LOCAL SUBROUTINE that receives it:
           how many subscripts it has is read from how it is used.  A matrix
           that is not an argument and is never dimensioned is still refused.

           IF MAT A = MAT B THEN ... compares two matrices.  They are equal
           only if they have the same shape and every element is the same
           string; = and # both work.  A and B are compared as the text of
           their elements, so 1 and 1.0 count as different.

           A READ, READU, READL, READV, READVU, READVL, READNEXT, READSEQ,
           READBLK, READLIST, OPEN, OPENPATH or OPENSEQ statement may be the
           condition of a WHILE or UNTIL, as in LOOP WHILE READNEXT ID FROM 1
           DO ... REPEAT: the condition is true if the statement would have
           taken its THEN branch.  Such a statement cannot be given THEN or
           ELSE clauses of its own there; that is a compile error.  It is not
           yet allowed for the other statements that have THEN and ELSE
           (LOCATE, FIND and the like).

18 Oct 26  FILE HANDLING: READ-ONLY AND NON-TRANSACTIONAL OPENS FROM THE VOC,
           A LOCK ON A HELD PRINT FILE, AND BLOCK DEVICES.

           Field 6 of a file's VOC entry (the F-type entry) can now hold mode
           letters.  R opens the file read-only whenever it is opened through
           that entry, so a write to it fails as it would on a file opened
           with the READONLY option; N opens it non-transactionally, so a write
           to it stays when a transaction that wrote it is rolled back.  The letters may be given
           together (NR) and in either case, and they apply to a file reached
           through a Q-pointer as well.  They affect that open only, as the
           OPEN statement's own options do.  The other letters the openQM
           manual lists for field 6 (B, C, L, M, S and X) are not acted on, as
           before.

           A print unit in mode 3 (SETPTR ... 3), whose output goes to a record
           of the $HOLD file under the default name or one given with AS name,
           now holds an update lock on that record from the moment the file is
           made until the unit is closed.  Another process that reads the
           record with a lock, or writes it, waits until the output is complete.
           A unit printing to AS PATHNAME is outside any SD file and is not
           locked; nor is a unit above 255.  A process that asks for the lock
           while another holds it waits, which stops its print job until the
           holder lets go.

           OPENSEQ can open a block device, a floppy disk drive among them, as
           it opens an ordinary file; FILEINFO(FL$SEQTYPE) says 4 for it.  Who
           may open one is decided by the operating system's permissions, and
           an ordinary user cannot read most of them: this has been tried only
           as far as the open being no longer refused as "not a sequential
           file".

           A command that needs exclusive access to a file (CNAME, CONFIGURE.FILE,
           DELETE.INDEX and the like) already stopped when another session had the
           file open and named the users holding it; that is now tested.

           The ids of records in a directory file may now contain bytes 128 to
           247, which they could not (anything above 126 was refused as an
           illegal record id).  They are passed to the operating system as they
           are given, so an id written with a UTF-8 e-acute is a file with that
           character in its name.  Control characters (below 32) and DEL (127)
           are still refused, and so are the system's own mark characters (248
           to 255), which are not ordinary characters.  This is the one place
           the rule that SD is English only has been lifted, at the owner's say,
           and it adds no other language support: SD does not interpret the
           bytes, fold their case or sort them in any national order.  Windows
           file names are UTF-16, so an id that is not valid UTF-8 (a lone
           Latin-1 byte, say) has not been tried here and may not come back as
           the same id.

18 Oct 26  LOCK AND DUMP DIAGNOSTICS: WHEN A LOCK WAS TAKEN, DEADLOCK=2,
           SYSTEM(1083) AND OBJECTS IN A PROCESS DUMP.

           The kernel now notes the time at which each record lock and file
           lock was taken and each wait began.  LIST.READU DETAIL shows it under
           the lock - "Locked 13 Oct 26 01:56:40" - and, with WAIT, under each
           waiting process - "Wait since 13 Oct 26 01:56:42" - in the time zone
           of the user who gave the command.  Without DETAIL nothing changes.
           A shared lock that is raised to an update lock keeps the time it was
           first taken.  The zone's offset is the one in force when the command
           is given, so a lock taken before a daylight saving change is shown an
           hour out after it, as other times SD reports are.

           DEADLOCK=2 is new.  DEADLOCK=1, as before, stops a process that
           would wait for a record lock held by a process that is itself
           waiting for it, and shows the locks involved.  DEADLOCK=2 does that
           and also writes every lock in the system to a file, deadlock.n,
           where n is the number of the process that was stopped.  It goes
           where a process dump goes: DUMPDIR, or the SDSYS directory, and only
           its owner can read it.  Each lock takes four lines, with a blank line
           between locks: the user number and name; the file number and its
           pathname; the lock type (RU, RL, FX or SX) and, for a record lock,
           the record id; and when it was taken.  The process is told where the
           file is.  DEADLOCK now takes 0, 1 or 2 and refuses other values when
           SD starts; any value but 0 used to be taken as 1.  The default is
           still 0, which lets a deadlock happen.

           SYSTEM(1083) returns the last deadlock the process was in, for a
           program that wants to report it after the abort: a field for each
           process in it, of three values - its user number, the pathname of the
           file and the record id of the lock it was waiting for, the id left
           out for a file lock.  It is null until there has been one.  A record
           id is as the lock table holds it, in capitals in a file that does not
           tell the cases apart.  It is the process that was stopped that can
           ask; the process that was left waiting did not see the deadlock.

           A process dump now shows what an object variable holds: under
           "OBJ: Object" come the object's public and private variables, with
           their names, one to a line like any other variable.  The names are
           found from the class module by the size of its group of variables, so
           a class that also has a COMMON block of exactly the same number of
           variables could show the wrong names (never the wrong values).  An
           object that holds an object is followed four levels down.

           A process that is waiting because the lock table is full still
           answers a request to log it out.  That was already so and is now
           tested.  What is worth knowing is that with the table completely
           full no other session can give a command at all, each command taking
           a lock for an instant to look itself up, so the LOGOUT command cannot
           be given; from the operating system, "sd -k n" logs user n out
           without needing a lock.  NUMLOCKS is the table's size.

           Not done: the openQM 4.0.9 dump of the other process in a deadlock
           (it is waiting, and SD has no way to make it dump itself while
           waiting), and the exception openQM can raise for a deadlock, which
           waits for system events to become exceptions.

18 Oct 26  FIVE NEW sd.conf PARAMETERS AND A LONGER CALL NAME.

           Each parameter below is optional.  With none of them in sd.conf
           SD behaves exactly as before, because every default is the number
           that used to be fixed.  A value outside the range given makes SD
           refuse to start and name the parameter, as it does for the others.
           CONFIG lists them; CONFIG() reads them in a program.

             MAXRLOCK=n   The most record locks one process may hold, 0..100000.
                          0, the default, is no limit.  A process that asks
                          for one lock more stops with "Record lock limit
                          (MAXRLOCK) reached", or, with an ON ERROR clause,
                          gets STATUS() 3060 and carries on.  It covers READU,
                          READL, READVU, RECORDLOCKU and OPENSEQ.  Asking again
                          for a lock the process already has is not a new lock,
                          and a release gives the place back.  There is no
                          way to except one account or one user, and no limit
                          on file locks.  openQM can instead raise a
                          SYS.LOCKS.LIMIT exception for TRY / CATCH; SD's
                          exceptions are only the ones a program THROWs, so
                          here the process stops or gets 3060 (the system
                          events as exceptions item is still to do).  CONFIG
                          MAXRLOCK n changes it for the process that gives
                          the command.

             DHCACHE=n    How many closed dynamic files SD keeps open ready for
                          the next OPEN, 0..1024.  The default is 10, which is
                          what it always was.  0 keeps none.  CONFIG DHCACHE n
                          changes it for that process; note that EXECUTE empties
                          the cache, so set it before the OPENs that matter.

             LGNWAIT=n    When every user place is taken, wait up to n tenths
                          of a second for one to be freed instead of refusing
                          the login at once, 0..32767.  The default 0 refuses at
                          once, as before.  A login that gives up is refused
                          with "User limit reached" as it always was.  SYSTEM(1036)
                          is how long this process waited, in tenths, and
                          SYSTEM(1055) counts since SD started, as fields:
                          logins that had to wait, that then got in, waiting
                          now, the most waiting at once, and those that gave
                          up.  It waits in tenth-second steps, so it is not a
                          queue: whoever looks first after a place is freed
                          gets it.

             PWDELAY=n    Seconds the API server waits after a refused login
                          before it answers, 0..3600.  The default is 3 (the
                          openQM default is 5, and SD already had 3 fixed).  A
                          longer delay slows anyone guessing passwords; 0
                          removes the slowing and is the administrator's
                          choice to make.

             USERPOOL=n   The highest user number SD hands out, 0..16383, or
                          NUMUSERS if that is larger.  The default 1023 is what
                          it always was.  0 means the lowest free number is
                          used again each time instead of cycling through the
                          pool: a user number is reused as soon as it is free.
                          SYSTEM(1053) returns this highest number (before
                          this change it returned NUMUSERS, which was never the
                          highest number SD handed out).

           The longest name a subroutine can be catalogued and CALLed by is the
           lower of 128 and MAXIDLEN.  MAXIDLEN is 63 unless you raise it, so
           nothing changes until you do; CATALOG, $CATALOG, CALL and the API
           server all use the same limit.  A program name also has to leave room
           for ".LIS" inside MAXIDLEN, because BASIC writes a listing record under
           it: at the default a program whose name is 60 or more characters could
           not be compiled before and still cannot.  The client libraries keep
           63, because a client cannot know the server's MAXIDLEN.

           Not done: IPADDR, to listen on one address only (the API listener
           binds every address; APIPORT switches it on); the @TTY name
           "startup" for a STARTUP process (SD has no STARTUP command: the
           parameter is refused); and switching FSYNC off while an index is
           built - an index build makes only four fsync calls in all, so there
           is nothing to save.

18 Oct 26  MORE FILEINFO() AND SYSTEM() KEYS, NUMBERED AS THE OPENQM MANUAL
           NUMBERS THEM.

           FILEINFO() gains the keys the openQM 4 manual lists from 1022 to
           1040.  The ones that report something real in SD:

             FL$SEQTYPE (1023)    a sequential file's kind: 1 serial port, 2
                                  character device (the null device), 3 FIFO, 4
                                  block device; 0 for a plain file.  Only the
                                  character device and the plain file have
                                  been tried.
             FL$INDEX.SCAN (1030) 1 for a hashed file
             FL$NON.TXN (1032)    opened as non-transactional
             FL$COLLECTION (1035) a data collection file
             FL$TXN.UPDATE (1038) written or deleted in the current
                                  transaction
             FL$LOCKS (1039)      the locks you hold in the file, one to a
                                  field: RU or RL, a value mark and the record
                                  id; FX or SX for a file lock

           The rest answer as openQM does for a file without the feature: no
           QMNet (FL$NETFILE 1022), replication, ECS, distributed file, SYNC,
           temporary file or integrity constraint (all 0), and a null string
           for the replication list, ECS map, encoding, sequential key, forced
           id casing and owner.  FL$COLLECTION was 1022 for a few days; the
           manual gives 1022 to FL$NETFILE and 1035 to FL$COLLECTION, so it has
           moved.  A program that used the number 1022 for it, instead of the
           name, must change; none was released.  FL$HOLDERS (1021) is
           unchanged and is not the manual's FL$WHO: it gives text, "12 (don)",
           where FL$WHO gives a list of user numbers.

           SYSTEM() gains, by the manual's numbers: 13 (always 1), 16 (the
           command level, @LEVEL), 22 (the user number, @USERNO), 33 (the name
           of the program that called this subroutine), 1019 (the time zone's
           offset from UTC in seconds), 1033 (COMO active), 1035 (seconds
           since 1 Jan 1970 UTC), 1037 (every open file and the users that
           have it open), 1038 (login time as an epoch value), 1052 / 1057 /
           1058 (a phantom's children, its parent), 1053 (the highest user
           number), 1065 (the transaction cache: transaction number, file
           number, record id and W or D for each cached write or delete), 1070
           (PRECISION), 1076 (the CALL depth), 1077 / 1078 (the user's group
           names and numbers), 1081 and 1082 (milliseconds since 1970 and since
           midnight).  Keys 1034, 1044, 1051, 1054 and 1080 answer 0 or null:
           COMO cannot be suspended, there is no ECS mode or USB installation
           or package, and no Proc.  Key 1040 answers 1: operating system file
           names are not case sensitive here.

           The array the STATUS statement and FILEINFO(f, FL$STATUS) return
           is now the manual's: the dates and times of last access and
           modification (fields 13 to 16) are in the user's time zone rather
           than UTC, and the operating system file name is repeated in field
           27 and the two times are given again as epoch values in fields 34
           and 35, which makes the array 35 fields long, not 21.  For a hashed
           file the later of the primary and overflow subfiles' times is used.
           OSPATH(path, 20) (OS$INFO) gives the same array for any operating
           system path, null if it is not there; OS$FULLPATHX is another name
           for OS$FULLPATH.  OSPATH() is
           still a function only the system's own programs may call, and SD's
           other OSPATH key numbers are the older ones, not the manual's.

           GETPU() gains keys 1020 to 1025: PU$FORM.QUEUE (0: there is no
           SP.ASSIGN), PU$FORM.FEED (the string sent between pages, CR FF or
           just FF with SETPTR ... FORM.FEED FF), PU$ENCODING and PU$FONT
           (null), PU$PATHNAME (the file the output is going to while one is
           open) and PU$FONT.SIZE (0).  A $SYSTEM subroutine for your own
           SYSTEM() keys, catalogued globally (CATALOG bp system GLOBAL $),
           was already given the key correctly; it is now tested.

           Not done: FL$WHO (see above), FL$LAST.PART (SD has no distributed
           files), OS$SET.LINK and the other OSPATH keys that take a third
           argument, FC$CRLF, SYSTEM(1071), which describes the file just read
           by OSREAD (SD has no OSREAD), and the many other SYSTEM() keys in
           the manual that need features SD does not have, such as 1073
           (phantoms grouped with their parent) and 1066 (SP.ASSIGN).  The
           lock table holds record ids in upper case, so FL$LOCKS shows them
           that way, as GETLOCKS() does.

18 Oct 26  TWO NEW FORMS OF FOR / NEXT.

             FOR i = 5, 7, 22                    the loop runs once for each
                ...                              value, in turn
             NEXT i

             FOR EACH v IN string {DELIMITER d}  the loop runs once for each
                ...                              value in a list
             NEXT v

           The first form takes constants or expressions: FOR i = a, a * 2,
           max(b, 3).  Each expression is worked out only when its turn
           comes.  A FOR with no TO that used to be a syntax error is now a
           list of values, so FOR i = 4 is a loop that runs once.

           FOR EACH walks the values of a list.  Without DELIMITER every mark
           (field, value, subvalue and so on) separates two values, as it does
           for REMOVE, which is what it uses; with DELIMITER only that
           character does, and any marks inside a value are part of it.  The
           list is copied when the loop starts, so changing the original in
           the loop has no effect.  A null string makes no pass at all; a null
           value inside the list, or after a final delimiter, is a pass with a
           null value.  This was a choice: the openQM manual does not say what
           a null string does.  The control variable must be a simple
           variable name (not an array element) in FOR EACH; the list form
           takes an array element as the classic FOR does.

           In both forms WHILE, UNTIL, CONTINUE, EXIT and NEXT work as in the
           classic loop, a variable called EACH is still an ordinary control
           variable (FOR EACH = 1 TO 5 is unchanged), and the control
           variable keeps the last value it was given on leaving the loop.
           $MODE FOR.STORE.BEFORE.TEST is honoured.  Programs already
           compiled are not affected.

18 Oct 26  A COLLECTION THAT HOLDS A FILE VARIABLE CAN BE WRITTEN TO A
           COLLECTION FILE.

           A data collection in a program can hold things a file has no way to
           keep, such as a file variable.  Writing a collection like that to a
           data collection file used to fail with error 1019.  The openQM
           manual says those elements are stored as SQL-style nulls, and now
           they are: the WRITE succeeds, the other elements are stored as they
           were, and the element reads back as a null.

           What it does not change: JBUILD() of such a collection still fails
           with 1019 unless the JB$INVALID mode is given, which asks for a
           null in the same way; a collection that contains itself still
           cannot be written (1019); and only a collection or JSON text that
           is an object or an array can be written to a collection file.

18 Oct 26  THE ELEMENT KEYWORD IN QUERIES.

             LIST file ELEMENT "detail/*/prodno" ...
             LIST file ... WITH ELEMENT "ship" = "Dock B"
             SORT file ... BY ELEMENT "n"

           ELEMENT names an element of a data collection file by its path,
           the way A n or F n names a field, so a query no longer needs a
           dictionary item for every element it touches.  It can be used
           wherever a field name can: in the display list, in WITH and WHEN
           clauses, with BY, BY.DSND and BY.EXP, with SUM, TOTAL and BREAK.ON,
           and in COUNT and SELECT.  The usual qualifiers (AS, COL.HDG, FMT,
           CONV, MULTI.VALUE, SINGLE.VALUE) work on it as on any other field.

           The path is the one a collection variable takes: names and array
           positions separated by /, as in detail/1/prodno.  An * in place of
           a position stands for every element of that array, so
           detail/*/prodno gives a list of values, one for each element, and
           detail/*/serial/* a value for each element of detail with a
           subvalue for each of its serial numbers.  A path with an asterisk
           is multivalued.  Quotes are needed only for a path that holds a
           blank or a character the command parser splits on.  A name is case
           sensitive and keeps the case it is typed in; an element that is not
           there is a null value, not an error.

           With no FMT the item is 10 characters, left justified, and its
           heading is the path.  Left justified means that a sort or a test on
           a number compares characters, so BY ELEMENT "n" puts 10 before 3;
           add FMT "5R" (and AS, to sort by the formatted item) for numbers.

           ELEMENT works only on a data collection file; on any other file it
           says so (12165), and ELEMENT with no path says "ELEMENT path not
           found where expected" (12166).  ELEMENT is now a keyword, like
           EVAL, so a dictionary item that happens to be called ELEMENT should
           be given another name.

18 Oct 26  ED, COPY, COPYP, COMPARE.FILES AND TRIGGERS WORK ON A DATA
           COLLECTION FILE.

             ED file id                  edit a record's JSON, a line to an element
             COPY FROM coll TO other ids   the JSON text goes into a file that is
                                           not a collection file, and the JSON
                                           text of a record goes into a
                                           collection file
             COPYP file ids              the same, with the destination asked
                                           at the To: prompt (T shows a
                                           collection record as JSON)
             COMPARE.FILES one two       records compared as their JSON text

           All three stopped with "Data cannot be converted to a string" when
           they met a record of a data collection file, because a program that
           reads such a record is given a collection, not text.  ED (and EDIT)
           now shows the record as CT does, one element to a line, and files the
           lines back as JSON text; the file makes a collection of it again and
           refuses it, with "Write error 1020 - Data not saved", if it is not a
           JSON object or array, leaving the stored record as it was.  The same
           holds for UNLOAD into a collection file, and LOAD of lines from a
           collection record gives the lines of its JSON.  COPY from a
           collection file to a file that is not one writes the JSON text, in
           the compact form the file itself holds; to another collection file
           it copies the collection, as before.  Text that is not JSON copied
           into a collection file is refused, "Error 1020 writing record", and
           stops the copy, as any write error does (with the D option of COPYP
           the source record that was not copied is not deleted).  COPYP and
           COPY.LISTP do all of this too; they stopped with the same message.
           The openQM manual says a copy must be between two collection files
           or two files that are not; SD also copies between the two kinds, as
           a deliberate addition, so that a collection file can be exported to
           (or loaded from) text files, which a directory file holds as files
           on disk.  COMPARE.FILES compares
           collection records by their JSON text, in which the names are always
           in order, so two equal collections are the same.

           Triggers on a collection file now behave as the records do.  A
           pre-write trigger was given a collection when the program wrote one
           but the text when it wrote text, and the post-write trigger was given
           something that was not a string or a collection: a trigger that used
           the record there stopped the whole process with "Fault type 11".
           Both triggers, and the read trigger, are now always given the
           collection.  Text written to a collection file is made into its
           collection before the trigger runs, so text that is not a JSON object
           or array is refused (error 1020) without the trigger seeing it, and
           what a pre-write trigger changes is what is stored.  The file holds
           the compact JSON of the collection, whatever layout the text had;
           a program reading the record gets the same collection as before.
           A trigger that refuses a write (a non-zero @TRIGGER.RETURN.CODE)
           stops it as usual, and the post-write trigger does not run.

           The API server now sends a client the JSON text of a record it reads
           from a collection file; it would have failed to send the collection.
           This one has been compiled but not yet run against a live API server.

           The other file commands already worked on these files and still do:
           CONFIGURE.FILE, CLEAR.FILE, DELETE, COUNT, LIST.ITEM, SORT.ITEM,
           DUMP and CT.  Not changed: MICRO and NANO, which need a terminal and
           were not tried on a collection record, READV by field number on a
           collection file (a collection has no fields), and ANALYSE.FILE, which
           does not say that a file holds collections.

18 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER SHOWS A DATA COLLECTION.

             /c                      the collection c: kind, elements, JSON
             /c{name}                the element a path names
             /c{a/3/k}   /c{'a','3'}  the same path written either way
             DUMP c{sub} pathname    the element's JSON, to a file

           The debugger knew nothing of collections: /c showed an empty line
           for one.  It now says "Collection (9 elements)"
           or "Array (3 elements)" and gives the JSON text, cut off after 10,240
           characters with "..." (DUMP writes all of it).  A path in braces
           shows one element: a number or a string as the debugger shows any
           variable, a collection or array as above, and a JSON true, false or
           null as "Boolean: true", "Boolean: false" or "Null", which the
           program itself sees as the integer 1 or 0 and an empty string.  The
           path is written as in a program, with slashes or commas between the
           names and quotes optional.  The variable name is not case sensitive,
           as before, but the path is.  An element that is not there gives
           "Element not found in the collection"; a path on a variable that is
           not a collection, or an index on a collection, gives the existing
           "Index not allowed with scalar variable".  A collection that
           contains itself is reported as "(cannot be shown)" and the debugger
           carries on.  WATCH on an element is not provided, and the help
           screens are unchanged.

18 Oct 26  INDICES ON E-TYPE ITEMS.

             CREATE.INDEX file ITEM            ITEM may be an E-type item
             BUILD.INDEX file ITEM
             SELECTINDEX ITEM, value           as for any index

           An E-type item of a data collection file can be indexed as a D-type
           item can, including one whose path has an asterisk (every element
           becomes a key).  The index is kept up to date as records are written
           and deleted, and a query with a WITH clause on the item uses it.  A
           D-type item with a field number is refused on a collection file, and
           an E-type item on any other file.

18 Oct 26  LINKED COLLECTIONS AND TRANS().

             ok = EXPAND(c{'path'})            join the record a link names
             ok = EXPAND(c{'path'}, filevar)   ...with a default file
             TRANS('file', id, 'ITEM', 'X')    an E-type item, by name
             TRANS(file, @ID, ITEM, 'X')       ...or in an I-type
             @RECORD{'a/b'}                    in an I-type, of a collection

           A link is a string in a collection of the form "filename:id", or
           ":id" when EXPAND() is given a file variable.  EXPAND() reads that
           record, which should be a data collection file's, and puts the
           collection it gives where the link was.  It returns 1 if the element
           is now a collection (including when it already was) and 0 if it is
           not a link, the file or the record is missing, or the record is not a
           collection, leaving the element as it was.  The file name is up to the
           first colon, so an id may have colons in it.  What is joined on is a
           copy; writing the parent back is up to the program.

           TRANS() on a data collection file accepts an E-type dictionary item
           for its third argument, and a program may now name any D or E-type
           item as a string: TRANS('CLIENTS', id, 'NAME', 'X').  In an I-type
           the item's name can be written without quotes as before.  A multivalued
           (asterisk) path comes back as it does from any TRANS(), with the
           marks lowered one level.  A D-type item with a field number gives a
           null string on such a file, and field -1 gives the whole record as JSON.

           In an I-type, a name that is an E-type item gives that element of the
           record, and a collection element reference can be written after any
           value: @RECORD{'city'}, @RECORD{'items', 1, 'p'}.  The query commands
           now keep the collection as @RECORD for a collection file, so an I-type
           sees it as a whole.

18 Oct 26  DATA COLLECTION FILES.

             CREATE.FILE name COLLECTION       a file whose records are collections
             READ c FROM f, id                 c is a collection
             WRITE c ON f, id                  c is written as JSON text
             FILEINFO(f, FL$COLLECTION)        1 for such a file
             LIST file NAME CITY               E-type dictionary items
             CT file id                        shows the record as JSON
             OCONV/ICONV 'HU'                  see the entry below

           A collection file is an ordinary hashed file whose records are held
           as JSON text, so locking, transactions, the file size controls and
           everything else underneath work as they do for any file.  READ,
           READU and READL give the record as a collection and WRITE and
           WRITEU take one; WRITE also accepts text, provided it is a JSON
           object or array, and refuses anything else.  A collection holding a
           value with no JSON form cannot be written.  A read or write trigger
           is called with the collection.  MATREAD is refused, and READV or
           WRITEV of a field number gives the usual error since there are no
           fields.  The dictionary of the file is an ordinary one.

           A new kind of dictionary item, E, is a D-type item whose field 2 is
           an element path instead of a field number: E, path, conversion,
           heading, format, S or M, association.  The path may have up to two
           asterisks, which make the item multivalued.  The query commands (LIST,
           SORT, SELECT, COUNT, SEARCH and the rest) use E-type items as they
           use D-type ones, in headings, WITH clauses, BY clauses and
           conversions.  A D-type item with a field number above 0 is refused
           on a collection file with a message that says to use an E-type item,
           and an E-type item is refused on any other file; @ID works as
           always.  SEARCH looks at the JSON text of the record.

           CT on a collection file shows the record as JSON, one element to a
           line.  JBUILD() has a mode for the same layout, JB$PRETTY (128), an
           addition of SD's.

           Not built: the collection editor and XPARSE().

18 Oct 26  COLLECTIONS: BOOLEANS AND NULLS, OPERATORS ON AN ELEMENT, THE HU
           CONVERSION.

             c{'on'} = JBOOLEAN(1)             store true (JBOOLEAN(0): false)
             c{'none'} = JNULL()               store a JSON null
             JTYPE(value)                      "boolean", "null", "number",
                                               "string", "collection",
                                               "array" or "other"
             c{'n'} += 5     -= *= /= :=       change an element in place
             OCONV(name, 'HU')  ICONV(name, 'HU')   escape and unescape

           SD has no boolean or null variable type, so these are ordinary
           values that carry a mark: JBOOLEAN(x) is 1 or 0 (false for 0,
           a null string and the text 0 or false in any case, true for
           anything else), JNULL() is a null string.  A collection element
           stored from one keeps its kind, JBUILD() writes true, false or
           null for it, and reading it back gives the mark again, so JTYPE()
           can tell.  The mark is lost the moment you do anything to the
           value: JBOOLEAN(1) + 1 is just 2.

           c{'n'} += 5 and the others read the element, apply the operator
           and store the result.  An element that is not there is a null
           string to start from, so += starts at 0 and := at nothing, and
           the levels of the path are made as for any assignment.

           OCONV(s, 'HU') writes everything but letters, digits and - . _ ~
           as %xx with lower case hex digits, so a URL can be a name in a
           collection without its slashes being taken for a path; ICONV
           undoes it and leaves a % that is not followed by two hex digits,
           and a +, as they are.

18 Oct 26  JSON: JPARSE() AND JBUILD().

             c = JPARSE(text)                  JSON text into a collection
             c = JPARSE(text, mode)
             text = JBUILD(c)                  and back
             text = JBUILD(c, mode)

           JPARSE() reads a JSON object or array.  An object becomes a
           collection, an array an array, a string a string, a number an
           integer when it is whole and fits in 32 bits and a floating point
           number otherwise.  true and false are kept as true and false and
           null as null, so JBUILD() writes them back as they were; read from
           the collection they are 1, 0 and a null string.  Spaces, tabs,
           returns, line feeds and all five mark characters between elements
           are ignored.  Names and strings may be in single or double quotes.
           \uXXXX is the single byte XX when it is 255 or less (so marks and
           8 bit characters come back as they went out) and UTF-8 above that.
           Nesting is limited to 500 levels.  A name that appears twice takes
           the later value.

           If the text is not JSON, or is not an object or an array,
           JPARSE() returns a null string, STATUS() is 1020 (ER$PARSE.ERROR)
           and INMAT() is the position of the character where it gave up.

           JBUILD() writes compact text with no spaces and the names in
           order.  Strings are escaped; marks, control characters and 8 bit
           characters become \u00XX, which JPARSE() reads back as the same
           byte.  A value with no JSON form (a file variable, say) or a
           collection that contains itself makes it return a null string with
           STATUS() 1019 (ER$BUILD.ERROR).

           The modes are added together and are named in the KEYS.H include:

             JP$NULL            a null is read as a null string
             JP$UNQUOTED.NAME   names may have no quotes
             JB$INVALID         a value with no JSON form is written as null
             JB$NULL            a null string is written as null
             JB$NUM             a string that is a JSON number is written as one
             JB$SQUOTE.NAME     names in single quotes
             JB$NOQUOTE.NAME    names with no quotes
             JB$SQUOTE.VALUE    strings in single quotes
             JB$NOQUOTE.VALUE   strings with no quotes

           The values (1, 2, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) are SD's own.

18 Oct 26  ARRAYS IN DATA COLLECTIONS.

             c{'list'} = MAT()                 an empty array
             c{'list'} = MAT(matrix)           a copy of a dimensioned matrix
             c{'list'} = MAT(matrix, 10)       at most 10 elements of it
             c{'list', n} = value              set element n (or add n+1)
             c{'list', -1} = value             add on the end
             INS value AS c{'list', n}         open a gap at n
             DEL c{'list', n}                  take n out, renumbering
             MAT target = MAT c{'list'}        copy it into a matrix
             INMAT(c{'list'})                  how many elements it has
             c{'orders/*/qty'}                 one value from every element

           An array in a collection is numbered from 1 and has no gaps:
           element n can only be set when 1 to n-1 exist, and a negative
           position adds on the end.  A matrix of two dimensions is copied
           row by row.  An element of a matrix nobody assigned becomes a null
           string.  INS and DEL renumber everything after them.  INS on a
           collection that is not an array just puts the element in, and
           INS MAT() AS c{'list'} puts in an empty array.

           An asterisk in a path stands for every element of an array, and
           the answer is a dynamic array: one value for each element, and
           with a second asterisk one subvalue for each element of the array
           below.  An element that does not have the item leaves its place
           empty.  Up to two asterisks may be used and only to read; setting
           or deleting through one is refused.

           AS is now a reserved word in expressions (it ends the value in
           INS value AS ...), so a variable called AS can no longer follow a
           value as a format.

18 Oct 26  DATA COLLECTIONS.  This is a kernel change, so it needs a rebuild.

             c = COLLECTION()                  a new, empty collection
             d = COLLECTION(c)                 an independent copy
             c{'name'} = value                 put an element in
             value = c{'name'}                 read it back
             DEL c{'name'}                     take it out
             ELEMENT.EXISTS(c, 'name')         1 if it is there, else 0
             ENUMERATE(c)                      its names, sorted

           A data collection is a set of named values, like a dynamic array
           whose elements are found by name instead of position.  The value
           of an element can be a number, a string or another collection, so
           collections nest as deeply as memory allows.  Names are case
           sensitive and ENUMERATE() gives them in simple left-to-right
           order, capitals before lower case.

           The braces take a name, several names or a path.  c{'a','b'},
           c{'a'}{'b'} and c{'a/b'} are all the same element, and the forms
           can be mixed.  Setting an element creates whatever levels are
           missing on the way; DEL of a level removes everything below it.
           Reading an element that is not there gives a null string and
           sets STATUS() to 1004.

           Assigning a collection (d = c) does not copy it: both names refer
           to the same collection, as with a file variable.  Use
           COLLECTION(c) for a copy; it copies nested collections as well.

           The collection editor is not built.  A collection that contains
           itself is never freed, which is how openQM behaves too.

18 Oct 26  FIELD AND VSLICE TAKE MORE, CATALOG ... GLOBAL c, THREE SHOW
           CHANGES AND SP.VIEW SORTING.

           SP.VIEW file BY field (or BY.DSND, with NO.CASE before or after
           the field name) sorts the list of records it offers.  It works as
           SELECT does, so the field is a dictionary item of the file, and
           like openQM's it replaces the default select list.  In a record,
           Return now pages forward (it did nothing) and Esc-Z pages back.

           FIELD(string, delimiter, n, *) and string[delimiter, n, *]
           return everything from the n-th field to the end.  Before, the
           last number had to be a count.  An asterisk anywhere else in an
           expression is still a multiplication.

           VSLICE(string, vpos, svpos) takes a slice of subvalues.  With a
           vpos of 1 or more it comes from that value only; with a vpos
           below 1 it comes from every value, and the value marks that keep
           the positions are kept.  Marks are written only in front of data,
           so empty values and fields at the end are dropped.  With svpos
           below 1 and vpos of 1 or more it is the ordinary value slice.
           VSLICE(string, vpos) is unchanged.

           CATALOG file name GLOBAL c puts the prefix character c (one of
           * ! _ $) in front of every call name that has none of its own, so
           a whole select list can be catalogued globally with a prefix.  A
           name that already has a prefix keeps it.  It does not accept a
           hyphen as a prefix.

           SHOW: B moves to the last page (T was the only way to move far).
           The keyword SORT.SUP stops SHOW sorting its list by record id when
           there is no BY clause, which matters when the order the file gives
           is the order wanted; a BY clause still sorts.  Record ids given on
           the command line that are not in the file are now listed, one per
           line as 'id' not found, and SHOW stops without drawing the screen
           or making a list.  Before, they were skipped without a word.

18 Oct 26  PTERM ERASE, CTRL.FILTER AND QUERY.

             PTERM ERASE ON | OFF | n | ^c | DEFAULT
             PTERM CTRL.FILTER ON | OFF
             PTERM QUERY

           ERASE chooses the key that rubs out a character in INPUT.  At the
           start of a session INPUT accepts ctrl-H, DEL and whatever the
           terminal type names (kbs), all together.  PTERM ERASE then makes
           one key the erase key and nothing else: ON is ctrl-H (character
           8), n is a character number (1 to 31, or 127 for DEL), ^c is the
           control key written with its letter (^D, ^H, ...), OFF means INPUT
           has no erase key at all, so the keys that were erase keys are
           ordinary characters.  DEFAULT, an addition, goes back to how a
           session starts.  PTERM ERASE on its own says which it is.  INPUT@
           is not affected; it keeps using the terminal type.

           CTRL.FILTER ON makes INPUT and INPUT@ throw away the control
           characters that are not keys they use (the erase key and Return
           still work).  It is set for one command level: a program run with
           EXECUTE starts with it off, and the level that set it has it on
           again when the program returns.  Off is how a session starts.

           QUERY shows what keys send, which is what you need to write a
           terminfo entry.  Press a key and it shows the bytes in
           hexadecimal, as characters (a dot for those that do not print) and
           for a control key or a sequence the way terminfo writes it (\E[A
           for ESC [ A).  A space ends it.  The mark translation of PTERM MARK
           is held off while it runs.

           PTERM DISPLAY now reports the erase key and the control filter.

18 Oct 26  PTERM MARK.

             PTERM MARK ON
             PTERM MARK OFF
             PTERM MARK

           With MARK ON, three control keys typed at the keyboard are
           turned into the delimiters a program cannot otherwise get from a
           terminal: ctrl-\ (character 28) becomes a subvalue mark,
           ctrl-] (29) a value mark and ctrl-^ (30) a field mark.  It
           applies to everything read from the keyboard, so INPUT, KEYIN()
           and the rest see the marks.  PTERM MARK on its own says whether
           it is on, and PTERM DISPLAY now reports it.  It is off when a
           session starts.

18 Oct 26  SCAN, SEARCH ... DISPLAY AND STRINGS.

             SCAN {DICT} file {selection} {ids} {ALL} {NO.CASE}
                  {ALL.MATCH} {FOR "text" ...} {STRINGS file record}
             SEARCH file ... DISPLAY

           SEARCH selects the records that hold a text string.  Now it can
           also show where: with DISPLAY, for each record it selects it
           prints the record id, then each line of the record that holds
           any of the strings, with the line number:

             SEARCH BP FOR "!SORT" DISPLAY
             SCAN BP FOR "!SORT"          (the same thing)

             prog1
             0012: call !sort(list, 'AL')
             0040: if x then call !sort(other, 'DR')

           A line is a field of the record; the value, subvalue and text
           marks in it are shown as ], \ and [.  The records are still
           selected as select list 0, and the usual count follows.  SCAN is
           SEARCH with DISPLAY and takes the same words: a selection
           clause, record ids, FROM, ALL (search the id too), NO.CASE,
           ALL.MATCH and FOR.  As before, with no FOR it asks for strings
           until a blank answer.

           New for both, and so for SEARCH without DISPLAY too:

             STRINGS file record

           takes the search strings from a record: each field is one
           string, and a null field is ignored.  It adds to any given with
           FOR.  A file or record that is not there is reported and nothing
           is searched.

18 Oct 26  HOLD.FILE.LOGGER, $SETPTR.DEFAULTS AND @SEQNO.

           HOLD.FILE.LOGGER.  When a print unit in mode 3 (hold file) or
           mode 6 (hold file and printer) opens its file, and again when
           it closes it, SD calls a catalogued subroutine named
           HOLD.FILE.LOGGER if there is one:

             SUBROUTINE HOLD.FILE.LOGGER(UNIT, OPENING, PATHNAME)

           UNIT is the print unit, OPENING is 1 for an open and 0 for a
           close, and PATHNAME is the name of the file as SD holds it,
           such as $hold/P0 or the pathname given to AS PATHNAME.  It can
           keep a log of print jobs or act once a job has been closed.
           Nothing happens if there is no such subroutine, and a plain
           printer job (mode 1) never calls it.

           $SETPTR.DEFAULTS.  An X-type record of that name in the VOC of
           the account, or if there is none in the system's VOC, gives
           options that SETPTR takes as if they had been written straight
           after the mode.  Field 2 is for mode 1, field 4 for mode 3,
           field 6 for mode 5, and so on: field mode + 1.  A record
           reading

             X
             NEWLINE CRLF, EJECT

           makes every SETPTR into mode 1 behave as if NEWLINE CRLF and
           EJECT had been written.  Options in the command itself come
           after them and so win.  It applies to a SETPTR with no options
           at all (SETPTR 0,,,,,3) as well.

           @SEQNO, GETPU(PU$SEQNO, unit).  The number the last AS NEXT
           hold file was given, as it appears in the file name (0012, not
           12).  @SEQNO is the last one this process made, whichever unit;
           GETPU is the unit's own.  Both are null until a NEXT job has
           started.

18 Oct 26  SETPTR FORM.FEED, AND THE DIGITS OF THE NEXT NUMBER.

             SETPTR unit, ... , FORM.FEED FF
             SETPTR unit, ... , FORM.FEED CRFF

           Chooses the sequence sent between pages of print data and wherever
           the data holds a form feed: FF is the form feed alone, CRFF a
           carriage return then the form feed.  CRFF is the default, and what
           SD has always sent.  SETPTR unit,DISPLAY now ends its list with
           the setting, FORM.FEED FF or FORM.FEED CRFF, as it does for
           NEWLINE, so a captured setting restores it.

           The AS NEXT option of SETPTR numbers successive hold file items
           from a counter in the $NEXT record of the dictionary of $HOLD.
           Field 2 of that record is the next number.  Field 3 may now say
           how many digits the number has, from 3 to 9; without it, or with
           anything else, it has four.  The numbers run from 1 up to the
           highest the digits hold (9999 for four) and then start again.

           Correction: the numbers used to start again after 1000, not
           9999, in the four digits they were written in.  A site that
           depended on that gets the longer run now; set field 3 to 3 for a
           run of 1 to 999.

           (The ENCODING option the same manual lists is not here; see the
           next entry for the rest of what the manual describes.)

18 Oct 26  SP.VIEW CAN DELETE THE ITEM IT IS SHOWING.

           While SP.VIEW shows a record, X now deletes it.  It asks first
           ("Delete item from file (Y/N)?") and only Y or y deletes; any
           other key leaves the item as it was.  A record that another user
           holds locked is reported and left alone.  Opened from the pick
           list of a file, the item is taken off the list when it goes and
           the cursor stays near where it was; opened by name, the viewer
           ends, since there is nothing left to show.  The prompt line
           now lists X-delete among the keys.

           It works on whatever file SP.VIEW has open, not only $hold, and
           with the access of the user who runs it, like DELETE.

18 Oct 26  TERM SETS THE DEFAULT PRINTER'S PAGE SIZE.

             TERM {columns} {, lines} {, prt.cols} {, prt.lines} {type}

           TERM already set the width and depth of the display.  It now
           takes two more numbers for the default printer, print unit 0:

             TERM 120,32               the screen 120 x 32
             TERM ,,132,66             the printer 132 x 66, the screen
                                       as it was
             TERM ,,,50                the printer's depth alone

           A number may be left out if its comma is not, and a number left
           out stays as it is.  The widths must be 20 to 32767 and the
           depths 10 to 32767, for the printer as for the screen.  With no
           arguments TERM now shows the printer's width and depth after the
           screen's.  TERM DEFAULT is unchanged: it sets the screen to 120
           x 36, which is what a session starts with (the openQM manual
           says 80 x 24; SD does not), and leaves the printer alone.

           Also corrected: a screen depth was checked against the largest
           width instead of the largest depth; both are 32767, so nothing
           that worked before stops working.

18 Oct 26  SELECTINDEX COMMAND.

             SELECTINDEX {DICT} filename indexname {value} {TO n}

           The command form of the SELECTINDEX statement, for the command
           prompt and paragraphs.  Without a value it builds select list 0
           (or n) from the values held in the alternate key index; with
           one, from the ids of the records that have that value.  It says
           how many it selected, as SELECT does.  The index name is found
           as typed, then in lower and then in upper case, and the file name
           the same.

             SELECTINDEX orders customer.name
             SELECTINDEX orders customer.name Smith
             LIST orders

           Also corrected: SELECT.DIR and GET.LIST, given TO with no list
           number, said "Error writing saved list record"; they now say
           "Select list number missing or invalid".

18 Oct 26  COPY.LISTP.

             COPY.LISTP {{DICT} src.file} {id.list | *} {(options}

           The Pick form of COPY.LIST, and the same program as COPYP: the
           options (B D I N O P S T) and the "To:" question are COPYP's.
           What differs is that the source file is $savedlists, where saved
           select lists are kept, unless one is named, so
           COPY.LISTP mylist asks where to copy the list called mylist, and
           COPY.LISTP * (T shows every saved list.

           When the command names two or more words before the options, the
           first is taken as the source file if it opens as a file, and
           every word is a list name if it does not.  A file and a list of
           the same name are therefore told apart by writing the file name
           first (COPY.LISTP $savedlists mylist).  After DICT the next word
           is always the file.

           A related defect found on the way: the COPYP record in the
           installer's bootstrap VOC (voc_template) had a description where
           its type should be.  It is a verb record now, as it is in the
           Windows port.

18 Oct 26  LISTDICT.

             LISTDICT filename {LPTR}

           Lists the dictionary of a file in the form Pick systems use: one
           line for each A, D, I and S record, with its type, its field
           number or expression, conversion, column heading, format, S/M
           and association, in that order of type.  LPTR sends the listing
           to the printer instead of the screen.

           It is a paragraph over LIST DICT, so the layout is the one LIST
           DICT gives and the columns follow the DICT.DICT dictionary.  What
           it adds is the choice of record types: phrases and every other
           type are left out.  LIST DICT filename still lists everything.

18 Oct 26  TRY / CATCH / THROW, CAUGHT() AND @EXCEPTION.

           An exception is a named event that a program raises with THROW
           and handles with TRY / CATCH, wherever in the program, or in the
           subroutines it CALLs, the THROW happens.  The program leaves
           every level below the TRY, then goes on in the CATCH clause that
           names the exception:

             LOOP
                READNEXT ACC.ID ELSE EXIT
                TRY
                   CALL PROCESS.ACCOUNT
                CATCH ACCOUNT.INVALID
                   DISPLAY 'Account ' : ACC.ID : ' is invalid'
                END
             REPEAT

             TRY {statement}
                statements
             CATCH exception {qualifiers} {, exception {qualifiers} ...}
                statements
             {CATCH ... any number of further clauses}
             END

             THROW exception {, data}

           The exception name is not case sensitive and may be up to 63
           characters.  In CATCH it may be written in quotes or without
           them; in THROW it is any expression.  A TRY may have several
           CATCH clauses and one clause may name several exceptions; the
           first clause that names it is the one used.  Two special names
           can be used in CATCH: SYS$ANY takes any exception that no clause
           names, and SYS$UNHANDLED takes an exception that no TRY anywhere
           in the program's call chain names or takes by SYS$ANY.

           In the CATCH clause these hold what was thrown:

             @EXCEPTION          the name, as it was written in THROW
             @EXCEPTION.DATA    the data given to THROW, null if none
             @EXCEPTION.ORIGIN  the name of the program that threw it, a
                                field mark and the line number of the
                                THROW (-1 where the program has no line
                                table), the program being named as error
                                messages and SYSTEM(1002) name it
             @EXCEPTION.STACK   the call stack, in the form of SYSTEM(1002),
                                when the CATCH name was followed by
                                SAVING.STACK; null otherwise

           Two qualifiers may follow an exception name in CATCH, and apply
           to that name only: DUMPING writes a process dump file, as PDUMP
           does, and SAVING.STACK fills @EXCEPTION.STACK; both are done
           when the exception is thrown, before the program leaves the
           levels below the TRY.  $MODE EXCEPTION.SAVE.STACK gives every
           CATCH in the program SAVING.STACK.

             CAUGHT(exception {, modes})

           is 1 if a TRY that is active now (in this program or in one that
           called it) has a CATCH for that exception, 0 if not, so that a
           subroutine can find out before it throws.  Modes adds up what
           else counts: 1 a SYS$ANY handler, 2 a SYS$UNHANDLED handler;
           without it only a clause that names the exception counts.

           An exception that nothing handles stops the program, with
           "Unhandled exception name" and the data after a colon, as a run
           time error does.

18 Oct 26  INMAT() AFTER MATCHES AND MATCHFIELD.

           A MATCHES template may hold several patterns separated by value
           marks; the string matches if any one of them does.  Until now
           there was no way to tell which one.  After MATCHES or
           MATCHFIELD(), INMAT() is now the number of the pattern that
           matched, counting from 1 in the order they are written in the
           template, and 0 when none of them matched:

             IF 'ab12' MATCHES '3N':@VM:'3A':@VM:'2A2N' THEN
                CRT INMAT()                 ;* 3
             END

           The first pattern that matches is the one reported, as it is the
           one used.  A template with a single pattern gives 1 when it
           matches.

18 Oct 26  EREPLACE(), FIX() AND MATSTR().

             EREPLACE(string, old, new {, occurrence {, start}})
                          The same as CHANGE() when old is not null: every
                          occurrence of old is replaced by new, or
                          "occurrence" of them (less than 1 means all)
                          starting at the "start"th.  When old is null, new
                          is put in front of the string.  A null new
                          deletes.

             FIX(number {, precision {, mode}})
                          The number as a string with exactly "precision"
                          decimal places, trailing zeros kept: rounded to the
                          nearest (halves away from zero) when mode is 0 or
                          left out, cut off when mode is not 0.  Without a
                          precision the program's PRECISION is used.
                          FIX(37.73629273, 2) is 37.74 and with mode 1 it is
                          37.73.

             MATSTR(matrix)
                          A dynamic array from a whole dimensioned matrix:
                          for a two dimensional matrix a field for each row
                          and a value for each column; for a one
                          dimensional one a field for each element, as
                          MATBUILD gives (trailing nulls left off).  The
                          zero element is not used and marks already in the
                          data are not escaped.  It also works on a matrix
                          passed into a subroutine.

           FIX works to 14 decimal places and to numbers of about 18 digits;
           beyond that it uses the plain C formatting.  For exact decimal
           arithmetic use SADD and the other S functions.

18 Oct 26  SETPTR INFORM, EJECT AND AS ... IN.  Syntax is SD's own where openQM's is not
           given.  SETPTR options are separated by commas.

             SETPTR 0,80,60,0,0,3,AS job,INFORM
                          Says "Print output is going to $hold/job" when the
                          job starts (modes 3 and 6).
             SETPTR ...,EJECT
                          Ends the job with a form feed, if anything was
                          printed.
             SETPTR ...,AS job IN dirfile
                          The job is held in the directory file named
                          instead of $HOLD.

18 Oct 26  FIVE NEW SUBROUTINES AND A SIZE FROM DIR().  DIR() is kernel code, so
           it needs a rebuild.  Where openQM has a function, SD's own
           programs have the same thing as a function only inside SD, so
           these are subroutines an application can CALL.

             CALL !MERGELIST(result, list1, list2, mode)
                          Merges two field mark lists in order.  Mode words:
                          U union (default), A all with duplicates, I in
                          both, D in list 1 not list 2, X in one only; AL,
                          DL, AR or DR for the order; N if the lists are not
                          in order.  A null list is just an empty one.
             CALL !OPTION(number, state)
                          Reads (state null) or sets (state 0 or 1) one of
                          the OPTION flags, by its number, OPT.xxx in
                          INT$KEYS.H.
             CALL !VOCPATH(name, path, status)
                          The full pathname that OPEN would use for a VOC
                          file name, following Q-pointers; DICT name works.
             CALL !TESTLOCK(n, owner)
                          The user number holding task lock n, or 0.
             DIR(path)    Each entry now has a third value, the size in
                          bytes: name, F or D, size.  Programs that took the
                          first two values are unaffected.

18 Oct 26  REBUILD.ALL.INDICES, REDO AND A PHANTOM STATEMENT.  Syntax is SD's own.

             REBUILD.ALL.INDICES {NO.QUERY}
                          Rebuilds the indices of every file in the account
                          that has any (BUILD.INDEX file ALL for each).  It
                          asks first unless NO.QUERY is given.
             REDO {TIMES n} {EVERY s} command
                          Runs the command again and again: n times in all
                          (default: until the break key), a run starting
                          every s seconds (default 1; whole seconds).
             PHANTOM "command" in a program
                          A statement that starts a phantom, the same as
                          EXECUTE "PHANTOM command" and with the same
                          CAPTURING, RETURNING and similar clauses.  The
                          user number is in @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE.

18 Oct 26  STRING ARITHMETIC, REGEX() AND A PRIVATE VOCABULARY.

             SADD(a, b)   Add, subtract, multiply and divide numbers held as
             SSUB(a, b)   strings, of any length, with an optional sign and a
             SMUL(a, b)   decimal point.  There is no rounding and no
             SDIV(a, b)   floating point: SADD("0.1", "0.2") is exactly 0.3,
                          and SMUL of two 20 digit numbers gives all 40
                          digits.  SDIV gives 15 decimal places, cut off
                          (not rounded), with trailing zeros dropped; a zero
                          divisor is a run-time error.
             SCMP(a, b)   -1, 0 or 1 as a is less than, equal to or greater
                          than b, numerically.
             REGEX(s, p)  1 if the extended regular expression p matches
                          anywhere in s, else 0.  Begin the pattern with
                          (?i) to ignore case.  A pattern that is not valid
                          stops the program with the reason.
             $PRIVATE.VOC A VOC record  X / filename  names a file laid out
                          as the VOC is; a command that is nowhere in the
                          VOC is looked for there last, so an application
                          can keep its own commands out of the account's VOC.

18 Oct 26  PHANTOM OPTIONS, PDUMP, LOGOUT GROUP.  The
           openQM notes give no syntax, so this is SD's own.

             PHANTOM NO.MSG command   Does not display "Started phantom".
             PHANTOM DATA command     Hands this process's DATA queue to the
                                      phantom (the queue here is emptied);
                                      the phantom's INPUT reads it.
             PHANTOM LOG file command The phantom's log record is made in the
                                      directory file named, not in $COMO.
                                      The words may come in any order, in
                                      front of the command.
             CALL !PHLOG(user, name)  The log record of the phantom with that
                                      user number (the newest PH<user>_...
                                      in $COMO), or null.
             LOGOUT n GROUP           Logs out process n and every phantom it
                                      started.
             PDUMP                    A BASIC statement: writes a process
                                      dump file of the running process and
                                      goes on.
             Process dump files       Show the length of each string
                                      variable, and list the environment
                                      (a value is left out when its name has
                                      PASS, SECRET, TOKEN or KEY in it).
             Q-pointers               A Q-pointer in the VOC whose file name
                                      (field 3) is null now points at the
                                      account's VOC.

18 Oct 26  FOUR BASIC ADDITIONS.  Syntax is SD's
           own where the openQM notes give none.

             CLEARCOMMON name        Sets the variables of the named common
                                     block to zero.  CLEARCOMMON alone still
                                     clears the unnamed block.
             $MODE TIME.MS           TIME() then returns the seconds since
                                     midnight to the millisecond, as a
                                     number with a fraction (it is
                                     SYSTEM(1020) / 1000).
             OPENSEQ file,id CREATING TO f
                                     Makes the file at once if it is not
                                     there, and takes the THEN clause.
             OPENSEQ file,id SHARED TO f
                                     Opens for appending (and makes it if
                                     needed) so that several processes can
                                     add to the same file, an audit log for
                                     example: each WRITESEQ is one whole
                                     line at the end of the file, and no
                                     process overwrites another's.  It is
                                     not buffered, so it is slower than a
                                     plain APPEND.

18 Oct 26  CREATE.FILE COMMENT, MULTIFILE AND RELATIVE PATHNAME.  Syntax is SD's
           own.

             CREATE.FILE name COMMENT "text"
                            Puts the text in the VOC record after the type
                            code, where a person listing the VOC sees it.
             CREATE.FILE name MULTIFILE
                            Makes the data portion a multifile with one
                            subfile named for the file, as CREATE.FILE
                            name,name would.
             CREATE.FILE name PATHNAME dir
                            A relative dir is now kept relative in the VOC
                            record, so the account can be moved or copied.
                            A full path is stored as before.

           Also found and not changed: SD's SET.TRIGGER already refuses a
           file that is not dynamic, so LIST.TRIGGERS has no trigger on a
           directory file to show.  FSTAT already takes file names and DICT.

18 Oct 26  MD5(), SAVED LISTS IN ANOTHER FILE, INDEX OPTIONS.

             MD5(string)    The MD5 digest of the string, as 32 lower case
                            hexadecimal characters:  MD5("abc") is
                            900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72.  MD5 is not
                            secure against a determined attacker; use it to
                            check data, not to protect passwords.
             SAVE.LIST name IN file
             GET.LIST name IN file
             DELETE.LIST name IN file
                            Use the file named, instead of $SAVEDLISTS, for
                            the saved lists.  SAVE.LIST does not create it.
             CREATE.INDEX file field ... DELETING
             MAKE.INDEX   file field ... DELETING
                            Delete an index of the same name that is already
                            there first, instead of refusing.
             $INDEX.PATH    A VOC record  X / pathname  makes that the place
                            new indices go when CREATE.INDEX or MAKE.INDEX
                            has no PATHNAME.  The directory must not exist
                            yet; the first index makes it.
             LIST.INDEX file ALL LPTR {n}
                            Sends the report to a printer.

18 Oct 26  CATALOGUE AND MAP OPTIONS, TWO NEW OPTIONS.  Syntax is SD's own.

             MAP LOCAL           Lists the programs catalogued locally in
                                 the account (marked L), as MAP lists the
                                 global (*) and private ones.
             MAP NO.PAGE         Heading once, no page breaks, on the screen.
             CATALOG ... LOCAL RELATIVE
                                 Stores the object file's path relative to
                                 the account when it lies below it, so the
                                 account can be moved or copied.
             OPTION CATALOGUE.LOCAL
                                 CATALOG and DELETE.CATALOG use the local
                                 catalogue when no mode is given.  A name
                                 with a global prefix is still global.
             OPTION DIR.SEL.OMIT.HIDDEN
                                 SELECT of a directory file leaves out the
                                 names that begin with a dot.

18 Oct 26  COMMAND LANGUAGE: COMO, IF IN AND EXISTS, INLINE PROMPTS.  Syntax is SD's own.

             COMO ON record APPEND   Adds to the record if it exists
                                     instead of replacing it.
             COMO ON record IN file  Writes the record in the directory file
                                     named, not in $COMO.
             IF EXISTS file record THEN ...      In a paragraph: true if the
             IF NOT.EXISTS file record THEN ...  record is in the file.
             IF value IN a b c THEN ...          True if the value is one of
             IF value NOT.IN a b c THEN ...      the words after IN.
             ... NO.CASE                         Put after IN's list, or
                                                 after the second value of
                                                 =, <>, < and so on, to
                                                 ignore case.
             <<a,Prompt>>   An inline prompt's control letters (A, C, F, I,
                            L, R, S, U, SUBR, SYSTEM) may now be lower case.
             <<A,"Prompt, with a comma">>
                            Quotes round the prompt text let it hold commas.

18 Oct 26  FILE UTILITIES, COMMON BLOCKS, AUTOMATIC INCLUDES AND NAMES WITH
           TWO UNDERSCORES.  The openQM notes give the purpose of these
           and not the syntax, so the syntax below is SD's own.  The BASIC
           tokenizer changed in the kernel, so it needs a rebuild.

             COMPARE.FILES file1 file2 {DETAIL}
                          Counts the records only in one, only in the
                          other, in both but different, and the same;
                          DETAIL lists the ids.  DICT file names a
                          dictionary.  The exit value is 0 when the files
                          are alike.
             !MULTISORT(data, key, order)
                          Sorts several related lists (one to a field,
                          elements separated by value marks) by one of
                          them and keeps the others in step.
             FIXDIR file {id ...}
                          Takes the carriage returns out of directory file
                          items that were made with CR LF line ends.
             DELETE.COMMON ALL EXCEPT name ...
                          Deletes every named common block but those
                          listed.
             DELETE        A record another user holds locked is now
                          reported and skipped; DELETE used to wait for it.
             newvoc.mods   If SDSYS has a file of that name, its records
                          are copied over the standard VOC records in each
                          new or updated account.  An account's own record
                          marked [locked] is left alone.
             $BASIC.OPTIONS  A line INCLUDE record (or INCLUDE file record)
                          makes the compiler include that record at the top
                          of every program it compiles.  Line numbers in
                          error messages and the debugger are unchanged.
             BASIC names   a__b, with two or more underscores together, is
                          now a valid name.

18 Oct 26  SSELECT SORT OPTIONS AND SSELECTN.

             SSELECT file RIGHT.ALIGNED     Sorts the ids as numbers where
                                            they are numbers (9, 10, 100)
                                            instead of as text (10, 100, 9).
             SSELECT file DESCENDING        Sorts the other way round.
             SSELECT file TO n ...          Both options may be given, in
                                            either order, before TO.
             SSELECTN file TO n             Same as SSELECT file TO n.

           A program compiled before this change keeps working: the old
           SSELECT instruction still sorts left aligned and ascending.

18 Oct 26  SIX MULTIVALUED FUNCTIONS.

             ADDS(a, b)    Adds a and b element by element, where each is
                           a dynamic array.  A list that runs out first
                           counts as zero.
             SUBS(a, b)    Subtracts.
             MULS(a, b)    Multiplies.
             DIVS(a, b)    Divides.
             QUOTES(a)     Puts double quotes round every element of a
             DQUOTES(a)    (the two names are the same function).

           They work on field, value and sub-value marks the way the
           existing ANDS, CATS and MODS do.

           SQUOTES is not built.

18 Oct 26  CONFIGURATION DEFAULTS, INVCASE AND sd -status.

             INTPREC    The default is now 11 (it was 13).  Thirteen
                        significant figures was found to be too many: the
                        last two or three of a calculated number showed
                        binary rounding noise.  A site that wants the old
                        value sets INTPREC=13 in the configuration file.
             SORTMEM    The default is now 4096 kb (it was 1024): sorts of
                        up to 4 Mb are done in memory, which is faster.
                        A site short of memory sets SORTMEM lower.
             INVCASE    New.  INVCASE=1 in the configuration file turns
                        terminal input case inversion on when a session
                        starts.  The default is 0 (off), as it has been
                        since 14 September, so nothing changes unless the
                        administrator asks.
             sd -status Prints the version, the time SD was started and the
                        number of interactive, phantom and API processes
                        against the user limit.

18 Oct 26  FOUR NEW SYSTEM() KEYS.

             SYSTEM(35)    The number of users logged in now, phantoms and
                           the API server's sessions included.
             SYSTEM(1016)  The number of user slots still free: the user
                           limit (SYSTEM(1013)) less SYSTEM(35).
             SYSTEM(1045)  The time SD was started, as a Unix epoch value
                           (seconds since 1 January 1970).
             SYSTEM(1047)  A new globally unique identifier, 36 characters
                           such as 3f2c9a52-...; every call returns a new one.

           The names SYS$USERS, SYS$START_TIME and SYS$GUID are in KEYS.H.
           1016 had a name but no value before; it returned nothing useful.

18 Oct 26  TWO BASIC DEFECTS FIXED.

             SEEK from the end   SEEK file, -3, 2 failed with error 3028
                                 although a negative offset from the end of
                                 a sequential file is the usual way to say
                                 "three bytes before the end".  It now
                                 positions there.  A positive offset with 2
                                 meant the same thing in SD before and still
                                 does, so no existing program changes; an
                                 offset that lands before the start of the
                                 file is still refused.
             LOCATE, right       LOCATE 5 IN s BY 'AR' where s held a single
             justified           item that was text, such as 'abc', said
                                 position 2.  Text sorts after numbers, so
                                 the number goes first: it now says 1, as it
                                 already did when the list held two or more
                                 text items.

18 Oct 26  FIVE CONVERSION CODE ADDITIONS AND TRIMW.

             MCN- and MC/N-   MCN keeps only the digits; MCN- also keeps a
                              minus sign at the start of the string.  MC/N-
                              deletes the digits and that minus.
             MCS              Capitalises the first letter of each sentence
                              and leaves the rest as it is.
             MCP*  MCP'*'     MCP shows a non-printing character as a dot.
                              A character after MCP, bare or in single or
                              double quotes, is shown instead.
             MT with M        The value is milliseconds since midnight
                              (MTSM, MTHSM).  The options H, S and M may be
                              in any order.
             MD options       The negative value action (+ - < ( C D) and P,
                              Z and T may come in any order.  They had to be
                              in that order before.
             TRIMW(s)         Like TRIM but for tabs, line feeds and the other
                              white space as well as spaces; a run inside the
                              string becomes one space.

           TRIMWS, the multivalued form, is not built.

18 Oct 26  DECATALOG, LIST.TERM.TYPES, CT ID.SUP AND NUM.SUP, A SPELLING OF
           $PRIVATE.CATALOG, AND TWO DEBUGGER COMMANDS.

             DECATALOG name ...    A new command.  Deletes the local
                                   catalogue entry and the object code it
                                   points to; DELETE.CATALOG ... LOCAL
                                   deletes only the entry.  A file name in
                                   front of the program name is accepted and
                                   ignored.
             LIST.TERM.TYPES       A new command.  Lists the terminal types
                                   SD has a definition for (the names under
                                   terminfo in the SD directory) and how
                                   many there are.
             CT ... ID.SUP         Leaves out the "file record" line.
             CT ... NUM.SUP        Leaves out the line numbers.
             CT DICT file item     The default display of a compiled
                                   dictionary item no longer shows the object
                                   code after the 15 lines of the item; HEX
                                   and BINARY still show all of it.
             $PRIVATE.CATALOG      The VOC record may be spelt this way as
                                   well as $PRIVATE.CATALOGUE.
             Debugger BRK          With no line number, lists the
                                   breakpoints that are set.
             Debugger blank line   Repeats the last command (not while
                                   walking an array or the source, where it
                                   steps on).

           The debugger changes are built but not exercised: they need a
           terminal.

           What this does not do.  The paragraph IF with EXISTS, IN and
           NO.CASE, COMO appending or naming another file, the stack editor's
           .C- and the stacks directory, CATALOGUE RELATIVE and the prefix
           option, the MAP options, the editor and SHOW and LIST.ITEM items,
           the WATCH condition, WINDOW, /*, the PTERM and terminfo items,
           DELETE.COMMON with an exception list, COMPARE.FILES, iOSCOPY,
           !MULTISORT and a NEWVOC.MODS file are not built: the release notes
           give no syntax, or the part of SD they change is not here.

18 Oct 26  SET.TRIGGER WORKS AGAIN, AND GAINS ALL; LIST.TRIGGERS; FSTAT'S
           COLUMNS ARE WIDER.

           SET.TRIGGER refused every function name.  Since the call-name
           change of 14 September it lower-cased the name and then checked it
           against upper-case letters, so a file could not be given a trigger
           with the command at all ("Illegal trigger function name").  It
           checks the upper-case form now.  A trigger set from a BASIC
           program or an older release was not affected.

             SET.TRIGGER file name ALL   Every trigger mode (pre- and
                             post-write, pre- and post-delete, read, pre-
                             and post-clear).
             LIST.TRIGGERS {file ...}    A new command: every file of the
                             account that has a trigger function, with the
                             function and its modes; with names, only those
                             files.  Dictionaries are looked at as well.
             FSTAT           The columns of the statistics are twelve wide,
                             not ten, so the counts of a fast disk fit.

           What this does not do.  FILE.STAT, LISTDICT, the LISTF WITH
           clause, CONFIGURE.FILE REPORTING and NO.MAP, a comment on
           CREATE.FILE, its MULTIFILE option, relative PATHNAME, the ANALYSE.FILE
           histogram and SET.FILE with chained Q-pointers are not built: the
           release notes give no syntax for them, or the command does not exist
           here to extend.

18 Oct 26  SAVED SELECT LISTS: KEEP, DELETING, COUNT.SUP, A DEFAULT NAME,
           SORT.LIST AND SELECT.DIR.

             SAVE.LIST name KEEP    Leaves the active select list as it was.
                                    SAVE.LIST has always emptied it.
             GET.LIST name DELETING Deletes the saved list once it is loaded.
             COUNT.SUP              On GET.LIST and SAVE.LIST: do not print
                                    the count of records.
             SAVE.LIST, GET.LIST    The name may be left out.  The list is
                                    then called after its number: SAVE.LIST
                                    saves list 0 as "0", and GET.LIST 0
                                    brings it back.  GET.LIST TO 2 and
                                    SAVE.LIST FROM 2 name the list number as
                                    before.
             SORT.LIST name         A new command.  Sorts a saved list into
                                    ascending order, as strings, in place.
                                    FROM file names another list file.
             SELECT.DIR pathname    A new command.  Makes a select list (0, or
                                    TO n) of the names of the files and
                                    subdirectories in an operating system
                                    directory.

           What this does not do.  There is no SELECT.DIR option to keep only
           files or only directories, and SORT.LIST has no descending order or
           numeric order.  SHOW.LIST, COPY.LISTP, the SELECTINDEX command, the
           index commands' new options, LIST.INDEX to a printer and the
           alternative list file on SAVE.LIST and GET.LIST are not built; the
           release notes do not give their syntax.

18 Oct 26  SP.VIEW SHOWS WHERE OUTPUT GOES FOR PRINT UNIT MODES 0, 4, 5 AND 6.

           When SP.VIEW asks which printer to use it shows the print unit's
           destination.  It did that for modes 1 and 3 only; for a unit in
           mode 0 (the screen), 4 (standard error), 5 (the auxiliary port) or
           6 (a printer and a hold file) the line was blank.  It now says what
           the destination is.  The other print system items in the release
           notes (SETPTR INFORM, EJECT, the form feed sequence, SET.QUEUE, the
           printer page size in TERM) change the print system in the kernel
           and are not built.

18 Oct 26  WHO USER, PSTAT USER name, A LOCK HOLDER'S NAME IN PSTAT, AND
           !LISTU.

             WHO USER        Also shows your login name after the user
                             number and the account.
             PSTAT USER name USER already took a user number.  A name that is
                             not a number now selects the processes whose
                             login name it is; if there are none the command
                             says so ("No process is running for user xx").
             PSTAT           When a process is waiting for a lock, the line
                             that says which user holds it now shows that
                             user's login name after the number.
             !LISTU          A subroutine for programs: CALL !LISTU(data)
                             returns the user table behind LISTU, one field
                             a user in user number order, each with the
                             values user number, pid, IP address, flags,
                             parent user number, login name, terminal, login
                             time.  The flags are the USR$ bits of INT$KEYS.H.

           What this does not do.  LISTU still does not show the account a
           user is in, nor the number of processes of each kind, nor which
           phantoms are grouped with their parent; PSTAT and LIST.READU do not
           say when a process began waiting for a lock; and the hot spot
           monitor has no input/output counters.  Each needs the kernel to
           record something it does not record now.

18 Oct 26  SIX NEW OPTION NAMES FOR COMPATIBILITY WITH OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS.

           All are off, as every option is until OPTION turns it on, so an
           existing application sees no change.  OPTION with no name lists them.
           (The kernel's table of options grew by six, so this release must be
           rebuilt, not just re-catalogued.)

             QUERY.STRING.COMP   In a query, EQ and NE always compare as
                                 strings.  By default "1", "1.0" and "01" are
                                 all equal to 1, as numbers.
             QUERY.STR.COMP.ALL  The same for every relation (LT, GT ...) on
                                 data from a right aligned dictionary item,
                                 which otherwise compare as numbers when both
                                 sides look like numbers.
             QUERY.SORT.NO.CASE  Sorts in the query processor ignore case.
                                 QUERY.NO.CASE already did this and more
                                 (it makes selection ignore case too).
             BACKSLASH.NOT.QUOTE Accepted and has no effect here: a backslash
                                 on a command line is a path separator and
                                 is always an ordinary character.
             DEFAULT.MV          A dictionary item whose single/multivalue
                                 flag is blank is treated as multivalued.
             CLEAR.SELECT        Select list 0 that was waiting when a command
                                 started is cleared when that command
                                 finishes, unless the command made it.

           What this does not do.  FORCE.RELOAD, QUERY.MERGE.PRINT,
           PAGINATE.ON.HEADING, QUERY.FOOTER.PAUSE, NUMERIC.EXPONENT,
           DIR.SEL.OMIT.HIDDEN, INHERIT.MFILE.PATH and the OPTION D3 and
           MVBASE shortcuts are not built; each needs a change in the kernel
           or the print system, or the release notes do not say which options
           the shortcuts set.

18 Oct 26  THE QUERY PROCESSOR: TWELVE ADDITIONS FROM OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS.

           LIST, SORT, SELECT, COUNT, REFORMAT and SEARCH.  None of this
           changes a query that does not use it, except where it says so.

           New keywords (they are VOC records, so an account made before this
           release gets them from the usual account update):
             ABSENT.IGNORE   In LIST, COUNT and REFORMAT, do not print the
                             "'xx' not found" list for ids that are not in
                             the file.
             SORTED.SAMPLE n Like SAMPLE, but the n records are counted after
                             the sort, so BY num SORTED.SAMPLE 2 is the two
                             records with the lowest num.  SAMPLE 2 BY num
                             sorts the first two it meets.
             COL.HDR.SPACE   A blank line under the column headings of a
                             tabular report.
             FOR             SEARCH file FOR "text" "text" gives the strings
                             on the command line instead of asking for them.
             REPORTING       On COUNT and SELECT: a line "n record(s)
                             examined" for every thousand records examined.

           Existing keywords that now do more:
             FROM NONE       Ignores an active select list 0 and goes to the
                             file.  (REQUIRE.SELECT with FROM NONE is
                             refused, because no list is being used.)
             NO.PAGE         May now be given on any query command; it was
                             accepted only on LIST and its relatives.
             CSV 3           Every item is quoted, numbers too.  CSV 1 and
                             CSV 2 are as they were.
             BREAK.ON "'N'"  Starts a new page and numbers it 1 again, which
                             "'P'" does not.
             BREAK.ON "'LU'" The L (suppress the break line) is ignored when
                             the U (underline) is also given.
             SEARCH ... ALL  Also searches the record id.
             SELECT file field  A field name that no clause uses is now
                             SAVING MULTIVALUED field, so the list holds the
                             values of the field and not the record ids.
                             THIS CHANGES an old SELECT that named a field it
                             did not otherwise use; add no field name, or use
                             SAVING, to say what you mean.
             REFORMAT ... @RECORD  Appends the whole of the source record, as
                             it is, to the new record.

           What this does not do.  SEARCH with strings from a file, the
           DISPLAY option and the SCAN command, VERTICALLY FMT, the X
           breakpoint code and @BPV, $VLIST, the longer $QUERY.DEFAULTS and
           the unbroken-line marker in vertical reports are not built: the
           release notes give the name but not the syntax, and the exact
           spelling matters for compatibility.  The query bug fixes in the
           release notes could not be reproduced here.

18 Oct 26  DICTIONARY EXPRESSIONS: SIX THINGS THEY COULD NOT DO BEFORE.

           These are in I-types and in the compiled forms of A, S and C items.
           Nothing that compiled before changes its answer.

           IF with a part missing.  IF cond THEN a  and  IF cond ELSE b  are
           now accepted, and the part that is not there gives the null string.
           Before, either was a compile error ("ELSE not found where expected
           in conditional expression").  IF with neither part is still refused.

           Field by number.  A name that is F and a number, such as F4, is
           field 4 when the dictionary has no item of that name.  An item that
           is called F4 still wins.

           A format after a value.  F1 '6"0"R'  with no operator between the
           value and the format string is the format operation, as it is in
           BASIC.  It was a compile error.

           Hyphens in names.  If an item is called HY-X, the expression HY-X + 1
           now reads it.  The compiler first tries a name, a minus and another
           name or number as one id, and only if no dictionary item has that id
           is the minus a subtraction.  So F1-F2 is still F1 minus F2 unless
           someone has made an item called F1-F2.

           TRANS() of an A or S item.  TRANS(file, key, name, ...) used to
           accept only a D-type or I-type name.  A or S items are now accepted,
           and so is C-type: one with no correlative gives its field, the
           others give the value they compute against the record read.

           Divide by zero.  Inside a dictionary expression, dividing by zero
           now gives zero with no error and no warning.  It used to stop the
           report with "Divide by zero error in dictionary expression".  This
           is the one change in the kernel (op_arith.c).  A BASIC program is
           not affected: OPTION DIV.ZERO.WARNING still decides what it does.

18 Oct 26  THE BASIC COMPILER LEARNS SOME THINGS OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS
           ALREADY DO.

           These are all in the compiler and its BASIC command.  None of them
           changes a program that does not use them, and none needs the
           kernel rebuilt.

           $MODE and $BASIC.OPTIONS.  $MODE takes more than one mode name,
           separated by commas, each with an optional leading minus to turn
           it off:  $MODE PICK.SUBSTR, -OPTIONAL.FINAL.END.  The MODE line of
           a $BASIC.OPTIONS record takes the same list, and a leading minus
           there turns a mode off, which was not possible before.
           A new mode, IMPLIED.STOP, makes the code the compiler adds after
           the last END a STOP instead of a RETURN, so a subroutine that runs
           off its end stops the program rather than returning.  Another,
           STRICT.EQUATE, makes the compiler warn when a name given to EQUATE
           or $DEFINE is also a statement or function name (the warning is
           message 12043; it is a warning, the program still compiles).

           New @ tokens.  @LINE is the source line number it stands on, and
           @BUILD.TIME is the time the program was compiled, in seconds since
           1 January 1970 in the compiling session's local time.

           $IFDEF and $IFNDEF accept AND and OR between token names.  AND
           binds tighter than OR; there is no NOT and no brackets, and
           $IFNDEF is the opposite of the same test with $IFDEF.  The
           single-name form is unchanged.

           NO.QUERY on cataloguing.  The BASIC command, the $CATALOGUE
           directive and the CATALOGUE line of $BASIC.OPTIONS now take
           NO.QUERY, which stops the compiler asking "Program is also in the
           private catalogue. Remove (y/<n>)?" and the two like it when it
           catalogues a program (the old entries are removed without asking,
           exactly as CATALOGUE NO.QUERY already does).

W1.1-3 - 10 Oct 2026
-------------
Changes since W1.1-1.

09 Oct 26  A RESTORED ACCOUNT NOW GETS EXACTLY THE REMOTE ACCESS ITS BACKUP SAYS.

           When a restore re-used the Windows user SD had made for an earlier
           account, it only added ssh or API access and never took any away,
           so an account restored with narrower access kept the old access
           while the closing line said it had none.  The restore now removes
           access the backup does not give.

09 Oct 26  THREE INSTALLED SCRIPTS NOW WORK WHEN TYPED INTO A POWERSHELL 7 WINDOW.

           install-sdsys.ps1 (the recovery the installer names when it cannot
           create the SDSYS account), install-service.ps1 and
           reclaim-profiles.ps1 each ask Windows PowerShell for its own
           modules before they do anything else.  Typed into a PowerShell 7
           window they used to inherit its module folders, and install-sdsys.ps1
           stopped at ConvertTo-SecureString with the SDSYS account not set up.

09 Oct 26  AN UPGRADE NOW OFFERS THE PYTHON INSTALL.

           Python is installed from a box in the installer, and an upgrade
           shows no tasks page, so a computer upgraded from a release that had
           no Python support was never asked.  An upgrade now shows one page
           with that single box, unticked, when the Python installer is beside
           the SD installer and no all-users Python 3.13 or later is already
           installed.  Nothing else about an upgrade is asked or changed.

08 Oct 26  SD'S OWN COMMANDS ARE LOWER CASE WHEN A MESSAGE QUOTES THEM.

           The messages and usage lines that name one of SD's own commands or
           the words it takes now write them in lower case: "restore.account
           latest name", "backup.account all [to directory]", "create.account
           user <user name> {ssh | api | both | none}", "modify.account
           <account> suspended | unsuspended", "update.accounts all",
           "set.backup.directory".  Nothing about what the commands accept
           has changed; they never cared about case.  Messages that name a
           language keyword (an IF without its END, say) are not part of this
           change.  Made to match SD Core for Linux.

08 Oct 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT TAKES A KEPT WINDOWS USER OUT OF SD'S GROUPS.

           When the Windows user behind an account was not made by SD (the
           person who installed SD, or any user an SD account was named
           after), DELETE.ACCOUNT leaves that user in place and says so.  It
           now also removes the user from sdssh, sdapi and sdusers, so a user
           whose SD account is gone no longer keeps a way in over ssh or the
           API, and a later SD account of the same name does not inherit one.
           Each removal prints "<user> removed from group <group>".  Made to
           match SD Core for Linux.

07 Oct 26  THE AUDIT TRAIL'S WORDS ARE LOWER CASE.

           Every word the audit trail writes before the first "=" is now lower
           case, the command and the event name alike: "login account=ann",
           "login refused account=ann reason=...", "logto refused ...",
           "create.account account=ANN type=user", "delete.account ...",
           "modify.account suspend ...", "remote.api on", "api refused ...",
           "account refused branch=2 ...", "elevation granted ...",
           "internal session admitted ...".  What follows an "=" is not
           changed: an account name keeps its case, and so do the text of a
           reason and the command a person typed.  Made to match SD Core for
           Linux.

           Lines already in an audit file are not rewritten, so a file that
           was started before this change holds both spellings.  Search it
           without regard to case.

07 Oct 26  A FEW MULTI-LINE MESSAGES, THE API AUDIT RECORDS AND SDSYS'S VOC.

           * The messages for CREATE.FILE ... CASE, CONFIGURE.FILE ... CASE, a
             CONFIGURE.FILE refused because two ids differ only by case, and
             MODIFY.ACCOUNT SUSPENDED each run over several lines.  They are
             now printed a line at a time; a line break could show as a stray
             character.
           * The audit trail's lines for an API login refused at the account
             (no such account, suspended, not allowed, SDSYS from a remote
             session) now say which account: account refused branch=2
             suspended account=ANN.  Before, they named the user but not the
             account.
           * SDSYS's own VOC now has the keyword that tests a soundex code (~),
             as every other account's has.

06 Oct 26  A SESSION FED FROM A FILE OR A PIPE ENDS AT THE END OF THE INPUT.

           When the commands come from a file or a pipe, the session now ends
           when the input does, even with no OFF at the end, as a shell does
           and as SD Core Solo and SD Core for Linux already did.  A terminal
           session is not affected.

06 Oct 26  PY_LISTCRTE IS A SECOND NAME FOR PY_LISTCREATE.

           SD Core for Linux's name for the function that creates a Python list
           is PY_LISTCRTE; ours has been PY_LISTCREATE.  Both names now work on
           both products, so a program written for one runs on the other.
           PY_LISTCREATE is the one the documentation uses.

06 Oct 26  THE APILOGIN SETTING IS RETIRED.

           APILOGIN= in sd.conf did nothing any more: the API has always asked
           for the account's password, whatever it said, and APILOGIN=0 never
           weakened that.  It is now removed rather than left looking like a
           switch.  An sd.conf that still has the line works and the line is
           ignored; new installs do not write it, and CONFIG no longer prints
           it.  Made to match SD Core for Linux.

06 Oct 26  ADMINISTRATOR CHANGES ARE RECORDED, AND A DELETED ACCOUNT TAKES ITS
           PASSWORD WITH IT.

           Made to match SD Core for Linux.
           * The audit trail now records CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT and
             RESTORE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT when it suspends, unsuspends, or
             changes an account's ssh and API routes or its os.users entry,
             and REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH when they change.  Only a change
             that happened is recorded.  Before, only adding an account to a
             group and removing it were.
           * REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH now set @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE (0 when the
             change was made, negative when it was refused or failed), as the
             account commands already did.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT now removes the account's SD password record.
             Before, it stayed behind, so an account later made under the same
             name without a new password (the account the installer makes for
             the person installing, for one) could be entered over the API
             with the old password.
           * Signing in to SDSYS at a terminal now prints one line saying that
             administration was granted and to which Windows account.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT accepts REMOVE.HOME, so one command line works on
             SD Core for Linux and here.  There it removes the user's home
             folder, which is otherwise kept; here the Windows profile is
             removed anyway, so the word changes nothing.
           * EDIT and MICRO no longer break when a record name, an account
             folder or a Windows user name contains an apostrophe.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT with no arguments lists the OTHER form it has
             always accepted.

06 Oct 26  A COPY THAT LEFT RECORDS IN PLACE NO LONGER SAYS IT DELETED THEM.

           COPY ... DELETING copied a record onto itself and kept it, and then
           said "copied and deleted".  When that happens it now says how many
           records were copied, how many were deleted, and how many were left
           in place.  Pressing Enter at "Delete all records in ...?" and at
           "Use file ...?" (CLEAR.FILE) now means No, as at SD's other yes/no
           prompts, and those two prompts and "Update VOC to new release?"
           show (y/<n>).

06 Oct 26  WHEN SD CANNOT START AS A SERVICE, THE REASON IS NOW KEPT.

           If the SD service could not start SD (a line in sd.conf that SD
           refuses, for one), the service log said "see sdsvc-sd.log for what
           sd -start said" and that file was empty.  The message is now written
           to C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsvc-sd.log, and each start adds to the file
           instead of replacing it.

06 Oct 26  THE OPENSSH SERVER INSTALLS FROM THE INSTALLER'S OWN FOLDER.

           When an ssh-server folder holding Microsoft's OpenSSH installer is
           beside the SD Core installer, ticking "Install the OpenSSH server"
           installs it from there as one step of the install, with no network.
           Without that folder the installer downloads the server from Windows
           Update as before, and now says on the Ready to Install page and on
           the progress line that this can take up to 30 minutes.
           * That OpenSSH is installed in C:\Program Files\OpenSSH; the one from
             Windows Update is in C:\Windows\System32\OpenSSH.  SD treats both as
             Windows' own ssh server: the installer's check for another ssh
             server, the ssh settings SD writes, and SSH.SERVER REMOVE all find
             whichever one is there.
           * The installer keeps a copy of that OpenSSH installer in the SD
             folder (about 6.5 MB), so SSH.SERVER INSTALL can install the
             server later with no network and no wait.  When it would
             download, it still asks first, as before.
           * SSH.SERVER REMOVE removes a server from the OpenSSH installer at
             once and says so.  The server from Windows Update is still removed
             at the next restart, and the message says that.

06 Oct 26  PHANTOM STARTS A PHANTOM, AND THE STARTUP SETTING IS REMOVED.

           PHANTOM looked for the program it starts in a folder that holds no
           program in an installed SD, so it could not start one.  It now finds
           the program beside the one that is running, and a phantom that
           cannot be started ends instead of carrying on in the background as a
           second copy of your session.
           * STARTUP= in sd.conf is gone.  It never ran, and what it asked for -
             a command run as SDSYS when SD starts - is reserved to an elevated
             Windows sign-in.  A line that still has it stops SD from starting,
             with a message that says to remove the line.  CONFIG no longer
             shows it.
           * The failed-login delay for the SD API now comes after the refusal
             is recorded, not before.  A client that hangs up during the delay
             can no longer leave the refusal unrecorded.  Replies and the delay
             are the same.

05 Oct 26  RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST CHOOSES THE NEWEST BACKUP THAT HOLDS THE ACCOUNT.

           LATEST used to choose from the file name, so a backup of ALL accounts
           (or of several, named by count) counted as holding every account.
           With one present, an account that was in no backup was refused only
           after LATEST had chosen, and an ALL backup made after an account was
           deleted hid an older backup that still held it.  LATEST now opens each
           candidate backup, reads its record of what it holds, and takes the
           newest one that holds every account you named.
           * If a newer backup made on this computer does not hold the account (or
             cannot be read), it says so and names both files before it asks.
           * If no backup holds the account it says so and changes nothing.
           * LATEST ALL is unchanged: the newest backup made with ALL.
           * Only the record of what a backup holds is read to choose; the
             restore still checks the whole file before it changes anything.

02 Oct 26  F1 TO F5, HOME AND END WORK FROM A TERMINAL THAT SENDS THEM THE
           XTERM WAY.

           Some terminals send F1 to F4 as ESC O P, ESC O Q, ESC O R and ESC O S,
           F5 as ESC [ 1 5 ~, Home as ESC [ H and End as ESC [ F.  SD only knew
           the spellings the Linux console uses, so it threw the first two bytes
           away and typed the last one: F1 typed a P, F2 a Q.  All seven are now
           recognised, and a terminal type that names the same bytes for a
           different key still wins.
           * At the command prompt F1 on an empty line shows the help message, and
             Home and End move the cursor to the start and end of the line.
           * The Linux-console spellings, the arrow keys, Backspace and Delete are
             as they were.

02 Oct 26  F1 AT THE COMMAND PROMPT SAYS WHERE THE CHANGELOG IS.

           The message F1 shows ("SD has no built-in help system") now also
           gives the full path of this file, which records what each release
           of SD changed, and why.
           * It tells you to type LISTV to see the commands this account has.
             It used to say LIST VOC, which lists every record in the VOC,
             files and pointers as well as commands.
           * After the message the prompt is shown again.  The cursor used to be
             left on a blank line with no prompt.

02 Oct 26  RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST RESTORES THE MOST RECENT BACKUP.

           RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST name {name...} | ALL puts back the newest
           backup in the backup folder that was made on this computer and holds
           every account you named, so you do not have to type the file name.
           It says which file it chose before it asks.  With ALL it takes the
           newest backup that was made with ALL.  If there is no such backup it
           says so and changes nothing.  A file name still works as before.
           * The choice is made from the file name, which carries the computer,
             the accounts and the time, so a backup renamed by hand may not be
             found.  The restore still checks the file against its own record
             of what it holds before it changes anything.

01 Oct 26  SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY REMEMBERS THE BACKUP FOLDER.

           SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY folder makes the folder if it does not exist,
           checks that SD can write in it, and remembers it, in sd.conf as
           BACKUPDIR=, so BACKUP.ACCOUNT and RESTORE.ACCOUNT no longer need it
           typed.  With no folder it shows the one that is remembered.  Like
           the two it serves, it is a command for SDSYS.
           * BACKUP.ACCOUNT without TO uses the remembered folder.  TO still
             works, for that one backup only.
           * RESTORE.ACCOUNT accepts a bare file name and looks for it in the
             remembered folder.  A name that includes a folder is used as
             given.
           * If no folder is remembered, BACKUP.ACCOUNT and RESTORE.ACCOUNT ask
             for one and remember the answer exactly as SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY
             would.  RESTORE.ACCOUNT with NO.QUERY has nobody to ask and
             refuses instead.
           * The folder must be given as a full path, such as C:\Backups.  A
             path that depends on the current folder is refused.

01 Oct 26  SD Core for Windows is started by the name sd.  SD Core Solo
           for Windows is started by sd-solo, so the two can be installed
           on the same computer and told apart.

01 Oct 26  THE API PORT IS 4247, AND NOTHING CAN CHANGE IT.

           The SD API used port 4243, which OpenQM and ScarletDME also use,
           so SD could not run beside either of them.  SD Core for Windows
           now listens on port 4247.  No setting, installer option or command
           takes a port, so every SD Core for Windows system uses the same one.
           * APIPORT in sd.conf now only switches the API on: any number above
             zero means on, and SD listens on 4247 whatever the number is.  An
             sd.conf that says APIPORT=4243 keeps working, and now means 4247.
           * A program that names port 4243 to reach SD must name 4247 instead.
             One that names no port needs no change: the client library and
             the BASIC !sdclient class both default to 4247.
           * An upgrade moves SD's own firewall rule, SD-API-In-TCP, from port
             4243 to 4247 and leaves who may reach it as it was.  A rule you
             made yourself for 4243 is not touched, and no longer does
             anything for SD.
           * REMOTE.API and its help text now say 4247.

02 Oct 26  ACCOUNTS CAN BE BACKED UP AND RESTORED.

           Three new commands, in SDSYS:
           * BACKUP.ACCOUNT name {name...} | ALL TO folder writes the named
             accounts, or every account but SDSYS, to one zip file in the
             folder, named after the computer, the accounts and the time.
             The zip holds each account's files and what is needed to make
             the account again: its type, routes, description, groups and
             os.users settings, and its globally catalogued programs.
           * RESTORE.ACCOUNT zipfile name {name...} | ALL puts accounts back.
             An account that exists is replaced; one that does not is made
             first, and asks for a new password, because a password is never
             in the zip.  It asks before it changes anything.
           * SETTINGS.REPORT {folder} writes a text report of the system's
             settings for you to keep.  Nothing reads it back.
           While a backup or restore runs, nobody else may be signed in to
           SD, and new sign-ins are refused until it finishes.

02 Oct 26  SD CORE FOR WINDOWS HAS SHARED MEMORY AND SEMAPHORES OF ITS OWN.

           The shared memory and semaphores every SD session uses had the
           same names as upstream SD's.  SD Core for Windows now has names
           of its own, different from upstream SD's and from each other SD
           Core product's.  The installer stops SD before an upgrade, as it
           did before; an SD left running from the old version could not be
           stopped by the new one.

W1.1-1 - 29 Sep 2026
-------------
Changes since W1.1-0.

29 Sep 26  RUN WORKS FROM A DEEPLY NESTED FOLDER, AND A LONG ERROR LINE IS SAFE.

           * RUN could fail with "Invalid runfile pathname" when the program's
             folder was about 128 characters deep or more.  A normal install
             is nowhere near that, but a copy of SD in a deep folder was.  The
             path can now be up to 255 characters.
           * An error message that named a long program could write past the
             end of its line buffer.  The text is now cut short instead.
           * The build now stops if the install dictionaries were not written,
             where it used to carry on and produce an SD with none.

29 Sep 26  SD CORE IS ENGLISH ONLY, AND THE LANGUAGE COMMANDS ARE GONE.

           SD Core for Windows has no support for other languages.  Support
           for them had crept into W1.1-0 without being asked for, and it is
           removed.

           * SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE no longer exist.  Their programs
             are no longer installed, so an administrator cannot put them
             back into an account's VOC (the 17 Aug 26 entry below said they
             could).  Messages, month names and day names are English.
           * The installer no longer has a special case for Turkish and Azeri
             systems (the 22 Aug 26 entry, removed).
           * NLS is back.  It was taken out of every VOC on 17 Aug 26 and this
             release puts the verb into new VOCs again, so you can view and
             set the currency symbol and the thousands and decimal separators
             from the prompt.  Accounts that already exist get it from
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  GETNLS and SETNLS in SD BASIC, and the
             CURRENCY, DECIMAL and THOUSANDS keywords, are unchanged.

27 Sep 26  ACCOUNT COMMANDS WORK WHEN SD IS STARTED FROM POWERSHELL 7.

           Commands that make Windows changes for SD - setting a password,
           creating or changing an account, the ssh and API settings - run
           steps in Windows PowerShell. Started from a PowerShell 7 window,
           SD passed that window's module folders on, and a step that needs
           one of Windows PowerShell's own modules could fail to load it, so
           the command reported that it had failed. The installer was fixed
           for the same fault on 24 Sep; SD's own commands now point Windows
           PowerShell at its own modules too.

W1.1-0 - 26 Sep 2026
-------------
Changes since W1.0-0.  Most of it
comes from SD Core for Linux, which found these faults in the Windows code
while porting from it.

26 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER CAN INSTALL PYTHON.

           The release zip carries python.org's Python installer in a
           python folder beside the SD installer, and the SD installer
           offers to install Python for all users from it - an unticked
           box, shown only when the computer has no all-users Python 3.13
           or later.  Nothing is downloaded.  Python is needed only for SD
           BASIC's PY_ functions; restart Windows after installing it.

26 Sep 26  THE CLIENT LIBRARIES ARE GPL.

           The client libraries (sdclilib, qmclilib) and their headers,
           tests and tools now carry the GNU General Public License,
           version 3 or later, like the rest of SD, with no linking
           exception. The licence text shipped with SD says the same.

24 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER MAKES THE SDSYS ACCOUNT ON MORE COMPUTERS.

           On some computers the installer finished by saying SD Core could
           NOT create its administrator account, so nobody could administer
           SD until the account was made by hand. The installer runs its
           steps in Windows PowerShell, and on those computers Windows
           PowerShell was picking up another version of one of its own
           modules - the kind PowerShell 7 installs - which it cannot load.
           The installer and uninstaller now point Windows PowerShell at its
           own modules only.

           What the installer's last page says is now also kept in
           C:\ProgramData\SD\install-summary.log.

24 Sep 26  INSTALLING THE OpenSSH SERVER WORKS ON MORE MACHINES.

           On some computers the installer's "Install the OpenSSH server"
           step failed straight away, and took the two ssh steps after it
           down too: the closing screen said the server could NOT be
           installed, ssh was NOT limited, and who may reach ssh had NOT
           been set. On those computers a Windows PowerShell command SD
           used to ask about the server was broken, although Windows' own
           servicing tool, dism, worked. The installer, and SSH.SERVER
           INSTALL and SSH.SERVER REMOVE, now use dism directly.

22 Sep 26  A WINDOWS ADMINISTRATOR NO LONGER GETS EXTRA SD PRIVILEGES IN
           THEIR OWN ACCOUNT.

           Being elevated (an administrator, running SD "as administrator")
           used to also make a few things work in your own ordinary account
           that an ordinary session couldn't do: CATALOG ... GLOBAL,
           DELETE.CATALOG of a global entry, SH/!/EDIT/MICRO without an
           OS.USERS grant, BREAK ON USER, and PDUMP of another user's
           process.

           None of that was ever meant to depend on Windows elevation - it
           is SDSYS's, and only SDSYS's. Being a Windows administrator still
           gets you an account, still lets you install SD, and (unchanged)
           is still what makes SDSYS itself reachable when you sign in as
           it. It no longer does anything extra in an ordinary account.

22 Sep 26  SH AND ! ARE BACK ON ORDINARY ACCOUNTS.

           An ordinary account's SH and ! verbs (running a shell command
           from within SD) had gone missing. They still worked for
           SDSYS, but no other account could even type the command - not
           merely be refused it.

           They are back. What decides whether the command actually
           runs is unchanged: by default nobody but SDSYS can reach the
           shell this way, and an administrator grants it to a specific
           account with

               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-ON

           (OS-ON is the separate switch for OS.EXECUTE from within a
           BASIC program, and is unaffected by this.) The verbs were
           only ever meant to be present-but-gated, the same as before;
           this restores that.

22 Sep 26  SDSYS CAN BE REACHED BY A LOCAL API CLIENT.

           SDSYS is administered at the computer it is signed in to.  It
           has no remote access of any kind - not ssh, not a network API
           connection - and it needs no SD password.

           What is new is that a program running on that same computer,
           under the Windows SDSYS account in an elevated session, can now
           reach the SDSYS account through SDConnectLocal() - the local
           client call that starts SD directly rather than connecting over
           the network.  It sends no password: you are identified by the
           Windows account the program runs as, exactly as at the console.

           Network API connections to SDSYS are refused as before.

22 Sep 26  MODIFY.ACCOUNT SAYS WHY IT WILL NOT CHANGE SDSYS.

           SDSYS has no remote access: not ssh, and not the API.  That is
           unchanged, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT has always refused the account.
           What changes is the message.  It used to answer "Account name
           is invalid", which told somebody typing

               MODIFY.ACCOUNT SDSYS SSH

           that they had the name wrong - the one part they had right.
           It now says that remote access is never available to SDSYS and
           that the account is administered at this computer.

           Nothing else about SDSYS changed.

21 Sep 26  THE voc_template DIRECTORY IS NO LONGER INSTALLED.

           The install used to leave a directory named voc_template
           beside the SDSYS files.  It held a copy of the records that
           become SDSYS's own VOC, and nothing on an installed system
           ever read it: the copy into SDSYS's VOC is made when the
           installer is built.  It is no longer installed, and an upgrade
           removes the one an earlier release left behind.  SDSYS's VOC
           is unchanged.

20 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER SAYS LESS.

           Every screen the installer shows was cut down to what you need
           in order to install: what Setup changes, what to do next, and
           what to run if a step did not complete.  The explanations of
           why were long and are gone.  Nothing Setup does has changed.

           The installer now gives the options and the results, and
           nothing else.  Every warning and caveat that used to appear on
           one of its screens is in the installation page of the
           documentation, under "Warnings and things to know".

           The two popups at the end of the install - the summary and the
           sshd_config result - are gone.  What Setup did, and any step
           that did not complete with the command to run, is listed on
           the wizard's last page, above the note about the window that
           opens when you click Finish.  That page now opens with "SD Core
           is installed."

           The window that opens when you click Finish no longer prints
           two lines of SD's own: "Internal session admitted ..." and
           "Account X has no password set.  Setting the first one."  The
           first is still shown for every other use of the internal door
           and is still audited; only this window, which the installer
           starts, is quiet.  The second follows -QUIET, like the
           explanation beside it.

           The "Before you install" page, which listed every change Setup
           makes to Windows, has been removed.  One of its statements -
           that SD Core users can read each other's files - was out of
           date: each account's directory is locked to that account.

           On a computer that already has the OpenSSH server, the box that
           popped up the moment the options page opened is gone.  The page
           now just says "The ssh server is already installed." above the
           choice of who may reach it.

           The password screen at the end now says plainly that pressing
           Enter keeps the SDSYS password as it is, and typing a new one
           changes it.  It used to say so only after you had pressed Enter.

20 Sep 26  THREE Y/N PROMPTS NOW LEAVE A SPACE BEFORE YOUR ANSWER.

           "Use file '...' (y/<n>)?" and the two "OK to delete DATA portion"
           and "OK to delete DICT portion" prompts ended at the question
           mark with no space after it, so what you typed sat hard against
           it.  They now match every other prompt in SD Core, and the
           upstream SD.  What they ask, and what pressing Enter answers,
           has not changed.

19 Sep 26  SD CORE NOW REQUIRES A COMPLEX PASSWORD, EVEN WHERE WINDOWS
           DOES NOT.

           A password must be at least 8 characters and must contain a
           lower-case letter, an upper-case letter, a digit and a symbol.
           A symbol means any other printable character, and a space
           counts as one.  Anything that is not a printable ASCII
           character - a tab, an accented letter - is refused.

           It applies everywhere SD Core sets a password: MODIFY.PASSWORD,
           CREATE.ACCOUNT, the password an elevated session asks you to
           set, and the SDSYS password the installer asks for.

           ***WINDOWS' OWN PASSWORD POLICY STILL APPLIES ON TOP.***  Where
           your machine or your domain asks for more than this, it still
           gets it.  SD Core's rule is a minimum, not a replacement.

           SD Core for Linux applies the same rule.

19 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW TELLS YOU WHEN THE ACCOUNT'S DIRECTORY DID
           NOT GO.

           It asks you to confirm, saying the files cannot be recovered -
           and then deleted the directory without ever checking that the
           deletion worked.  A file still open in that directory, or an
           access rule that denies the delete, left it on disk and nothing
           said so.

           Everything else about the deletion always happened and still
           does.  What is new is the message naming the directory and
           asking you to remove it by hand.

19 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT'S WARNING OFFERED SOMETHING THAT NO LONGER
           EXISTS.

           It suggested suspending the account instead, and said a
           suspended account "can be given a tier again later".  Account
           tiers were removed from SD Core in this release.  It now names
           the command that reverses a suspension:

               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> UNSUSPENDED

19 Sep 26  THE "THIS ACCOUNT NEEDS A PASSWORD" MESSAGE SAID SOMETHING YOUR
           OWN MACHINE DISPROVED.

           It said that until an account has a password it "cannot be used
           at all: not here at the keyboard, not over ssh, and not through
           the SD API".  The first of those three has not been true since
           the install began creating your account for you: you can sign
           in at this computer's keyboard without an SD Core password.

           What a missing password really costs is the REMOTE doors.  The
           message now says that, and adds that the account still works at
           the keyboard.  Four places said it - the two messages, the
           refusal a silent install prints, and MODIFY.PASSWORD's own
           explanation - and all four are corrected.

           Nothing about the behaviour has changed.  An elevated session
           still asks you to set a password and still will not go on
           without one; an ordinary session still does not ask.

19 Sep 26  YOU CAN CHANGE YOUR OWN SD CORE PASSWORD.

           MODIFY.PASSWORD is now a command every account has.  Typed on
           its own it changes your own password: it asks for your current
           one, then the new one twice.

           ***NAMING SOMEBODY ELSE'S ACCOUNT STILL NEEDS ADMINISTRATOR
           RIGHTS.***  MODIFY.PASSWORD <account> sets that account's
           password without asking for the old one - an administrator
           resetting a forgotten password does not know it - and an
           ordinary account is refused.

           Before this the command belonged to SDSYS alone, so an ordinary
           account had no way to change its own password.

19 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER MAKES YOU SAY HOW AN ACCOUNT IS
           REACHED.  THE ANSWER DEFAULTS TO BOTH.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> used to refuse unless you added
           SSH, API, BOTH or NONE.  Now the keyword is optional: leave it
           out and the account gets both ssh and the API, which is what
           every account is entitled to.

               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER james
               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER james BOTH     (the same thing)
               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER james API      (the API only)
               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER james NONE     (neither; LOGTO only)

           ***NOTHING IS TAKEN AWAY.***  All four words still work and
           still mean what they meant, NONE included - an application
           account you reach only with LOGTO is still made by saying NONE
           and meaning it.  What has gone is the refusal when you say
           nothing at all.

           ***AND IT IS STILL THE ADMINISTRATOR'S CHOICE, BOTH WAYS.***
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT <name> takes the same four words and can turn
           either route off and back on again at any time.

18 Sep 26  THE INSTALL NOW GIVES YOU AN SD CORE ACCOUNT OF YOUR OWN.

           Installing SD Core creates an ordinary SD Core account for the
           person doing the installing, named after their Windows account.
           It is an ordinary account, not an administrator: administration
           is SDSYS's.  It can be reached over ssh and through the API,
           and MODIFY.ACCOUNT narrows that at any time.

           ***WHY IT HAPPENS AT INSTALL TIME AND NOT LATER.***  SD Core
           only admits accounts it creates itself, and it will not create
           one for a Windows account that already exists - it answers
           "Windows account <name> already exists".  Anybody installing SD
           Core signed in to Windows before SD Core was there, so the
           install is the one moment an account can be made for them.
           Running the installer again is not another one.

           ***NOTHING ABOUT YOUR WINDOWS ACCOUNT CHANGES.***  Your Windows
           password is untouched and is still what signs you in at this
           keyboard.  Your Windows sign-in rights are left exactly as they
           were.

           ***THE INSTALL ASKS YOU FOR ONE SD CORE PASSWORD FOR THAT
           ACCOUNT***, in the window that opens after the installer
           finishes.  It is not your Windows password and does not replace
           it: it is what lets you reach SD Core FROM ANOTHER COMPUTER,
           over ssh or through the SD API, which the account is allowed to
           do.  Signing in at this keyboard never asks for it.

           To change it later, type MODIFY.PASSWORD in SD Core.  You are
           asked for your current password and then the new one, twice.

           ***SIGN OUT AND BACK IN BEFORE TYPING sd.***  Windows decides
           what groups your session belongs to when you sign in, so the
           session that ran the installer does not yet carry the access
           the new account was given.

           If the step cannot be completed, the window at the end of the
           install says so, names the command that puts it right, and
           points at C:\ProgramData\SD\attach-account.log.

18 Sep 26  THE TIERS ARE REMOVED, AND WITH THEM THE ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNT.

           STANDARD, PROGRAMMER and ADMINISTRATOR are gone.  Every account
           is one kind of account: it has the whole vocabulary, ssh and
           the API are its to choose (CREATE.ACCOUNT and MODIFY.ACCOUNT),
           and nothing SD does is tiered.  The only limit on what an
           account can do is the one imposed by Windows.

           The owner's ruling, 18 Sep 2026: an administrator should be
           able to reach the machine, and to reach the database through
           the API.  The restriction in the earlier 18 Sep entry below (no
           ssh for an administrator, API only from this computer) is
           REPLACED by this change: ssh and the API are for every account.

           ***WHAT GOES.***  CREATE.ACCOUNT no longer takes a tier keyword
           - standard, programmer and administrator are refused as syntax
           errors - and MODIFY.ACCOUNT no longer accepts them either.
           LIST ACCOUNTS no longer shows a tier column; it shows the
           account's suspension state instead.  The one account in SD with
           special powers is SDSYS, and it is reached only by the elevated
           Windows SDSYS account.

           ***WHAT STAYS.***  Suspension.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account>
           SUSPENDED denies the account every SD door - local login, ssh,
           the API and logto - and changes nothing on Windows.  It is
           lifted with the new keyword UNSUSPENDED; naming a tier used to
           be the only way to lift it, and the tier keywords are gone,
           which is why the new keyword exists.

           ***WHAT MIGHT STOP WORKING.***  An account no longer gains
           Windows administrator membership, or the administration
           vocabulary, when it is created - the elevated Windows SDSYS
           account is the way to administer the system.  Scripts that
           named a tier keyword when creating or modifying accounts must
           drop the keyword, and scripts that lifted a suspension by
           naming a tier must say UNSUSPENDED.

           ***THE INSTALL NO LONGER MAKES YOU AN SD ACCOUNT, AND THE ONE
           PASSWORD IT ASKS FOR IS WINDOWS'.***  It used to adopt your Windows
           account as an SD account and then open SD at the end of the install
           so you could give that account a password - and, since 4 Sep 2026,
           give SDSYS one as well, which is why it asked twice.  What it makes
           now is SDSYS, reached by signing in to Windows as SDSYS, and SDSYS
           signs in to SD with its WINDOWS password.  ***THE INSTALL ASKS YOU
           FOR THAT PASSWORD***, in the window it opens after the wizard: 18 Sep
           2026, and the first install to generate one instead is why - "the
           password for the SDSYS account was never asked for or printed so no
           way to get in".  Press Enter on an empty line and the generated
           password is printed in that same window, and it is written to
           install-sdsys.log in the SD data directory.
           Nothing in the install asks for an SD password, and no SD session
           opens when the install finishes.

           ***WHAT MIGHT STOP WORKING.***  If you set a password during an
           install and expected that account to be there afterwards, it is not
           made any more.  Sign in to Windows as SDSYS, start SD Core from an
           elevated prompt, and create the accounts you want with
           CREATE.ACCOUNT; the password of an SD account you create is yours to
           set with MODIFY.PASSWORD.

18 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN NO LONGER SIGN IN OVER ssh AT ALL, AND CAN
           USE THE API ONLY FROM THIS COMPUTER.
           ***REPLACED LATER THE SAME DAY BY THE ENTRY ABOVE.***

           An account created with the ADMINISTRATOR keyword is no longer
           put in the sdssh group, and the ssh server is told not to accept
           the administrators group either - so an administrator's ssh
           login is refused before it reaches SD Core, from any machine
           including this one.

           THE API STILL WORKS, FROM A PROGRAM RUNNING ON THIS COMPUTER.
           That is how an administrative application reaches the database.
           A sign-in from another machine is refused, as it has been since
           W1.0-0.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT refuses to change either route for an
           administrator: the ssh is off and the API is on, and neither is
           a setting.

           ***WHAT MIGHT STOP WORKING.***  If you administered SD Core by
           signing in over ssh - including to this machine - that is now
           refused, and ssh says the account is not allowed rather than SD
           Core saying it.  Use an ordinary SD Core account for remote
           work, or administer at the console, or through a remote desktop
           or remote-control product installed as a service.

           WHY ssh AND NOT THE API.  A sign-in that does not come from the
           console is not filtered by Windows' own administrator prompt, so
           an administrator who signs in from another machine with a
           password holds full administrator rights straight away.  A
           program running on this computer as an administrator could
           already do that much, so allowing it changes nothing - which is
           why the API stays and ssh does not.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW REFUSES A CONTRADICTION INSTEAD OF IGNORING
           IT.  "CREATE.ACCOUNT USER name ADMINISTRATOR BOTH" and
           "... ADMINISTRATOR SSH" ask for ssh, and "... ADMINISTRATOR
           NONE" asks to give up the API; all three now stop and say so,
           rather than creating an account whose access differs from what
           you asked for.  Leave the route keyword off, or say API.

18 Sep 26  PORT FORWARDING IS TURNED OFF FOR ssh SESSIONS.

           Every ssh session already went straight into SD Core rather than
           a command prompt.  The ssh server is now also told to refuse port
           forwarding, so an SD Core account cannot use its ssh login to
           reach another service on the computer, or to reach another
           computer through it.

           ***WHAT MIGHT STOP WORKING.***  "ssh -L", "ssh -R" and "ssh -D"
           against this machine will be refused.  scp and sftp were already
           unavailable, because the session command is forced.

17 Sep 26  AN API SESSION NOW RUNS AS THE USER WHO LOGGED IN.

           When you log in over the API, SD now starts your session as your
           own Windows user.  Until now the session ran as the service
           account, which on Windows is LocalSystem - the most privileged
           account on the machine - and only pretended to be you for some
           checks.  Files your program created came out owned by you, but the
           session could still reach anything LocalSystem could.

           WHAT YOU GET.  The session can read and write exactly what your
           Windows user may, and nothing more.  A file your user has no
           permission on is now refused, and the audit trail records the
           session under your name.

           ***WHAT MIGHT STOP WORKING, AND IT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A
           FAULT.***  An API program that used to read or write a file your
           Windows user has no access to WILL NOW BE REFUSED.  That worked
           before only because the session was effectively the system
           account.  If something that used to work now says it cannot open a
           file, check the file's Windows permissions for the account you log
           in as - that is the answer, not a bug.

           OS COMMANDS FROM AN API SESSION DO NOT WORK IN THIS RELEASE.
           OS.EXECUTE and SH from a session that logged in over the API
           cannot start a program: Windows will not let a process started this
           way launch anything that needs a desktop, which includes
           PowerShell.  Console SD sessions and ssh are unaffected.  If your
           API code shells out, keep that work on the console for now.

           LOGGING IN IS ALSO A LITTLE FASTER.  The check for which accounts
           you may enter used to start PowerShell every time; it now asks
           Windows directly.

17 Sep 26  PRINTING GOES TO A WINDOWS PRINTER.

           SETPTR mode 1 - the spooled print unit - now sends the job to a
           Windows printer: the one named with AT, or your default printer
           when none is named, the same as OpenQM does.  Until now a mode-1
           job was handed to a Linux print command that does not exist on
           Windows, and the job disappeared without a message.

           WHAT TO KNOW.  "Your default printer" means the default for the
           Windows user the session runs as.  At the console that is you.  A
           session over the API or ssh runs as your SD account's Windows user
           with no desktop signed in, and may have no default printer set -
           name the printer in SETPTR (SETPTR 0,80,60,0,0,1,AT "Printer Name")
           for those.  The job is sent as plain text in the printer's default
           form; the BANNER, LANDSCAPE and printer-option settings of the
           Linux spooler are accepted and ignored.  COPIES works.  If Windows
           refuses the job - no such printer, no default printer - the session
           says so in two lines: Windows' own reason ("Settings to access
           printer 'x' are not valid.") and then "Print job not sent: ...".

           ONE WINDOWS SETTING TO KNOW ABOUT.  Windows 10 and 11 ship with
           "Let Windows manage my default printer" turned ON, and in that mode
           your default printer silently becomes the last printer you used
           from any program - which may be "Microsoft Print to PDF".  If you
           have more than one printer, either turn that setting off in
           Settings > Printers & scanners and choose your default, or name the
           printer with AT in SETPTR.

17 Sep 26  SENDMAIL SAYS IT IS NOT AVAILABLE, INSTEAD OF PRETENDING.

           The BASIC SENDMAIL statement relied on a Linux mail program that
           does not exist on Windows.  It used to report success and send
           nothing.  It now sets STATUS() to 12, "This operation is not
           supported on this platform", and sends nothing.  Sending mail from
           SD on Windows would need a mail server to be configured; nobody has
           asked for it, so it is not built.

17 Sep 26  A SESSION THAT DIES WITHOUT SIGNING OFF IS NOW CLEARED UP WITHIN
           FIVE MINUTES.

           If an SD session ended without OFF - a crashed program, a closed
           terminal, a lost network connection - its user slot and any record
           locks it held stayed behind until an administrator ran "sd -cleanup"
           by hand, elevated.  Meanwhile anybody else reading those records
           waited or was refused.

           The SD service checks for such sessions every five minutes and was
           finding them; the step that should have followed - running the
           cleanup - never ran on an installed system, and nothing said so.
           It runs now, and the error log records both halves: the session
           that was found gone ("Lost user ... running sd -cleanup") and what
           the cleanup removed ("Cleanup removed user ...").  Running
           "sd -cleanup" by hand still works and is still the immediate remedy
           if you do not want to wait for the next check.

16 Sep 26  THE API'S ENCRYPTION NO LONGER RUNS WITH SYSTEM PRIVILEGE.

           Since 15 Sep every API connection has been TLS 1.3 (below).  The
           part of SD that handles the encryption - the "relay", one small
           program per connection - used to run with the same privilege as
           the SD service itself, which on Windows is the system account.
           That meant a flaw in the encryption code could, in the worst case,
           hand a remote caller that privilege before they had signed in.

           The relay now runs as a dedicated local account, "sdrelay", with
           every privilege removed and at low integrity - the least an
           account can have.  It cannot read the database, the settings, or
           the server's own key file (SD hands it the key privately when it
           starts).  This is the same arrangement SD Core for Linux uses,
           where the relay runs as "nobody".

           WHAT YOU WILL SEE.  A new local account, sdrelay, appears in
           Computer Management under Users.  Nobody can sign in as it - it is
           denied every kind of sign-in - and it belongs to no group.  Leave
           it alone: it is created when SD's service is installed and removed
           when SD is uninstalled.  A new program, sdtlsrelay.exe, sits beside
           sd.exe in Program Files\SD\usr\bin; while an API session is open
           you will see one sdtlsrelay.exe process per session in Task
           Manager, owned by sdrelay.  If that account is deleted by hand,
           every API connection is refused until SD's service is reinstalled;
           the SD error log says "cannot log the relay account sdrelay on".

16 Sep 26  UPGRADING TO A NEW RELEASE NO LONGER STOPS AND ASKS ABOUT THE VOC.

           Installing a new release over an existing one could appear to hang,
           for around five minutes at a time, and then report that steps of the
           upgrade had failed.

           The cause was an old question.  When an account's vocabulary was at
           an older release level than the program, signing in printed "Your
           VOC is at release level ..." followed by "Update VOC to new
           release?", and waited for an answer.  Parts of the upgrade sign in
           behind the scenes, where there is no screen to show the question on
           and nobody to answer it, so each of those steps waited until it gave
           up.

           That question is no longer asked.  The installer already updates
           every registered account's vocabulary as part of an upgrade - that
           is what makes a command a new release adds usable in an account that
           existed before it - so asking the same thing again at sign-in was
           both unnecessary and the thing that stopped the upgrade.  Signing in
           to an account whose vocabulary is at an older level now goes
           straight in, and the upgrade completes without waiting.

16 Sep 26  UPGRADING NOW SAYS WHAT IT IS DOING.

           Upgrading brings three things forward into your existing database:
           the dictionaries, the vocabulary of every account, and a check of
           every file for record ids that differ only by case.  Each of those
           ran with nothing on the screen to say which one was in progress.

           Each step now names itself on the installer's page before it starts.
           Two of them walk every account and every file you have, so they take
           longer the more you have, and that is where a wait will be.

16 Sep 26  UNINSTALLING NOW ASKS ABOUT YOUR SETTINGS FILE SEPARATELY.

           Uninstalling has always offered to remove your SD Core database,
           and choosing to remove it also took sd.conf - the settings file -
           because that file lives inside the same folder.  There was no way
           to keep your settings while discarding the accounts, or to discard
           the settings while keeping the accounts.

           There are now two questions instead of one, and you can answer them
           independently.  Both still default to keeping, each says exactly
           what it would remove and where, and an unattended uninstall (one
           run with /VERYSILENT) still removes neither.

           If you remove the database but keep the settings file, the SD Core
           folder remains, holding only sd.conf, and installing SD Core again
           will reuse those settings.

16 Sep 26  REMOVING THE WINDOWS ACCOUNTS AT UNINSTALL NOW REALLY REMOVES THEM.

           The uninstaller offers to remove the Windows accounts that
           CREATE.ACCOUNT made.  If you also chose to remove the database, that
           offer could not do anything: removing the database deleted the
           sdusers group first, and that group is the list of which accounts SD
           created.  The sweep then found no list, said there was nothing to
           remove, and finished as though it had succeeded - leaving the
           accounts in place and still able to sign in to Windows.

           The two steps now run in the right order, so the list still exists
           when the accounts are removed.  If the list is ever missing, the
           sweep now says so and refuses instead of reporting that there was
           nothing to do.

           Uninstalling when the SD Core folder has already been removed by
           hand now offers the same question.  It used to pass straight over
           it and delete the list on the way out, so those accounts stayed on
           the machine, still able to sign in, with nothing left that could
           say which ones SD Core had made.  An unattended uninstall still
           removes no accounts.

16 Sep 26  UPGRADING NO LONGER REPORTS A FAILURE ABOUT A FILE THAT IS IN USE.

           Upgrading checked every file for record ids differing only by case.
           One of those files, $ipc, is used by the running SD Core service, so
           it could never be taken for rebuilding.  Every upgrade therefore
           ended by reporting that a file could not be read or rebuilt, and
           suggested re-running the tool by hand - which met the same running
           service and gave the same message.

           That file is now skipped, as the account vocabulary in use is
           already skipped and for the same reason.  Nothing is lost by it: it
           holds working records used while SD Core is running, not ids that
           anyone types.

15 Sep 26  AN ORDINARY ACCOUNT CAN WRITE ITS OWN FILES OVER THE API AND SSH.

           A user who was not an administrator, signing in to their own account
           over the API or over ssh, could be refused every write to a file in
           that account - its own VOC included - with "Update to read-only
           file", even though the account owns the file and Windows would allow
           the write.  Creating a file, cataloguing a program, and anything
           that changes the VOC could all fail this way.

           The cause was the way SD asked Windows whether it could write: the
           question was answered from the file's owner alone, and the files SD
           makes when it creates an account are owned by the administrator who
           ran CREATE.ACCOUNT, not by the account's user.  SD now decides by
           actually opening the file for writing and only falls back to
           read-only if that is refused, so an account writes its own files as
           Windows permits.

           Administrators were never affected, which is why this did not show up
           until now.

15 Sep 26  SDSYS IS REACHED ONLY FROM AN ELEVATED START.

           Running sd from an elevated prompt (Run as administrator) puts an
           administrator straight into SDSYS, as before.  What has changed is
           the other direction.  An sd started from an ordinary, unelevated
           prompt lands in your own account and can no longer get into SDSYS
           at all: "logto sdsys" is refused there, even for an administrator.
           Being an administrator is not enough on its own; you have to have
           started elevated, which is when Windows asked you to consent.

           In return, an elevated session keeps that right for as long as it
           runs.  It can "logto" down to a personal account to work there and
           "logto sdsys" back up again freely.  Until now leaving SDSYS ended
           the elevated session, so coming back was refused.

           The audit trail records a refusal as "session did not start
           elevated", so the reason is the same one this note gives.

15 Sep 26  delete.account NO LONGER STOPS WHEN SOME OTHER ACCOUNT'S VOC CANNOT
           BE OPENED.

           Before deleting an account, delete.account looks through every
           other account's VOC for references to it, so it can warn you.  If
           any one of those VOCs could not be opened - the account's directory
           damaged, moved, or not readable - the whole command aborted with
           "Error 3001 opening file", before it had asked whether to go on.
           One damaged account made every other account undeletable.

           The scan now skips an account it cannot look into, prints

             Account X could not be opened to check for references to the
             account being deleted, so it was skipped. The deletion continues.

           and the deletion goes ahead.  The scan only ever produced a
           warning; it should never have been able to stop the command.

15 Sep 26  THE API IS ENCRYPTED: EVERY CONNECTION IS TLS 1.3.

           Until now only the API login was protected.  The password never
           crossed the network, but everything after it did - every command,
           every record read or written, every result - as plain text that
           anyone able to see the traffic could read.  That was the weakness
           of the old QM-style API, and this closes it.

           Every connection to the SD API, over the network port (4243) and
           over the local one alike, is now encrypted with TLS 1.3 before SD
           sends a single byte.  The login is also bound to that encrypted
           connection.  A machine placed between a program and the server
           cannot log in on the program's behalf or read what passes, even by
           pretending to be the server: the program's proof of its password is
           only good on the connection the program itself set up, and the
           program will not go on unless the server proves it knows the account
           too.

           THE CLIENT LIBRARY (sdclilib, sdclient) AND THE BASIC !sdclient
           CLASS do all of this automatically.  A program built against an
           older copy of the library cannot connect: the server waits for
           encryption to begin and closes the connection after ten seconds.
           Rebuild or relink it against the current library.

           The server's key is made on the first API connection, in
           C:\ProgramData\SD\sd-tls, and only Administrators and the system can
           read it.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: programs do not yet check the server's
           certificate.  The bound login is what stops a machine in the middle
           from getting in or reading data, but such a machine could still
           collect a login attempt and try to guess the password offline.  Use
           SD passwords that are long and not used anywhere else.


15 Sep 26  PY_LISTCREATE CREATES A PYTHON LIST.

           A BASIC program could append to a Python list and read one back,
           but had no way to make one.  PY_LISTCREATE(listname) now does, and
           returns 0 on success.  It is declared in SDPYFUNC.H with the other
           PY_ functions, which now number twenty-one.

15 Sep 26  COPY ... DELETING NO LONGER DELETES A RECORD COPIED ONTO ITSELF.

           When the source and target are the same file and the two record ids
           name the same record - identical, or differing only in case, since
           every file now treats ids without regard to case - COPY with
           OVERWRITING DELETING wrote the record and then deleted it.  It now
           leaves the record in place and says it was copied onto itself.

15 Sep 26  SD'S OWN BASIC SOURCE RECORDS ARE LOWER CASE.

           The programs in GPL.BP are named in lower case, like the catalogue
           entries built from them.  Nothing you type changes: GPL.BP and its
           record names are found in any case.

14 Sep 26  NO TWO RECORD IDS MAY DIFFER ONLY BY CASE.

           Every file now treats record ids without regard to case, so a file
           can hold "jack" or "JACK" but never both - writing one when the
           other is present updates the same record.  This is how it has always
           behaved for the system files (VOC, dictionaries); it now holds for
           every file, including your own.  CREATE.FILE ... CASE is no longer
           accepted.

           When you upgrade, each of your files is converted.  Every file is
           checked first: if one already holds two ids that differ only by
           case, it is left exactly as it was rather than losing one of them,
           and both ids are named in the file nocase-upgrade.log in your SD
           Core data folder.  Rename or delete one id of each pair listed
           there, then convert that file yourself with CONFIGURE.FILE NO.CASE.
           A file with alternate key indexes is also left for you to convert
           the same way, and named in that log.  Nothing is changed in a file
           that would lose a record.

14 Sep 26  PROGRAM AND CATALOGUE NAMES ARE LOWER CASE.

           CATALOG now stores every name in lower case, and SD's own
           programs are catalogued that way: MAP lists $cproc, !parser and
           so on, and CATALOG and DELETE.CATALOG report the lower-case name.
           The names in a compiled program (CALL, SUBROUTINE, FUNCTION,
           PROGRAM and CLASS) are lower case too, and a call made in any
           case, including from a program compiled by an earlier release,
           still finds its subroutine.  A program you catalogued locally with
           an earlier release is still found, and cataloguing it again renames
           its VOC entry to lower case rather than adding a second one.
           Names of COMMON blocks are unchanged.

14 Sep 26  THE DICTIONARIES SD SUPPLIES NAME THEIR FIELDS IN LOWER CASE.

           The fields in the dictionaries of voc, of dictionaries themselves,
           of $map, accounts, os.users, batch.jobs and $hold are now named in
           lower case - type, desc, f1, data.name, @id and so on - and
           LIST DICT shows them that way.  Typing the old upper-case names
           still works everywhere, as the entry below describes.  A new file
           made by CREATE.FILE, and a new account's $hold, get an @id record
           rather than @ID.  When SD is upgraded, a field held under its old
           upper-case name is replaced by the new one rather than kept beside
           it; fields you added yourself under other names are not touched.
           Dictionaries in your own files, and $hold in accounts made before
           this release, keep the names they already have.

14 Sep 26  DICTIONARY FIELD NAMES ARE FOUND HOWEVER YOU TYPE THEM.

           A field defined in a dictionary in lower case can now be named in
           upper case, and the other way round: in LIST, SORT, SELECT and
           the other query commands, in COMPILE.DICT (CD), in CREATE.INDEX,
           in SHOW, and inside an I-type or A-type expression.  Each is
           looked up as typed, then in lower case, then in upper case, the
           same way VOC names already were.  Before, an I-type expression
           could only name fields whose dictionary names were upper case,
           and CD could not find an item named in a different case.

14 Sep 26  FOUR MORE VOC ENTRIES AND THE SYSCOM INCLUDE FILES ARE NAMED IN
           LOWER CASE.

           $ACC, $MAP, $RELEASE and SD.VOCLIB are now $acc, $map, $release
           and sd.voclib.  Typing the old names still works.  When an
           account made by an earlier release is updated (UPDATE.ACCOUNTS,
           or answering Y to "Update VOC to new release?"), an entry held
           under its old upper-case name is renamed, rather than a second
           lower-case copy being added beside it.  The same applies to any
           VOC entry SD renames in future.

           The include records in SYSCOM are now named in lower case, for
           example keys.h and err.h.  $INCLUDE finds them however the name
           is typed.

14 Sep 26  SET.FILE, .S AND CNAME NAME NEW VOC ENTRIES IN LOWER CASE, AND
           NO LONGER MAKE A SECOND COPY IN ANOTHER CASE.

           A new pointer from SET.FILE, a sentence saved with .S, and the new
           name given to a file by CNAME are now stored in lower case, as
           CREATE.FILE's new files already are.  Each looks for an existing
           entry as you typed the name, then in lower case, then in upper
           case, and treats a match as the same entry: SET.FILE reports that
           the pointer name is already in VOC, .S asks whether to overwrite
           it, and CNAME refuses the new name.  Before, typing a name in a
           different case from an existing entry quietly created a second
           entry beside it.

           CNAME also finds the file you are renaming however you type its
           name, and now renames a lower-case file's dictionary directory
           along with its data.  CNAME MYFILE TO myfile renames an old
           upper-case entry to lower case.  Catalogued program names are
           unchanged and stay in upper case.

           SET.FILE finds the target account's file however you type its
           name.

14 Sep 26  ".S" NOW HAS A DEFAULT WHEN IT ASKS TO OVERWRITE.

           "Overwrite VOC record 'x'?", asked when .S saves onto an existing
           entry, now ends (y/<n>)?.  Pressing Enter answers N and keeps the
           existing entry.  Before, an empty answer asked again, for ever if
           the input had run out.

13 Sep 26  TWO MORE QUESTIONS NOW HAVE A DEFAULT, AND DELETE.FILE CAN CANCEL.

           "Use active select list (First item 'x')?", which CD, COPY, CT,
           DELETE, DELETE.FILE and ED ask when a select list is active, now
           ends (y/<n>)?.  Pressing Enter answers N: the command stops and
           nothing is changed.  Before, an empty answer asked the question
           again, for ever if the input had run out.

           DELETE.FILE on a multifile asks "Delete all data components of
           multifile" and now offers three answers: Y deletes all the data
           components, N deletes the dictionary only (as before), and C
           cancels without deleting anything.  C is also what Enter does.
           Before, neither answer left the file alone and an empty answer
           asked again for ever.

13 Sep 26  FIXED: "SUPPRESS PAGINATION" AT A REPORT'S PAGE PROMPT NOW SHOWS
           THE REST OF THE REPORT.

           When a LIST, SORT or similar report fills the screen it asks
           "Action (Abort/Quit/Next/Suppress pagination)".  Answering S
           stopped the prompts, but every later page still cleared the
           screen and repeated the heading, so only the last page could be
           seen.  S now does what the NO.PAGE keyword does: the remaining
           lines run on without clearing the screen or repeating the
           heading.

13 Sep 26  NEW FILES ARE NAMED IN LOWER CASE, AND DELETE.FILE FINDS THEM
           HOWEVER YOU TYPE THE NAME.

           CREATE.FILE now stores a new file's VOC entry, and names its
           directory, in lower case: "CREATE.FILE ORDERS" makes the file
           orders.  Commands look a file name up as you typed it, then in
           lower case, then in upper case, so ORDERS and orders both reach
           it.  Files
           that already exist are not renamed.  If you need the name kept
           exactly as typed, use OPTION CREATE.FILE.UPCASE first - despite
           its name, that option keeps the case you typed.

           DELETE.FILE had one lookup that did not try lower case, so
           "DELETE.FILE ORDERS" on a file created this way would have said
           "No VOC record found".  It now finds it without asking.

13 Sep 26  FIXED: A FAILED "MODIFY.ACCOUNT ... <tier>" COULD SAY "NOTHING
           HAS CHANGED" WHEN PART OF THE VOC HAD.

           Changing an account's tier applies the vocabulary in layers and
           writes the register last.  If a later step failed, the message
           read "Unable to change the tier of NAME; nothing has changed" -
           but an earlier layer might already have been written, or the
           vocabulary might have been rebuilt and only the register left
           unwritten.  The message now reads "... its tier in the register
           is unchanged", which is true whatever failed: the register is
           the authority on the tier and it is only ever written at the end.

13 Sep 26  THE TIER POLICY LISTS MOVED TO THEIR OWN FILE.

           The two lists that decide which verbs each account tier gets -
           the verbs a STANDARD account is denied, and the ones an
           ADMINISTRATOR gains on top - used to live in SDSYS NEWVOC as the
           records TIER.OMIT.STANDARD and TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR.  They are
           not vocabulary entries, so they did not belong in a VOC file,
           where LIST VOC would show them as malformed records.

           They now live in a dedicated file, SDSYS tier.policy, as the
           records omit.standard and add.administrator.  IF YOU CUSTOMISE
           what a tier starts with, edit them there.  Nothing else changes:
           existing accounts are untouched, and each tier gets exactly the
           verbs it did before.

12 Sep 26  PYTHON IS AVAILABLE FROM SD BASIC AGAIN.

           The PY_ functions are back - PY_INITIALIZE, PY_RUNSTRING,
           PY_RUNFILE, PY_GETATTR, the dictionary and list families and
           the rest, twenty in all.  A BASIC program can run Python
           code, set and read Python variables, and pass strings, lists
           and dictionaries in both directions.

           WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM BEFORE.  Python no longer runs inside
           sd.exe.  It runs in a separate program, sdpy.exe, installed
           alongside SD, and your session talks to it down a pipe.  You
           do not have to start it or stop it: the first PY_ call in a
           session starts it, and it goes away when the session ends.

           The reason for the change is that an interpreter inside the
           database process could crash it, could see everything it
           could see, and tied SD to one exact Python version.  A
           separate program has none of those properties.

           YOU NEED PYTHON INSTALLED, and it must be an all-users
           install - one done "for me only" cannot be used.  Python
           3.13 or later.  If there is no Python, SD installs and runs
           exactly as before and every PY_ function returns -12040;
           nothing else is affected.

           WHO MAY USE IT.  The same people who may reach the operating
           system - the OS.EX setting in OS.USERS, or an administrator.
           Python can read and write files and start programs, so it is
           governed as the shell is, and a session that may not use
           OS.EXECUTE cannot get an interpreter either.  In that session
           PY_INITIALIZE returns -12041, as does every PY_ function that
           returns a status; the ones that return a value return an
           empty string and set STATUS() to -12041.  If SD could not
           tell - the OS.USERS record exists but could not be read, or
           has no second field - the code is -12042 instead, and one
           line in the errlog says which.  The decision is made once, at
           the first PY_ call, and stands for the rest of the session.

           $include SDPYFUNC.H declares all twenty functions.  Each
           returns a status - 0 for success, or an error code listed
           in SYSCOM ERR.H.

11 Sep 26  FIXED: DELETE.FILE ... NO.QUERY STILL ASKED ONE QUESTION.

           Deleting a file whose data part lives in the system account,
           with NO.QUERY given, printed one more prompt after saying it
           would not ask:  OK to delete DATA portion '' - with nothing
           between the quotes, because there was no file left to name.

           WHY IT MATTERED.  NO.QUERY exists so that scripts, build
           steps and anything else driving SD without somebody at the
           keyboard do not stop on a question.  When the answers come
           from a file or a pipe, an unexpected prompt takes the NEXT
           COMMAND as its answer - so the command is lost and the
           prompt is answered by accident.

           The file itself was always dealt with correctly.  What was
           wrong was the extra question.

11 Sep 26  FIXED: THE COMMANDS SD PRINTS FOR YOU TO RUN NOW WORK.

           When something needs doing by hand - setting the firewall
           rule, limiting ssh, running the installation check - SD and
           the installer print the command to run.  Every one of them
           began "powershell -File", and on a stock Windows machine that
           is refused: "running scripts is disabled on this system".

           WHY IT MATTERED MORE THAN IT SOUNDS.  Windows does not allow
           scripts to run by default, and the refusal names neither SD
           nor whatever you were trying to fix.  Worse, most of these
           commands are printed when something has ALREADY gone wrong,
           so the advice failed exactly when it was needed.

           Every printed command now says "powershell -ExecutionPolicy
           Bypass -File", which applies to that one command only and
           changes no setting on your computer.

11 Sep 26  FIXED: LIST.READU AND GETLOCKS COULD CRASH THE SESSION.

           If a lock was still held for a session that had already gone,
           listing locks looked up a user who was no longer there and
           used the answer without checking it.  Such a lock now shows
           its owner as "(gone)" and the listing finishes normally.

           WHY IT MATTERS.  On Linux the same fault stopped every
           session, because it happened while two internal locks were
           held and they were never released.  Windows handles those
           locks differently, so what would have happened here has not
           been established - but the fault itself was the same.

11 Sep 26  FIXED: MODIFY.ACCOUNT DID NOT CHANGE AN ACCOUNT'S VERBS.

           Moving an account DOWN a tier - say programmer to standard -
           reported the change but removed nothing, so the account kept
           the verbs it was supposed to lose.  Moving it UP added the
           verbs but stored them without their descriptions, so listf
           showed a bare letter where a sentence belongs.

           Both are fixed, and both had one cause: the rebuild still
           trimmed each record the way CREATE.ACCOUNT used to before
           that was corrected on 2 September.

11 Sep 26  PROMPTS BEFORE A DELETION NOW SHOW THEIR DEFAULT.

           Seven prompts now end (y/<n>) and take Enter as no: the DATA,
           DICT and "use file" questions in DELETE.FILE, the three
           catalogue questions in CATALOG, and .D's "delete VOC record".

           WHY IT MATTERS.  Enter used to be neither yes nor no, so the
           question was simply asked again.  When the answers came from
           a file or a pipe rather than a keyboard, there was nothing
           left to read and the question repeated without end.

           TWO PROMPTS ARE DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED, because no is not a
           safe answer to either: "delete all data components of
           multifile", where no still deletes the dictionary, and "use
           active select list", where no cancels the whole command.

11 Sep 26  FIXED: SDTIC REPORTED SUCCESS ON A TRUNCATED SOURCE FILE.

           If the last entry in a terminfo source ran out part way
           through - the usual cause being a final line left ending in a
           comma - sdtic stopped, wrote the database without it, and
           exited 0.  It now counts that entry as failed, says so, and
           exits 1.

W1.0-0 - 06 Sep 2026
-------------
This section covers the conversion from Linux to Windows.  It is the record of
what changed for someone using SD; PROJECT_STATUS.md and HISTORY.md in the
repository root carry the reasoning, the state of the work and the traps.
Entries are newest first, by date.  This is the first Windows release, so
everything below it is what W1.0-0 contains rather than what changed since a
previous one.

06 Sep 26  THE POST-INSTALL WINDOW NOW ENDS BY TELLING YOU TO SIGN OUT.

           After the install collects your passwords and offers the
           installation check, the window clears and shows one
           instruction: sign out and back in, or restart.

           WHY IT MATTERS.  The installer adds you to a Windows group
           called "sdusers", and that membership is what gives you
           access to the SD database.  Windows only applies a new group
           when you sign in, so until you do, SD cannot open its files.
           This was already said in the installer's own pages and in the
           documentation - it is now also the last thing on screen,
           because it is the one thing you have to do.

           IT DOES NOT SAY IT WHEN IT IS NOT TRUE.  If you are
           reinstalling and your sign-in already carries the group,
           the check ends as it always did.

           The prompt offering the check now shows its default, (y/<n>).

05 Sep 26  FIXED: SD'S ADMINISTRATIVE COMMANDS FAILED ON A FRESH WINDOWS
           MACHINE WITH "running scripts is disabled on this system".

           On a newly installed Windows PC, typing

             logto sdsys

           could fail with a PowerShell error saying that running scripts
           is disabled on this system. The same error could appear from
           append.sd.path, remote.api, remote.ssh and the editor.

           WHY IT HAPPENED. Several SD commands do their Windows-side work
           by running a small PowerShell script that ships with SD. Windows
           desktop editions refuse to run any script by default, and SD was
           not telling PowerShell that these are its own installed scripts.
           It now does. Windows Server editions allow scripts by default,
           which is why this did not affect every machine.

           NOTHING FOR YOU TO CHANGE. In particular you do NOT need to
           alter your machine's execution policy - if you changed it to
           work around this, you can put it back. SD only lifts the
           restriction for the scripts it installed itself, and never for
           an interactive shell you open with "sh".

05 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN NO LONGER SIGN IN OVER ssh OR THROUGH THE
           API. ADMINISTRATION HAPPENS AT THE CONSOLE.

           If your SD account is an ADMINISTRATOR account, SD now refuses
           the connection outright when the session has no screen - that
           means ssh and the API. You will see:

             An administrator may not sign in over ssh or the API.

           WHERE ADMINISTRATION HAPPENS NOW:

             * at the machine's own console, or
             * through a remote desktop or remote-control product
               (AnyDesk, RDP and similar) INSTALLED AS A SERVICE.

           A per-user install of a remote-control tool is not enough. It
           cannot show the Windows consent prompt, and the operator sees a
           frozen screen instead.

           NOTHING CHANGES FOR ORDINARY ACCOUNTS. Standard and programmer
           accounts keep ssh exactly as before, and so does a Windows
           administrator whose SD account is an ordinary one - it is the SD
           account tier that decides, not Windows group membership.

           WHY. Administration needs a screen Windows can draw its consent
           prompt on; that prompt is what makes an elevation something a
           person agreed to rather than something that merely happened. An
           ssh session has no screen. Until now SD assumed that meant ssh
           could not obtain administrator rights at all. That assumption
           was wrong: OpenSSH runs as a system service and builds the sign-in
           token itself, so a member of the Windows Administrators group
           arrived over ssh holding FULL administrator rights, with nobody
           asked to consent to anything.

           IF YOU RELY ON ssh FOR ADMINISTRATION, this will stop working
           when you upgrade, and that is deliberate. Use the console or a
           service-installed remote desktop.

           SCHEDULED TASKS ARE AFFECTED TOO. A task that runs unattended has
           no screen either. If it signs in as an administrator account it
           will now be refused; give it an ordinary account and list the
           command it runs in the SD system file batch.jobs.

04 Sep 26  THE CLIENT LIBRARIES NOW SHIP WITH SD, IN
           C:\Program Files\SD\usr\clients

           If you write a Windows program that talks to SD, it does so
           through a client DLL. There used to be two separate installers
           for those, downloaded and run on their own. There are not any
           more: the DLLs are built with SD and are installed with it.

             usr\clients\client64   sdclilib.dll   sdclient.dll
             usr\clients\client32   qmclilib.dll   qmclient.dll

           COPY THE ONE YOU NEED next to your application's .exe, or into
           windows\system32. Nothing is added to your PATH, by design - a
           program finds its DLL because you put it there, and never by
           accident.

           WHICH ONE:

             * 64-bit, new work                    sdclient.dll
             * 64-bit, already built against SD    sdclilib.dll
             * 32-bit, already built for QMClient  qmclilib.dll
             * 32-bit, new work                    qmclient.dll

           The two names in each pair are the same library, built together
           from the same source, so they cannot come to disagree with each
           other or with the server. Your program asks for one by name.

           Each DLL is a single file that needs nothing else installed: it
           uses only Windows' own system libraries.

           CONNECT OVER THE API, to 127.0.0.1 if SD is on the same machine.
           The API port is off unless you turned it on when you installed
           SD; the SD Core API section of Setup is where that is chosen.

           SDConnectLocal is the exception and cannot be used from a copied
           DLL. It starts SD by looking for sd.exe beside itself, so it
           works only from usr\bin, where the server's own copy lives. The
           32-bit DLLs have never provided it at all.

04 Sep 26  UPGRADING NOW GIVES EVERY ACCOUNT THE NEW RELEASE'S COMMANDS.
           YOU NO LONGER HAVE TO RUN ANYTHING AFTERWARDS.

           An upgrade replaces the vocabulary SD ships, but each account runs
           from its own copy, made when the account was created. So until now
           a command added by a release existed on the machine and could not
           be typed in any account that already existed - SDSYS included -
           and the only cure was to remember to run  update.accounts  by hand
           and answer Y. The closing box asked you to. Nothing checked that
           you had, and nothing told you what you were missing if you did not.

           Setup now does it for you, as the last part of an upgrade. It
           walks every registered account and refreshes its vocabulary from
           the one this release ships.

           WHAT IT WILL NOT DO TO YOU:

             * It keeps to each account's own tier. An account is never given
               a command its tier does not allow.

             * It leaves any VOC record you have marked [locked] alone, and
               names the ones it left, so you know which corrections in this
               release you have chosen not to receive.

             * Verbs are the exception and always have been: a verb is what
               SD runs, so a held-back one would go on calling a program this
               release has changed. Those are brought forward and named.

           If the step cannot run - SD will not start, or your database is
           older than the  update.accounts  command itself - the closing box
           says so and tells you the one command to run. It does not claim to
           have done something it has not.

           A first install is unaffected: its accounts are built from this
           release to begin with.

           You can still run  update.accounts  yourself at any time. It now
           also accepts  update.accounts all , which does every registered
           account without asking first; with no word after it, it updates
           the account you are in and offers to do the rest, exactly as
           before.

04 Sep 26  THE PASSWORD PAGE AT THE END OF AN INSTALL READS BETTER.
           ***THE PAGE IS GONE AS OF 18 Sep 2026 - SEE THE ENTRY AT THE TOP OF
           THIS SECTION.  There is no password page, because there is no
           password to collect: the install makes SDSYS, and its password is
           its Windows one.***

           It asks for two passwords - your own account and SDSYS - and it
           used to repeat the same five-line paragraph about what pressing
           Enter does, once before each. That paragraph is now said once, at
           the top, where you read it before you are asked anything.

           The page also had three different left margins and three different
           line widths, because two programs were writing to it and neither
           knew what the other was doing. One of the explanatory lines was
           long enough to run off the right-hand edge of an 80-column window
           and break a word in half. Everything the installer writes is now
           wrapped to one width, and each step's heading lines up with the
           output underneath it.

           Nothing about what is asked, or what your answers do, has changed.

           If you run  modify.password  yourself, you still get the full
           explanation. It is suppressed only when SD is started with -QUIET,
           which is how the installer starts it.

04 Sep 26  A TRANSACTION THAT FAILS PART WAY NOW PUTS BACK WHAT IT HAD
           ALREADY WRITTEN.

           A transaction is meant to be all or nothing. Until now, if a
           COMMIT got part way through and then could not apply one of its
           actions - a record locked by something else, a read-only file, a
           disk problem - it stopped there and left everything it had already
           applied on disk. The records before the failure were written; the
           ones after it were not. There was no way to undo it and nothing
           told you which was which.

           SD now records what each record held immediately before the commit
           overwrote it, and puts them back if the commit fails. A record
           that existed is restored to its previous contents. A record the
           transaction was creating is removed again, since it never existed.

           WHAT IS WRITTEN TO THE ERROR LOG. Whether or not everything could
           be put back, SD writes a line to sdsys/errlog saying so:

               Transaction commit failed part way: 3 record(s) restored,
               1 removed, 0 could not be undone

           If a particular record could not be put back - because whatever
           stopped the commit is also stopping the repair - it is named
           individually, with its file, so you know exactly what to look at.
           SD undoes everything else it can rather than stopping at the first
           problem.

           One thing worth knowing: the record the commit actually failed on
           is also restored, and so may appear in that count. A write that
           fails half way can leave a record in a state nothing can describe,
           so SD puts it back rather than assuming it was untouched.

04 Sep 26  RECORDS WRITTEN OR DELETED INSIDE A TRANSACTION, IN A DIRECTORY
           FILE, WITH CERTAIN CHARACTERS IN THE RECORD ID.

           This affects directory files only, and only record ids containing
           one of these characters:

               *  ,  =  >  <  %  /  +  :  ;  ?  \  "

           or an id starting with a dot or a tilde. In a directory file each
           record is a separate file on disk, and because those characters
           are awkward or illegal in a filename, SD stores them encoded - the
           id "=" is held in a file called "%E", and SD converts back and
           forth for you so you never see it.

           That conversion was not being done for a write or a delete made
           inside a transaction.

           A WRITE inside a transaction stored the record under the raw id
           instead of the encoded one. The write reported success, but a
           later read of the same id looked in the encoded place, found
           nothing, and reported the record as missing.

           A DELETE inside a transaction tried to remove the raw name, which
           was not there. SD treats "already gone" as success, so the delete
           reported success and the record was still there afterwards.

           Both are fixed. Neither needed anything to go wrong to happen -
           the transactions committed normally - and neither produced an
           error, a status or a log entry, so a site that hit this would have
           seen only a record that would not read back, or one that would not
           stay deleted.

           The same statements OUTSIDE a transaction were always correct, and
           records already on disk are unaffected.

04 Sep 26  "CANNOT GAIN EXCLUSIVE ACCESS TO FILE" NOW TELLS YOU WHICH FILE
           AND WHO IS HOLDING IT - AND YOU CAN CLEAR A DEAD SESSION YOURSELF.

           If your connection drops, SD does not always find out. The session
           you lost is still listed, and it is still holding open whatever
           files it had open. The next time you try something that needs a
           file to itself - build.index, delete.index, set.trigger,
           configure.file, cname - it was refused with

               Cannot gain exclusive access to file

           which named neither the file nor the session in the way. There was
           nothing there to act on.

           It now names both, and says what to do about it. If the session
           holding the file is one that has died, clearing it releases the
           file:

               logout <user number>

           THAT COMMAND NOW WORKS ON A SESSION THAT HAS ALREADY GONE. It used
           to signal the process and wait for a reply that could never come,
           so the entry sat marked "(logout pending)" for ever and the file
           stayed locked. It now reclaims the slot and releases every file,
           record and task lock the dead session still held. If the process is
           still running it is asked to log out, exactly as before - so check
           with the person first.

           AND "logout" IS NO LONGER ADMINISTRATOR-ONLY. Programmers have it
           too, limited to their own sessions: you can clear a session of your
           own that has died without needing an administrator. Standard
           accounts do not have the command.

04 Sep 26  INSTALLING NOW ASKS FOR TWO PASSWORDS, AND SAYS WHY.
           ***REPLACED 18 Sep 2026 - SEE THE ENTRY AT THE TOP OF THIS SECTION.
           Installing does not ask for a password any more, and the two
           accounts below are not the ones it makes: SDSYS is, and it uses its
           WINDOWS password.***

           If you are an administrator you have TWO SD accounts, and until now
           the installer set a password for only one of them.

               your own account   where SD puts you when you open it normally
               SDSYS              where SD puts you when you run it as
                                  administrator

           They are separate accounts and each needs its own password. Because
           only one was set, the first time you ran SD as administrator it
           asked for a password again - and the one you had just given during
           the install looked as though it had been ignored. It had not; you
           were being asked for the other account.

           The installer now asks for both, one after the other, and tells you
           which account each one is for. You may use the same password for
           both. Nothing about how you sign in has changed.

04 Sep 26  SD NOW RECORDS WHICH VERSION OF EACH EDITOR IT FOUND.

           SD writes a line for each full-screen editor when it installs, to

               C:\ProgramData\SD\install-editors.log

           That line names the editor's version, and for micro it used to stop
           short and say nothing - micro does not carry a version number the
           way most Windows programs do, so there was nothing to read. If you
           ever needed to answer "which micro is this?", the one place that
           should have known did not.

           It now asks micro itself, and records "version 2.0.15". If an
           editor cannot be identified at all, the line gives its size and
           checksum instead, so the field is never simply blank.

03 Sep 26  THE FULL-SCREEN EDITORS NOW COME WITH SD. INSTALLING NO LONGER
           DOWNLOADS THEM.

           The two editors the EDIT and MICRO verbs use - Microsoft Edit and
           micro - used to be fetched from the internet while SD installed.
           They are now part of the installer, and they are put beside SD's
           own programs in

               C:\Program Files\SD\usr\bin

           Three things change for you. The install is quicker, and it no
           longer stops near the end waiting for a download. It works on a
           machine that is offline, behind a proxy, or where the tool that
           did the downloading is blocked or absent - which was the case on
           Windows Server, and on those machines you previously got no MICRO
           at all. And the versions are fixed: micro 2.0.15 and Microsoft
           Edit 1.2.1, which are the versions the editor documentation was
           written from, so the keys it lists are the keys you have.

           EDIT AND MICRO NOW RUN SD'S OWN COPY. They look beside SD first,
           whether or not you ticked "Add SD to PATH" when you installed, and
           fall back to searching your PATH as before. If you already keep
           your own micro or Microsoft Edit somewhere, it is untouched - but
           these two verbs will use SD's, so that a question about a release
           has one answer.

03 Sep 26  COMPILING A PROGRAM WITH AN UNFINISHED "BEGIN TRANSACTION" NO
           LONGER HANGS. IT NOW TELLS YOU WHICH LINE IS WRONG.

           "BEGIN TRANSACTION" opens a block that only "END TRANSACTION"
           closes. If you reached the end of the program without closing it -
           a typo, or a missing line - the compiler did not report the
           mistake. It simply never finished, and there was nothing on screen
           to say why.

           It now reports "Unterminated transaction construct", in about half
           a second, the same way every other unfinished block already did.

           IF YOU HAVE HIT THIS BEFORE, IT MAY HAVE COST YOU MORE THAN THE
           WAIT. A compile that never finishes has to be stopped from
           outside, and doing that could leave the session still counted as
           logged in - which in turn could stop the SD service from shutting
           down cleanly, for a reason that looked unconnected to the program
           you were compiling. That cannot happen from this cause any more.

03 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER TELLS YOU THAT YOU ARE NOT AN ADMINISTRATOR WHEN WHAT
           IT MEANS IS THAT IT COULD NOT FIND OUT.

           SD asks Windows three questions about privilege: is this account an
           administrator, is this session running elevated, and is this user
           allowed to reach the operating system. Each of those checks could
           fail to complete - most often because your account is a domain
           account and the domain controller cannot be reached - and when one
           did, SD treated "I could not find out" as "no".

           The refusal itself was correct and has not changed. SD still
           refuses when it cannot establish that you have the right, which is
           the safe direction. What was wrong was the explanation: an
           administrator in an elevated window could be told to "start the
           shell with Run as administrator", which they had already done, and
           the audit trail recorded "reason=not an administrator" as though
           that had been established.

           SD now says which of the two happened. Where it cannot tell, it
           says so and names the reason, and it writes a line to the error log
           reading "PRIVILEGE CHECK UNDETERMINED". If you are locked out of
           something you believe you have the right to, that line is the one
           to look for: it means the check did not complete, not that your
           rights were found wanting.

03 Sep 26  "list accounts" AND "list os.users" NO LONGER SHOW ACCOUNTS THAT
           HAVE BEEN DELETED.

           A Windows account can be removed without SD being told - "net user
           /delete", a change in Active Directory, a decommissioning script.
           SD kept its own record of that account regardless, so "list
           accounts" and "list os.users" listed accounts that no longer
           existed, and "create.account" refused to make the name again,
           saying "Account already exists" about an account that did not.

           SD now checks its register against Windows every time the SD
           service starts, which is every time the computer starts, and
           removes the records for accounts that have gone. The account's
           directory goes with the record, because deleting an account means
           deleting its data.

           IF YOU WANT TO KEEP AN ACCOUNT'S DATA, SUSPEND IT INSTEAD OF
           DELETING IT: "modify.account <name> suspended" withdraws the
           access and keeps everything else.

           Nothing is removed on a guess. An account of the "GROUP" or
           "OTHER" kind has no Windows login at all, and neither has SDSYS,
           so those are left alone; so is any account whose directory is not
           where SD put it, and any account at all if Windows cannot answer
           the question - for example while a domain controller is
           unreachable. Whatever was left alone, and why, is written to
           "reconcile-accounts.log" beside the database.

03 Sep 26  "remote.api" AND THE INSTALLATION CHECK NOW TELL YOU WHO MAY REACH
           THE API, NOT ONLY THAT IT IS LISTENING.

           A listening API is not a reachable one: the firewall rule can be
           absent, or scoped to this computer. Until now both of these reported
           only the socket, so a machine that could no longer be reached over
           the network still looked fine.

           "remote.api" with no keyword now prints who may reach it as well as
           whether SD opens the port - the same two-part answer "remote.ssh"
           already gives. And "Check the SD installation" adds a line under the
           listening API: whether other computers may reach it, and, when no
           firewall rule admits them, that "remote.api on" opens it.

03 Sep 26  REINSTALLING OVER A DATABASE YOU KEPT NOW TELLS YOU WHAT DID NOT
           COME BACK WITH IT.

           The box that says "YOUR DATA IS UNTOUCHED" was true, and it was
           fair to read it as more than it said. Your data is kept. Who may
           REACH it is not part of your data: uninstalling deletes the Windows
           groups that decide that, and a Windows group takes its membership
           with it.

           So when SD Core was not installed on this computer at the moment
           Setup started, that same box now also says, in the same breath:

             - the ssh-only confinement IS put back, from the account
               register;
             - ssh comes back for everyone in the "sdusers" group, so an
               account whose ssh you had withdrawn can reach this machine
               again;
             - API access does NOT come back, and no install ever grants it.

           And it names the command that sets any of them, per account:

               modify.account <name> ssh | api | both | none

           THE KEYWORD SETS THAT ACCOUNT'S ACCESS TO EXACTLY WHAT IT NAMES.
           "api" on its own takes ssh away; "both" is ssh and the API.

           An ordinary upgrade, over an SD Core that is still installed, loses
           no membership and says none of this.

03 Sep 26  AND THE INSTALLER NOW CHECKS WHETHER ANYTHING CAN ACTUALLY REACH
           THE API, RATHER THAN ONLY WHETHER IT IS RUNNING.

           Uninstalling removes SD Core's own firewall rule for the API port,
           on purpose: leaving a rule behind for a service that is gone would
           be worse. Your sd.conf is kept, so a later reinstall still provides
           the API - and Setup does not ask who may reach it, because that
           setting is yours and it will not overrule you.

           The result was an API that listened and admitted nobody, with
           nothing said about it anywhere. The installer now looks for the
           rule, and when there is none it says so and names the way back:

               remote.api on

           or "remote.api local" for the same rule scoped to this computer
           only. If the rule is there, or SD Core cannot find out, it says
           nothing rather than guessing.

02 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NOW EXPLAINS BOTH REASONS "sd" DOES NOT RUN YET.
           IT USED TO NAME ONLY ONE OF THEM.

           There are two, they look nothing alike, and signing out happens to
           cure both - which is why nobody noticed that the explanation on
           screen matched only one of them.

           A command window that was already open while the installer ran does
           not have the new PATH, and answers that "sd" is not recognized.
           Opening a new window is enough for that one on its own.

           A window opened since has the PATH but still carries the group
           membership you signed in with, and answers that SD Core cannot open
           its files. Only signing out and back in cures that one.

           "Check the SD installation" in the Start Menu now reports the PATH
           as well, and says which of the two you are looking at.

02 Sep 26  listf IN AN ACCOUNT SD CREATED NOW DESCRIBES newvoc, INSTEAD OF
           SHOWING A BARE "F" FOR IT.

           Every other file in the listing carried its description. This one
           record was missing the text in the copy new accounts are built
           from, so it was the only one that came out bare.

02 Sep 26  THE VERB IS NOW update.accounts, NOT update.account. IT ALWAYS
           UPDATED MORE THAN ONE.

           Run in SDSYS as an administrator it refreshes SDSYS and then offers
           to refresh every registered account, which is what it was doing
           under the old name. The plural says so.

           YOU NO LONGER NEED TO VISIT EACH ACCOUNT. The closing page used to
           say "sign in and run this once in each account". That was never
           necessary, and it could not be done at all in a STANDARD or
           PROGRAMMER account, because the verb belongs to administrators. One
           run in SDSYS, answering Y, does all of them.

02 Sep 26  YOU CAN NOW PROTECT A VOC RECORD YOU HAVE CHANGED. PUT [locked] IN
           FIELD 1 AND update.accounts WILL LEAVE IT ALONE.

           An upgrade refreshes each account's VOC from the shipped one, which
           is how you get commands added by a new release. Until now that also
           meant a VOC record you had customised was replaced, with nothing
           said and no way to prevent it.

           Add [locked] anywhere in field 1 of the record, AFTER the type code,
           and update.accounts will never replace it. The first character of
           field 1 is the type code itself - two for a P - so [locked] must not
           come first: write "V [locked]" and not "[locked]V". Upper or lower
           case both work.

           THE RECORD THEN KEEPS THE VERSION YOU HAVE, INCLUDING ANY FAULT WE
           HAVE SINCE CORRECTED IN IT. That is the point of the lock and it is
           your decision, so update.accounts tells you which records it skipped
           every time it runs. Remove [locked] and run it again to take the new
           version.

           VERBS ARE THE ONE EXCEPTION: A LOCKED VERB IS UPDATED ANYWAY, AND
           YOU ARE TOLD. A verb is what SD runs, so holding an old one back
           would leave your account calling a program, or an internal routine,
           that the release has changed - and for the verbs dispatched by
           number that goes wrong silently rather than loudly. The lock is
           honoured on every other kind of record.

           IF YOU NEED A VERB TO BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, ADD YOUR OWN. Give it a
           name SD does not ship and model it on the system one. update.accounts
           only ever visits records SD ships, so a verb of your own is never
           touched and needs no [locked] marker.

02 Sep 26  ACCOUNTS SD CREATES NOW KEEP THE DESCRIPTIONS IN THEIR VOC RECORDS.

           "listf" in the SDSYS account shows a description beside every file -
           "File - Account register", "File - BASIC program source". In an
           account created with create.account it showed a bare "F" instead,
           because the descriptions were being stripped as the records were
           copied in. Keyword records lost a second description the same way.

           They are kept now. An account created from this release on will read
           the way SDSYS always has. Existing accounts pick the descriptions up
           the next time update.accounts runs in them.

02 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER OFFERS TO SET "NO PASSWORD FOR NOW". A PASSWORD IS
           REQUIRED, AND THE MESSAGES NOW SAY SO.

           The password prompt used to offer an empty line as a way to skip the
           step, and said the account would still work at this computer from an
           administrator session - just not over ssh or the API. That account
           could never exist. SD asks for a password every time you sign in
           until one is set, and ends the session if you do not give one, so
           there was no way to reach it.

           Nothing about what SD does has changed: the empty line ended the
           session before and still does. What changed is that the prompt, the
           refusal, and the installer's closing page now describe that instead
           of describing something else. Until an account has a password it
           cannot be used at all - not at the keyboard, not over ssh, and not
           through the SD API.

01 Sep 26  ssh.server install NOW WARNS ABOUT THE DOWNLOAD TIME AND ASKS BEFORE
           IT STARTS.

           Installing the OpenSSH server pulls it from Windows Update, which can
           take from a few minutes to about an hour with little on screen. The
           command now says so and waits for a yes before starting, so it can be
           aborted rather than appearing to hang. (ssh.server remove already
           confirmed; now install does too.)

01 Sep 26  create.account / modify.account NOW WARN WHEN AN ACCOUNT HAS NO WAY
           TO SIGN IN YET.

           If you set an account up to sign in over ssh but no ssh server is
           installed, or over the API but no API listener is running, the
           command now says so and names how to turn that transport on. It is a
           heads-up, not a refusal: the access is already granted and takes
           effect the moment you install the ssh server or turn on the API, with
           nothing more to do to the account.

01 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER INSTALLS THE OpenSSH SERVER BY DEFAULT.

           Installing the OpenSSH server is now an unticked choice in the
           installer rather than something done automatically.  Ticking it
           still installs it; leaving it unticked skips a download Windows
           fetches from Windows Update, which is a few minutes on a fast
           machine and up to about an hour on a slow one.

           This does not stop you creating or using SD Core accounts.  An
           account signs in over ssh once a server is installed, or through the
           SD Core API if you provide that instead; and an administrator uses
           SD Core by typing "sd" at an elevated prompt either way.  If you skip
           the server now, install it later by running the installer again and
           ticking the box.

01 Sep 26  delete.file NO LONGER REPORTS A FAILED INDEX DELETE ON A FILE THAT
           NEVER HAD AN INDEX.

           Deleting an ordinary file printed "Failed to delete index directory"
           and set an error return code, even though the delete had completely
           succeeded.  A file that really did have an index deleted quietly and
           correctly, so the message appeared on exactly the files it did not
           apply to.

           SD was asking where the index directory would live, getting an
           answer for every file, and then treating "there was nothing there to
           delete" as "the delete was refused".

           It now checks whether the directory is actually there first, and
           reports a failure only when one exists and cannot be removed.  A
           delete that worked no longer hands a script an error code.

01 Sep 26  A FAILURE WHILE EXTENDING AN INDEX NO LONGER DAMAGES THE INDEX.

           When SD needed a new block for an alternate key index and could not
           get one - a disk error, a full disk, a file held open by something
           else - it carried on and used block zero.  Block zero is the index
           header.  The write went over it, and the index was then permanently
           damaged: every select, list or query on that key read from a header
           that had a data block written on top of it.

           The error was always reported, but only after the damage was done,
           so the message named a temporary problem and left a permanent one.

           SD now stops the operation and reports the error before writing
           anything.  The index is left as it was.

           A healthy disk never reached this, and nothing about normal indexing
           changes.  If you have an index that has been behaving oddly after a
           disk problem, rebuild it.

01 Sep 26  A DELETE THAT FAILS INSIDE A TRANSACTION IS NO LONGER REPORTED AS
           DONE.

           Deleting a record from a directory file inside a transaction did not
           check whether the delete actually happened.  If the file was
           read-only, locked by another program, or otherwise protected, the
           commit reported success and the record stayed on disk - so a later
           read, select or list returned a record you had been told was gone.

           It now reports the error, the same way a delete outside a
           transaction always has.

01 Sep 26  DELETING AN ACCOUNT NOW WARNS THAT THE DATA GOES TOO, AND OFFERS
           SUSPENDING INSTEAD.

           The confirmation named what would be removed - the account, its
           directory, the Windows login - but not that everything the account
           holds goes with them, and it did not mention that there is another
           way.

           It now says both, before it asks.  The question and its default
           answer of "no" are unchanged.

           If you only want to stop somebody using SD, suspend the account
           instead.  Suspending keeps everything.

01 Sep 26  SUSPENDING AN ACCOUNT NOW SAYS WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.

           A suspended account loses all access to SD - local login, ssh, the
           API and logto.  Nothing at the Windows level changes: the Windows
           account, its password and its group memberships are all left alone,
           including administrator rights.

           That is correct and has not changed.  What is new is that suspending
           says so at the time, rather than leaving an administrator to assume
           one way or the other.  It also says how to lift it.

01 Sep 26  AN EMPTY DIRECTORY NAMED AFTER A DRIVE LETTER IS NO LONGER LEFT IN
           THE DATA TREE.

           Creating an account left a stray, empty directory beside the system
           files, named "C:" after the drive letter of the account's own path.
           It appeared once per installation, on the first account created,
           and nothing used it.

           It was hard to see, which is why it lasted: a colon cannot appear
           in a Windows file name, so anything that asked for this directory
           by name was told it did not exist.  Listing the folder showed it.

           An existing one is not removed by the upgrade, only never made
           again.  Deleting it by hand is safe.

01 Sep 26  THE AUDIT TRAIL NO LONGER RECORDS AN ELEVATION THAT DID NOT HAPPEN.

           When an administrator signs in and is put into SDSYS, the trail
           recorded "ELEVATION GRANTED" at the point the elevation was
           obtained - which is well before the rights are actually given, and
           two things can still refuse the login in between: SDSYS being
           suspended, and its directory being unreadable.

           Either of those produced a trail carrying both "ELEVATION GRANTED"
           and "LOGIN REFUSED" for the same session.  The record contradicted
           itself, and the false half was the half about privilege.

           The record is now written after the rights exist, so it cannot
           disagree with them.  If granting them does not work the login is
           refused rather than continuing as though it had.

           A refused login whose account directory could not be entered also
           records why, instead of "reason=unspecified".

01 Sep 26  A TIER CHANGE NO LONGER REPORTS A REMOVAL THAT DID NOT HAPPEN.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT removes things when an account moves down a tier:
           the VOC records the higher tier had, and the os.users record an
           administrator gets.  Both removals were reported as done without
           anything checking that they had been.

           So a VOC record that could not be deleted was still counted in
           "VOC: n records added, n removed, n left alone", leaving the
           account holding a command it should have lost while being told the
           tier had changed.  And a grant record that could not be deleted
           still produced "os.users: the record for X is removed".

           Both now report what actually happened.  A record that could not be
           removed is counted separately and named - it is not folded into
           "left alone", which means something else - and the os.users failure
           says so instead of claiming the record went.

01 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW NOTICES WHEN A GROUP MEMBERSHIP WAS NOT ADDED.

           Making an account puts the new user into several Windows groups -
           sdusers, sdsshonly, and sdssh or sdapi depending on the access it
           was given.  Each of those was reported as having worked without
           anything checking whether it had.

           The check that was there looked at the wrong thing.  It read the
           last operating-system error, which had been a sound test when this
           code ran an external command, and stopped meaning anything when
           that was replaced with a direct call.  It could sit at zero - "no
           error" - through any number of failed additions.

           The additions themselves were never broken, and nothing about a
           successful CREATE.ACCOUNT changes.  What changes is that a failure
           is now reported instead of being announced as a success, with the
           status code the call actually returned.

31 Aug 26  WEOFSEQ AND OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE NO LONGER REPORT SUCCESS WHEN
           THEY FAILED TO SHORTEN THE FILE.

           Both of these do their work by truncating a file, and neither
           looked at whether the truncate had worked.

           WEOFSEQ put 0 - success - on the stack whatever happened, so a
           program had no way to find out.  What this costs is a stale tail:
           the old content past the new end survives, so a file written by a
           later and shorter run can still carry rows from an earlier, longer
           one.  A file produced that way is the answer somebody takes away,
           which is why this was worth fixing even though nobody has reported
           it.

           OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE promises the previous content is gone.  If
           the truncate failed the old file stayed as it was, and later writes
           replaced only the front of it.

           Both now report the failure the way the rest of these statements
           already do: STATUS() gives the I/O error, an ON ERROR clause runs,
           and a statement written without one stops rather than carrying on.
           Nothing that used to work behaves differently - the change is only
           visible when the truncate actually fails, which needs a read-only
           file, a mandatory lock or a full disk.

31 Aug 26  DELETE.FILE NOW SAYS SO WHEN IT CANNOT REMOVE A RELOCATED INDEX.

           If a file's alternate key indexes had been moved into a directory
           of their own, DELETE.FILE removed the data and dictionary portions
           and reported each of them, then tried to remove the index directory
           without looking at the result.  A directory it failed to remove was
           left on disk in silence.

           It now prints "Failed to delete index directory" and sets a
           non-zero status, which is what it already did for a data or
           dictionary portion it could not remove.  The file itself was, and
           still is, deleted correctly - what was missing was the report.

31 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR IS NO LONGER SHUT OUT OF ACCOUNTS BY LOGTO.

           Administrators may enter any account without being granted access
           to it, and that had stopped working in two ways.  Signed in as
           yourself, the very first LOGTO was refused with "User not allowed
           in requested account" - which is every session reached over ssh,
           because ssh sessions are never elevated.  Starting elevated, the
           first LOGTO worked and every one after it was refused, including
           going back to SDSYS.

           The cause was that LOGTO asked whether the SESSION still held
           administrative privilege, and leaving SDSYS deliberately gives that
           privilege up.  Entering an account and being allowed to run the
           restricted commands are separate questions, and giving up the
           second no longer takes the first away.

           If you had been reconnecting to get back in, you should not need to
           any more.

31 Aug 26  BEING A WINDOWS ADMINISTRATOR IS NO LONGER ENOUGH ON ITS OWN TO
           REACH SDSYS.

           SD administration needs BOTH: membership of the Windows
           Administrators group, and an SD account created with the
           ADMINISTRATOR tier.  Until now only the Windows half was checked
           when deciding where a session lands, so somebody added to
           Administrators outside SD - by a domain group or a policy - arrived
           in SDSYS even though SD had never granted them anything, and could
           then enter any account.

           They now land in their own account like anybody else, keeping
           everything that account allows.  What they lose is SDSYS and the
           restricted administrator commands.

           NOTHING CHANGES FOR ACCOUNTS SD CREATED.  CREATE.ACCOUNT ...
           ADMINISTRATOR is what adds someone to the Administrators group in
           the first place, and it writes the tier at the same time, so both
           halves have always agreed for accounts made through SD.  The
           account the installer adopts is an ADMINISTRATOR account too.

           If an administrator finds themselves in their own account instead
           of SDSYS after this, their SD account is not an ADMINISTRATOR one:
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT sets the tier.

31 Aug 26  ERROR MESSAGES ARE NO LONGER CUT OFF AFTER THE FIRST LINE.

           Anything SD reported as a program error was truncated at about 84
           characters, so a message written as three lines lost the last two -
           usually the part saying what to do about it.  The space had always
           been there; the limit was simply set wrong.  Long messages now
           print in full.

31 Aug 26  A WRITE THAT NEEDED A LOCK NO LONGER BLAMES THE DISK.

           Writing a record without holding its lock - which a WRITE inside a
           transaction always needs, and so does any system set to MUSTLOCK=1
           - reported "Error 3023 (o/s 0) writing record (Possible full
           disk?)".  The disk was never involved and nothing had been written.

           It now says that no lock is held, that the stored record is
           unchanged, and that READU takes the lock (READVU for a single
           field).  The old wording is still used for real write failures,
           where a full disk genuinely is one of the causes.

31 Aug 26  "sd -cleanup" NOW RELEASES A DEAD SESSION'S TASK LOCKS.

           It already gave back the file, record and group locks a lost
           session was holding, but never its task locks - so a LOCK taken by
           a program that was then killed stayed taken, and no recovery an
           administrator had would shift it.  A nightly job guarded by LOCK 3
           would not run again until SD was restarted.

           If you have been restarting SD to clear a stuck task lock, you
           should not need to any more.

30 Aug 26  PRESSING ENTER AT A Y/N QUESTION NOW ANSWERS IT, AND THE PROMPT
           SAYS WHICH ANSWER YOU GET.

           Prompts are written (y/<n>) or (<y>/n) - the answer in angle
           brackets is what Enter gives you. Before this, several of them
           simply asked again until you typed a letter.

           The default is the cautious answer everywhere it could be: N at
           every question that deletes, overwrites, or replaces something. In
           the editor that covers copying and moving blocks, deleting a block,
           deleting the whole record, and overwriting an existing one.

           Two questions with more than two answers work the same way. Enter
           declines; Q to quit and A for "all" still have to be typed.

30 Aug 26  F1 AT THE COMMAND PROMPT NO LONGER DOES NOTHING.

           It now tells you that SD has no built-in help system, and points at
           the two things on this machine that can answer questions instead:
           "list voc" for the commands this account has, and the Start Menu
           entry SD -> Check the SD installation.

30 Aug 26  A STANDARD ACCOUNT GETS sort.item.

           It has always had list.item, and the two are the same program - one
           sorts and one does not - so withholding only one of them achieved
           nothing. A standard account now has 355 commands rather than 354.
           Programmer and administrator accounts are unchanged.

30 Aug 26  THE SD API CAN AGAIN BE PROVIDED WITHOUT OPENING IT TO THE NETWORK.

           The installer's API question is now two boxes. The first provides
           the API on port 4243; the second, underneath it, lets other
           computers reach it. Tick only the first and the port is open to this
           computer alone, which is what a program running on this machine
           needs.

           Leaving both unticked still means SD opens no API socket at all.
           remote.api on | local | off changes any of this afterwards.

30 Aug 26  A LOGTO THAT IS REFUSED THE DIRECTORY NOW EXPLAINS ITSELF.

           "Unable to change to new directory" is still printed, and is now
           followed by the reason it is nearly always true: Windows fixes your
           group membership when you sign in, so a group create.account added
           afterwards is not in your current sign-in yet. Sign out and back in.

           If that is not it, the message says so and names the directory SD
           tried, rather than insisting on a cause it cannot check.

30 Aug 26  delete.account NOW TAKES AWAY OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS WHENEVER IT
           TAKES AWAY THE WINDOWS LOGIN.

           An administrator-tier account is given permission to reach the
           operating system, recorded against the person's Windows login name.
           delete.account removed that permission only when it had also managed
           to remove the account's Windows profile - and a profile cannot be
           removed while its registry hive is still loaded, which is the usual
           state for someone who has recently signed in. So on the ordinary
           path the permission outlived the login it named.

           That mattered because the permission is keyed by NAME. Windows will
           happily issue the same name to a different person later, and they
           would have inherited it.

           It is also taken away now when delete.account finds the Windows
           login already gone - the half-removed account, where the SD side
           outlived the Windows side. Nothing else about the verb changes, and
           an account whose Windows login SD did not create is untouched as
           before.

30 Aug 26  THREE NEW ADMINISTRATOR COMMANDS: remote.api, remote.ssh AND
           ssh.server.

           Until now, whether this machine offered the SD API or accepted ssh
           from other computers could only be decided while installing. These
           change it afterwards, from inside SD. They are administrator
           commands and are refused to anyone else.

               remote.api on | local | off
               remote.ssh on | off
               ssh.server install | remove

           Each reports the current setting if you give it no keyword, and
           changes nothing when it does.

           REMOTE.API HAS THREE SETTINGS, not two. ON means SD listens on port
           4243 and other computers may connect. LOCAL means SD listens and
           only this computer may connect - which is what an application on
           this machine needs. OFF means SD opens no API socket at all.

           ON and OFF change whether SD opens a socket, and SD decides that
           when it starts, so they offer to restart SD. THE RESTART ENDS EVERY
           SD SESSION INCLUDING YOURS, and the question says so before you
           answer it. LOCAL and ON differ only in the firewall and take effect
           at once.

           REMOTE.SSH takes effect immediately and disconnects nobody. OFF does
           not stop the ssh server, it limits it to this computer - SD accounts
           sign in at this keyboard by connecting to "localhost", so that has
           to keep working.

           SSH.SERVER INSTALL downloads the server from Windows Update. It can
           take several minutes with nothing on screen and usually needs a
           restart afterwards. SSH.SERVER REMOVE asks first, because accounts
           SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing else, so removing it leaves
           them no way in at all - and the removal only completes when Windows
           next restarts, so ssh going on working until then is expected.

           None of these touches the ssh client. ssh, scp and sftp are a
           separate part of Windows and are left alone.

30 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER ASKS ABOUT ssh PROPERLY, AND THE STAND-ALONE
           INSTALLATION IS GONE.

           Installing the ssh server and letting other computers reach it are
           two separate questions now, and you are asked both.

           If this machine has no ssh server, you are offered one - and the
           "let other computers connect" box sits underneath it, greyed out
           until you ask for the server. It cannot be ticked on its own,
           because without a server there is nothing to allow.

           If this machine ALREADY has an ssh server, you are asked the second
           question on its own. That question was not asked at all before: the
           installer silently left the firewall exactly as it found it, so a
           machine that already had ssh could not be limited to loopback during
           an install. It can now. The box starts out MATCHING THE FIREWALL
           RULE THIS COMPUTER ALREADY HAS, so if you leave it alone nothing
           changes - tick it to let others in, untick it to limit ssh to this
           machine. SD still does not touch the configuration of an ssh server
           it did not install.

           Declining the ssh server no longer installs one anyway. It used to:
           the ssh box only ever controlled the firewall, so leaving it blank
           still downloaded the OpenSSH capability, started it and rewrote
           sshd_config. An install with no ssh server means nobody can sign in
           to an SD account, including at this keyboard - accounts reach SD by
           "ssh localhost" - so you use SD yourself, as an administrator, by
           typing "sd".

           THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION HAS BEEN REMOVED. It offered no ssh,
           no API and a quick install, and it is now what you get by leaving
           both boxes unticked - the same result without a separate choice, and
           without the one decision this installer made that could not be
           undone afterwards. To add ssh or the API later, run the installer
           again and tick the boxes.

           "create.account user" is no longer refused outright on a system with
           no ssh server. It used to be, because such an account could sign in
           nowhere. You already have to say how each account is reached - SSH,
           API, BOTH or NONE - so that is now decided per account, where it is
           actually known, instead of for the whole installation.

           THE API BOX MEANS SOMETHING DIFFERENT. It used to decide who could
           reach a listener that was always started. It now decides whether SD
           opens one at all: unticked, SD installs a configuration with no
           APIPORT and listens on nothing. Note that there is no longer a
           "listening, but only to this machine" setting - if a program on THIS
           computer talks to SD over the API, tick the box.

           If you had a stand-alone installation, it keeps working. The
           $standalone file in SDSYS is now ignored rather than deleted; its
           own text explains what it used to do.

30 Aug 26  SETTING A PASSWORD WORKS FROM SDSYS WHICHEVER WAY YOU REACHED IT.

           There are two ways into SDSYS: start SD from an elevated session, or
           log in to your own account and use LOGTO SDSYS. Only the first could
           set a password. From the second, CREATE.ACCOUNT and MODIFY.PASSWORD
           set the Windows password and then failed with "status 3035" or
           "SD's own credential could not be written", leaving the account
           unfinished - ssh would let it in and the API would refuse it.

           Windows was refusing the write, and it was right to. SD's credential
           store is readable only by the system and by administrators, and
           LOGTO does not change what Windows thinks your session is allowed to
           do, however much authority SD itself has after it. The half of the
           work that changes the Windows password already went through SD's
           elevation helper; the half that writes the credential did not.

           It does now, and the record is read back and checked before SD tells
           you the password is set - so "Password set for account X" means the
           credential is there and can be read, not merely that a write was
           attempted.

30 Aug 26  A FAILED CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER LEAVES A WINDOWS ACCOUNT BEHIND
           WITHOUT SAYING SO.

           When CREATE.ACCOUNT could not set the password and you answered N,
           it said "An account must have a password. Nothing was created." It
           was meant to remove the Windows account it had just made, and it
           tried - but it never looked at whether that worked, and printed the
           same sentence either way.

           From a session that reached SDSYS with LOGTO rather than by starting
           SD elevated, the removal was refused by Windows and the account
           stayed. It was in none of SD's groups, so nothing in SD could see
           it, DELETE.ACCOUNT could not remove it, and the account clean-up an
           uninstall offers could not find it either. It was also not confined
           the way accounts SD finishes creating are, so it could sign in to
           Windows with the password that had been set for it.

           Two changes. The removal now runs the same way every other change
           SD makes to Windows runs, so it works from either kind of session.
           And the result is checked: if the account really cannot be removed,
           SD says so, names it, and says what is true about it - rather than
           telling you nothing was created.

           IF YOU HAVE ONE OF THESE ALREADY, SD cannot help you find it. Look
           for a Windows account with an SD-shaped name that is in none of the
           sd groups, and remove it with Windows' own tools - or run
           CREATE.ACCOUNT for the same name from an elevated session, which
           takes it over and finishes the job.

29 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR REACHES THE OPERATING SYSTEM AGAIN WITHOUT
           ELEVATING.

           An administrator who had not elevated could not use SH, the !
           prefix, OS.EXECUTE, or the full screen editors - EDIT reads the
           same permission. They had to start SD from an elevated terminal to
           get any of it. That was not intended: an administrator is meant to
           have the operating system, ssh and the API by default.

           It happened because the permission record an ADMINISTRATOR account
           used to be given was removed while SD briefly put administrators
           straight into SDSYS at login, where the permission came from being
           elevated instead. That arrangement was withdrawn the same day and
           the record was not put back. It is back now: creating an
           ADMINISTRATOR account writes it again, with both fields set.

           WORTH KNOWING, BECAUSE IT IS DELIBERATE. The record is keyed on the
           Windows user, not on the SD account, and LOGTO does not change the
           Windows user. So an administrator who logs to another account KEEPS
           the operating system there, even though the account itself may not
           have it. ssh and the API do not behave that way - both are checked
           when the connection is made, so neither follows a LOGTO.

           You can still turn it off for a person by editing or removing their
           OS.USERS record; that is what the file is for, and an existing
           record is never overwritten.

           You will see this on a new installation. Installing does not
           overwrite an existing database, so a system that already has one
           keeps the permissions it already has.

29 Aug 26  LISTF NOW DESCRIBES SDSYS'S OWN FILES INSTEAD OF PRINTING "F".

           In the SDSYS account, LISTF printed a bare F in the Description
           column for ten of the sixteen files it lists - $hold, accounts,
           bp, bp.out, gpl.bp, gpl.bp.out, messages, newvoc, os.users and
           qfile. The other six showed real text.

           F is the file's type code, not a description. It appeared because
           the description is looked up in NEWVOC, the vocabulary a newly
           created account is given, and those ten files belong to SDSYS
           alone - so they are not in it, and with nothing to look up the
           display fell back to showing the type code.

           The ten now carry a description of their own. Nothing else about
           them changes, and no account gains access to anything it did not
           have before.

           Two verb entries, EDIT and MICRO, carried their description where
           the type code goes. Both worked - a type code may be followed by
           comment text - but they were the last two entries inconsistent
           with every other verb, and they are now a plain V like the rest.

           You will see this on a new installation. Installing does not
           overwrite an existing database, so a system that already has one
           keeps the vocabulary it already has.

29 Aug 26  UNINSTALLING NOW OFFERS TO REMOVE THE WINDOWS ACCOUNTS SD CREATED,
           AND SAYS SO EITHER WAY.

           Uninstalling removes SD's ssh configuration - the AllowGroups that
           said who could connect and the ForceCommand that put an ssh session
           into SD rather than at a shell prompt. It did not remove the
           Windows accounts SD had created. So those accounts stayed, with
           their passwords, and became ordinary accounts with a shell on the
           machine: the confinement went and the accounts did not.

           Nothing said so. The closing page listed what uninstalling keeps as
           your database, the ssh server and the sdusers group, and never
           mentioned the accounts.

           Two changes:

             * A second question, after the one about the database and like
               it defaulting to No: remove the Windows accounts SD created,
               with their sdu_ and sdg_ groups and their profiles.

             * The closing page now names the accounts in what uninstalling
               keeps, whichever way you answer.

           YOUR OWN ACCOUNT IS NEVER REMOVED by that prompt, and the question
           names it so you can see which one is being kept before you answer.
           The sweep also refuses outright if it would remove the last account
           able to sign in to Windows as an administrator - that refusal
           overrides a Yes.

           It records what it removed and what it kept in a log file, and
           names it when it finishes. A profile whose registry settings are
           still loaded cannot be deleted until the next restart; the log
           names any that were left behind.

29 Aug 26  A COMMIT INSIDE ANOTHER TRANSACTION NO LONGER THROWS AWAY THE
           OUTER TRANSACTION'S WRITES.

           If you began a transaction, wrote a record, began a second
           transaction inside it, and committed that inner one, the outer
           transaction was quietly abandoned at that point. Its writes were
           then lost when you committed it - no error, no warning, nothing in
           the log. The inner records landed and the outer ones did not.

           That is the one thing a transaction exists to prevent, so if you
           have avoided nesting transactions because results looked
           inconsistent, this is why.

           A commit now ends only its own transaction and puts you back in
           the one that contained it, which is what a rollback has always
           done.

           system(1008), the transaction nesting level, is also correct
           again. It only ever counted upwards before - it was raised when a
           transaction began and never lowered when one was committed - so
           after your first commit it could not be used to ask whether you
           were inside a transaction. system(1007) was unaffected and stays
           the reliable test on older releases.

29 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR NOW SIGNS IN TO THEIR OWN ACCOUNT, AND REACHES
           SDSYS BY ELEVATING - NOT AUTOMATICALLY.

           There are two ways into SDSYS and both are deliberate acts.  Start
           SD from a terminal you have already elevated and you land in SDSYS.
           Otherwise you land in your own account, exactly like anybody else,
           and type "logto sdsys" when you want it - which asks for consent at
           that point.

           What changed is the second case.  Being an administrator was enough
           on its own for a moment: any administrator starting SD was sent to
           SDSYS and asked to consent, whether they meant to go there or not.
           Now it is the session that decides, not who you are, so ordinary
           work happens in your own account and administrator rights are
           something you ask for.

           Two consequences worth knowing:

             * Administrators can use ssh again.  An ssh session cannot be
               elevated - the consent dialog has nowhere to appear - so it
               lands in your own account and uses that account's rights.

             * A Windows administrator with no SD account of their own is now
               refused, with "this user is not registered for SD use".  That
               already applied to everybody else; it applies to
               administrators too.  Create an account for them with
               create.account, as you would for anyone.

           Be clear about what the last one is worth: a Windows administrator
           can put themselves back in the group, read the database directly,
           or run as the system account.  It is an explicit step and a record
           of who took it, not a wall.

29 Aug 26  CHANGING AN ACCOUNT'S TIER NOW TELLS YOU WHOSE ACCESS IT BROKE.

           Raising an account's tier can strand a grant.  If b is a standard
           account that alice has been given access to, "modify.account b
           programmer" leaves alice a member of the account's Windows group -
           so nothing looks wrong - while logto refuses her, because a grant
           may go down or sideways and never up.

           modify.account now lists the people whose access it has just made
           useless, and says what to do about it: raise their tier, or revoke
           the grant so the membership does not outlive the access.

           It reports only what that command changed.  A membership that was
           already refused before you ran it - one made outside SD, or left by
           an earlier promotion - is not listed, because it is not this
           command's doing.  Lowering a tier strands nobody and says nothing.

           If the account's group cannot be read, it says so.  Silence means
           "nobody was affected", so the one case where nothing could be
           checked is spelled out rather than looking like good news.

29 Aug 26  ACCESS TO AN ACCOUNT CAN NO LONGER BE GRANTED UPWARDS, AND SDSYS
           CANNOT BE GRANTED AT ALL.

           Accounts have a tier - standard, programmer or administrator - and
           a grant may now go down or sideways, never up.  A programmer may be
           given access to a standard account; a standard user may not be
           given access to a programmer account.  Where that was allowed
           before, standing in the other account gave you its whole verb set,
           so a grant could hand somebody more than their own tier permits.

           Entry to SDSYS is never granted to anybody, whatever their tier.
           It belongs to Windows administrators, who reach it by signing in or
           with LOGTO.

           The rule is applied in three places and they are not equivalent.
           GRANT and MODIFY.ACCOUNT ... ADD refuse at the time you ask, so you
           find out immediately.  LOGTO refuses at the door, and that is the
           one that actually holds - a grant is membership of a Windows group,
           so it can be made outside SD, and an account's tier can be raised
           after a grant was legitimately given.  An existing membership that
           the rule no longer permits therefore stops working at LOGTO rather
           than being quietly honoured.

           REVOKE and MODIFY.ACCOUNT ... DELETE are unaffected.  Taking access
           away is always allowed.

28 Aug 26  A DELETED ACCOUNT'S WINDOWS PROFILE IS NOW CLEARED UP BY ITSELF,
           AND CREATE.ACCOUNT REFUSES A NAME WHOSE OLD PROFILE IS STILL THERE.

           Windows cannot delete a user profile while it still has that
           profile's registry settings loaded, which is the ordinary state
           after somebody has signed in - so DELETE.ACCOUNT could remove the
           account and not its home directory under C:\Users.  Only a restart
           releases it.

           What that cost was invisible and turned up much later.  Windows
           will not put a new profile where one already sits: an account
           created again under the same name was given a DIFFERENT home
           directory - the name with your computer's name added - and
           anything in the old one, ssh keys included, was not found there.
           The symptom people met was ssh refusing a key that had been
           working, days after the deletion, with nothing connecting the two.

           Two changes:

             * DELETE.ACCOUNT now keeps both halves of a profile it cannot
               remove and records it.  THE NEXT TIME THE COMPUTER STARTS, the
               SD service clears the directory and the registry entry
               together, without being asked.  Nothing has to be done by
               hand, and no restart is asked for at the time - it rides the
               next one that happens anyway.

             * CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses to create an account whose profile
               directory is still sitting there, and names the directory,
               rather than quietly giving the account the wrong home.  On a
               machine where SD deleted the old account, restarting and
               trying again is the whole of the answer.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT says which of the three states it reached, and one
           of them still needs a person: if SD could not record the profile
           for clearing, it says so and tells you what to remove.

           There is also a new folder, C:\ProgramData\SD\profile-reclaim.  It
           holds those records and nothing else, it is readable only by
           administrators, and it is normally empty.

28 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW SETS THE PASSWORD FOR THE API AS WELL AS ssh.

           The password you are asked for when an account is created is used
           for both ways in.  Before, it was set only as the Windows password,
           which is what ssh checks; SD keeps its own credential for the API,
           and nothing wrote one until MODIFY.PASSWORD was run.  So a new
           account created with BOTH was told

               SD routes for <name>: ssh and the API.

           and the API then refused it, with a message that could not say why:
           the same refusal covers "no such account" and "you were not granted
           this account", deliberately, so the API does not tell an outsider
           which accounts exist.

           If you have an account that the API refuses and ssh admits, run

               modify.password <name>

           as an administrator and it will work.  Accounts created from now on
           need nothing.

           The two are still separate credentials, and MODIFY.PASSWORD still
           changes SD's without touching the Windows one.  Creation simply
           gives them the same value, which is what typing "the password for
           this account" is meant to mean.

28 Aug 26  ENCRYPT.FIELD IS NO LONGER IN AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VOC.

           The verb was in every administrator account and could not work:
           it points at a program called $CRYPTO which is not part of this
           distribution, so typing it answered

               Unable to load '$CRYPTO' object code at line 1550 of $CPROC

           which names neither the verb you typed nor anything you could act
           on.  "Verb not found" is the honest answer, and that is what you get
           now.  Nothing in the documentation referred to the verb, and field
           encryption was not available through it before.

           An ADMINISTRATOR account's VOC therefore holds 416 records rather
           than 417.  STANDARD (354) and PROGRAMMER (396) are unchanged.

28 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT SAYS WHY A PASSWORD WAS NOT SET.

           It warned that the account was created but the password was not,
           offered a retry, and gave no reason.  The two likeliest causes need
           opposite responses: if the two entries simply did not match, typing
           them again fixes it, but if WINDOWS refused the password for not
           meeting the machine's policy, retyping the same one will fail for
           ever.  Each case now says which it was, and a session that is not
           running elevated is told that retrying cannot help.

28 Aug 26  SEVEN FIXES TO EVERYDAY COMMANDS.

           NO.QUERY IS NOW HONOURED WHEN A FILE LIVES IN THE SYSTEM ACCOUNT.
           "delete.file <name> no.query" could still stop and ask whether to
           delete the file from the system account.  Typed at a terminal that
           was merely surprising; driven from a script it was worse, because
           the question read the script's next line as its answer.  NO.QUERY
           now takes the safe answer - the VOC reference goes, the file in the
           system account stays - and says that is what it did.

           DELETE.FILE NO LONGER ASKS TWICE ABOUT A NAME TYPED IN LOWER CASE.
           The file name is stored upper case and was compared against the name
           you typed, so "delete.file zzwork" asked "OK to delete DATA portion"
           and "OK to delete DICT portion" about a file that was exactly where
           it should be.  The comparison now ignores case.

           DELETE.INDEX ACCEPTS AN INDEX NAME IN LOWER CASE.  "list.index zzak
           f1" found the index and "delete.index zzak f1" answered
           "Unrecognised index name (f1)".  Both now match the way LIST.INDEX
           always did.  A name that really is unknown is still reported exactly
           as you typed it.

           ".D name" FINDS A RECORD SAVED IN LOWER CASE.  A paragraph saved as
           "daily" could be listed with ".L DAILY" and recalled with ".R DAILY"
           but not deleted with ".D DAILY".  It also now says "not found in
           VOC" when there is no such record, instead of silently doing
           something based on whatever it had read last.

           QSELECT SAYS WHICH LIST IT FILLED.  Every successful QSELECT ended
           with "records selected to select list " and no number.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD AND DELETE ARE NOW AUDITED.  Adding or removing
           somebody's access to an account with MODIFY.ACCOUNT wrote no record
           in SD's audit file, while GRANT and REVOKE - which make the same
           change - always did.  Both routes now leave a trail.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER SEEMS TO CONTRADICT ITSELF.  It printed
           "<name> may sign in over ssh only" and then "<name> may sign in over
           ssh and use the API", which cannot both be true of one thing.  They
           were about two different things: how you reach the COMPUTER, and
           which SD ROUTES the account has.  Each line now names its subject.

27 Aug 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW CLEARS THE WINDOWS PROFILE RECORD TOO.

           If you deleted an account and then created another one with the
           SAME NAME, the new account quietly got a DIFFERENT home directory -
           C:\Users\<name> with a suffix added, rather than C:\Users\<name>.
           Anything kept in the old home was then not where the account looked
           for it, and the clearest symptom was ssh: a key that had worked
           stopped being accepted, with a message that said nothing about why.

           Deleting an account removes the Windows account and its profile.
           Removing the profile has two halves - the folder, and a record
           Windows keeps of where that folder is - and when the folder could
           not be removed, which is normal if anybody had used the account
           recently, NEITHER half was removed.

           EITHER HALF ON ITS OWN IS ENOUGH TO CAUSE THIS.  Windows will not
           put a new profile where a record already points, and it will not put
           one where a folder already sits; in both cases it adds a suffix to
           the name instead.  So both halves are now removed in their own
           right, and recreating an account under an old name gives it the home
           directory you would expect.

           THE FOLDER OFTEN CANNOT BE REMOVED, AND THAT IS WINDOWS RATHER THAN
           SD.  If the account has ever signed in, Windows keeps its registry
           settings mounted afterwards, which locks a file inside the folder;
           while that lasts the folder cannot be deleted or even renamed, by
           anybody, however privileged.  Only restarting the computer releases
           it.

           So DELETE.ACCOUNT says which half was left and what to do about it,
           and neither warning is cosmetic: until that half is cleared, the
           next account of the same name will still be pushed elsewhere.  For
           an account that never signed in there is nothing mounted and both
           halves go cleanly.

27 Aug 26  TWO EDITOR AND TERMINAL FIXES.

           MICRO NO LONGER REPORTS "Permission denied" WHEN YOU SAVE.  Every
           save from an ordinary account showed

               Permission denied. Save with sudo not supported on Windows

           in red at the bottom of the screen.  YOUR WORK WAS ACTUALLY SAVED
           EVERY TIME - the message was wrong, not the save - but there was no
           way to know that from the screen.

           The cause was where micro kept its settings.  It was told to use a
           directory under Program Files, which ordinary accounts can read and
           not write, and micro writes there whenever it saves.  It now uses
           one in your own home directory instead:

               C:\Users\<you>\.micro

           created for you the first time you run "micro", with SD's BASIC
           syntax colouring copied into it.  Nothing to set up, and your micro
           settings now persist between sessions.  If your account has no
           writable home directory, SD uses a temporary one instead, and the
           editor still works.

           An elevated session was never affected, which is why this went
           unnoticed at first.  The EDIT verb - Microsoft Edit - never had the
           problem at all.

           TERM DEFAULT NOW RESTORES 120 x 36, not 20 x 24.  "term default"
           was setting the page to the smallest size SD allows, which wrapped
           every standard report.  It now sets the documented default width and
           depth, the same size a session starts with when nothing else tells
           it otherwise.

27 Aug 26  AN ACCOUNT'S TIER CAN BE CHANGED AFTER IT IS CREATED, AND THERE IS
           A FOURTH ONE: SUSPENDED.

           Until now the tier was fixed when the account was made.
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT can now move it in either direction:

               modify.account don programmer both
               modify.account ann administrator
               modify.account bob suspended
               modify.account bob standard

           The keyword says what the account IS, the same way SSH, API, BOTH
           and NONE do, and the first three words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's.

           SUSPENDED DENIES ACCESS WITHOUT CHANGING ANYTHING ELSE.  It is for
           an account that should stop working for a while - somebody on
           leave, a login being investigated - and it is refused at all three
           ways in:

               ssh or the console      Account BOB is suspended
               logto from another      Account BOB is suspended
               the API                 User not allowed in requested account

           Nothing is taken away on Windows and the account's VOC is left
           exactly as it is, so lifting a suspension puts the account back as
           it was with nothing to remember.  The tier it displaced is kept, and
           LIST ACCOUNTS shows both.

           THERE IS NO "RESUME" KEYWORD.  Coming back always names the tier the
           account is to have:

               modify.account bob programmer

           and no intermediate step is ever needed - an account can go
           programmer, standard, suspended and back to any of them directly.

           THE VOC CHANGES AT ONCE, not at the next login.  A downgrade removes
           the verbs the lower tier does not get and an upgrade copies them in,
           and the verb says how many of each:

               Account ANN is now STANDARD
               VOC: 0 records added, 41 removed, 1 left alone

           "LEFT ALONE" IS THE ONE TO READ.  A record is only removed if it is
           still exactly what SD put there.  Anything edited or replaced in the
           account is counted and kept, because it is somebody's work under a
           verb's name rather than the verb.

           LEAVING ADMINISTRATOR TAKES AWAY MORE THAN THE VERBS, and one part
           has to be spelt out:

               the Windows Administrators group    removed
               the OS.USERS record                 removed
               ssh and the API                     you say

           The first two were the account's BECAUSE it was an administrator.
           ssh and the API are a rule for an administrator and a setting for
           everybody else, so the command has to say which:

               :modify.account don programmer
               Say what remote access DON is to have: ssh, api, both or none
               :modify.account don programmer both
               DON may sign in over ssh and use the API
               Account DON is now PROGRAMMER

           WHAT IS REFUSED:

             - Suspending your own account, or the one you are standing in.
             - Making a group account an ADMINISTRATOR; there is no single
               person behind it to put in a Windows group.
             - SDSYS, which MODIFY.ACCOUNT has never accepted.

           AN ELEVATED SESSION CAN STILL "LOGTO" A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT.  That is
           deliberate: looking at a suspended account is the usual reason to
           have one, and anyone elevated could lift the suspension anyway.  A
           suspension denies the account's own user.

           LIST ACCOUNTS NOW SHOWS THE TIER, and this changes its default
           columns - the tier replaces the Windows group, which was always
           "sdu_" or "sdg_" followed by the account name and told you nothing
           the id did not.  Both are still there by name:

               list accounts
               list accounts path descr group tier
               list accounts tier prior.tier

27 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR NO LONGER HAS TO ELEVATE TO REACH THE OPERATING
           SYSTEM.

           An SD account made with the ADMINISTRATOR tier is now added to the
           SD system file OS.USERS as it is created, with both fields set to
           "yes".  That is the list SD reads to decide who may use the shell
           and who may run a program outside SD, so from an ordinary,
           unelevated session an administrator can now use:

               sh  and  !                the shell
               OS.EXECUTE                from a BASIC program
               edit  and  micro          the full-screen editors

           ssh and the API already worked this way - an administrator has had
           both since 21 Aug and no keyword can take either away.  OS.EXECUTE
           was the one route still asking for elevation, and on a fresh
           install OS.USERS was empty, so the person who installed SD was
           refused the editors in their own account.

           IT APPLIES TO THE ACCOUNT THE INSTALLER MAKES FOR YOU, which is an
           ADMINISTRATOR account, so this is the ordinary case rather than a
           special one.

           FOR EVERY OTHER TIER IT IS A GRANT, and there are now keywords
           for it:

               create.account user ann programmer sh-on
               create.account user bob programmer os-on
               modify.account ann os-on
               modify.account ann sh-off

           sh-on and os-on on CREATE.ACCOUNT; sh-on, sh-off, os-on and os-off
           on MODIFY.ACCOUNT.  They are four switches over two fields rather
           than four names for one state, so sh-off leaves OS.EXECUTE alone.
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT needs an elevated session, as it always has - you
           elevate to grant somebody the right not to have to.

           FOR AN ADMINISTRATOR ALL THREE ROUTES ARE A RULE, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           refuses to change any of them:

               :modify.account don os-off
               don is an administrator and always reaches the operating system

           ssh and the API have refused this since 21 Aug; the operating
           system now refuses it too, in both directions.  The whole purpose of
           the ADMINISTRATOR tier is that it grants unlimited access.

           The record itself is ordinary data and can also be edited by hand:

               logto sdsys
               ed os.users don

           Field 1 is SH and field 2 is OS.EXECUTE, each "yes" or anything
           else.  The name is the WINDOWS login name, not the SD account name,
           because the permission belongs to the person and does not change
           when they LOGTO somewhere else.

           AN ACCOUNT OF ANY OTHER TIER IS UNCHANGED: no record, and no
           operating system, until an administrator adds one.  An elevated
           session still passes whatever the file says, so an empty OS.USERS
           cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.

           AND DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW TAKES THE RECORD AWAY AGAIN, but only when
           it is deleting the Windows account itself.  Deleting an SD account
           whose Windows login stays behind leaves the person's permission
           alone, which is the right way round for a list keyed by person.

26 Aug 26  SYSTEM(1010) SAID "Linux".  IT NOW SAYS "Windows".

           A BASIC program asking SD what platform it was running on was told
           Linux, on Windows.  The name was inherited from the Linux original,
           where it is correct, and never changed - the same oversight as
           SYSTEM(91), which was fixed on 17 Aug.

           IT ALSO CHANGES A COMPILER TOKEN.  The BASIC compiler defines a
           token from this name for use with $IFDEF, so what used to be
           SD.LINUX is now SD.WINDOWS.  Nothing shipped with SD tests either
           name, so no supplied program compiles differently - but if you have
           written "$IFDEF SD.LINUX" against this port, it is now SD.WINDOWS,
           which is the name you would have expected in the first place.

           SYSTEM(91) - "is this Windows" - has answered 1 since 17 Aug and is
           the one to use for a straight platform test.

26 Aug 26  CONFIG() WITH A LONG NAME NO LONGER STOPS YOUR PROGRAM.

           Configuration parameter names are at most eight characters.  Asking
           for a longer one - CONFIG("NOSUCHKEY") is nine - used to abort the
           program with "Data cannot be converted to a string", which named
           nothing to do with the real problem.

           It now does what an unknown eight-character name has always done:
           returns an empty string and sets STATUS() to 1004, item not found.
           A name that is too long is a name that does not exist, and you can
           now test for it instead of guarding against it.

           Names are also CASE SENSITIVE and upper case - CONFIG("NUMLOCKS")
           answers, CONFIG("numlocks") does not.  That has not changed.

26 Aug 26  SET.SOCKET.MODE CAN NOW TURN KEEP-ALIVE OFF.

           SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE, 0) returned 1, for
           success, and switched keep-alive ON.  The value you passed was read
           and then discarded.  SOCKET.INFO(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE) was
           telling the truth all along, so the two disagreed.

           The setting is now whatever you asked for.  If you have code that
           worked around this by not calling it, the workaround is no longer
           needed.

26 Aug 26  CLEAR SCREEN AND CURSOR POSITIONING NOW WORK IN A POWERSHELL
           WINDOW.

           They already worked from cmd and in Windows Terminal.  Started from
           PowerShell, "@(-1)" and "@(x,y)" did nothing at all - output ran
           down the screen as though the terminal had no cursor control.

           WHY, AND IT WAS NEVER ABOUT POWERSHELL ITSELF.  The default terminal
           type is "windows", a clone of xterm, so SD draws with ANSI escape
           sequences.  A Windows console only acts on those when the flag
           ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING is set on it, and that flag
           belongs to the WINDOW rather than to the program running in it.
           cmd.exe sets it; Windows Terminal is built around it; Windows
           PowerShell 5.1 does not set it.  SD had no opinion of its own - it
           used whatever its launcher happened to leave behind.

           SD NOW TURNS IT ON FOR ITSELF at start-up, and puts it back the way
           it found it on the way out.  Nothing to configure, and no difference
           between the three windows any more.

           This does not apply to a session over ssh, which was never affected
           - your ssh client interprets the escapes at your end.

26 Aug 26  MISTYPING THE PASSWORD AT THE END OF AN INSTALL NO LONGER LEAVES
           THE ACCOUNT WITHOUT ONE.

           The installer finishes by opening SD so you can set a password.
           Typing the two entries differently used to end it there - "Passwords
           do not match.  Password not changed." - and the install carried on
           to its check with no password set.  ONE MISTYPE, ONE CHANCE.

           You now get three, and it tells you which attempt you are on.

           AND IT NOW TELLS YOU WHAT ENTER DOES BEFORE YOU PRESS IT.  The
           prompt is preceded by:

               Press Enter on an empty line to set no password for now.
               The account then works only at this computer, from a
               session run as administrator; ssh and the SD API will
               refuse to connect until a password is set.  SD asks
               again the first time you open the account.

           Changing a password you already have says the other thing that
           is true there instead: Enter leaves it unchanged.

           THAT IS THE FIRST LINE ONLY, and the prompts look alike, so it is
           worth saying: pressing Enter at "Repeat new password" is not
           declining.  It simply fails to match and costs one of the three
           attempts.

           PRESSING ENTER ON AN EMPTY LINE DECLINES AT ONCE and is not counted
           as an attempt.  Declining is a decision; being re-asked would turn
           it into an argument.

           NOTHING WAS EVER LOST BY IT.  SD asks again the first time you open
           the account, and that prompt is bounded at three now as well.

26 Aug 26  CONFIG GPL AND CONFIG CONTRIB WORK AGAIN.

           Both did nothing on Windows, and for two reasons either of which
           was enough on its own:

           * they ran an external pager, "less", which is not on a Windows
             machine and which SD does not install; and
           * they reached it through "!", the shell escape, so an ordinary
             user was refused before that even mattered - with a message
             about the operating system shell that named neither CONFIG nor
             the licence.

           SHOWING A LICENCE SHOULD NOT REQUIRE SHELL PERMISSION, so neither
           command shells out now.  The text is held inside SD as a record in
           the SDSYS VOC and printed with SD's own pagination, which works at
           the console and over ssh alike and needs nothing installed.

           THE LICENCE FILE STILL SHIPS, in the data tree as before.  That is
           deliberate rather than left over: SD is GPL-3 and that file is the
           only copy of the licence in the distribution, so it has to be
           readable by somebody who has not started SD.

           AND THE LICENCE TEXT NAMED A LICENCE SD DOES NOT USE.  It said the
           install and delete scripts were under the Blue Oak Model License
           v1.0.0.  Those were the Linux shell scripts, which this port
           replaced entirely; nothing in SD Core carries that licence and no
           source file ever mentioned it.  The line is gone.

           What is left: SD, including the API, is GPL v3.0, and anything not
           marked otherwise is GPL v3.0.

26 Aug 26  THERE ARE FULL-SCREEN EDITORS AGAIN: EDIT AND MICRO.

           Two verbs, two editors, and they work the same way.  Each opens a
           record in a terminal editor, saves it back, and offers to compile
           and catalogue a BP record - the loop MICRO had before it was
           removed on 17 Aug.

               edit  bp myprog          Microsoft Edit
               micro bp myprog          micro
               edit  dict customers name

           WHO HAS THEM, AND IT IS TWO THINGS RATHER THAN ONE.

           * THE VERB: programmer and administrator accounts get both.  A
             standard account gets neither, for the same reason it has no ED -
             it is not a development account.
           * OS.EXECUTE PERMISSION: an editor runs outside SD, so reaching one
             is reaching the operating system.  That is field 2 of your record
             in the SD system file os.users - the same field that governs
             OS.EXECUTE from inside a program - and only an administrator can
             change it.

           An elevated session passes on its own, as it does for SH, so an
           empty list cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.  A
           missing record means no.

           IF YOU HAVE THE VERB AND NOT THE PERMISSION you are told so by name
           and told what to ask for, rather than the command appearing to do
           nothing.  A session with no terminal - an API session, or a piped
           script - is refused first and separately: there is nowhere to draw
           a full screen.

           THEY WORK OVER ssh, which is the point of a terminal editor.  But
           an ssh session can never be elevated, so an administrator arriving
           that way needs an os.users entry the same as anybody else.

           ONLY MICRO HIGHLIGHTS SD BASIC.  Microsoft Edit has no syntax
           highlighting, and that is the one real difference between the two
           verbs.  MICRO colours statements, reserved words, intrinsic
           functions, @variables, $directives, labels, strings, numbers and
           comments.

           It applies to a BP record and to nothing else - SD names the
           working copy so micro can recognise the language, and a record
           edited out of any other file is plain text.

           The word lists are GENERATED FROM THE COMPILER's own tables - 218
           statements, 37 reserved words and 176 intrinsic functions - so the
           highlighting cannot drift from the language.  If a name you expect
           is not coloured, that is worth reporting.

           NOTHING IS INSTALLED INTO YOUR PROFILE.  SD ships the rules with
           itself and points micro at them, so they work for every account on
           the machine - including the ones that cannot log in to Windows and
           so have no profile to put them in.

           TO GRANT IT, from an elevated session:

               logto sdsys
               ed os.users don

           Two lines and nothing else: field 1 is SH, field 2 is OS.EX.  "yes"
           in field 2 is what the editors need.  The name is the WINDOWS login
           name, not the SD account name, and the change takes effect on that
           person's next command rather than at their next login.

           WHAT THE EDITORS ARE GOOD FOR: BASIC source, VOC records and simple
           dictionary records - anything whose content is lines of text.  SD
           writes one field per line, so moving between fields is moving
           between lines.

           MULTIVALUES AND SUBVALUES ARE TYPEABLE, AND THIS IS NEW.  Value
           and subvalue marks are control characters an editor cannot show, so
           each has a token you type instead:

               ~~   a value mark
               ~`   a subvalue mark
               ~!   a text mark
               ~-   a literal ~, where one would otherwise be misread
               ~,   a literal , where one would otherwise be a separator

           EVERY TOKEN IS ~ AND ONE MORE CHARACTER, and ~ is the only escape
           character.  SD converts marks to tokens on the way into the editor
           and back again on the way out.  SMITH~~JONES~~BROWN is a
           three-value field; RED~`BLUE~~GREEN is two values, the first with
           two subvalues.

           * MARKS IN A ROW ARE SEPARATED BY A COMMA.  Written token against
             token a run of marks cannot be read, so a text mark, a text mark
             and a value mark, one after another, is:

                 ~!,~!,~~

             Type the comma yourself when you enter marks in a row.  It is a
             separator and not data, which is why a literal comma standing in
             exactly that position - between two marks - is written ~,.
           * THE CONVERSION IS LOSSLESS.  No record is refused and none is
             mangled, whatever it contains.  SD writes a tilde as ~- only
             where the character after it would otherwise make the pair look
             like a token - another ~, a backtick, a !, a hyphen, a comma, or
             a mark.  EVERYWHERE ELSE A TILDE IS LEFT ALONE, so ordinary text
             reads exactly as you wrote it and a~b is still a~b.  Type ~-
             yourself when you want a literal ~ in one of those positions.
           * A compiled dictionary record is truncated to its first 15 fields
             while you edit it and recompiled with CD when you save, which is
             what MICRO always did.

           ***GIVE THESE VERBS ONLY TO PEOPLE YOU TRUST.***  An editor can
           write anywhere its user can write.  It opens the record you named,
           but nothing then stops the person opening any other file on the
           machine their Windows account may open, inside the SD data tree or
           outside it.  That is not a hole in SD - it is what an editor is -
           and it is why these verbs are behind OS.EXECUTE permission and not
           merely behind the programmer tier.

           ED IS UNCHANGED and is still there.  If you preferred the line
           editor, nothing has been taken away.  What HAS changed is that
           "EDIT" used to be a second name for ED, so if you have been typing
           EDIT to get the line editor you will now get a full screen.

           THE EDITOR IS FOUND, NOT ASSUMED.  Current Windows builds carry
           edit.exe already; micro never ships with Windows.  The installer
           installs whichever is missing, with winget, and if that cannot be
           done - no winget, no network, a policy in the way - the install
           still succeeds and says so in
           C:\ProgramData\SD\install-editors.log.  The verb then refuses
           with the command that installs it rather than appearing to do
           nothing.  To do it by hand, from an elevated prompt:

               winget install -e --id Microsoft.Edit  --scope machine
               winget install -e --id zyedidia.micro  --scope machine

           WHY THE SCOPE MATTERS: without it winget installs into the profile
           of whoever ran it, and accounts SD creates cannot log in to Windows
           at all, so a per-user copy is one they can never reach.

           WHAT IT MEANS FOR CONTAINMENT, because 17 Aug removed MICRO on
           exactly this ground.  An editor can open any file the person
           running it is allowed to open, so these verbs widen what a
           programmer can reach beyond SD's own files.  Neither is a shell -
           neither editor can run a command - and neither is available to
           standard accounts or over the API.  The line SD draws is the
           account tier, not the OS.USERS list that governs SH.

26 Aug 26  THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLER NO LONGER OFFERS TO OPEN THE API PORT.

           A stand-alone install opens no network port at all - it writes an
           sd.conf with no APIPORT line, so SD never creates the socket.  The
           installer's "Remote access" page was nevertheless still showing the
           option "Let other computers on your network connect to the SD API
           (port 4243)".

           Ticking it did nothing, and nothing was ever left exposed by it:
           the firewall step already checked the install mode and stopped.  But
           the option appeared two screens after the mode page had said that no
           port would be opened, which is a contradiction whether or not it has
           any effect.  It is now hidden on a stand-alone install, the same way
           the matching ssh option already was.

           A full installation is unchanged and still offers both.

25 Aug 26  VIRTUAL FILE SYSTEM SUPPORT IS GONE FROM THE LANGUAGE TOO.

           SD has never been able to open a virtual file system.  Nothing in
           the file-opening code has ever recognised a "VFS:" pathname.  What
           the language carried was the outline of one - a file type FTYPE
           would report, three error numbers, and a set of names in the SYSCOM
           include files - and none of it could be reached.  A VOC F-pointer
           written as "VFS:something" was reported as a virtual file system,
           passed the name resolver, and then failed to open with an unrelated
           error.

           ALL OF IT HAS BEEN REMOVED, so the language no longer offers a
           feature it cannot perform.

           WHAT THIS CHANGES FOR YOU.  These names are no longer defined, and
           a program that mentions one will no longer compile:

              FL$TYPE.VFS            SYSCOM KEYS.H
              ER$VFS.NAME            SYSCOM ERR.H
              ER$VFS.CLASS           SYSCOM ERR.H
              ER$VFS.NGLBL           SYSCOM ERR.H

           FTYPE no longer returns "VFS" for a "VFS:" pathname - it treats it
           as an ordinary pathname, which will not open either.  Error numbers
           3038, 3039 and 3040 are retired and will not be given a new meaning.

           IF ONE OF YOUR PROGRAMS REFERS TO ANY OF THESE, it was testing for
           a state SD could not reach, and the test can be deleted.

           TWO MORE UNREACHABLE PIECES WENT WITH IT.  SD used to install a
           program called _EXTENDLIST into the SDSYS GPL.BP file and load it
           at every start-up; it existed only to serve a virtual file system,
           and nothing has ever called it.  It is gone.  And the debugger no
           longer offers "(Networked)" as a file type - remote files over
           SDNet were removed earlier in this port, so no file could report
           it.

25 Aug 26  THE FIVE PROGRAMS SD SHIPPED INTO THE SDSYS BP FILE ARE GONE.

           SD used to install PCL and PCL.GRID (printer control), U0032 and
           U50BB (user exits) and VFS.CLS (a template class module) into the
           SDSYS account's BP file.  None of them is needed in this version,
           and SD does not support virtual file systems, so the template was
           describing something you cannot use.  PCL is unaffected as a
           printer feature - the PCL keyword and the catalogued PCL routine
           are both still there; what has gone is a second, older copy of the
           source sitting in BP.

           SD NOW SHIPS NOTHING INTO THE SDSYS BP FILE.  It is created empty
           and is yours to use.

           AND BECAUSE OF THAT, BP AND ITS COMPILED OBJECTS ARE NOW PRESERVED
           WHEN YOU UPGRADE, alongside your accounts, the private catalogue
           and the rest of your own data.  Anything you have written into the
           SDSYS BP file, and anything you have compiled from it, survives an
           upgrade untouched.

25 Aug 26  LETTING OTHER COMPUTERS REACH THE SD API IS NOW OFF UNTIL YOU
           ASK FOR IT.

           The installer offers two remote-access options: one for ssh and one
           for the SD API.  The ssh one has always been off unless you tick
           it.  The API one used to be ON unless you cleared it, so an
           installation left at its defaults opened port 4243 to your local
           network.

           BOTH ARE NOW OFF UNLESS YOU TICK THEM.  On a default installation
           the SD API can be reached from this computer only.

           NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE API CHANGED.  It is still switched on, still
           listening on port 4243, and programs running on this computer
           connect to it exactly as before.  What changed is the firewall rule
           the installer leaves behind.

           IF YOU WANT A CLIENT ON ANOTHER COMPUTER, tick "Let other computers
           on your network connect to the SD API" during the install.  If you
           have already installed and need it afterwards, run this from an
           elevated PowerShell prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\api-firewall.ps1" -Open

25 Aug 26  THERE IS NOW A STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION, FOR ONE PERSON AT ONE
           COMPUTER.

           The installer asks which kind you want, on a new page just after
           the welcome page, and describes both in full before you choose.
           You are only asked on a first install: upgrading keeps whatever
           the computer already is.

           CHOOSE THE FULL INSTALLATION if more than one person will use SD,
           or if a program needs to connect to it.  It is what the installer
           has always done and it is still the default.

           CHOOSE THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION to learn SD or try some code
           out on your own machine.  It is not meant to be a production
           system.  On it:

             - No ssh server is installed and no ssh configuration is
               changed.  If you already have an ssh server, of any make, SD
               leaves it alone.  scp and sftp GO ON WORKING - on a full
               installation they stop working for everyone.
             - No network port is opened.  The SD API is switched off in
               sd.conf, so SD is not listening on anything and there is no
               firewall rule to open.
             - There is one SD account and it is yours.  You use SD by
               typing  sd  at this computer.
             - create.account user  is refused, and says why.  The Windows
               account it would make is denied the console and Remote
               Desktop because it is meant to arrive over ssh, so with no ssh
               server it could sign in nowhere at all.
             - create.account group  still works, and is how you keep
               separate work separate.  Use  logto <name>  to move into one,
               from a session run as administrator.  No Windows account and
               no sign-in is involved.

           NOTHING ELSE IS CUT DOWN: same SD, same language, same database,
           same commands.  What is missing is the ways in from somewhere
           else.

           TO CHANGE YOUR MIND you have to uninstall and install again.
           Nothing inside SD converts one into the other, and deleting the
           marker file SD leaves in sdsys does not do it either - no ssh
           server would be installed and the API would still be off.

25 Aug 26  INSTALLING A NEW RELEASE OVER AN EXISTING ONE NOW UPDATES YOUR
           DATABASE.  UNTIL NOW IT SILENTLY DID NOT.

           Your database lives in C:\ProgramData\SD, and the installer used to
           leave it completely alone if it was already there.  That protected
           your data, but it also meant a new release's programs and messages
           never reached a computer that already had SD - so a fix could be
           released and you would not get it, with nothing to say so.

           NOW THE INSTALLER REPLACES THE PARTS SD SHIPS AND LEAVES YOUR OWN
           ALONE.  Replaced: the catalogue and compiled programs, the BASIC
           source, the messages, the include records, the VOC templates, the
           library routines, the SDSYS BP programs, terminfo, the licence and
           the contributor list.

           KEPT, AND NOT TOUCHED: your accounts and their passwords, the
           private catalogue, which Windows users are linked to which SD
           accounts, the commands each account may run, your print queue and
           held reports, everything under your own accounts, and sd.conf.
           Anything SD created while it was running - your VOC included - is
           left exactly as it is.

           THE DICTIONARIES ARE BROUGHT UP TO DATE FOR YOU.  Upgrading now
           reapplies the dictionary definitions this release ships, so a field
           added since your last install is recognised.  It ADDS AND UPDATES
           the entries SD ships and leaves alone any you added yourself - if
           you have written your own dictionary items, they survive.  If that
           step cannot run, the installer says so at the end rather than
           finishing quietly, and upgrade-dicts.log in C:\ProgramData\SD says
           what happened.

           ONE THING YOU STILL DO YOURSELF.  Run  update.accounts  in each
           account after upgrading, to bring that account's VOC up to date
           with the new release.  That is what the verb is for, and this
           changelog has been saying so for years.

           If you would rather start clean, uninstalling still offers to
           remove the database and still defaults to keeping it.

25 Aug 26  THE CHANGELOG - THIS FILE - HAS MOVED TO
           C:\Program Files\SD\changelog.

           It used to be installed into the database, which the installer
           never overwrote.  So your copy of it was frozen on the day you
           first installed SD, and could never tell you about anything that
           changed afterwards - in the one file whose entire job is telling
           you what changed.

           In its new home it is replaced every time you install a new
           release, and removed when you uninstall.  Nothing else about it
           changes.  The old frozen copy in the database is removed for you
           when you upgrade, so there is only ever one.

25 Aug 26  SD NOW REFUSES TO INSTALL IF THIS COMPUTER HAS A DIFFERENT ssh
           SERVER, OR IF SOMEBODY HAS ALREADY CONFIGURED THE WINDOWS ONE.

           Accounts SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing else, and SD
           configures the ssh server so that those accounts land in SD and
           cannot get a command prompt.  SD can only promise that about a
           server it installed and configured itself.

           SO THE INSTALLER NOW CHECKS FIRST, BEFORE IT CHANGES ANYTHING,
           and stops with an explanation if it finds:

              * another ssh server using port 22, or
              * an ssh service that is not part of Windows, installed even
                if it is not running, or
              * Windows' own ssh server with settings somebody has already
                changed.

           It names what it found - the service and where it is, or the
           setting that was added - so you can decide what to do.  Nothing on
           the computer is changed when it refuses.

           WHY IT WAS WORTH DOING.  Until now SD asked only whether Windows'
           own ssh server was present.  On a computer running a different one
           it decided there was no server at all, installed Windows' server,
           and that server could not use port 22 because the other one already
           had it.  SD's whole sign-in path was broken and the install said
           nothing.

           IF YOU HIT THIS.  Remove the other ssh server, or put this
           computer's ssh configuration back the way Windows shipped it, and
           run the installer again.

25 Aug 26  THE "Limit ssh" CHECKBOX IS GONE.  IT IS NOW A STATEMENT.

           SD limits ssh to SD users and administrators and puts every ssh
           session straight into SD.  That has been ticked by default for some
           time and was not really meant to be turned off - so showing it as a
           checkbox suggested a choice that was not one.

           IT IS NOW DESCRIBED ON THE "Before you install" page instead,
           alongside the other things SD does without asking, including the
           cost said plainly: scp and sftp stop working for everyone on the
           computer, because the command is forced and there is no subsystem
           left to run.  Remote-control tools that copy files are unaffected,
           and so are the console and Remote Desktop.

           NOTHING ABOUT THE BEHAVIOUR CHANGED - only how it is presented, and
           that it can no longer be declined.  The reason it can no longer be
           declined is the change above: the computer it existed for, the one
           with somebody else's ssh server, is now turned away before the
           install starts.

           Your existing sshd_config is still kept as sshd_config.before-sd,
           and uninstalling SD still puts it back.

25 Aug 26  SECURITY: WHEN SD INSTALLED ssh FOR YOU, PORT 22 WAS LEFT OPEN
           TO YOUR NETWORK EVEN IF YOU DID NOT ASK FOR THAT.

           SD installs the OpenSSH server whenever the machine does not
           already have one, because SD accounts sign in over ssh and
           nothing else.  Whether OTHER computers may then reach it is the
           tasks-page checkbox "Let other computers on your network connect
           to this one over ssh (port 22)", which is OFF unless you tick it.

           WHEN IT WAS OFF, THE INSTALLER WAS SUPPOSED TO NARROW THE
           FIREWALL RULE TO THIS MACHINE ONLY.  IT NEVER MANAGED TO.  The
           step failed every single time it ran, and the installer said so
           on its closing page:

              Setting who may reach ssh FAILED, and Windows' own default
              is in force - port 22 open to your local network.

           The recovery command it offered failed for the same reason, so
           there was no way to reach the intended setting.

           WHO IS AFFECTED.  Only machines where SD installed ssh itself.
           If you already had an ssh server before installing SD, SD does
           not touch its firewall rule and never did - none of this applies.

           WHAT TO DO IF YOU SAW THAT MESSAGE.  Install this build, or run
           this once from an elevated prompt:

              powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\ssh-firewall.ps1" -Installed -Restrict

           It now reports "ssh is reachable FROM THIS MACHINE ONLY".  You
           can check the setting at any time with -Show instead, or look at
           the rule "OpenSSH SSH Server (sshd)" in wf.msc.

           CONNECTING TO SD ON YOUR OWN MACHINE IS UNAFFECTED, over IPv4
           and IPv6 alike.  Windows does not filter traffic that never
           leaves the machine, so "ssh localhost" keeps working whichever
           way the setting is left.

24 Aug 26  THE "Limit ssh" WIZARD OPTION NOW WARNS FIRST, PROMISES SECOND.

           If you are installing SD on a machine that already uses ssh for
           anything - a shell for administrators, scp, sftp - the "Limit
           ssh" checkbox on the tasks page will break those uses.  It has
           always done that; the description now leads with the warning
           instead of finishing with it in a parenthesis:

              old:   Limit ssh to SD users and administrators, and put
                     every ssh session straight into SD (disables scp and
                     sftp)
              new:   DISABLES scp and sftp for everyone: puts every ssh
                     session straight into SD, and limits ssh to SD users
                     and administrators

           NOTHING ELSE CHANGES.  The task is still ticked by default; it
           can still be UNticked in the wizard; the behaviour when it is
           ticked is identical.  If sshd_config already has an AllowGroups,
           AllowUsers, DenyGroups or DenyUsers line, the install still
           refuses to touch it (allow-ssh-groups.ps1's second refusal).

24 Aug 26  SDSYS'S BP HAS BEEN TRIMMED TO ITS UTILITY PROGRAMS.

           Fifteen developer test programs that were installed alongside
           SDSYS's utility programs no longer ship - they were harmless but
           had no business being on an end user's disk.  What is left in
           SDSYS's BP is the shape the product ships with:

              PCL, PCL.GRID          printer control (Ladybridge)
              U0032, U50BB           the two user exits (0032, 50BB)
              VFS.CLS                template VFS class module

           The removed programs were BIGSTR_TEST, MSGTEST, PCODE_LIST,
           SDTEST_V8, SD_ENCRYPT, SD_ENCRYPT_B64, SD_ENCRYPT_EXT, SD_EXT,
           TEST.THEN.ELSE, TESTCRED, TESTSCRAM, TESTSDCLI, TESTSZ, pref_t,
           sdTests and tilde_test.  If you were using one of these
           deliberately - none of them are documented product functions -
           they are still in the source repository under
           sdb_ai/sd64/sdsys/bp/ up to this commit, and TESTSDCLI (kept
           because the SCRAM verifier drives it) is now at
           sdb_ai/sd64/gplbld/testsdcli.bp.

24 Aug 26  "sd <command>" NO LONGER ASKS YOU TO SET A PASSWORD.

           Running a single command - "sd COUNT VOC" - is a batch operation,
           not a session you are signing in to, so it now runs the command and
           exits instead of stopping to ask for a password.

           * NOTHING ELSE CHANGES.  Plain "sd", with no command, still asks an
             administrator whose account has no password to set one, so the
             account can be reached from the API or from another machine.
             An ssh session is unaffected: it arrives as plain "sd".
           * IT WAS ALSO A HANG.  At a console with nobody watching - a script
             or a scheduled command run in a window - the prompt had nothing to
             read and the command sat there for ever.  It now runs.
           * A scheduled task was never affected and is not changed: it has no
             terminal, so it was never asked.
           * THE INSTALLER STILL ASKS, and asks the same way it always has -
             one window, straight after setup, closing by itself when you are
             done.  It now does it by running MODIFY.PASSWORD rather than by
             relying on the login prompt, so the wording is the product's own:
             "Account <name> has no password set.  Setting the first one."
             Pressing Enter without typing one still declines, as before.


24 Aug 26  SD'S SYSTEM DIRECTORIES ARE NOW READ-ONLY TO ORDINARY SD USERS.

           Seven parts of the SD data area could be changed by anyone in the
           "sdusers" group.  They are now readable by everyone who needs them
           and writable only by an administrator:

               sdsys\accounts    the register of accounts
               sdsys\$map        sdsys\messages    sdsys\newvoc
               sdsys\bp          sdsys\cat         SD's own programs
               sd.conf           the configuration read at start-up

           * NOTHING AN ORDINARY USER DOES NEEDS TO WRITE THEM, which was
             measured on a real session before the change was made - fifteen
             commands including the spooler and saved lists, and a phantom.
           * THE COMMANDS THAT DO WRITE THEM ARE ALREADY ADMINISTRATOR
             COMMANDS and are unaffected: CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT,
             CATALOG and CONFIG.  Each is run from an elevated session, which
             still has full access.
           * sdsys\$ipc IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED.  Every session writes to
             it, and it is how a PHANTOM is given its command.
           * A new install applies this.  An existing installation is not
             changed, because the installer never overwrites a data area that
             is already there.  To apply it to one you already have, run this
             once from an elevated PowerShell prompt, for each of the seven:

                 powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\secure-sysdirs.ps1"
                     -Path "C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\accounts"

           This follows the same reasoning as the global catalogue and the
           pcode library, which were locked in the same way earlier.


24 Aug 26  MODIFY HAS BEEN REMOVED FROM SD CORE.

           MODIFY, the full-screen record editor inherited from OpenQM, is no
           longer part of the product.  No account of any tier receives it.
           ED remains, and is the way to edit a record.

           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT AND MODIFY.PASSWORD ARE NOT AFFECTED.  They are
             different verbs with different programs behind them, and both
             remain administrator commands.
           * A programmer account's VOC drops from 396 records to 395, and an
             administrator's from 417 to 416.  A standard account is
             unchanged at 354, because MODIFY was already withheld from it.
           * An account created before this update keeps whatever was in its
             VOC at the time - SD only ever adds records at an update, never
             removes them.


24 Aug 26  THREE MORE ERROR CODES HAVE MESSAGE TEXT, and the OPGEN
           developer tool is gone.
           * Errors 4100 (Undefined Server Error), 4101 (Invalid File
             Number) and -10303 (a SCRAM primitive failed or was given bad
             input) had no message text.  They have it now, so ERRTEXT and
             error displays name them instead of showing a bare number.
           * GPL.BP/OPGEN has been removed.  The 13 Aug entry below recorded
             that it could not be run on an installed system: it generates
             GPL.BP/OPCODES.H from the C header opcodes.h, and no install
             carries C source.  Nothing in SD calls it and no VOC pointed at
             it.  OPCODES.H is unaffected and still ships; it is regenerated
             from the repository by gplbld/gen_includes.py, which already
             produced SYSCOM/ERR.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H and GPL.BP/REVSTAMP.H.
           * Regenerating OPCODES.H dropped OP.SDPYOBJ, left behind when
             embedded Python was removed on 13 Aug.  Nothing referred to it.


24 Aug 26  THE THREE-TIER SPLIT IS SETTLED.  What each account gets on
           creation now reflects the whole split rather than a starter cut.

           STANDARD accounts get 77 verbs - what an application needs and no
           more.  Query and list (SELECT, LIST, GET.LIST and their family),
           spool and print, session and environment (LOGTO, DATE, WHO, SET),
           screen and message, prompt and input state, and eight read-only
           inspectors (SEARCH, LIST.DIFF, LIST.ITEM, LIST.COMMON, LIST.VARS,
           REPORT.SRC, REPORT.STYLE, FORMAT).  Everything an application built
           on SD invokes and nothing that edits code or data in bulk.

           PROGRAMMER accounts get all of STANDARD plus 42 more - the
           compilers, editors and cataloguer (BASIC, ED, CATALOG, RUN, MAP,
           DEBUG, MODIFY, PHANTOM, GENERATE, COMPILE.DICT and their aliases),
           the file and index definition verbs (CREATE.FILE, CONFIGURE.FILE,
           HSM, SET.TRIGGER, CREATE.INDEX and their family), the bulk record
           editors (COPY, DELETE, RENAME, REFORMAT, CNAME) and the process
           introspection verbs (PSTAT, PDEBUG, PDUMP, DUMP).

           ADMINISTRATOR accounts get everything - the 21 administration
           verbs above PROGRAMMER cover account and grant administration
           (CREATE.ACCOUNT, GRANT, UNLOCK, MODIFY.PASSWORD, ENCRYPT.FIELD and
           their family), system-wide state (CONFIG, LISTU, LIST.LOCKS,
           CLEAR.LOCKS, LOGOUT, SET.DATE), and the shell escapes (SH, !).

           WHAT MOVED SINCE THE OWNER'S FIRST-PASS CUT.  The debug family
           (PSTAT, PDEBUG, PDUMP, DUMP) moved from ADMINISTRATOR to
           PROGRAMMER - a programmer needs these to debug their own code.
           The read-only inspectors moved from PROGRAMMER to STANDARD - they
           read only, they need no BASIC or catalogue, an application can
           invoke them.

           UMASK WAS REMOVED FROM EVERY TIER.  UMASK controls POSIX file-mode
           bits, which Windows does not use for security - all file access
           runs through the ACLs an SD account already receives.  Setting a
           umask on Windows produces no security effect and would only be
           misleading.  The record types stay compiled but no VOC in any tier
           dispatches to them.

           TWELVE VERB RECORDS LEFT NEWVOC.  config, listu, list.readu,
           list.locks, clear.locks, lock, logout, set.date, sh, !,
           clean.account and umask are no longer copied into any account's
           VOC.  Administrators still receive them, from VOC_TEMPLATE, via
           TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR.  A programmer or standard account created
           before this update keeps whichever of them was in its VOC then -
           SD only ever adds records at an update, never removes them.


24 Aug 26  SD NOW WRITES WINDOWS LINE ENDINGS TO FILES YOU CAN OPEN.

           Anything SD writes that an ordinary Windows program can open now
           ends its lines the way Windows does, with CR+LF.  That covers
           records in a directory file, WRITESEQ and WRITECSV output, command
           output captured with COMO, printer output sent to a file, and the
           error log.

           WHY.  A directory file exists so that its records ARE ordinary
           files - you can open one in Notepad, or point Excel at a CSV SD
           produced.  Those files were being written with the Unix line
           ending, so Notepad ran the lines together and a CSV was not quite
           the format it claimed to be.  SD's CSV statements are documented as
           following the RFC 4180 standard, and that standard asks for CR+LF.

           WHAT IS NOT AFFECTED.  Dynamic files are stored in SD's own format
           and are not readable by other programs, so nothing about them
           changes.  Nothing about how SD reads files changes either - it
           accepts both endings, as the entry below describes.

           EXISTING FILES ARE LEFT ALONE.  Only newly written data uses the
           new ending, so a file can contain both.  SD reads either, so this
           is untidy rather than a problem.


24 Aug 26  FILES EDITED IN NOTEPAD, OR SAVED FROM EXCEL, NOW READ CORRECTLY.

           Directory files exist so that you can edit their records with an
           ordinary Windows editor.  Windows editors end each line with CR+LF.
           SD only ever looked for the LF, so it kept the CR - and put it on
           the end of the data.

           You would have seen it as an invisible extra character at the end
           of every line: comparisons failing for no visible reason, a name
           that would not match, a trailing space that was not a space.

           WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE.  Reading a CSV file saved by Excel is
           the clearest case.  The last column of every row picked up that
           stray character, because a comma ended the other columns and the
           line ending only ever touched the last one.  READCSV, READSEQ and
           reading a directory file record are all corrected.

           A CR ON ITS OWN IS STILL DATA and is left exactly as it is.  Only
           the CR+LF pair that ends a line is treated as a line ending, so
           this cannot alter data that happens to contain a CR.

           Files SD itself writes are unchanged for now - it still writes LF.
           Reading was the half that lost data, so it is the half fixed here.


24 Aug 26  AN API SESSION NOW KEEPS YOUR IDENTITY WHEN IT OPENS AND WRITES
           FILES.  This closes the gap described in the 23 Aug entry below.

           WHAT WAS WRONG.  Taking on your Windows identity applied only to the
           one thread that did it.  While switching to your account the session
           starts a short-lived helper process, and starting it quietly put the
           session back to the service's own identity - with no error and
           nothing in the log - before it had opened a single file of yours.
           So the login half worked and the part you would actually notice did
           not: records came out owned by the system account rather than by you.

           WHAT CHANGES FOR YOU.  Records an API session creates are now owned
           by the account that logged in, and the session reaches files with
           your access rather than the service's.  If your account may not read
           something, the API session may not read it either.

           IF YOUR IDENTITY CANNOT BE TAKEN ON, THE LOGIN IS REFUSED rather
           than continued with the service's identity.  A session that believes
           it is you while holding the service's rights is worse than one that
           never started.

           The error-log alarm in the entry directly below stays as it is.  A
           healthy session writes nothing to it, so silence there is now the
           normal case rather than a sign the alarm is untested.


24 Aug 26  THE ERROR LOG NOW SAYS IF AN API SESSION LOSES YOUR IDENTITY.

           The entry below for 23 Aug explains that an API session takes on
           your Windows identity when you log in, but does not yet use it when
           it opens files.  This adds the missing alarm for that.

           If the session ever believes it is you while Windows says it is not,
           a line is written to the error log naming where it happened:

             API IDENTITY LOST at record write - session believes it is ...

           Nothing is written when the two agree, so a healthy session is
           silent and you will not see this at all once the underlying problem
           is fixed.  It changes no behaviour: nothing is refused or slowed
           because of it.  It is there so the condition cannot go unnoticed
           again, which is how it went unnoticed in the first place.


24 Aug 26  THE SIGN-ON BANNER NOW SAYS "SD CORE".

           The line printed when you sign on reads "SD Core for Windows, the
           Essential Multivalue String Database".  It is a name change only -
           nothing about what the product does has changed with it.  The point
           is to tell this build apart from regular SD at a glance, so that a
           screenshot or a support question says which one it came from.


23 Aug 26  AN API SESSION TAKES YOUR WINDOWS IDENTITY AT LOGIN, BUT DOES NOT
           YET USE IT FOR FILES.  Revised 24 Aug 26.

           This entry first said an API session runs as the user who logged in.
           Measuring it on 24 Aug showed that only the login half of that is
           true, and the file half - the half you would actually notice - is
           not.  What follows is what happens today.

           WHAT WORKS.  After a successful login the session takes on your
           Windows identity.  No password is involved: the server never has one
           to use, and the identity is granted by Windows on the service's own
           privilege.  If that step fails the login fails - you are told that
           authentication succeeded but the session could not take your Windows
           identity, and the connection is refused.  It is deliberately not
           allowed to carry on with the service's rights instead.

           WHAT DOES NOT.  That identity does not reach the files.  A record
           written by an API session is still owned by the account the SD
           service runs as, not by you, and files the session opens are still
           opened with the service's rights.  So do not rely on file ownership,
           or on per-user file permissions, to tell one API user from another
           yet.  This is being worked on.

           WHAT IS UNAFFECTED.  A session can still only reach the accounts the
           caller is entitled to.  That containment is a separate mechanism and
           it has been checked.  And sessions reached at the keyboard or over
           ssh were always the user already - only the API path is in question
           here.

23 Aug 26  SD CANNOT BE INSTALLED SILENTLY ANY MORE.

           /SILENT and /VERYSILENT are now refused, with a message saying why.
           There is no switch to override it.

           Installing ends by asking you for a password, and a silent install
           has nobody to ask, so it used to finish with no password set for any
           account - and said nothing about it.  The result looked like a
           complete installation but could not be reached over ssh or through
           the SD API at all, and could be used only at that computer from a
           session run as administrator.

           Unattended deployment is not supported.  Install at the computer's
           keyboard, or through Remote Desktop or similar remote-control
           software - both work, because a person is there to answer.  Nothing
           changes for a normal install.

23 Aug 26  YOU ARE NOW TOLD WHAT SETTING NO PASSWORD ACTUALLY COSTS.

           Both the end of the installer and SD's own prompt used to say that
           without a password you could not use ssh or the API, and stopped
           there.  That was true and easy to read as "some things will not
           work".

           It is stronger than that, and both now say so: with no password the
           account can be used ONLY at that computer - at the keyboard, or
           through Remote Desktop or similar remote-control software - and only
           from a session run as administrator.

           You can still choose it, and SD still asks again the next time you
           open the account.

23 Aug 26  THE PCODE LIBRARY IS NO LONGER WRITABLE BY SD USERS.

           <sysdir>\bin holds the pcode library - the interpreter itself,
           which SD loads into shared memory when it starts and every session
           then runs.  Until now any member of the sdusers group could write
           to it, so one SD user could have replaced what everybody else's
           session executes, including an administrator's.

           That directory is now readable by SD users and writable only by
           administrators, which is the same protection the global catalogue
           has had since 18 Aug.  Nothing needs to write it after an install:
           only the process that starts SD reads it, and that is already
           elevated.

           If you have anything that writes into <sysdir>\bin, it will now be
           refused and will need to run elevated.

23 Aug 26  THE MESSAGE AT THE END OF INSTALLATION IS EASIER TO READ.

           It said the same things, in one tall narrow column that ran most of
           the height of the screen.  It is now wider and 40% shorter.

           It also names your SD account the way the rest of SD does.  It used
           to print the name in capitals - "named DON" - where LIST ACCOUNTS
           and the account's own directory both say "don".

23 Aug 26  PROC, SED AND UPDATE.RECORD HAVE BEEN REMOVED.

           SED was the full-screen editor and UPDATE.RECORD the full-screen
           record editor.  Both are gone, along with the PROC language: a VOC
           item of type PQ now reports that PROC is not supported instead of
           running.  Your PQ records are left alone - it is the interpreter
           that has gone, not the records.

           ED, the line editor, is not affected and is the editor this system
           uses.  Neither is anything you run with LIST, COUNT, SELECT or
           SORT: those are the query processor, which is a different thing
           from PROC despite the similar name.

22 Aug 26  A SCHEDULED JOB CAN NOW RUN AN SD COMMAND, AND ONLY THE COMMANDS
           AN ADMINISTRATOR HAS NAMED FOR IT.

           Until now, typing a command after "sd" - "sd MY.REPORT" - needed a
           session started with "Run as administrator".  A scheduled task does
           not run that way, so there was no way to have SD do anything on a
           timer without handing the job administrator rights.

           Now there are two ways past that.  An elevated session still runs
           anything, exactly as before.  Anything else runs what is listed for
           its account in the SD system file batch.jobs, which only an
           administrator can change: one record per account, one command name
           on each line.

           The command must be a single name with nothing after it, and it
           must be a paragraph or a sentence in that account.  There is no
           password anywhere in this - the job signs in as its own Windows
           account and SD puts it in the matching SD account.

           Nothing changes for a session you type at: commands entered at the
           ":" prompt are unaffected.

22 Aug 26  A WINDOWS PATH TYPED AT THE COMMAND PROMPT IS NO LONGER CUT OFF
           AT THE FIRST BACKSLASH.

           Typing a path such as C:\Data\Sales was read as just "C:".  The
           rest was treated as though you had typed a second, separate thing.
           CREATE.ACCOUNT OTHER, which is given a folder to put the account
           in, was the command most likely to show it: the account went to
           the wrong place, or the command failed for a reason that made no
           sense from what you had typed.

           Typing the same path with forward slashes always worked, and
           still does.  Both are now read the same way.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK NO LONGER STOPS WITH "Access is denied"
           WHEN YOU RUN IT BEFORE SIGNING OUT.

           The installer puts you in the "sdusers" group, and Windows only
           applies that when you next sign in - so on the sign-in that ran
           the installer, the database cannot be read yet.  The check knows
           about this and is meant to say so, in as many words, and tell you
           to sign out and back in.

           Instead it stopped dead with "Access is denied" and told you
           nothing, because looking for a folder it is not allowed to read
           raises an error rather than simply answering "no".  That was the
           one case the check exists to handle kindly, and it was the only
           case it had never been run in.

           It now says the database could not be read on this sign-in, and
           that this is the same membership it has just told you about
           rather than a second fault.

           For the same reason it no longer claims the network API is
           switched off when what actually happened is that it could not
           read the settings file.

22 Aug 26  SD NO LONGER LOSES TRACK OF WHAT YOUR TERMINAL CAN DO WHILE YOU
           ARE LOGGING IN.

           SD reads your terminal's abilities from a description filed
           under the name in your TERM setting.  If there was no
           description of that name - and there is none for
           xterm-256color, which is what a terminal usually reports here
           even when you have not set TERM at all - SD carried on with no
           terminal abilities whatsoever until the account's login
           paragraph set the type a second time.

           That happened a moment too late.  Everything the login sequence
           itself does came before it, so the screen was not cleared when
           you signed on.

           This is the reason behind the Backspace fault listed below, and
           that fault was already repaired separately - the password prompt
           no longer depends on the terminal description at all, and works
           either way.

           SD now checks that the description it asked for was actually
           found, and falls back to  windows  when it was not.

           If you have set TERM to a name SD does have - windows, xterm,
           linux, ansi and about sixty others are supplied - nothing
           changes for you.

22 Aug 26  ACCOUNT NAMES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK.

           SD keeps one file per account in sdsys\accounts, and that file
           used to be named in capitals - DON - while everything else
           belonging to the same account was lower case, including its own
           directory in user_accounts.  New accounts are now recorded in
           lower case, so the two match.

           NOTHING YOU TYPE CHANGES.  Account names have never been case
           sensitive and still are not: create.account, logto and the rest
           accept whatever case you type, and existing accounts keep the
           names they already have.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK CLOSES ON A KEYPRESS.

           Its window used to stay open at a PowerShell prompt, so the only
           way to be rid of it was to know to type  exit  there.  It now
           ends with "Press any key to close this window" and closes.

22 Aug 26  YOU NO LONGER HAVE TO TYPE  off  AFTER SETTING YOUR PASSWORD.

           The window the installer opens closes SD by itself once the
           password is set, and goes straight on to checking the install.

           The installer's last page now also says that this window is
           going to open, so a console appearing after Setup has closed is
           not a surprise.

           If no password is set - because you pressed Enter to skip it -
           the window says so, and says that SD will ask again the first
           time you open the account.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NOW FINISHES BEFORE IT ASKS YOU ANYTHING ELSE.

           The installer used to open SD for your password while its own
           window was still on screen, and then offer the installation
           check as a tick box on its last page - so one action asked you
           twice, once on that page and again in the check itself.

           Now the installer closes first.  A single window then opens and
           does two things in turn: SD asks for your account password, and
           when you type  off  the same window checks the installation.

           The tick box is gone.  The check still asks before it runs, so
           you can decline it, and it is on the Start Menu whenever you
           want it: SD > Check the SD installation.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK NOW SAYS WHEN IT HAS FINISHED.

           Its window stays open on purpose, so that you can read what it
           found - but nothing told you the work was over, which left you
           wondering whether it still wanted something.  It now ends with
           "Test completed - you can close this window now."

22 Aug 26  BACKSPACE NOW WORKS WHEN YOU ARE ASKED FOR A PASSWORD.

           Typing a password at an SD prompt - the one the installer asks
           for, and any other - ignored the Backspace key, so a mistake
           could not be corrected.  It was worse than it looked: the key
           was added to what you had typed rather than removed from it, so
           trying to correct a slip made the password longer.

           Both kinds of Backspace key now erase, whichever your terminal
           sends.

           The same prompt now explains itself.  It says what the password
           is for - you need it only when connecting from another computer,
           over ssh or the API, never at this keyboard - and it tells you to
           type  off  when you have finished.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER CAN NOW CHECK ITSELF WHEN IT FINISHES.

           The last page of the installer has a new tickbox, on by default:
           "Check that SD installed correctly".  Leave it ticked and a window
           opens listing what was checked - the program files, the SD service,
           your access to the database, whether SD's programs were built, and
           the network options if you turned them on.

           IT ONLY LOOKS.  It changes nothing at all, so you can run it as
           often as you like.  There is now a Start Menu entry for it as well:
           SD > Check the SD installation.

           SOME CHECKS WILL SAY "not yet", AND THAT IS NORMAL.  The installer
           puts you in a group called sdusers, which is what lets you open the
           database - but Windows only works out which groups you are in when
           you sign in, so it does not take effect until you sign out and back
           in.  Until you do, the check cannot read the database and says so
           instead of pretending something is broken.  Sign out, sign back in,
           and run it again from the Start Menu.

           If you run it as an administrator it will tell you that the answers
           are about the administrator account rather than about you.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER ASKS WHERE TO PUT SD.

           SD installs to C:\Program Files\SD and its database to
           C:\ProgramData\SD.  The wizard used to let you change the first
           of those but never the second, which meant the two could end up
           apart and only half the install had moved.

           The page that asked has been removed, so both now go where they
           have always been documented to go.  If you installed an earlier
           version somewhere else, this one will not follow it there.

           Nothing you do day to day changes.

21 Aug 26  FAILED API LOGINS ARE NOW WRITTEN TO THE AUDIT TRAIL.

           Until now the audit file recorded only one kind of API refusal: an
           account that authenticated correctly but had not been given API
           access.  Everything else - a wrong password, an account with no API
           password set, a password set before the SCRAM change, a user name
           the server will not accept - was refused and left no trace at all.
           If you were asked "who has been trying to log in?", there was
           nothing to answer with.

           Every refusal is recorded now, in sdsys\audit, one line each:

               API REFUSED user=jim reason=wrong password

           The reasons are plain: wrong password, no credential, credential is
           version 1 - run MODIFY.PASSWORD, name rejected by valid_os_name,
           not in sdapi, and a few for malformed or out-of-order messages.

           WHAT THE CLIENT SEES HAS NOT CHANGED.  It is still told the same
           thing for every credential failure - "Invalid username or password"
           - after the same pause.  The detail goes only to the audit file,
           which ordinary SD accounts can add to but cannot read.

           A user name is recorded as it was typed, except that anything
           outside letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen becomes "?",
           and a long one is cut to 32 characters and marked "...".  So
           GITORLI\jim is recorded as GITORLI?jim.  That is deliberate: the
           name has not been checked at that point, and a name containing a
           new line could otherwise forge a line in the trail.

           Note that anyone who can reach the API port can now cause a line to
           be written, at most one per connection.  If the file grows more
           than you want, it is safe to move it aside; SD starts a new one.

21 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NOW FINISHES BY OPENING SD, SO YOU CAN GIVE YOUR OWN
           ACCOUNT A PASSWORD.

           When you close the installer's last dialog, a console window opens,
           already logged in to the SD account the installer has just made for
           you, and asks you to set a password for it.  Then it leaves you at
           the prompt like any other session; type "off" when you have
           finished.

           It can run before you sign out and back in, which the same dialog
           tells you is needed, because it borrows the installer's rights.
           Your own "sd" still has to wait for the sign-out.

           THAT PASSWORD IS NOT FOR THIS CONSOLE.  Typing "sd" on the machine
           itself has never asked for one and still does not - Windows has
           already established who you are.  The password is what reaches the
           account from SOMEWHERE ELSE: the API, and anything else that has to
           prove who it is over a network.  Without one, the account works
           perfectly here and cannot be reached from anywhere.

           You can close the window without setting one.  SD will ask again the
           next time you open it - see the entry below.  An unattended
           (/VERYSILENT) install never opens the window at all, for the obvious
           reason that there is nobody there; those accounts are asked the
           first time somebody uses them.

21 Aug 26  AN ACCOUNT WITH NO PASSWORD IS ASKED TO SET ONE WHEN IT IS OPENED.

           If an SD account has no password, opening it from an ELEVATED
           console asks for one before you reach the prompt, and the session
           ends if you decline.  It is the same two hidden prompts
           modify.password uses.

           WHO THIS CAN HAPPEN TO IS A SHORT LIST.  Every account created since
           the previous release has a password from the moment it is made, so
           in practice this is the account the installer gives the person
           installing SD, and anything left over from an older version.

           IT ONLY ASKS WHERE YOU CAN ANSWER.  Not in an ordinary
           (unelevated) session - the credential store is readable only by
           administrators, so an ordinary session could not set one anyway, and
           it is not asked to.  Not when SD is reading a piped script, which
           has no one at the keyboard.  Not for a group account, which has no
           password by design.

21 Aug 26  A FAILED create.account NOW REPORTS FAILURE TO A SCRIPT.

           Only affects you if you TEST the result in a PROC or a paragraph.
           Nothing about what you see on screen changes.

           When create.account could not create the Windows account it set
           @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE to 6 instead of -6.  Error codes there are
           negative; positive values mean "this many things were done", which
           is how copy and delete report their counts.  So a PROC asking the
           usual "if @system.return.code < 0" was told the account had been
           created when it had not, and carried on to the next name.  The
           message on screen said otherwise the whole time.

           The same missing minus is fixed in ed, which preset its
           command-error code the same way.

21 Aug 26  ACCOUNTS SAY HOW THEY ARE REACHED, AND set.password IS NOW
           modify.password.  Five changes to the account verbs.

           1. CREATE.ACCOUNT USER NEEDS TO BE TOLD ABOUT REMOTE ACCESS.

                  create.account user <name> ssh
                  create.account user <name> api
                  create.account user <name> both
                  create.account user <name> none

              One of the four is REQUIRED.  There is no default: an account
              that should only ever be reached with LOGTO says "none" and
              means it, and the old silent "ssh yes, API no" could not tell
              that apart from somebody who had not thought about it.

              An ADMINISTRATOR account always gets both and needs no keyword.
              GROUP accounts take none of this - they have no Windows account
              and are reached with LOGTO or an F pointer.

           2. MODIFY.ACCOUNT TAKES THE SAME FOUR WORDS, and they REPLACE the
              old SSH / NO.SSH / API / NO.API pairs.

              THE KEYWORD NOW SAYS WHAT THE ACCESS IS, NOT WHAT TO ADD.  So

                  modify.account fred api

              gives Fred the API AND TAKES ssh AWAY.  If you want both, say
              "both".  The message afterwards always names both routes, so you
              can see what you have left him with.

              It refuses on an administrator's account: administrators always
              have both.

           3. ADMINISTRATORS CAN USE THE API.  Until now they could not - an
              administrator's account joined neither group, and the API
              requires membership, so it refused them.  Nothing you need to do;
              accounts created from now on are correct.

           4. DELETE.ACCOUNT ASKS ONCE, THEN REMOVES EVERYTHING.  It used to
              ask only about the account directory and remove the rest without
              asking.  The single question names exactly what will go,
              including the Windows account when there is one to remove.

              IT STILL WILL NOT DELETE A WINDOWS ACCOUNT SD DID NOT CREATE.
              That is unchanged, and the question uses shorter wording in that
              case rather than promising something it will not do.

           5. set.password IS NOW modify.password.  Same verb, same
              behaviour - the name changed because every account now has a
              password from the moment it is made, so there is nothing to
              "set" for the first time.  A user account cannot be created
              without one: refusing the prompt now creates nothing at all,
              where before it left an account you could not sign in to.

21 Aug 26  THE API IS REACHED AT ITS OWN PORT NOW, not through an ssh tunnel.

           If you connect a client with something like
           "ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 user@host" you no longer need to:
           point the client straight at port 4243 on the server instead.
           The tunnel still works, but it is no longer what the design
           expects and it is not tested.

           WHAT CHANGED, in three parts:

           * sd.conf now sets APIPORT=4243.  It used to ship commented
             out, so the API was off until you turned it on.
           * The server accepts API connections on any network interface.
             It used to listen on 127.0.0.1 only, which is why a tunnel
             was needed.
           * Installation offers "Let other computers on your network
             connect to the SD API (port 4243)", TICKED BY DEFAULT.  Untick
             it and the port is reachable from the machine itself only.

           WHAT STILL STANDS BETWEEN A STRANGER AND YOUR DATA.  Reaching
           the port is not the same as getting in.  A caller must:

           * complete a SCRAM-SHA-256 exchange against a password held for
             that account - so an account with no password cannot connect
             at all;
           * be a member of the "sdapi" group, which an account joins
             only when you give it API access; and
           * pass the account's own group check.

           A session that does get in is confined to the account it signed
           in to.  It cannot open SD's credential store and cannot run
           operating-system commands.

           TO GO BACK TO LOCAL-ONLY at any time, from an elevated prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\api-firewall.ps1" -Restrict

           and to turn the API off altogether, comment out the APIPORT line
           in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf and restart SD.

           Uninstalling SD removes the firewall rule it created.

21 Aug 26  "Limit ssh to SD users and administrators" is now ticked by
           default during installation.  It was offered unticked before.

           WHAT IT DOES, and it is worth reading before you accept it:

           * Only members of "sdssh" and the administrators group may ssh in.
           * Every ssh session goes straight into SD rather than to a shell.
           * BECAUSE OF THAT SECOND ONE, scp AND sftp STOP WORKING for
             everyone on the machine.  If you copy files over ssh, untick it.
           * The original sshd_config is kept as sshd_config.before-sd.

           * IT REFUSES BY ITSELF IF YOUR sshd_config ALREADY SAYS WHO MAY
             CONNECT.  An existing AllowGroups, AllowUsers, DenyGroups or
             DenyUsers line is somebody's policy, and SD will not widen it,
             replace it or merge into it - it leaves the file alone and tells
             you so.
           * AN UNATTENDED INSTALL NOW APPLIES IT TOO, where before it applied
             nothing.  If you install SD silently onto machines with ssh
             servers you manage separately, this is the change to look at.

           You can untick it during installation, and you can undo it
           afterwards with:

              powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Remove

21 Aug 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT now removes the Windows account as part of the job,
           instead of stopping to ask, and it removes that account's Windows
           profile with it.

           * IT ONLY EVER REMOVES AN ACCOUNT SD CREATED.  If the Windows
             account was already there when CREATE.ACCOUNT was run - which is
             the ordinary case, since an SD account is normally named after a
             Windows user who already exists - it is left alone and
             DELETE.ACCOUNT says so.  That check is what protects a borrowed
             login, and it has not changed.
           * WHY THE PROMPT WENT: not deleting was not the quiet outcome.  It
             left a Windows account and its profile behind with nothing
             pointing at them and nothing ever coming back for them.
           * THE PROFILE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS.  Deleting only the
             C:\Users\<name> folder leaves a registry entry behind, and the
             next account of the same name then gets its profile put in
             C:\Users\<name>.<COMPUTERNAME> instead.  Both halves go now.
           * THE ACCOUNT'S OWN DIRECTORY IS STILL ASKED ABOUT SEPARATELY, as
             before.  That is your data; this is the login SD made to reach it.
           * IF THE PROFILE CANNOT BE REMOVED - someone is signed in as that
             user, or a file is open - the account is still deleted and you are
             told the profile was left behind.

21 Aug 26  A session that arrives over the API is now confined to the account
           it is standing in.  Sessions at the keyboard and over ssh are not
           affected in any way.

           WHY: until this version, a client connecting over the API could open
           any file on this machine - including the file SD keeps passwords in.
           Holding one ordinary account's password was enough, and no
           administration command was needed.  If you have used the API, treat
           the passwords of every account as having been reachable.

           * WHAT AN API SESSION CAN STILL DO: everything inside its own
             account, and the shipped SDSYS files every account needs -
             messages, syscom, the dictionaries, sd.voclib.  Ordinary programs
             are not affected.
           * WHAT IT CAN NO LONGER DO: open, rename, delete or list anything
             else.  A refused OPEN takes the ELSE branch and STATUS() is 3035,
             which means "not permitted" rather than "not found".
           * IF YOUR DATA LIVES OUTSIDE AN ACCOUNT - a shared directory that
             VOC entries point at - name it in the new NETDIRS setting in
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf, separating several with a semicolon.
             Nothing else needs changing.  "CONFIG('NETDIRS')" prints what is
             in force.
           * THE PASSWORD FILE, THE PROGRAM CATALOGUE AND THE ACCOUNT REGISTER
             ARE NEVER REACHABLE from an API session, and cannot be added to
             NETDIRS.

21 Aug 26  OS.EXECUTE and SH are no longer available to a session that arrived
           over the API.

           They were, and they ran as the LocalSystem account - so a remote
           client could run any command on the machine with full privilege,
           which is more than the administrator sitting at the keyboard gets.
           Measured from inside such a session on 21 Aug 2026.  An API session
           is no longer treated as an administrator for any purpose, which is
           what SD's own code already assumed and did not enforce.

21 Aug 26  "Limit ssh to SD users and administrators" is now offered on every
           install, not only on a machine that had no ssh server beforehand.

           It is still unticked by default and still does nothing unless you
           tick it.  Before this, SD's own first install put an ssh server on
           the machine permanently, so the option was never offered again -
           while uninstalling always removed the setting.  The result was that
           the restriction could be applied once and never re-applied, and on
           this machine it had silently lapsed.

           * IF YOU HAVE UPGRADED BEFORE AND USE ssh, check whether the
             restriction is still in place; it may have been removed by an
             earlier uninstall.  To put it back:

                powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Installed

           * IT WILL REFUSE if your sshd_config already says who may connect,
             rather than overwrite somebody else's policy.
           * TICKING IT DISABLES scp AND sftp for everyone on the machine,
             which is why it is an option and not a default.

21 Aug 26  You can now say, per account, whether it may reach this machine over
           ssh, through an API client, both, or neither.  Two new
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT actions, from SDSYS:

              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH        may sign in over ssh
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.SSH     may not
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API        may use the API
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.API     may not

           * A NEW ACCOUNT GETS ssh AND NOT THE API.  That is a change: until
             now every account could use the API as soon as the port was open.
             Say "MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API" for each account that needs it.
           * WHY THE API STARTS OFF: an API session currently runs with more
             privilege than the person connecting - see the entry below, dated
             20 Aug.  Until that is fixed, an account should only be able to
             use the API because somebody decided it should.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP ssh AND LOSE NOTHING ELSE.  Installing this
             version copies everyone who could ssh in before into the new group
             that now controls it, so nobody is locked out.  Nobody is put into
             the API group, so if you were using the API, grant it again to the
             accounts that need it.
           * BOTH TAKE EFFECT AT THE NEXT CONNECTION.  There is no need to sign
             out of Windows, and no need to restart SD.
           * "LIST.GRANTS" is unrelated and unchanged - that answers "who may
             use this account", which is a different question from "how may
             this person reach the machine".

21 Aug 26  RDPACCOUNT is gone, and with it NO.RDPACCOUNT.

           * WHAT IT DID: it let an account created by SD sign in to Windows.
             It was named for Remote Desktop but it did not stop there - the
             same Windows setting covers Remote Desktop and the physical
             keyboard together, so an account created with RDPACCOUNT could
             also walk up to the machine and log in.  That was never the
             intention.
           * THE RULE IT GOT IN THE WAY OF, and which now holds without
             exception: NOBODY SD CREATES CAN LOG IN TO WINDOWS AT THIS
             MACHINE UNLESS THEY ARE ALREADY A WINDOWS ADMINISTRATOR.  SD
             accounts reach the machine over ssh or through an API client.
           * If you type RDPACCOUNT now, CREATE.ACCOUNT stops with
             "Unexpected token (RDPACCOUNT)" and makes no account.
           * ACCOUNTS ALREADY CREATED WITH IT KEEP THEIR WINDOWS SIGN-IN.
             Nothing goes round and takes it back, because SD did not record
             which accounts they were.  If you have any, either delete and
             recreate them, or take them out of the Windows group by hand:
                 net localgroup sdsshonly <name> /add
           * If you want Remote Desktop, you want Windows Server and RDP
             client access licences, and probably a commercial product built
             for it.  It is outside what this port is for.

20 Aug 26  The API port is OFF by default again, and you should leave it off
           for now.  This reverses the entry below it, made earlier the same
           day.
           * APIPORT is commented out in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf on a new
             install.  Uncomment it to switch the API on.
           * WHY, PLAINLY: an API session currently runs with more privilege
             than the person connecting.  SD's background service runs as
             LocalSystem and starts each API session itself, so the session
             inherits the service's privileges instead of the client's.  A
             programmer-level account connecting over the API was measured
             reading and writing SD's password store, which no ordinary user
             can touch.
           * WHAT THAT MEANS FOR YOU.  If you switch the API on, treat anyone
             with an API password on a programmer or administrator account as
             having full control of the machine.  Standard accounts appear far
             more limited, but that has not been proved and should not be
             relied on.
           * Turning the port off is enough to avoid it.  Nothing else about
             SD is affected, and terminal sessions are not affected at all -
             they run as you, which is what makes them safe.
           * This will be fixed and the port will go back to being on by
             default.  Until then the default errs the other way.

20 Aug 26  The error log now records who connects to the API port.
           * Every accepted API (SDClient) connection adds a line to
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\errlog naming the Windows process and
             account at the other end:
                 API connection from 127.0.0.1:59314 - pid 11448, GITORLI\don
           * NOTHING IS REFUSED ON THE STRENGTH OF IT.  This records who
             connected; it does not decide who may.  The API's own checks are
             unchanged - the port is loopback only, a client must complete the
             SCRAM exchange against a stored credential, and the account must
             pass the group check.
           * A CONNECTION FORWARDED OVER ssh SHOWS sshd, not the person at the
             far end.  The tunnel ends on this machine, so the process that
             connects genuinely is sshd.  What the line distinguishes is a
             client running ON this machine from one arriving through a
             tunnel; it cannot name a remote person.
           * "peer process not identified" means the client had already gone
             by the time the connection was looked up.  It is not an error and
             the connection proceeds normally.
           * ERRLOG NOW APPLIES TO THESE LINES TOO.  The background daemon
             used to append to the error log without ever trimming it - it
             only wrote at startup and on failure, so it never grew.  Now that
             it writes per connection, it discards the oldest part of the log
             on reaching the ERRLOG size in sd.conf, exactly as SD sessions
             have always done.  If you have set ERRLOG unusually large,
             consider what an entry per connection adds to it.
           * AFTER THE LOG IS TRIMMED, ITS FIRST LINE MAY HAVE NO TIMESTAMP.
             An entry is two lines - a timestamped header and the message
             indented below it - and trimming restarts the file at a line,
             not at an entry, so the first message can be left without its
             header.  This is not damage and no entry after it is affected.
             SD has always trimmed the log this way; it is only visible now
             because the log turns over more often.

20 Aug 26  A new kind of account: RDPACCOUNT, which may sign in to Windows.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER jim RDPACCOUNT
             CREATE.ACCOUNT USER jim PROGRAMMER RDPACCOUNT
             The account may sign in at the console, over Remote Desktop, and
             run other Windows programs - as well as over ssh.  Without the
             keyword an account is confined to ssh, which is unchanged and
             stays the default.
           * IT IS NOT A FOURTH TIER.  It is orthogonal to STANDARD,
             PROGRAMMER and ADMINISTRATOR: give it with any of them, in either
             order.  The tier decides what the account can DO inside SD; this
             decides how the person reaches the machine.
           * AN EXISTING ACCOUNT CAN BE MOVED EITHER WAY:
                 MODIFY.ACCOUNT JIM RDPACCOUNT      may sign in to Windows
                 MODIFY.ACCOUNT JIM NO.RDPACCOUNT   confined to ssh again
           * AN ADMINISTRATOR CANNOT BE CONFINED TO ssh, and NO.RDPACCOUNT
             refuses rather than doing it.  Confining an administrator would
             deny them console and Remote Desktop sign-in, and they would be
             unable to get back in after the session they typed it in.
           * WHAT IT COSTS YOU TO KNOW.  An RDPACCOUNT user has a desktop, so
             they can reach files with Explorer and run programs SD does not
             control.  Each account's own directory is locked to that account
             (see the entry below), which is what makes this safe to offer;
             but it is a wider kind of access than an ssh-only account has,
             and it is not the default for that reason.

20 Aug 26  The API port is now open by default.
           * APIPORT=4243 is set in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf on a new
             install, instead of being commented out for you to switch on.
             SD listens for API (SDClient) connections as soon as it starts.
           * IT LISTENS ON 127.0.0.1 ONLY, never on a network interface, so
             nothing off this machine can reach it.  A client on another
             machine still forwards the port over ssh:
                 ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 <user>@<host>
           * AN OPEN PORT IS NOT AN OPEN DOOR.  A client still has to complete
             the SCRAM login against a password held for that account, and
             then pass the account's group check.  No account has an API
             password until you run SET.PASSWORD for it, and until then every
             login attempt is refused.
           * WHY IT CHANGED.  Until 20 Aug the API login sent your password in
             clear, so opening the port by default would have been careless.
             That login is gone - see the entry below - and the reason for the
             cautious default went with it.  Installing also deletes and
             rebuilds the data tree, so the old default meant switching the
             port back on by hand after every upgrade.
           * TO TURN IT OFF, comment the APIPORT line out and restart SD.  No
             port is then opened.  The setting is read when SD starts, not
             when a session begins.

20 Aug 26  Your account directory is locked to you, and the installer now
           finishes the job on every account instead of stopping at the first
           awkward one.
           * WHAT THE LOCK IS.  C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts\<name> is
             granted to that account's own group, to administrators and to the
             system, and to nobody else.  Before it, every SD user could read
             and rewrite every other user's account files from Explorer or a
             command prompt - outside SD, so none of SD's own permission
             checks applied.  SD itself has always refused to let you LOGTO an
             account you are not a member of; this makes the files agree with
             that.
           * NOTHING YOU TYPE CHANGES, and your own account is unaffected -
             you are a member of its group.
           * THE FIX.  The installer re-applies the lock to accounts that
             already existed, and it used to STOP at the first account whose
             Windows group had been removed - reporting that it could not
             secure the account directories, and leaving every account after
             that one unlocked.  It now says which account it skipped and
             carries on with the rest.
           * A skipped account is left alone deliberately: locking a directory
             to a group that no longer exists would take the account away from
             the person who owns it.

20 Aug 26  The old pre-fork product name is gone from the login code.
           * A banner naming the database SD was forked from was still in
             LOGIN, commented out.  Nothing printed it, but it was one
             character away from appearing at every sign-in.  Removed.
           * NOTHING YOU SEE CHANGES.  The banner you actually get is the
             one below it and is untouched, as is the copyright line.

20 Aug 26  SET.PASSWORD now refuses a password typed on the command line.
           * IT USED TO IGNORE IT SILENTLY.  "SET.PASSWORD DON mypassword"
             set the account to DON, threw the rest away without a word, and
             asked you for the password as though nothing had happened - so
             everything looked like it worked, and you had no way of knowing
             your password had gone into the command stack for nothing.
           * It now stops and says so.  The password is still read from the
             two hidden prompts, exactly as before; nothing else changes.
           * If you have typed one this way, it is worth setting a different
             password, since the old one was recorded where you did not
             intend it to be.

20 Aug 26  The old cleartext API login is gone.  Old clients stop working.
           * THE CHANGE OF 19 AUG ADDED THE NEW LOGIN AND LEFT THE OLD ONE IN
             PLACE.  This removes it.  A client that still sends a password in
             clear is now refused with "Cleartext login is no longer
             supported; this server requires SCRAM authentication".
           * WHAT YOU HAVE TO DO: use a client library from this release or
             later, and run SET.PASSWORD again for every account that uses the
             API.  An account whose password has not been re-set is refused,
             and the refusal reads as a wrong password because from the
             server's point of view there is no credential to check.
           * PROGRAMS THAT USE THE SDCLIENT CLASS ARE COVERED.  The !sdclient
             class module that BASIC programs use to reach another SD server
             now speaks the new login too.  Your code does not change -
             connect() takes the same arguments - but the program has to be
             running under this release at both ends.
           * SDLocal connections are unaffected: they send no password and
             never did.

19 Aug 26  API logins no longer send your password over the connection.
           * The API used to send the user name and password as plain text.
             Anything able to watch the connection could read the password.
             It now uses SCRAM-SHA-256, a challenge and response: the server
             sets a puzzle only someone who knows the password can answer, and
             the password itself is never sent in any form.
           * THE SERVER NOW PROVES ITSELF TO YOU AS WELL.  It finishes by
             returning a value only the real server can compute, and the
             client refuses the connection if it does not match.  Another
             program that grabbed the port before SD started cannot pretend to
             be SD to collect passwords.
           * NOTHING CHANGES IN YOUR APPLICATION.  QMConnect takes the same
             arguments and returns the same things.
           * YOU MUST RUN SET.PASSWORD AGAIN FOR EVERY ACCOUNT THAT USES THE
             API.  The stored credentials changed shape and the old ones
             cannot be converted - the password was never kept anywhere, by
             design, so there is nothing to convert them from.  An account
             whose password has not been re-set will be refused.
           * You also need the new client library.  An older one still works
             for now, but it still sends the password in clear, and a later
             release will stop accepting it.
             [20 Aug 26: that release is the entry above.  An older client is
             refused outright from now on.]

19 Aug 26  OS.EXECUTE now needs permission, as SH already did.
           * UNTIL NOW ANY ACCOUNT THAT COULD WRITE A PROGRAM HAD THE
             OPERATING SYSTEM.  SH at the command prompt was refused unless
             you were listed in OS.USERS or in an elevated session, but
             OS.EXECUTE inside a program was not checked at all - so the
             restriction could be walked around by anyone able to type BASIC.
           * OS.USERS has always had two fields: field 1 is SH and field 2 is
             OS.EX.  Field 2 was written down and never read.  It is now what
             decides whether OS.EXECUTE may run.
           * WHO IS STILL ALLOWED: an account with OS.EX set to "yes"; an
             elevated session, exactly as with SH; and SD's own system
             programs, so CREATE.ACCOUNT and the SH verb work as before.
           * NO FILE AND NO RECORD BOTH MEAN NO, so an installation that has
             never set OS.USERS up denies OS.EXECUTE to ordinary accounts.
             If you have programs that shell out, add the accounts that run
             them to OS.USERS with OS.EX set to yes.
           * The refusal reads: <name> is not permitted to use OS.EXECUTE.

19 Aug 26  The $COMMAND.STACK VOC record is now $command.stack.
           * This is the record that decides whether your command stack is
             saved when you leave SD and restored when you sign in.  It is the
             last VOC name that was still upper case.
           * NOTHING CHANGES FOR YOU.  SD looks for the new name first and the
             old one second, so an account made before this release keeps its
             command stack working with no action from you, and a new one uses
             the new name.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT writes the new name from this release.

19 Aug 26  The system file names in your VOC are lower case.
           * VOC, NEWVOC, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES, SYSCOM, QFILE, DICT.DICT, MD,
             SD.ACCOUNTS and OS.USERS are now voc, newvoc, accounts, messages,
             syscom, qfile, dict.dict, md, sd.accounts and os.users.  LISTF and
             LIST VOC show the new spelling.
           * YOU DO NOT HAVE TO CHANGE ANYTHING.  Typing a name in either case
             still finds the file, in a command and in a program, so existing
             programs and paragraphs keep working.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THE OLD SPELLING until their VOC is
             updated - answer Y to "Update VOC to new release?" or run
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  Both spellings work either way.
           * DELETE.FILE still refuses to delete your VOC, whichever case you
             type.

19 Aug 26  Backspace works inside the full-screen editors, and the arrow keys
           no longer type themselves into your record.
           * IN SED AND THE UPDATE.RECORD SCREENS, Backspace deleted the
             character to the RIGHT of the cursor instead of the one to the
             left.  Those editors read the keyboard directly and carry their
             own key tables, so the fix that repaired Backspace at the command
             prompt did not reach them.
           * WHY.  The table said the DEL byte means "Delete".  On a Windows
             console DEL is what the Backspace key sends, and the Delete key
             sends something else entirely.  Backspace now means Backspace, and
             the Delete key has its own entry, so both work and neither has
             taken the other's job.
           * WORSE, IN UPDATE.RECORD THE ARROW KEYS WERE NOT RECOGNISED AT ALL,
             and an unrecognised key is not ignored - its characters were
             entered into the field.  Pressing the right arrow put a "C" in
             your data.  All four arrows now work, as do Home, End, Page Up,
             Page Down, Insert and Delete.
           * ED is unaffected and always was: it is the line editor and reads
             whole lines, so it already had a working Backspace.
           * SED reads its key assignments from a file, so a site that wants
             the old behaviour back can still set it.

19 Aug 26  The arrow keys work, and the default terminal type is now WINDOWS.
           * LEFT, RIGHT, UP and DOWN DID NOTHING in cmd, PowerShell or
             Windows Terminal.  The key was discarded and the cursor stayed
             where it was.
           * WHY.  A terminal has two different ways of spelling an arrow key.
             In its ordinary state it sends ESC [ D for Left; it sends the
             other spelling, ESC O D, only after the application has asked it
             to switch, and SD never asks.  The vt100 definition SD was
             defaulting to since 18 Aug lists only the second spelling, so the
             key SD was listening for was one no Windows console ever sends.
           * The LINUX definition has always had this right - it lists the
             ordinary spelling, and DEL for Backspace, which is exactly what
             every Windows console sends.  Its name describes an operating
             system, but what matters is the byte protocol.
           * SD now ships a WINDOWS terminal definition, an exact copy of
             LINUX, and the LOGIN paragraph in every account's VOC selects it.
             TERM on its own should now say
                 Device    : windows
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THEIR OLD SETTING until their VOC is
             updated, which happens when you answer Y to "Update VOC to new
             release?" or run UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  Until then, TERM WINDOWS sets
             it for the session.
           * You can still choose your own - TERM WYSE60, say.  All 62
             definitions are still shipped.

19 Aug 26  The terminal definition compiler no longer fails silently.
           * SDTIC compiles terminfo.src into the definitions SD reads.  If one
             definition in the file was malformed it could stop part way
             through, leaving some terminal types missing, and still report
             success - so the first sign of trouble was a terminal that did not
             work.
           * It now compiles every other definition correctly, names the bad
             one with its line number, and returns an error.

19 Aug 26  The Backspace key works.
           * IT DID NOTHING AT ALL in cmd, PowerShell or Windows Terminal, and
             in PuTTY unless you had changed its "Backspace key" setting to
             Control-H.  Typing simply carried on and the character stayed.
           * WHY.  A terminal sends one of two bytes for Backspace - Control-H
             or DEL - and SD accepted only whichever one the terminal type said
             it would.  Every Windows console sends DEL; the terminal type SD
             assumes when TERM is not set is vt100, which says Control-H.  So
             the two never met.
           * Choosing a different terminal type could not have helped: of the
             62 definitions SD ships, 51 say Control-H and only xterm and linux
             say DEL.  That is why vt100-w made no difference.
           * SD now accepts BOTH bytes as Backspace whatever the terminal type,
             so it works out of the box and you need change nothing.  A
             terminal type that deliberately uses DEL for its Delete key - the
             AccuTerm vt100-at definition does - still gets Delete.
           * NOT FIXED YET, and it is the same family: inside the full-screen
             editors (ED and the UPDATE.RECORD screens) Backspace on a terminal
             that sends DEL still deletes forwards.  Those read the keyboard
             directly and carry their own key tables.

19 Aug 26  BASIC no longer creates an object file it can never open again.
           * COMPILING WITH A LOWER-CASE FILE NAME USED TO POISON THE ACCOUNT.
             "BASIC bp MYPROG" built the object file name from what you typed,
             giving bp.OUT, while the directory on disk was made as BP.OUT.  SD
             matches a name as typed, then lower, then upper - never a mixture
             - so nothing could reach bp.OUT afterwards, and the next
             "BASIC BP MYPROG" stopped with "Data pathname 'BP.OUT' already
             exists".  Permanently: nothing cleared it but deleting the file.
           * BASIC now names the object file after the VOC entry that actually
             answered, and the .out suffix follows its case.  A file called bp
             gets bp.out; a file called BP still gets BP.OUT, so nothing that
             works today changes.
           * IF YOU ALREADY HAVE ONE, delete it: DELETE.FILE bp.OUT FORCE, then
             compile again.  FORCE is needed because DELETE.FILE asks about the
             data and dictionary parts separately.

19 Aug 26  The BP and GPL.BP entries in your VOC are now bp and gpl.bp.
           * They join $hold, $savedlists and the commands.  BP.OUT and
             GPL.BP.OUT moved with them.  As always, what you type does not
             change: BASIC BP MYPROG, RUN BP MYPROG and LIST BP all work.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THEIR OWN SPELLING and go on working.  An
             account created now gets bp.
           * MICRO's "Compile?" prompt is back for lower-case file names.  It
             decided by comparing the last two characters of the file name
             against "BP", which stopped matching once the file was called bp,
             so the offer silently disappeared.  It compares without regard to
             case now.
           * Still upper case, and next: VOC, NEWVOC, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES,
             SYSCOM, QFILE and $COMMAND.STACK.

19 Aug 26  The system files are now lower case on disk.
           * SD's own files inside C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys are named gpl.bp,
             newvoc, voc_template, messages, syscom, sd.voclib, accounts, bp,
             voc, voc.dic, dict.dic, $hold, $map, $ipc, $cred, os.users and so
             on, instead of GPL.BP, NEWVOC and the rest.  So is the voc file
             inside each account directory.
           * WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE.  These are names on disk, not names
             in your VOC: LISTF, CT VOC and OS_CWD show them, and that is the
             point of the change, but you still type LIST VOC, BASIC BP PROG
             and COUNT ACCOUNTS exactly as before.  The VOC entries themselves
             are renamed separately, one at a time.
           * EXISTING SYSTEMS AND ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED, and there is nothing
             to migrate.  Windows matches file names without regard to case, and
             every one of these files is reached through a name recorded in a
             VOC entry, so a system or an account created before this goes on
             working under its old spelling.  Only newly created ones get the
             new names.
           * The private catalogue (cat), the global catalogue (gcat) and the
             print queue (prt) were already lower case and are unchanged.

18 Aug 26  The commands are now spelled in lower case.
           * LIST, COUNT, SELECT, CREATE.FILE, SETPTR and the rest are stored
             in the VOC as list, count, select, create.file, setptr and so on,
             and that is how they appear in LIST VOC, LISTV and CT VOC.
           * WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE.  SD tries a name as you typed it,
             then in lower case, then in upper case, so LIST, list and List all
             run the same verb.  Every command you already know still works,
             typed the way you already type it, and so does every keyword -
             WITH, BY, NO.PAGE and the rest.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED.  An account created before this
             keeps the upper-case names in its VOC and goes on working.  There
             is nothing to migrate.  An account you create now, or one you
             refresh with UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, gets the new spelling.
           * The account tiers are unaffected: a standard account is still
             refused the same 18 capabilities, and an administrator still gets
             the same 10 administration verbs.
           * NOT RENAMED, and both on purpose: the file names in your VOC - VOC,
             BP, NEWVOC, GPL.BP, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES, SYSCOM, QFILE - which move
             with the file-naming change rather than with the commands; and the
             $ records, which are being renamed one at a time.

18 Aug 26  LIST now finds a record whatever case you type its name in.
           * CORRECTION to the entry below, which says the old spelling still
             works when you type it.  That was true of COUNT, CT and ED and NOT
             true of LIST: LIST VOC $HOLD answered "'$HOLD' not found" on the
             very record CT VOC $HOLD showed you.  The two verbs disagreed
             about the same name.
           * LIST, SORT, SELECT and the rest of the query language now try a
             record name as you typed it, then in lower case, then in upper
             case - the same order everything else uses.  A name that matches
             exactly still wins, so nothing that works today changes.
           * A name that exists in NO case is still reported as not found.

18 Aug 26  Names are matched the same way in several more places.
           * SD tries a name as typed, then lower case, then upper case.  A few
             places had been missed and still demanded upper case, so they
             would have stopped working as SD's own names move to lower case.
           * UPDATE.RECORD now recognises its keywords whatever case they are
             stored in; the query processor does the same for VOC entries; the
             LOGIN paragraph is still found by an interactive session and by an
             API session; and SET.FILE still finds the default QFILE pointer.
           * DELETE.FILE's refusal to delete VOC and $ACC no longer depends on
             the case you type.  It could not be got round before, because
             those names are upper case; it could have been once they are not.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM.  Every name SD ships is
             still upper case, so the added attempts find nothing that the
             existing ones did not already find.

18 Aug 26  The hold file is now called $hold.
           * SETPTR, SP.VIEW, CLEAN.ACCOUNT, PRINTER FILE and the MICRO editor
             all name it, and the messages they print name it, so $hold is the
             spelling you will see from now on.  SETPTR with no file of its own
             now reports "Hold file: $hold P1" rather than "$HOLD P1".
           * THE OLD SPELLING STILL WORKS WHEN YOU TYPE IT.  COUNT $HOLD,
             CT VOC $HOLD and ED $HOLD recname all still reach the file,
             because SD tries a name as typed, then in lower case, then in
             upper case.  What you will notice is that SD answers in the new
             spelling: type CT VOC $HOLD and the heading says VOC $hold.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  An
             account created before this keeps $HOLD in its VOC and goes on
             working - the same fold reaches it upwards.  Nothing to migrate.
           * BP and $COMMAND.STACK have NOT moved.  This is still one file at
             a time, not a sweep.

18 Aug 26  A silent install no longer stops and waits for someone to click.
           * Installing with /VERYSILENT put one message box on screen - the
             one saying SD will not touch an OpenSSH Server you already have -
             and waited there.  Nothing was copied until somebody clicked OK,
             so an unattended install appeared to hang.
           * The box is now skipped in silent mode, along with the others.  It
             also no longer explains why two options are "absent from this
             page" to somebody who was never shown a page.
           * ONLY SILENT INSTALLS ARE AFFECTED.  A normal install shows the
             same messages it always did.

18 Aug 26  The saved select list file is now called $savedlists.
           * SAVE.LIST, GET.LIST, DELETE.LIST, MERGE.LIST, COPY.LIST,
             SAVE.STACK, CLEAN.ACCOUNT and EDIT.LIST all name it, and the
             messages they print name it, so the new spelling is what you will
             see from now on.
           * THE OLD SPELLING STILL WORKS WHEN YOU TYPE IT, and nothing you
             type has to change.  ED $SAVEDLISTS, COUNT $SAVEDLISTS,
             CT VOC $SAVEDLISTS and COPY.LIST ... FROM $SAVEDLISTS all still
             reach the file, because SD tries a name as typed, then in lower
             case, then in upper case - for record ids as well as file names.
             What you will notice is that SD answers in the new spelling: type
             CT VOC $SAVEDLISTS and the heading it prints back says
             VOC $savedlists.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  An
             account created before this keeps $SAVEDLISTS in its VOC and goes
             on working unchanged - the upward half of that same fold is what
             reaches it.  There is nothing to migrate.
           * $HOLD, BP and $COMMAND.STACK have NOT moved.  This is one file at
             a time, not a sweep.

18 Aug 26  Programs can now find a file whatever case its name is stored in.
           * A companion to the change further down this file, which fixed the
             same thing for names you TYPE.  It turns out there are two
             separate lookups, and only one of them had been fixed.
           * Commands typed at the colon prompt found a lower-case file.  An
             SDBasic program's OPEN statement did not, because it goes through
             a different route that still matched the name exactly.
           * So a program containing OPEN "$SAVEDLISTS" would have stopped
             working the moment that file was renamed to lower case.  The order
             for OPEN is now the same as everywhere else: as typed, then lower
             case, then upper case.
           * Q-pointers are included: one naming a file that has since been
             renamed still resolves.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM, for the same reason as
             before - every name SD ships is upper case, so the new attempt
             finds nothing until lower-case names appear.

18 Aug 26  New accounts get their files with lower-case names on disk.
           * An account you create now holds $hold, $hold.dic, $svlists and bp
             instead of $HOLD, $HOLD.DIC, $SVLISTS and BP.  LISTF and CT VOC
             show the new spelling.
           * The names you TYPE are unchanged - $HOLD, $SAVEDLISTS and BP still
             work, and are still what appears in the VOC.  Only the directory
             name and the path stored against it have moved.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  Each
             account's VOC names its own files, and Windows matches file names
             without regard to case, so both spellings keep working.  There is
             nothing to migrate.
           * This is the first step of a longer change towards lower case
             throughout.

18 Aug 26  CORRECTION to the UNLOCK entry further down, which is wrong.
           * That entry says UNLOCK had never worked, because its VOC entry
             carried the text "Verb to unlock records" in the field where the
             record TYPE belongs.  The record was indeed written that way.  The
             conclusion drawn from it was not right.
           * SD ALLOWS THAT ON PURPOSE.  A type code may be followed by comment
             text with no space in between - the same rule PI, PI/open and
             UniVerse follow - and SD tests only the characters belonging to
             the code it is looking for.  So "Verb to unlock records" reads as
             type V with a comment after it, and UNLOCK dispatched normally.
           * So UNLOCK was not repaired, and if you have been restarting SD to
             clear a stuck record lock, UNLOCK would have done it.  Sorry.
           * COPYP carried the same style of entry and was likewise working;
             it was checked before being changed.
           * BOTH RECORDS HAVE STILL BEEN CHANGED to a bare V, because every
             other verb in the file is written that way, and it was the
             inconsistency that produced the wrong conclusion.  Neither verb
             behaves differently.

18 Aug 26  SDNet, the remote file access system, has been removed.
           * SD could open a file held on another SD server by putting
             "server;file" in a VOC entry.  That is gone.  A VOC entry
             containing a semicolon is now simply a file name that does not
             resolve.
           * WHY.  Each server's user name and password were kept in sd.conf,
             obscured with a simple letter substitution that is not encryption,
             and the session ran over port 4245.  There was also no way to
             switch the feature off: the NETFILES setting was read at startup
             and then never consulted by anything.
           * THE API IS NOT AFFECTED.  SDClient and the remote API are a
             separate mechanism, they are unchanged, and they stay protected by
             requiring an ssh tunnel.
           * SET.SERVER, DELETE.SERVER and LIST.SERVERS have gone with it.  Two
             of those three had never worked in any case - their VOC entries
             were malformed.
           * NETFILES is still ACCEPTED in sd.conf, so an existing
             configuration file will not stop SD starting.  It now does
             nothing, which is also what it did before.

18 Aug 26  UNLOCK works.  It never has, on any release this port was built
           from.
           * Its VOC entry carried the description text, "Verb to unlock
             records", in the field where the record TYPE belongs.  SD reads
             that field to decide what the entry is, so UNLOCK was not a verb
             as far as SD was concerned.
           * UNLOCK is what you use to clear a record lock left behind by a
             session that died while holding one.  Until now the only way to
             release such a lock was to stop and restart SD, which disconnects
             everybody.
           * It is an administrator verb; standard and programmer accounts do
             not get it.

18 Aug 26  A file or VOC entry whose name is not upper case can now be found
           whatever case you type it in.
           * SD looked for the name exactly as you typed it and then, if that
             failed, for the UPPER-case version - and nothing else.  So a name
             that is stored in lower case could only ever be reached by typing
             it exactly right.
           * CREATE.FILE testlc is the case that shows it: the file is
             registered as "testlc", the message says "Created DATA part as
             TESTLC", and COUNT TESTLC then answered "File not found" while
             COUNT testlc worked.
           * The order is now: as typed, then lower case, then upper case.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM.  Every name SD ships is
             upper case, so the new attempt never finds anything until
             lower-case names start to appear.  This is groundwork.
           * Account names are deliberately NOT included - they are still
             matched in upper case, which is what makes signing in
             case-insensitive.

18 Aug 26  Adding to or removing from the system-wide catalogue now requires
           administrator rights, whichever way you ask for it.
           * CATALOG already required them for the spelled-out form,
             "CATALOG BP MYPROG GLOBAL".  It did NOT require them when the same
             thing was asked for by putting a *, !, _ or $ in front of the
             name - "CATALOG BP $MYPROG" - which is the form most people use.
           * DELETE.CATALOG required nothing at all, by either route.
           * This matters because the system-wide catalogue holds the programs
             SD runs for everybody, $LOGIN among them.  Replacing one ran your
             code in every session on the machine, administrators included;
             deleting one stopped everybody signing in.
           * NOTHING CHANGES FOR LOCAL AND PRIVATE CATALOGUING, which is what
             programmers use day to day:
                 CATALOG BP MYPROG          private catalogue, this account
                 CATALOG BP MYPROG LOCAL    this account's VOC
             Both still work, in any account you are allowed to LOGTO into.
           * The only thing you can no longer do as an ordinary user is
             catalogue a program whose name starts with *, !, _ or $, because
             those characters mean "system-wide".  Name it without one.
           * The system-wide catalogue is now locked at the Windows level as
             well, not only inside SD: SD users may run what is in it and
             nothing else.  So a program you did not install cannot replace
             one that everybody runs, whichever way it is reached.
           * TO CATALOGUE SYSTEM-WIDE YOU NOW NEED AN ELEVATED SESSION, not
             just an administrator account.  Start SD from an elevated window
             and LOGTO SDSYS.  Entering SDSYS from an ordinary window still
             gives you administrator rights inside SD, but Windows itself now
             refuses the write - so use the same elevated window you already
             need in order to recompile the system programs.

18 Aug 26  The default terminal type is now VT100, not LINUX.
           * Every account's VOC holds a LOGIN paragraph that sets the
             terminal type when you sign in.  It said LINUX, inherited from
             the Linux original; on Windows the sensible default is VT100.
           * TERM on its own reports what you are using.  It should now say
                 Device    : vt100
           * You can still choose your own - "TERM WYSE60", say - and you can
             edit the LOGIN paragraph in your VOC if you want a different
             default for one account.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP LINUX until their VOC is updated, which
             happens when you answer Y to "Update VOC to new release?" or run
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  The release number changed in this same version,
             so you will be asked anyway on your next sign-in.

18 Aug 26  The release is W1.0-0.  The login header now reads "version W1.0-0".
           * The W is for Windows.  1.0-1 and 1.0-2 were releases of SD this
             port was built from, not releases of the port itself.
           * IF YOU ALREADY HAVE AN OLDER TREE INSTALLED, signing in will ask
             "Your VOC is at release level 1.0-2 / Update VOC to new release?"
             Answer Y.  It compares the two for difference, not for which is
             newer.
           * The installer is now sd-setup-W1.0-0.exe.
           * Nothing else moved.  The QM revision SD reports for file and
             compatibility purposes is still 2.6-6.

18 Aug 26  Who may use SH is now a list you keep, not a matter of elevation.
           * SH used to need an elevated session, and an ssh session can never
             be elevated - so programmers, the people who actually need a
             shell, were the ones who could never have one.
           * Name them instead.  From SDSYS:
                 ED OS.USERS don
             one record per person, keyed by the name they sign in with, NOT
             the account name.  Field 1 is SH and field 2 is OS.EX; put "yes"
             in field 1 to give that person a shell.  A missing record, or a
             missing file, means no.
           * The name is the LOGIN name.  It follows the person, so it does
             not change when they LOGTO somewhere else.
           * ONLY AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN EDIT THE LIST.  It is read-only to
             everybody else on disk, which is the whole of what stops a user
             granting themselves a shell.  Do not loosen those permissions.
           * A LISTED PERSON GETS A REAL SHELL - pipes, redirection and
             chaining all work:
                 SH dir | more
             Anyone not on the list who reaches SH by being elevated still
             gets the restricted form, which rejects those characters.
           * An elevated session keeps SH whatever the list says, deliberately,
             so an empty list cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.
           * FIELD 2, OS.EX, IS STORED BUT NOT YET ENFORCED.  It is there for
             a later release that will gate OS.EXECUTE from inside a program.
             Until then, anyone who can write and run SDBasic can still reach
             the operating system that way, whatever field 2 says.

17 Aug 26  Signing in over ssh now takes you straight into SD.
           * An ssh session lands in SD instead of at a Windows prompt.  That
             applies to everyone who may connect, administrators included:
             the rule is about the route in, not about who took it.
           * SCP AND SFTP NO LONGER WORK INTO THIS MACHINE, for anybody.  The
             command is forced, so there is no file-transfer subsystem left
             to run.  This is the cost of the above and is deliberate.
           * TO COPY FILES, PULL THEM RATHER THAN PUSHING.  Sit at the machine
             or connect with Remote Desktop, and use WinSCP or scp from there
             to fetch what you need.  Outbound connections are not affected -
             only sessions coming in.
           * Remote Desktop and the local console are untouched, and are how
             an administrator reaches the machine itself.
           * IT IS NOT APPLIED BY THE INSTALLER on a machine that already had
             an ssh server.  Run this once from an elevated prompt:
                 powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Installed
             It checks the result with "sshd -T" and puts the original back if
             anything is wrong, keeping a copy as sshd_config.before-sd.

17 Aug 26  Queries against a directory file now match ids case insensitively.
           * SELECT and LIST against a DIRECTORY file now match a record id
             whatever case you type it in, which is how Windows treats the
             file names those ids are:
                 SELECT BP WITH @ID = "sue"      finds record SUE
           * This never worked before.  SD asks itself whether it is running
             on Windows, and on this port it was answering NO, so the code
             that does case insensitive matching was never reached.
           * DYNAMIC FILES ARE UNCHANGED and stay case sensitive unless the
             file itself is marked otherwise, so nothing you already rely on
             changes:
                 SELECT VOC WITH @ID = "who"     still finds nothing
           * SYSTEM(91) now answers 1 rather than 0.  If you have written a
             program that asks it, it will start telling the truth.

17 Aug 26  Record ids in directory files are no longer case sensitive.
           * Windows treats SUE and sue as the same file, but SD did not, so
             for a DIRECTORY file it could believe there were two records
             where the disk held one.  It now agrees with Windows.
           * WHAT YOU WILL NOTICE is locking.  A program holding a record
             lock on 'sue' now correctly blocks another one asking for
             'SUE'; before, both could hold what they thought were separate
             locks on the same file.  Transactions match ids the same way.
           * FILEINFO(f, FL$NOCASE) answers 1 for a directory file, where it
             used to answer 0.
           * DYNAMIC FILES ARE NOT AFFECTED.  They keep the setting recorded
             in their own file header, exactly as before, so nothing about an
             existing file changes and there is nothing to convert.
           * Nothing is stored differently and no file needs rebuilding.

17 Aug 26  SECURITY: stored passwords are protected from other SD users.
           * On installs made before this, every SD user could WRITE to the
             file SD keeps account passwords in.  No password is stored there
             - SD keeps a scrambled verifier, and that cannot be turned back
             into a password - but being able to REPLACE one was enough: a
             user could put in a verifier for a password of their own
             choosing and then sign in as somebody else, including through
             the API.
           * The installer was always meant to lock that file down and never
             did.  The step ran, reported nothing, and had no effect, because
             of a quoting fault in how it was called.  Nothing in the install
             looked wrong.
           * IF YOU HAVE ALREADY INSTALLED SD, REINSTALLING IS WHAT FIXES IT.
             You can check without reinstalling: from an ordinary (not
             administrator) PowerShell prompt, run
                 icacls "C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\$CRED"
             If that prints permissions, the file is still open.  If it says
             "Access is denied", it is protected, which is what you want.
           * Nothing you use day to day changes.  SET.PASSWORD still works
             for administrators, and API sign-in is unaffected.

17 Aug 26  SET.PASSWORD can be reached at last, and administrators have it.
           * An SD account can now be given a password.  The command existed
             and no VOC pointed at it, so there was no way to type it.
           * SET.PASSWORD <account> sets somebody else's and needs
             administrator rights.  SET.PASSWORD on its own sets your own, and
             asks for your current one first.
           * IT IS AN ADMINISTRATOR COMMAND, so only ADMINISTRATOR accounts
             have it to begin with.  If you want your users setting their own
             passwords, copy SET.PASSWORD into their VOC - the command already
             knows the difference and will still ask them for the current one.
           * You need this before the API will let anybody in: APILOGIN=1
             means an account with no password cannot be reached at all.

17 Aug 26  A program on another computer can reach SD again, over ssh.
           * SD can now listen for API (SDClient) connections.  It is OFF
             until you turn it on: put APIPORT=4243 in sd.conf and restart SD.
             Nothing is opened on a system that does not ask for it.
           * The port is bound to this machine only.  Reach it from elsewhere
             by forwarding it over ssh, which is how SD is meant to be used
             from off the machine:
                 ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 <user>@<host>
             then point your client at 127.0.0.1 port 4243 on your own end.
           * Your program still signs in with an account name and password,
             and can only enter accounts it has been granted, exactly as a
             person at a terminal can.  Opening the port does not hand
             anybody your data.
           * ON LINUX THIS JOB WAS DONE BY xinetd OR systemd.  Windows has
             neither, so SD now does it itself; you do not install or
             configure anything else.
           * SAY CONFIG('APIPORT') to see the port SD is listening on.
           * Note for anyone moving a configuration file from a Linux
             installation: the Linux build reaches SD through a socket file
             under /tmp, which does not exist here and cannot be forwarded on
             Windows.  APIPORT replaces it.

17 Aug 26  Connecting a program to SD on the same machine now actually works.
           * SDConnectLocal() in the client library opens a session on this
             computer with no network and no password: SD takes your identity
             from the account your program is running as.
           * It was reported as built once before and did not work.  It does
             now - a session opens, commands run, and an account you have not
             been granted is refused.
           * If you wrote anything against it in the last day, no change is
             needed on your side; the fix is inside the library.

17 Aug 26  An SD administrator's account now holds the administration verbs,
           and the tier an account was given is remembered.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR now puts CREATE.ACCOUNT,
             DELETE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT, UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, GRANT, REVOKE,
             LIST.GRANTS, UNLOCK and ENCRYPT.FIELD into the account's own VOC.
             Until now they were in SDSYS alone, so an administrator had to
             LOGTO SDSYS before they could administer anything.
           * The verbs still check for themselves that you are an
             administrator, so having them in a VOC is not what permits them.
           * MODIFY, COMPILE.DICT, CD, GENERATE and PHANTOM have joined the
             list a standard account does not get.  They edit records, compile
             dictionaries or start background programs, which is work for a
             programmer.
           * WHICH TIER AN ACCOUNT WAS CREATED AS IS NOW RECORDED, in field 5
             of its record in the ACCOUNTS file.  Say UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, or
             answer Yes when a new release offers to update your VOC, and a
             standard account no longer gets the withheld verbs back.  That
             was a way round the whole thing and needed no privilege.
           * Accounts created before this have nothing in field 5, and are
             treated as they always were - a VOC update gives them every verb.
           * The account of the person who installs SD is created as an
             administrator, so their VOC is complete.

17 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT has a PROGRAMMER keyword, and a plain account is
           now smaller than it was.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> PROGRAMMER gives the account the full
             set of verbs: BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, ED, EDIT, SED, COPY, COPYP,
             DELETE.CATALOG, SH and !.  ADMINISTRATOR includes all of that.
           * WITHOUT EITHER KEYWORD the account no longer gets those verbs.
             It can run an application; it cannot compile, catalogue, edit or
             shell out.  Until now every account got exactly the same verbs
             whichever keyword you used.
           * If somebody needs one of them, copy the verb into their VOC from
             SDSYS - that is a normal COPY and takes a moment.  This is a
             starting posture, not a lock.
           * The list lives in SDSYS tier.policy as the record omit.standard
             (it was NEWVOC/TIER.OMIT.STANDARD until 13 Sep 26 - see that
             date's entry), so you can change what a standard account starts
             with.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED.  This applies to accounts made
             from now on.

17 Aug 26  MICRO and the language verbs are gone.
           * MICRO launched an external editor, which is a way out of SD onto
             the machine underneath it.  It is removed for the same reason SH
             is restricted.  REVERSED ON 26 Aug 26 - see the EDIT entry at the
             top.  A full-screen editor is back, under the name EDIT, limited
             to programmer and administrator accounts.
           * NLS, SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE are removed: SD is English
             only, and they were the only callers of the message-language
             machinery.
           * If you had a use for MICRO, say so - an administrator can put the
             verb back into an account's VOC, and the program behind it is
             still installed.  (SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE are removed for
             good in W1.1-1, and NLS is back - see the 29 Sep 26 entry at the
             top.)

17 Aug 26  Connecting a program to SD on the same machine now works.
           * SDConnectLocal() in the client library opens a connection to SD
             on this computer, with no network and no password: SD identifies
             you from the account you are running as.
           * It could not have worked before, on this or any other version.
             The library started SD with the name of a pipe, and SD only
             understood a pair of file numbers, so it stopped immediately.
             The library also looked for sd.exe inside the database folder
             rather than where it is installed.
           * The client library's own README and user guide said this function
             was not provided on Windows.  They were wrong, and are corrected.

17 Aug 26  The API only lets you into accounts you have been granted.
           * Connecting to an account through the API now applies the same
             rule LOGTO applies inside SD: you may enter an account if you
             are a member of the Windows group SD created for it.  GRANT and
             REVOKE are how that membership is given and taken away.
           * Until now an API session that authenticated could switch to ANY
             account by name.  The check existed before the Windows port and
             was lost in it, because it tested a Linux group.
           * A refusal reads "User not allowed in requested account", which
             is also what you get for an account that does not exist.  That
             is deliberate: it stops the API being used to find out which
             accounts are there.
           * SDSYS is not reachable through the API, by design.  Administra-
             tion needs elevation and an API session cannot have it.

17 Aug 26  The API checks the SD account password, not a Windows one.
           * Set it with SET.PASSWORD, from inside SD.  An account that has
             never had one set cannot be reached through the API at all.
           * This replaces a check against the operating system's own password
             file, which does not exist on Windows - so the API could not
             accept a login on this platform at any point before now.  It
             refused everyone rather than letting anyone in, but it refused
             everyone.
           * The ssh tunnel is still the first gate and is unchanged.  Two
             gates, not one: reaching the machine is not reaching SD.
           * NOT YET USABLE END TO END.  The transport the Linux client uses -
             ssh forwarding a local port to a UNIX socket - cannot work on
             Windows, so this fixes the login and not yet the connection.

16 Aug 26  The CREATUSR configuration parameter is gone.
           * CONFIG no longer lists it, and config('CREATUSR') no longer
             returns a value.  It stopped having any effect on 14 Aug 26,
             when CREATE.ACCOUNT and DELETE.ACCOUNT stopped consulting it:
             SD has accounts, not accounts and users, so creating an account
             creates the operating system account and there is nothing to
             opt in to.
           * A CREATUSR line in sd.conf is still accepted and ignored, so a
             configuration file carrying one - your own, or one brought from
             a Linux install - still works.  You can delete the line.

16 Aug 26  The installer now ALWAYS installs an OpenSSH server, instead of
           offering it as a choice that was off by default.

           The reason is that accounts SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing
           else - they are refused at the console and over Remote Desktop.
           Without an ssh server on the machine those accounts had no way in
           at all, so an install that declined ssh was one that nobody but the
           person who installed it could use.  That is true even on a machine
           with no network and one person at the keyboard: you reach SD by
           connecting with ssh to "localhost", which works with no network of
           any kind.

           IF YOUR MACHINE ALREADY HAS AN SSH SERVER, SD LEAVES IT ALONE.  It
           installs nothing, restarts nothing, and changes neither its
           configuration nor its firewall rule.  That has not changed.

           Installing it is a download from Windows Update.  It can take
           several minutes with nothing on screen, and it usually asks for a
           restart afterwards; until you restart, no SD account except your
           own can sign in.  The installer says so before you start and again
           when it finishes.  If the download is blocked - by company policy,
           or a metered or absent connection - SD still installs and works for
           you, and the closing message tells you plainly that nobody else can
           use it until an ssh server exists.

16 Aug 26  New option: "Let other computers on your network connect to this
           one over ssh".  It is off by default, and off means ssh works only
           from the machine itself.

           This is stricter than what came before.  Installing the OpenSSH
           server makes Windows open port 22 to your local network, so ticking
           the old "install OpenSSH" box quietly did that too.  Now that the
           server is always installed, letting other computers reach it is a
           separate decision that you have to make deliberately.

           To change your mind later, from an elevated PowerShell prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\ssh-firewall.ps1" -Installed -Open

           and -Restrict puts it back to this machine only.  Uninstalling SD
           does not reopen the port.

16 Aug 26  The message at the end of the installer is about half as long.  It
           now tells you only what could not be known in advance - what
           happened to the ssh server, to the firewall setting and to your SD
           account - plus the one thing you have to act on, which is signing
           out and back in.  Everything else it used to say is on the new page
           at the start, where you can read it before deciding to install.

16 Aug 26  The installer now begins with a page listing everything it changes
           on your computer - the groups it creates, the sign-in rights it
           denies, the permissions it rewrites on the database, the service it
           installs, the PATH entry, and the OpenSSH install with its minutes
           and its restart.  You can read it and click Cancel before anything
           has happened.

           It is there because most of that was previously only discoverable
           after the fact, and because the ssh question above is one you were
           being asked to answer with no context.

16 Aug 26  The option to limit ssh to SD users and administrators has moved to
           the main options page.  It used to be a sub-option under "install
           OpenSSH", which no longer exists.  Its description now also says
           that it stops scp and sftp working, which it always did.

16 Aug 26  Uninstalling SD no longer leaves an empty entry on the system PATH.
           It had been removing the SD directory but leaving the separator
           behind, so every install-and-uninstall cycle added one more empty
           slot - visible as a run of semicolons in the PATH, which looks
           alarmingly as though SD was never added at all.  The next uninstall
           clears the whole accumulated run, not just the one it creates.

           If SD looks absent from your PATH just after installing, check a
           NEW command prompt first.  Windows tells running programs the PATH
           has changed, but a shell that is already open keeps the copy it
           started with.

16 Aug 26  SECURITY.  When SD does privileged work for you - creating an
           account, setting a password, editing a group - it writes a short
           script to a file and runs it, rather than putting a password on a
           command line where anyone could read it.  Those files were being
           written into the database directory, which every SD user can write
           to.  Two things followed, and the second is the serious one:

             * Another SD user could read a new Windows password out of the
               file in the moment before it was deleted.

             * Another SD user could REPLACE the script between SD writing it
               and SD running it, and their version would run with full
               administrator privilege.

           The scripts now go in a directory of their own, PSTMP inside the
           database directory, where each file belongs to the session that
           wrote it.  Other SD users cannot read those files or change them;
           administrators still can.  The installer creates the directory and
           empties it, so a script left behind by an interrupted session does
           not survive an upgrade.

           If that directory is missing, SD refuses to do the privileged work
           rather than falling back to the old location.  You would see the
           command fail rather than quietly running unprotected.

16 Aug 26  SD now keeps a log of the elevation helper's work, in the file
           "sd-elevate.log" in C:\ProgramData\SD, beside the service log.  It
           records when a helper started for a session, each script it was
           asked to run and the exit code that came back, and when it stopped.

           ONLY ADMINISTRATORS CAN READ OR WRITE IT.  Ordinary SD users are not
           on its permissions at all - they cannot read it, add to it, change
           it or delete it.  That is different from the audit trail, which SD
           users CAN add to because SD writes it as them; nothing unelevated
           ever writes this one.

           It is a diagnostic, not the audit trail.  If you want to know who
           obtained privilege and when, read "audit".  This file is for the
           question "the account was not created - what actually happened",
           which previously had no answer at all.

           If the file is missing, nothing is logged and SD does not create
           one.  That is deliberate: a log created on the fly would inherit
           permissions letting every SD user rewrite it, and a record of
           privileged work that its own subjects can edit is worse than none.
           A reinstall keeps whatever is already there.

16 Aug 26  LIST.GRANTS works again.  It had been answering "Cannot read the
           members of group <name>, status: 5" for every account, whoever
           asked.  Reading a group's membership needs no special privilege and
           never did; the check that was refusing it should not have been on
           that command at all.  GRANT and REVOKE were unaffected.

16 Aug 26  Entering SDSYS no longer needs an elevated window.  Type "sd" at an
           ordinary command prompt, then "LOGTO SDSYS", and Windows asks you to
           confirm - the usual "Do you want to allow this app to make changes?"
           box.  Say yes and you are in SDSYS and can do the work that needs
           privilege, such as CREATE.ACCOUNT.  Say no and the LOGTO is refused.
           Leaving SDSYS with another LOGTO gives the privilege up again.

           This reverses the 14 Aug entry below, which said an elevated window
           was the only route into SDSYS.  That was never the intention.  An
           elevated window makes EVERYTHING you type privileged; this way, only
           what SD does on your behalf is privileged, and only while you are
           in SDSYS.

           There is no ELEVATE command and there will not be one.  Entering
           SDSYS is the only thing that asks Windows for privilege.

           IT STILL DOES NOT WORK OVER ssh, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.  Windows
           draws that confirmation box on the desktop and an ssh session has
           no desktop, so the LOGTO is refused there.  Administering SD means
           being at the machine, or using a remote-control tool that gives you
           a real desktop session - and such a tool must be INSTALLED AS A
           SERVICE, or it cannot show you the box and your screen will appear
           to freeze instead.

           The audit trail records this: "ELEVATION GRANTED" when you consent,
           "ELEVATION RELEASED" when you leave SDSYS, and a refused LOGTO
           giving "elevation refused or unavailable" as the reason when you say
           no or when there is no desktop to ask on.

16 Aug 26  SD now keeps an audit trail, in the file "audit" beside the
           database in C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys.  It records every login,
           every login that was refused, every LOGTO, every LOGTO that was
           refused, and every GRANT and REVOKE, each with the date, the time
           and the Windows user it belonged to.  A line looks like

              2026-08-16 11:42:07 user=don uid=1 pid=8624 LOGTO account=SDSYS

           The refusals are the interesting half.  An entry saying somebody
           who is not an administrator asked for SDSYS by name, or asked for
           an account they have not been granted, is the thing worth seeing.

           This file is NOT the error log and does not behave like it.  The
           error log throws away its oldest half when it fills up; the audit
           file is renamed with the date and time and a new one started, so
           nothing is ever discarded.  Removing the old ones is your decision
           - SD will not do it for you, and they will accumulate.

           SD users can ADD to this file and can do nothing else to it.  They
           cannot read it, cannot change a record already in it, cannot empty
           it, and cannot rename or delete it - Windows itself refuses, not SD.
           So a user cannot quietly remove the line that records what they
           did.  Administrators can read it, and can do anything else to it
           as well: this raises the floor against ordinary users rather than
           trying to constrain somebody who owns the machine.

           Windows keeps its own separate record of account and group changes
           in the Security event log.  Read the two together.

16 Aug 26  SD now starts after a restart even if the last shutdown was not a
           clean one.  If SD is stopped abruptly - the power goes, or the
           process is killed - it leaves a shared memory segment behind, and
           on Windows that survives a reboot where on Linux it would not.
           SD used to refuse to start on the next boot and say "Run sd -stop
           to clear it", which meant the machine came up with SD unavailable
           to everybody until somebody logged in and typed it by hand.
           Nothing from before a restart can still be using that segment, so
           SD now discards it and starts normally, printing

              Discarding the shared segment left by the previous boot -
              SD did not shut down cleanly.

           This changes nothing while the machine is running: a segment that
           belongs to a live SD is still never touched, and "sd -start" still
           refuses to disturb a system that is already up.

15 Aug 26  SD now runs as a Windows service, called "String Database (SD)".
           The installer creates it and starts it, and Windows starts it
           again after every restart - so SD is simply up, and nobody has to
           type "sd -start" any more.  Stopping the service stops SD and ends
           every session on the machine, which is the same thing "sd -stop"
           has always done.  Uninstalling removes the service.

15 Aug 26  You now always log in to your OWN account, and move with LOGTO.
           Before, an elevated session that typed plain "sd" went straight
           into SDSYS, and "sd -Asomebodyelse" let an administrator open
           anyone's account without ever being in their own.  Now everybody
           arrives in the account named after their Windows login, including
           administrators, and reaches other accounts with

              LOGTO <account>

           which is where SD checks whether you are allowed in.  An
           administrator still reaches every account - just afterwards
           rather than instead.  "sd -A<name>" is refused unless <name> is
           your own account.

15 Aug 26  Three new verbs let you share an account with somebody else:

              GRANT <account> TO <user>
              REVOKE <account> FROM <user>
              LIST.GRANTS <account>

           All three need an elevated session, and they say so rather than
           failing obscurely.  There is one thing about them worth reading
           twice, because it is the most confusing part of how SD controls
           access: WINDOWS FIXES GROUP MEMBERSHIP WHEN YOU SIGN IN, so a
           grant does not reach the person until they sign out of Windows
           and back in - and, more importantly, SOMEBODY YOU HAVE JUST
           REVOKED KEEPS THE ACCOUNT UNTIL THEY DO THE SAME.  Both verbs
           print that reminder every time.

           "LIST.GRANTS" also shows the account's own user, which is always
           there and is not a grant.  It is listed anyway so that what you
           see matches the Windows group it is reporting.

           Behind this: entry to an account is membership of the Windows
           group named in the account's record, so these verbs edit that
           group and change nothing inside SD.  The old field 4 of an
           ACCOUNTS record, a list of accounts allowed in, has been removed -
           nothing had consulted it since 14 Aug.  "LIST ACCOUNTS" no longer
           shows a "Granted to" column; LIST.GRANTS answers that now.

15 Aug 26  The installer no longer suggests "net localgroup sdusers <name>
           /add" for giving somebody who already has a Windows account access
           to SD.  It did not work: being in sdusers lets you read SD's files,
           but signing in needs an SD account linked to your Windows one, and
           somebody added to the group and nothing else is turned away with
           "Account X not in register".  Use CREATE.ACCOUNT, which makes both
           halves together.

15 Aug 26  Connecting over ssh puts you straight into SD.  Sessions used to
           arrive at a Windows shell prompt, which an SD account has no use
           for and no business with.  Now every ssh session runs SD instead -
           including an administrator's, because an ssh session cannot be
           elevated in any case and lands in your own account rather than
           SDSYS.  The console, Remote Desktop and remote-control tools are
           NOT affected; this is ssh only.  It does mean NOTHING CAN BE RUN
           OVER ssh WITHOUT A PERSON AT IT: scp and sftp stop working, and so
           does "ssh thismachine somecommand" - the command is discarded and
           SD runs instead, so a script that expects output waits for ever at
           SD's ":" prompt.  Send OFF on standard input if you need such a
           session to end by itself.

15 Aug 26  Anything typed after "sd" now needs an elevated session.  That
           covers the switches you would type at a prompt - -start, -stop,
           -k, -l and -u among them - and it also covers a command:
           "sd LISTF" ran LISTF for anybody, and no longer does.
           Typing plain "sd" to use your own account is unchanged, and that is
           the whole of what an ordinary session needs: accounts SD creates
           arrive in SD over ssh anyway.  NOTE: SD does not start at boot, so
           after a restart somebody with administrator rights has to start it.

15 Aug 26  SD will not start a second time inside itself.  If you leave SD
           with SH and then type "sd" in that shell, it says so and returns
           you to the session you already have.  Worth knowing alongside it:
           SH itself needs an elevated session, so an ordinary account cannot
           leave SD this way at all, and one reached over ssh never can.

15 Aug 26  Installing SD now gives you an SD account.  Until now the person who
           installed SD was the one person who could not use it: every SD
           account brings its own Windows account, and yours already existed,
           so typing "sd" answered that your account was not in the register.
           The install now attaches an SD account to your Windows login, and
           the closing dialog says so.  Nothing to set, and no password: your
           Windows session is what identifies you.

15 Aug 26  An administrator is never confined to ssh.  Accounts SD creates
           normally sign in over ssh only, and that restriction was being
           applied to accounts it merely attached to an existing Windows login
           - including the installer's.  The effect was invisible until the
           next sign-in, and then locked the machine's own administrator out of
           their console and Remote Desktop.  Two rules now hold: an account SD
           only attaches to keeps exactly the Windows sign-in rights it already
           had, and no Windows administrator is ever put in the ssh-only group,
           whatever route created the account.  An administrator created inside
           SD can therefore use both the computer and SD, which is the point of
           creating one.

14 Aug 26  LIST ACCOUNTS says "Windows Group", not "Linux Group".  Existing
           databases keep the old label until they are rebuilt - the wording
           lives in the dictionary, which is built when SD is installed.

14 Aug 26  Accounts: DELETE.ACCOUNT is careful about your Windows accounts, and
           CREATE.ACCOUNT says what it will not do.
           * An SD account brings its own Windows account.  CREATE.ACCOUNT
             creates one, and it now REFUSES a name that already exists in
             Windows instead of failing with "Create User Failed, OS Error: 1",
             which is what it used to say and which read like a fault.  The one
             exception is the account of whoever installs SD, which the install
             attaches to their existing Windows login.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT only offers to remove the Windows account if SD
             created it.  If it was there before SD, it is left alone and the
             verb says so - deleting an SD account can no longer take somebody's
             Windows login with it.  If the Windows account has already gone,
             it says that too, and cleans up the SD side.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT now removes the account's sdu_ group, which it
             never actually did.  It asked Windows a question in a Linux way
             and was told the group did not exist, every time.
           * The same mistake made SD unable to tell whether a Windows user
             existed.  Both are fixed.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT removes the register entry last.  If something
             fails part way through, the account is still registered and you
             can simply run the verb again.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER x NO.QUERY now explains itself: creating a
             Windows account means setting its password, and that needs a
             prompt, so NO.QUERY cannot work for a user account.

14 Aug 26  "sd -start" and "sd -stop" no longer claim things about the sdwind
           daemon that are not true.  Both used to answer from the shared
           memory segment, which outlives the daemon, so both could report
           success while doing nothing.
           * "SD is already started" is now only said when the daemon really
             is running, and it tells you the process id - the Windows one,
             the number Task Manager and Stop-Process use.
           * If the segment is there but the daemon is not, which is what a
             killed or crashed SD leaves behind, "sd -start" says so and tells
             you to run "sd -stop" first.  It used to say "SD is already
             started" and do nothing, leaving the system unusable while the
             command that fixes it reported success.  It does not clear the
             wreckage for you: that would end any sessions still attached to
             it, and the count of those is printed so you can decide.
           * "sd -stop" now checks that the daemon actually stopped.  One
             started from an elevated session cannot be stopped from an
             ordinary one - Windows refuses the signal - and that used to be
             silent, leaving a daemon running against a segment nothing else
             could see.  You now get a warning naming the process id and the
             command to stop it with.
           * Known limit: if the segment has already gone, "sd -stop" has
             nowhere left to read the daemon's process id from and cannot
             report on it at all.  Check with "Get-Process sdwind".

14 Aug 26  The sign-on banner now says "SD for Windows" and no longer carries
           the "(AI modified)" note.

14 Aug 26  Signing in asks for nothing, and administration belongs to Windows.
           This reverses the 13 Aug identity entry further down.  Built,
           installed and VERIFIED END TO END: an ordinary account signs in over
           ssh and lands in its own account with nothing asked, an unelevated
           session is refused at both doors, LOGTO into SDSYS and into somebody
           else's account are both refused, and an elevated session reaches
           SDSYS.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 4 for what was observed.
           * Type "sd" and you are in your own account.  Windows has already
             signed you in, so SD asks it who you are and takes no password.
             The account is the one named after your Windows user name.
           * No SD account of that name means no login.  You are refused at the
             door rather than asked to name another account.
           * You must be in the sdusers group to use SD at all.
           * Run SD elevated - "as administrator" - and you land straight in
             SDSYS with nothing typed.  That is the only route into SDSYS.
             "sd -ASDSYS" and LOGTO SDSYS are both refused to a session that is
             not elevated.
           * Being an administrator is not enough by itself; you have to have
             used it.  An administrator who has not elevated is an ordinary SD
             user, which is what "sudo" means on Linux.
           * ELEVATION IS DELIBERATELY LOCAL.  Windows does not offer it over
             ssh, so SDSYS is reached from the machine itself or from a remote
             client that gives you a real desktop session.  Administrators
             having less remote access than ordinary users is the intent, not
             an oversight.
           * LOGTO into another account is allowed if you are a member of that
             account's group - the sdu_ group CREATE.ACCOUNT makes for it - so
             granting somebody a second account means adding them to that
             group.
           * GRANTS ARE GROUP MEMBERSHIP, and the "Granted to" list is on its
             way out.  ACCOUNTS field 4 is still there and LIST ACCOUNTS still
             shows it, but nothing reads it any more, so any grant recorded on
             13 or 14 Aug has stopped having an effect.  Give somebody an
             account by adding them to its sdu_ group instead.  The column will
             be removed, and GRANT and REVOKE verbs will do the group edit for
             you - neither exists yet, so for now it is Windows' own group
             tools, from an elevated session.
           * SOMEBODY YOU GRANT AN ACCOUNT TO MUST SIGN OUT AND BACK IN BEFORE
             THEY CAN USE IT.  Windows fixes your group membership when you sign
             in, so a session that was already open cannot see the change, and
             being elevated does not help - the elevated token comes from the
             same sign-in.  Until they sign back in, SD refuses them with "User
             not allowed in requested account", which looks exactly like the
             grant not having worked.
           * LOGTO SDSYS no longer asks for your password a second time.  The
             gate is applied when you sign in, so there is nothing to step up
             into, and messages 10030 and 10031 are no longer used.
           * Account passwords are NOT gone.  SET.PASSWORD, the $CRED register
             and the stored verifiers all remain, and become the API's
             credential rather than the console's.  Nothing on the console asks
             for one today.

14 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT is confirmed working, ssh restriction and all.
           * An account created with CREATE.ACCOUNT USER was signed in over
             ssh with the password SD set for it, and refused a local sign-in
             at the same moment.  So the account you get from the verb really
             is the ssh-only account it says it is.
           * Everything else it makes was checked too: the Windows account is
             created and enabled, it joins sdusers and sdsshonly and its own
             sdu_ group, it is NOT an administrator unless you say
             ADMINISTRATOR, and the account directory, VOC, BP and the
             register entry are all there.
           * You will see "Warning unable to setgid bit on Group Folder,
             status: 1" every time.  It is harmless and can be ignored - it is
             a leftover of the Linux version and does nothing on Windows.
           * Remote Desktop is still the one part nobody has watched refuse.

14 Aug 26  Reinstalling does not update your database, and it never did.
           * Worth knowing before you report a bug that is already fixed.  The
             installer deliberately leaves an existing database alone, so
             installing a newer SD over an older one replaces the programs but
             NOT the system files under C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys - which is
             where SD's own commands live.
           * So a fix to a command can be installed and still not be present.
             On 14 Aug 26 that cost a full investigation of a CREATE.ACCOUNT
             failure that had been fixed an hour earlier.
           * There is no upgrade path yet.  To get a genuinely current system,
             uninstall, delete C:\ProgramData\SD, and install again - which
             deletes your accounts and data, so it is only reasonable while
             there is nothing in there you want.

14 Aug 26  The installer can also limit who may ssh in at all.
           * A new tick box, under the OpenSSH one: "Also limit ssh to SD users
             and administrators".  It writes an AllowGroups line into
             sshd_config naming "sdusers" and your administrators group, so
             nobody else on the machine can ssh in even if they have a Windows
             account.  Off by default.
           * It is only offered when SD is installing OpenSSH itself.  If you
             already had an ssh server, SD does not touch its configuration -
             it may be there for something else.
           * Your original sshd_config is kept as sshd_config.before-sd, and
             uninstalling SD takes the line back out again.
           * If OpenSSH has just been installed and asked you to restart, there
             is no configuration file to edit yet and the installer will say
             so, with the one command to run after restarting.
           * CONFIRMED BY REAL SSH CONNECTIONS on 14 Aug 26.  An account in
             the allowed groups still got in; an account in neither was
             refused, with the reason written to the ssh server's log.  The
             machine's own administrator kept ssh throughout, which was the
             risk worth checking.
           * If you turn this on, be aware the person refused sees the same
             "Permission denied" as somebody who simply typed the wrong
             password.  The ssh server's log is the only place that says which
             it was.

14 Aug 26  The installer now tells you how accounts are really made.
           * Its closing message used to end with "net localgroup sdusers
             <name> /add", as though adding people to a group were the way to
             give them SD.  It is not, and it never made the Windows account.
           * It now leads with CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name>, which makes the
             Windows account and the SD account together and asks for the new
             password; says that this needs an elevated window, because
             creating a Windows user does; says that accounts made that way
             sign in over ssh only; and gives the ADMINISTRATOR keyword for an
             unrestricted one.
           * The net command is still there, as what it always was - the way to
             give SD to somebody who already has a Windows account.

14 Aug 26  The ssh-only restriction is confirmed working.
           * An account in "sdsshonly" was signed in over ssh, with a password
             and with a key, and ran a command - while being refused a local
             sign-in at the same moment.  So the restriction closes the screen
             in front of you without closing ssh, which is the whole point of
             it.
           * Remote Desktop is not yet confirmed.  The right that blocks it is
             applied, but nobody has watched it refuse a connection.
           * A NEW ACCOUNT MUST SIGN IN WITH ITS PASSWORD ONCE BEFORE AN SSH
             KEY WILL WORK.  This is how Windows behaves, not something SD
             chooses: an account that has never signed in has no profile
             folder, and ssh looks for authorized_keys inside it.  Sign in
             once with the password, then set up a key.

14 Aug 26  SD runs without "run as administrator", and starts by hand.
           * You no longer need an elevated window to use SD.  Being an
             administrator of the machine is what counts, not whether a
             particular window was started as one.
           * You DO have to sign out and back in once after installing, before
             SD can open its files.  The installer says so.  Until you do, it
             fails with "Error 13 allocating semaphores".
           * SD DOES NOT START AUTOMATICALLY when Windows starts.  Run
             "sd -start" after each restart.  Running SD as a Windows service
             is not done yet.
           * The installer's OpenSSH option may report that a restart is
             needed.  That means it worked - Windows registers the ssh service
             only after a reboot.  Restart, and ssh will be running.

14 Aug 26  Accounts SD creates can sign in over ssh only.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> now makes an account that can reach
             this machine ONLY over ssh.  It cannot sign in at the screen in
             front of you, and it cannot use Remote Desktop.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR is unchanged and is not
             restricted: administrators get an ordinary Windows account and
             can sign in locally as usual.
           * This works by putting the account in a new "sdsshonly" group.
             The installer denies that group the two local sign-in rights, so
             you can see who is restricted by looking at who is in the group.
             Removing someone from it lifts the restriction.
           * "sdusers" is unchanged and is a different thing - it grants
             access to the database files, and administrators are in it too.
           * Tested through ssh on 14 Aug 26 - see the entry above.

14 Aug 26  The installer's "install OpenSSH Server" option now works.
           * It never did.  The command it ran had a syntax error, so ticking
             the box installed nothing and reported nothing - no ssh server,
             no service, no message.  If you ticked it before and wondered why
             you had no ssh, that is why.
           * Be aware it can take several minutes.  Windows downloads OpenSSH
             on demand, and the installer cannot show progress while it does.
             It is not stuck.  Do not cancel it or end the Windows Modules
             Installer task, and expect to be asked to restart afterwards.

14 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT works on Windows, and can make an administrator.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> creates the Windows account as well
             as the SD one, prompts you for its password, and adds it to
             sdusers so it can reach the database.  It is a STANDARD local
             account.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR makes it a Windows
             administrator instead, which is what makes it an SD
             administrator.  Leave the keyword off unless you mean it.
           * Run it from an elevated session.  Creating a Windows account
             needs administrator rights that an ordinary session does not
             carry, and without them it stops with an access denied error.
           * The CREATUSR configuration parameter is no longer consulted when
             creating an account.  SD has accounts, not accounts and users, so
             creating an account is creating the operating system account and
             there was nothing to opt in to.
           * Accounts could not be created at all before this: the check on
             the account pathname rejected every native Windows path, so the
             Windows account was created and then everything else stopped with
             "Invalid account pathname".

14 Aug 26  If you are a Windows administrator, you are an SD administrator.
           * There is no separate SD administrator group and nothing to join.
             Whoever installs SD administers it, because installing requires
             administrator rights in the first place.
           * It is your ACCOUNT that counts, not whether a particular window
             is "run as administrator".  Actions that genuinely need elevation
             - creating a Windows user among them - still do.
           * The installer no longer offers to set an SDSYS password.  It is
             not what makes you an administrator any more, and the step did
             not work: it needed a running server that the installer never
             started.  Accounts still have passwords; SET.PASSWORD still sets
             them.
           * "sdusers" is unchanged and is still needed.  It grants access to
             the database FILES, which is a different question from who
             administers SD - and an administrator who has not elevated needs
             it just as an ordinary user does.

14 Aug 26  The background daemon is now called sdwind, and it starts.
           * sdlnxd is now sdwind.  The old name meant "SD Linux daemon",
             which is wrong for a Windows system.  If you have scripts or
             monitoring that look for an sdlnxd process, change them.
           * IT WAS NOT RUNNING AT ALL on an installed system.  "sd -start"
             looked for it inside the database directory, where the Linux
             version used to keep the programs, and on Windows the programs
             are in C:\Program Files\SD instead - so it was never found.
             Nothing reported this: SD started, and everything you could type
             at it worked, because the daemon does none of that.
           * What you were missing is the clearing up of sessions that end
             without logging out - a machine switched off, or a connection
             dropped.  Those left entries behind that nothing removed.
           * "sd -start" now says so if the daemon cannot be started, instead
             of reporting success.

14 Aug 26  There is a Windows installer.
           * SD installs to C:\Program Files\SD and its database to
             C:\ProgramData\SD, and appears in Settings > Apps like any
             other program.
           * The database directory is locked down during installation, to
             SYSTEM, Administrators and a new "sdusers" group.  Before this it
             was readable by every account on the machine.
           * You are added to sdusers automatically, but WINDOWS ONLY APPLIES
             GROUP MEMBERSHIP WHEN YOU SIGN IN - so sign out and back in
             before using SD, or it will fail with "Error 13 allocating
             semaphores".  Add other users with
             "net localgroup sdusers <name> /add".
           * Optionally installs OpenSSH Server so people on your network can
             reach SD.  Off by default, and not offered if you already have
             one, which it will not touch.
           * Uninstalling does NOT remove your accounts, data or sd.conf.  It
             offers to, and the answer defaults to no.
           * Installing over an existing database leaves it alone; upgrading
             one in place is not yet supported.

14 Aug 26  SD finds its configuration file by itself.
           * It is %ProgramData%\SD\sd.conf, normally
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf, and nothing need be set in the
             environment for an installed system to start.
           * The environment variable that overrides it is SD_CONFIG, for the
             server and the client library alike.  The server used to read
             SCARLET_CONFIG and the client SD_CONFIG, so setting the one you
             would expect configured only one of them.  SCARLET_CONFIG IS NO
             LONGER READ - if you set it, set SD_CONFIG instead.
           * The server no longer falls back to /etc/sd.conf, and the client
             library no longer falls back to sd.ini in the Windows directory.
             Writing to C:\Windows has needed administrator rights since
             Vista.

14 Aug 26  "sd -stop" no longer kills the program that ran it.
           * It sent SIGTERM to every entry in the user table without
             checking the process id was valid, and a process id of zero
             means "every process in my process group" - so stopping SD from
             a script or a terminal could terminate the script, the shell, or
             anything else running alongside it.  No error was reported.

14 Aug 26  An SD account can be installed anywhere, and the install no longer
           needs Python or a compiler on the target machine.
           * The system is now compiled when the install is built rather than
             when it is installed, so installing is a file copy.
           * New build scripts gplbld/bootstrap.py and
             "gplbld/stage.py --bootstrap".

14 Aug 26  Configuration file fixes.
           * A CRLF sd.conf no longer corrupts every text setting.  The
             carriage return was left on the end of the value, so SDSYS
             pointed at a directory that did not exist.  Files written by
             Notepad and most Windows editors were affected; numeric settings
             were not.
           * SH and SH1 may be up to 255 characters, was 80.  A longer value
             used to overwrite the settings stored after them, and the error
             named SORTMRG - a parameter the file need not even contain.
             Over-long values are now refused with a message that names the
             right setting.

14 Aug 26  SH, ! and OS.EXECUTE now run PowerShell instead of bash.
           * SD used to shell out through /bin/bash, which is not present on
             a Windows machine and is not part of an SD install, so anything
             that shelled out worked only where MSYS2 happened to be
             installed.  PowerShell ships with Windows, so this works
             everywhere.
           * Commands passed to OS.EXECUTE are PowerShell, not shell script.
             Existing programs that shell out will need their command text
             changed.
           * SH and SH1 in sd.conf name the shell and can be set to something
             else.  The path must not contain spaces.

14 Aug 26  Creating an SD account creates a Windows account again, as it
           created a Linux one before.  Deleting one deletes it.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER makes the Windows local user, adds it to
             sdusers, and creates the account's own local group.  It asks for
             the new user's Windows password itself and sets it; the account
             is created disabled and is enabled only once the password is
             set, so it is never briefly usable with no password.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT offers to delete the Windows user, and removes
             the account's group.  The user's profile directory under
             C:\Users is left alone.
           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD and DELETE add and remove Windows local group
             members.
           * THESE NEED AN ELEVATED SESSION.  Windows will not let an
             ordinary process create a user or change a local group, so
             account creation reports "OS Error: 5" unless SD was started
             elevated.  Everything that does not touch a Windows account
             works normally either way.
           * The Windows password is never placed on a command line, where
             any local user could read it.
           * Group membership is read from Windows directly.  It used to be
             read from /etc/group, which does not exist on this platform, so
             every membership test answered "no" - which, among other things,
             refused every login with "This user is not registered for SD
             use".

14 Aug 26  Pathnames containing spaces and backslashes are accepted where a
           pathname is checked before being passed to the operating system,
           so C:\Program Files\... no longer fails validation.

13 Aug 26  Embedded Python is removed.  SD for Windows is a back end data
           store reached through the API, and the interpreter was not part of
           that.
           * The SDEXT python keys, the SDPYOBJ function, and the PY_*
             subroutines in GPL.BP are all gone, along with SYSCOM/SDPYFUNC.H
             and the PY_* examples in the SDSYS BP file.  A program calling
             any of them will no longer compile.
           * Error codes -12001 to -12036 no longer exist.
           * The SDPYOBJ opcode is retired rather than reused, so existing
             compiled programs are unaffected unless they called it.
           * An installed system is about 40% smaller and needs three fewer
             support libraries.  Building no longer needs python-devel or
             gettext-devel.

13 Aug 26  An installed system no longer contains a copy of the C source, and
           does not need one to compile BASIC.
           * GPL.BP/APISRVR and GPL.BP/ERRTEXT each ran a build tool during
             compilation - REVSTAMP and ERRGEN - which read ./gplsrc from the
             account directory.  Both are now switched off, as CPROC's already
             was.  Compiling either program on a system without the C source
             used to fail, and in ERRGEN's case it emptied SYSCOM/ERR.H first.
           * New build script gplbld/gen_includes.py regenerates GPL.BP/
             REVSTAMP.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H and SYSCOM/ERR.H from the C headers.
             Run it after changing gplsrc/err.h or gplsrc/revstamp.h;
             "--check" reports what is out of date without writing anything.
           * 42 error codes had no message text - the crypto, SDEXT and
             embedded Python errors added since July 2024.  They have text
             now, so ERRTEXT and error displays name them instead of showing
             a bare number.
           * Those same 42 codes were defined in SYSCOM/ERR.H with the wrong
             sign and with C spelling.  They now match the C header:
             SD$Mem.Err is -10100, not SD_Mem_Err 10100.  No shipped program
             referred to them.
           * GPL.BP/OPGEN still reads ./gplsrc and so cannot be run on an
             installed system.  It is a developer tool for regenerating
             OPCODES.H and nothing calls it.

13 Aug 26  Administrator rights belong to the SDSYS account and to nothing
           else.
           * They are set on entry to SDSYS and given up on the way out, so
             they no longer follow a session into whatever account it moves
             to next, and being in a Windows group no longer confers them.
           * Only a program compiled $internal may set them, and only SDSYS
             may compile one.  Before this, any account could compile a three
             line program that made itself an administrator.
           * The no-password entry an administrator used to get for running an
             internal command is gone.
           * "sd" with no account named asks which account you want, instead
             of putting members of a Windows group straight into SDSYS.

13 Aug 26  Identity: accounts have their own passwords, and SD no longer
           consults operating system users or groups at all.
           * Every account carries a password.  Entry is by prompt, whether
             from "sd -Aname" at the shell or LOGTO inside SD.
           * New $CRED register in SDSYS holds a salt and an Argon2 verifier
             per account.  The password itself is never stored.
           * New verb SET.PASSWORD [account] sets one.
           * New ACCOUNTS field 4, ACC$USERS: the list of accounts allowed to
             LOGTO into that account.  Grants are recorded on the account
             being entered, so withdrawing one is a single edit.  Visible as
             "Granted to" in LIST ACCOUNTS, editable with MODIFY ACCOUNTS.
           * LOGTO SDSYS asks for your own password again, not an SDSYS one.
           * SDSYS reaches every account without exception.
           * LOGTO takes a registered account name only.  It used to accept a
             pathname, which reached an account directory without consulting
             its grant list.  APISRVR's account switch is name-only for the
             same reason.
           * @LOGNAME does not change on LOGTO, so what is done in another
             account stays attributed to whoever logged in.
           * The sdusers group test at login and the ACC$GROUP group test on
             LOGTO are gone, along with the sdadmins group requirement for
             ordinary use.  Knowing the SDSYS password is what confers
             administrator rights now.
           * New messages 10030 and 10031.

13 Aug 26  Accounts move to C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts and
           C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts (USRDIR and GRPDIR in sd.conf).
           /home/sd made sense while an SD account was an operating system
           user, which it no longer is.

13 Aug 26  Paths may now be written the Windows way.  A drive letter is
           understood as an absolute root and a backslash as a separator, so
           C:\ProgramData\SD, C:/ProgramData/SD and /c/ProgramData/SD all
           reach the same place (sdrealpath in linuxlb.c).  Every drive-letter
           spelling previously failed with "file not found".

13 Aug 26  Windows build.  Shared memory and semaphores use the POSIX calls
           rather than System V, which Windows does not have.  The client
           library is a native Win32 DLL and needs no MSYS2 runtime.

13 Aug 26  No binaries are kept in the repository.  Everything is built from
           source, so installing means building.

